msgid ""
"In our example, the first two lines of the function definition are the "
"@dfn{header}.  Its purpose is to state the function's name and say how it is "
"called:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:629
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int\n"
"fib (int n)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:635
msgid ""
"says that the function returns an integer (type @code{int}), its name is "
"@code{fib}, and it takes one argument named @code{n} which is also an "
"integer.  (Data types will be explained later, in @ref{Primitive Types}.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:638
#, no-wrap
msgid "function body"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:639
#, no-wrap
msgid "recursion"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:647
msgid ""
"The rest of the function definition is called the @dfn{function body}.  Like "
"every function body, this one starts with @samp{@{}, ends with @samp{@}}, "
"and contains zero or more @dfn{statements} and @dfn{declarations}.  "
"Statements specify actions to take, whereas declarations define names of "
"variables, functions, and so on.  Each statement and each declaration ends "
"with a semicolon (@samp{;})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:654
msgid ""
"Statements and declarations often contain @dfn{expressions}; an expression "
"is a construct whose execution produces a @dfn{value} of some data type, but "
"may also take actions through ``side effects'' that alter subsequent "
"execution.  A statement, by contrast, does not have a value; it affects "
"further execution of the program only through the actions it takes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:658
msgid ""
"This function body contains no declarations, and just one statement, but "
"that one is a complex statement in that it contains nested statements.  This "
"function uses two kinds of statements:"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:660 c-intro-and-ref.texi:8125
#, no-wrap
msgid "return"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:663
msgid ""
"The @code{return} statement makes the function return immediately.  It looks "
"like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:666 c-intro-and-ref.texi:8145
#, no-wrap
msgid "return @var{value};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:671
msgid ""
"Its meaning is to compute the expression @var{value} and exit the function, "
"making it return whatever value that expression produced.  For instance,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:674 c-intro-and-ref.texi:723
#, no-wrap
msgid "return 1;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:678
msgid "returns the integer 1 from the function, and"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:681 c-intro-and-ref.texi:730
#, no-wrap
msgid "return fib (n - 1) + fib (n - 2);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:686
msgid ""
"returns a value computed by performing two function calls as specified and "
"adding their results."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:687
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{if}@dots{}@code{else}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:691
msgid ""
"The @code{if}@dots{}@code{else} statement is a @dfn{conditional}.  Each time "
"it executes, it chooses one of its two substatements to execute and ignores "
"the other.  It looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:697
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"if (@var{condition})\n"
"  @var{if-true-statement}\n"
"else\n"
"  @var{if-false-statement}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:702
msgid ""
"Its meaning is to compute the expression @var{condition} and, if it's "
"``true,'' execute @var{if-true-statement}.  Otherwise, execute "
"@var{if-false-statement}.  @xref{if-else Statement}."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:708
msgid ""
"Inside the @code{if}@dots{}@code{else} statement, @var{condition} is simply "
"an expression.  It's considered ``true'' if its value is nonzero.  (A "
"comparison operation, such as @code{n <= 2}, produces the value 1 if it's "
"``true'' and 0 if it's ``false.'' @xref{Numeric Comparisons}.)  Thus,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:714
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"if (n <= 2)\n"
"  return 1;\n"
"else\n"
"  return fib (n - 1) + fib (n - 2);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:720
msgid ""
"first tests whether the value of @code{n} is less than or equal to 2.  If "
"so, the expression @code{n <= 2} has the value 1.  So execution continues "
"with the statement"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:727
msgid "Otherwise, execution continues with this statement:"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:734
msgid ""
"Each of these statements ends the execution of the function and provides a "
"value for it to return.  @xref{return Statement}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:742
msgid ""
"Calculating @code{fib} using ordinary integers in C works only for @var{n} < "
"47 because the value of @code{fib (47)} is too large to fit in type "
"@code{int}.  In GNU C, type @code{int} holds 32 bits (@pxref{Integer "
"Types}), so the addition operation that tries to add @code{fib (46)} and "
"@code{fib (45)} cannot deliver the correct result.  This occurrence is "
"called @dfn{integer overflow}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:747
msgid ""
"Overflow can manifest itself in various ways, but one thing that can't "
"possibly happen is to produce the correct value, since that can't fit in the "
"space for the value.  @xref{Integer Overflow}, for more details about this "
"situation."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:749
msgid "@xref{Functions}, for a full explanation about functions."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:751
#, no-wrap
msgid "The Stack, And Stack Overflow"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:752
#, no-wrap
msgid "stack"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:753 c-intro-and-ref.texi:757
#, no-wrap
msgid "stack frame"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:754
#, no-wrap
msgid "stack overflow"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:755
#, no-wrap
msgid "recursion, drawbacks of"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:763
msgid ""
"Recursion has a drawback: there are limits to how many nested levels of "
"function calls a program can make.  In C, each function call allocates a "
"block of memory which it uses until the call returns.  C allocates these "
"blocks consecutively within a large area of memory known as the @dfn{stack}, "
"so we refer to the blocks as @dfn{stack frames}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:767
msgid ""
"The size of the stack is limited; if the program tries to use too much, that "
"causes the program to fail because the stack is full.  This is called "
"@dfn{stack overflow}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:768
#, no-wrap
msgid "crash"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:769
#, no-wrap
msgid "segmentation fault"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:776
msgid ""
"Stack overflow on GNU/Linux typically manifests itself as the @dfn{signal} "
"named @code{SIGSEGV}, also known as a ``segmentation fault.'' By default, "
"this signal terminates the program immediately, rather than letting the "
"program try to recover, or reach an expected ending point.  (We commonly say "
"in this case that the program ``crashes.'')  @xref{Signals}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:783
msgid ""
"It is inconvenient to observe a crash by passing too large an argument to "
"recursive Fibonacci, because the program would run a long time before it "
"crashes.  This algorithm is simple but ridiculously slow: in calculating "
"@code{fib (@var{n})}, the number of (recursive) calls @code{fib (1)} or "
"@code{fib (2)} that it makes equals the final result."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:786
msgid ""
"However, you can observe stack overflow very quickly if you use this "
"function instead:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:796
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int\n"
"fill_stack (int n)\n"
"@{\n"
"  if (n <= 1)  /* @r{This limits the depth of recursion.}  */\n"
"    return 1;\n"
"  else\n"
"    return fill_stack (n - 1);\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:804
msgid ""
"Under gNewSense GNU/Linux on the Lemote Yeeloong, without optimization and "
"using the default configuration, an experiment showed there is enough stack "
"space to do 261906 nested calls to that function.  One more, and the stack "
"overflows and the program crashes.  On another platform, with a different "
"configuration, or with a different function, the limit might be bigger or "
"smaller."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:806
#, no-wrap
msgid "Example: Iterative Fibonacci"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:807
#, no-wrap
msgid "iterative Fibonacci function"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:808
#, no-wrap
msgid "Fibonacci function, iterative"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:815
msgid ""
"Here's a much faster algorithm for computing the same Fibonacci series.  It "
"is faster for two reasons.  First, it uses @dfn{iteration} (that is, "
"repetition or looping) rather than recursion, so it doesn't take time for a "
"large number of function calls.  But mainly, it is faster because the number "
"of repetitions is small---only @code{@var{n}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:825
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int\n"
"fib (int n)\n"
"@{\n"
"  int last = 1;   /* @r{Initial value is @code{fib (1)}.}  */\n"
"  int prev = 0;   /* @r{Initial value controls @code{fib (2)}.}  */\n"
"  int i;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:842
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  for (i = 1; i < n; ++i)\n"
"    /* @r{If @code{n} is 1 or less, the loop runs zero times,}  */\n"
"    /* @r{since in that case @code{i < n} is false the first time.}  */\n"
"    @{\n"
"      /* @r{Now @code{last} is @code{fib (@code{i})}}\n"
"         @r{and @code{prev} is @code{fib (@code{i} - 1)}.}  */\n"
"      /* @r{Compute @code{fib (@code{i} + 1)}.}  */\n"
"      int next = prev + last;\n"
"      /* @r{Shift the values down.}  */\n"
"      prev = last;\n"
"      last = next;\n"
"      /* @r{Now @code{last} is @code{fib (@code{i} + 1)}}\n"
"         @r{and @code{prev} is @code{fib (@code{i})}.}\n"
"         @r{But that won't stay true for long,}\n"
"         @r{because we are about to increment @code{i}.}  */\n"
"    @}\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:845
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  return last;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:851
msgid ""
"This definition computes @code{fib (@var{n})} in a time proportional to "
"@code{@var{n}}.  The comments in the definition explain how it works: it "
"advances through the series, always keeps the last two values in @code{last} "
"and @code{prev}, and adds them to get the next value."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:853
msgid "Here are the additional C features that this definition uses:"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:855
#, no-wrap
msgid "Internal blocks"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:860
msgid ""
"Within a function, wherever a statement is called for, you can write a "
"@dfn{block}.  It looks like @code{@{ @r{@dots{}} @}} and contains zero or "
"more statements and declarations.  (You can also use additional blocks as "
"statements in a block.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:863
msgid ""
"The function body also counts as a block, which is why it can contain "
"statements and declarations."
msgstr ""

#. type: ifnottex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:865 c-intro-and-ref.texi:7930
msgid "@xref{Blocks}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:866
#, no-wrap
msgid "Declarations of local variables"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:873
msgid ""
"This function body contains declarations as well as statements.  There are "
"three declarations directly in the function body, as well as a fourth "
"declaration in an internal block.  Each starts with @code{int} because it "
"declares a variable whose type is integer.  One declaration can declare "
"several variables, but each of these declarations is simple and declares "
"just one variable."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:880
msgid ""
"Variables declared inside a block (either a function body or an internal "
"block) are @dfn{local variables}.  These variables exist only within that "
"block; their names are not defined outside the block, and exiting the block "
"deallocates their storage.  This example declares four local variables: "
"@code{last}, @code{prev}, @code{i}, and @code{next}."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:882
msgid "The most basic local variable declaration looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:885
#, no-wrap
msgid "@var{type} @var{variablename};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:888 c-intro-and-ref.texi:906
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5821
msgid "For instance,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:891
#, no-wrap
msgid "int i;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:896
msgid ""
"declares the local variable @code{i} as an integer.  @xref{Variable "
"Declarations}."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:900
msgid ""
"When you declare a variable, you can also specify its initial value, like "
"this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:903
#, no-wrap
msgid "@var{type} @var{variablename} = @var{value};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:909
#, no-wrap
msgid "int last = 1;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:914
msgid ""
"declares the local variable @code{last} as an integer (type @code{int}) and "
"starts it off with the value 1.  @xref{Initializers}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:915
#, no-wrap
msgid "Assignment"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:918
msgid ""
"Assignment: a specific kind of expression, written with the @samp{=} "
"operator, that stores a new value in a variable or other place.  Thus,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:921
#, no-wrap
msgid "@var{variable} = @var{value}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:926
msgid ""
"is an expression that computes @code{@var{value}} and stores the value in "
"@code{@var{variable}}.  @xref{Assignment Expressions}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:927
#, no-wrap
msgid "Expression statements"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:930
msgid ""
"An expression statement is an expression followed by a semicolon.  That "
"computes the value of the expression, then ignores the value."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:934
msgid ""
"An expression statement is useful when the expression changes some data or "
"has other side effects---for instance, with function calls, or with "
"assignments as in this example.  @xref{Expression Statement}."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:944
msgid ""
"Using an expression with no side effects in an expression statement is "
"pointless; for instance, the expression statement @code{x;} would examine "
"the value of @code{x} and ignore it.  That is not useful.@footnote{Computing "
"an expression and ignoring the result can be useful in peculiar cases.  For "
"instance, dereferencing a pointer and ignoring the value is a way to cause a "
"fault if a pointer value is invalid.  @xref{Signals}.  But you may need to "
"declare the pointer target @code{volatile} or the dereference may be "
"optimized away.  @xref{volatile}.}"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:945
#, no-wrap
msgid "Increment operator"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:949
msgid ""
"The increment operator is @samp{++}.  @code{++i} is an expression that is "
"short for @code{i = i + 1}.  @xref{Increment/Decrement}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:950
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{for} statements"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:953
msgid ""
"A @code{for} statement is a clean way of executing a statement "
"repeatedly---a @dfn{loop} (@pxref{Loop Statements}).  Specifically,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:957
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"for (i = 1; i < n; ++i)\n"
"  @var{body}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:962
msgid ""
"means to start by doing @code{i = 1} (set @code{i} to one) to prepare for "
"the loop.  The loop itself consists of"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:966
msgid "Testing @code{i < n} and exiting the loop if that's false."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:969
msgid "Executing @var{body}."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:972
msgid "Advancing the loop (executing @code{++i}, which increments @code{i})."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:978
msgid ""
"The net result is to execute @var{body} with 1 in @code{i}, then with 2 in "
"@code{i}, and so on, stopping just before the repetition where @code{i} "
"would equal @code{n}.  If @code{n} is less than 1, the loop will execute the "
"body zero times."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:982
msgid ""
"The body of the @code{for} statement must be one and only one statement.  "
"You can't write two statements in a row there; if you try to, only the first "
"of them will be treated as part of the loop."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:985
msgid ""
"The way to put multiple statements in such a place is to group them with a "
"block, and that's what we do in this example."
msgstr ""

#. type: chapter
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:988
#, no-wrap
msgid "A Complete Program"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:989
#, no-wrap
msgid "complete example program"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:990
#, no-wrap
msgid "example program, complete"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:995
msgid ""
"It's all very well to write a Fibonacci function, but you cannot run it by "
"itself.  It is a useful program, but it is not a complete program."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1000
msgid ""
"In this chapter we present a complete program that contains the @code{fib} "
"function.  This example shows how to make the program start, how to make it "
"finish, how to do computation, and how to print a result."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1009
#, no-wrap
msgid "Complete Program Example"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1013
msgid ""
"Here is the complete program that uses the simple, recursive version of the "
"@code{fib} function (@pxref{Recursive Fibonacci}):"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1016
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"#include <stdio.h>\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1025
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int\n"
"fib (int n)\n"
"@{\n"
"  if (n <= 2)  /* @r{This avoids infinite recursion.}  */\n"
"    return 1;\n"
"  else\n"
"    return fib (n - 1) + fib (n - 2);\n"
"@}\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1033
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int\n"
"main (void)\n"
"@{\n"
"  printf (\"Fibonacci series item %d is %d\\n"
"\",\n"
"          20, fib (20));\n"
"  return 0;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1037
msgid "This program prints a message that shows the value of @code{fib (20)}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1039
msgid "Now for an explanation of what that code means."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1041
#, no-wrap
msgid "Complete Program Explanation"
msgstr ""

#. type: ifnottex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1046
msgid ""
"Here's the explanation of the code of the example in the previous section."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1050
msgid ""
"This sample program prints a message that shows the value of @code{fib "
"(20)}, and exits with code 0 (which stands for successful execution)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1055
msgid ""
"Every C program is started by running the function named @code{main}.  "
"Therefore, the example program defines a function named @code{main} to "
"provide a way to start it.  Whatever that function does is what the program "
"does.  @xref{The main Function}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1060
msgid ""
"The @code{main} function is the first one called when the program runs, but "
"it doesn't come first in the example code.  The order of the function "
"definitions in the source code makes no difference to the program's meaning."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1066
msgid ""
"The initial call to @code{main} always passes certain arguments, but "
"@code{main} does not have to pay attention to them.  To ignore those "
"arguments, define @code{main} with @code{void} as the parameter list.  "
"(@code{void} as a function's parameter list normally means ``call with no "
"arguments,'' but @code{main} is a special case.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1072
msgid ""
"The function @code{main} returns 0 because that is the conventional way for "
"@code{main} to indicate successful execution.  It could instead return a "
"positive integer to indicate failure, and some utility programs have "
"specific conventions for the meaning of certain numeric @dfn{failure "
"codes}.  @xref{Values from main}."
msgstr ""

#. type: code{#1}
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1073
#, no-wrap
msgid "printf"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1079
msgid ""
"The simplest way to print text in C is by calling the @code{printf} "
"function, so here we explain very briefly what that function does.  For a "
"full explanation of @code{printf} and the other standard I/O functions, see "
"@ref{Input/Output on Streams, The GNU C Library, , libc, The GNU C Library "
"Reference Manual}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1080
#, no-wrap
msgid "standard output"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1087
msgid ""
"The first argument to @code{printf} is a @dfn{string constant} "
"(@pxref{String Constants}) that is a template for output.  The function "
"@code{printf} copies most of that string directly as output, including the "
"newline character at the end of the string, which is written as @samp{\\n"
"}.  The output goes to the program's @dfn{standard output} destination, "
"which in the usual case is the terminal."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1094
msgid ""
"@samp{%} in the template introduces a code that substitutes other text into "
"the output.  Specifically, @samp{%d} means to take the next argument to "
"@code{printf} and substitute it into the text as a decimal number.  (The "
"argument for @samp{%d} must be of type @code{int}; if it isn't, "
"@code{printf} will malfunction.)  So the output is a line that looks like "
"this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1097
#, no-wrap
msgid "Fibonacci series item 20 is 6765\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1108
msgid ""
"This program does not contain a definition for @code{printf} because it is "
"defined by the C library, which makes it available in all C programs.  "
"However, each program does need to @dfn{declare} @code{printf} so it will be "
"called correctly.  The @code{#include} line takes care of that; it includes "
"a @dfn{header file} called @file{stdio.h} into the program's code.  That "
"file is provided by the operating system and it contains declarations for "
"the many standard input/output functions in the C library, one of which is "
"@code{printf}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1111
msgid ""
"Don't worry about header files for now; we'll explain them later in "
"@ref{Header Files}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1115
msgid ""
"The first argument of @code{printf} does not have to be a string constant; "
"it can be any string (@pxref{Strings}).  However, using a constant is the "
"most common case."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1117
#, no-wrap
msgid "Complete Program, Line by Line"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1123
msgid ""
"Here's the same example, explained line by line.  @strong{Beginners, do you "
"find this helpful or not? Would you prefer a different layout for the "
"example? Please tell rms@@gnu.org.}"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1128
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"#include <stdio.h>      /* @r{Include declaration of usual} */\n"
"                        /*   @r{I/O functions such as @code{printf}.}  */\n"
"                        /* @r{Most programs need these.}  */\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1140
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int                     /* @r{This function returns an @code{int}.}  */\n"
"fib (int n)             /* @r{Its name is @code{fib};}  */\n"
"                        /*   @r{its argument is called @code{n}.}  */\n"
"@{                       /* @r{Start of function body.}  */\n"
"  /* @r{This stops the recursion from being infinite.}  */\n"
"  if (n <= 2)           /* @r{If @code{n} is 1 or 2,}  */\n"
"    return 1;           /*   @r{make @code{fib} return 1.}  */\n"
"  else                  /* @r{Otherwise, add the two previous}  */\n"
"                        /* @r{Fibonacci numbers.}  */\n"
"    return fib (n - 1) + fib (n - 2);\n"
"@}\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1148
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int                     /* @r{This function returns an @code{int}.}  */\n"
"main (void)             /* @r{Start here; ignore arguments.}  */\n"
"@{                       /* @r{Print message with numbers in it.}  */\n"
"  printf (\"Fibonacci series item %d is %d\\n"
"\",\n"
"          20, fib (20));\n"
"  return 0;             /* @r{Terminate program, report success.}  */\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1151
#, no-wrap
msgid "Compiling the Example Program"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1152
#, no-wrap
msgid "compiling"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1153
#, no-wrap
msgid "executable file"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1158
msgid ""
"To run a C program requires converting the source code into an "
"@dfn{executable file}.  This is called @dfn{compiling} the program, and the "
"command to do that using GNU C is @command{gcc}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1162
msgid ""
"This example program consists of a single source file.  If we call that file "
"@file{fib1.c}, the complete command to compile it is this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1165
#, no-wrap
msgid "gcc -g -O -o fib1 fib1.c\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1171
msgid ""
"Here, @option{-g} says to generate debugging information, @option{-O} says "
"to optimize at the basic level, and @option{-o fib1} says to put the "
"executable program in the file @file{fib1}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1174
msgid ""
"To run the program, use its file name as a shell command.  For instance,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1177
#, no-wrap
msgid "./fib1\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1182
msgid ""
"However, unless you are sure the program is correct, you should expect to "
"need to debug it.  So use this command,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1185
#, no-wrap
msgid "gdb fib1\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1191
msgid ""
"which starts the GDB debugger (@pxref{Sample Session, Sample Session, A "
"Sample GDB Session, gdb, Debugging with GDB}) so you can run and debug the "
"executable program @code{fib1}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1199
msgid ""
"Richard Stallman's advice, from personal experience, is to turn to the "
"debugger as soon as you can reproduce the problem.  Don't try to avoid it by "
"using other methods instead---occasionally they are shortcuts, but usually "
"they waste an unbounded amount of time.  With the debugger, you will surely "
"find the bug in a reasonable time; overall, you will get your work done "
"faster.  The sooner you get serious and start the debugger, the sooner you "
"are likely to find the bug."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1202
msgid ""
"@xref{Compilation}, for an introduction to compiling more complex programs "
"which consist of more than one source file."
msgstr ""

#. type: chapter
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1204
#, no-wrap
msgid "Storage and Data"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1205
#, no-wrap
msgid "bytes"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1206
#, no-wrap
msgid "storage organization"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1207
#, no-wrap
msgid "memory organization"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1212
msgid ""
"Storage in C programs is made up of units called @dfn{bytes}.  A byte is the "
"smallest unit of storage that can be used in a first-class manner."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1218
msgid ""
"On nearly all computers, a byte consists of 8 bits.  There are a few "
"peculiar computers (mostly ``embedded controllers'' for very small systems) "
"where a byte is longer than that, but this manual does not try to explain "
"the peculiarity of those computers; we assume that a byte is 8 bits."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1226
msgid ""
"Every C data type is made up of a certain number of bytes; that number is "
"the data type's @dfn{size}.  @xref{Type Size}, for details.  The types "
"@code{signed char} and @code{unsigned char} are one byte long; use those "
"types to operate on data byte by byte.  @xref{Signed and Unsigned Types}.  "
"You can refer to a series of consecutive bytes as an array of @code{char} "
"elements; that's what a character string looks like in memory.  @xref{String "
"Constants}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1233
msgid ""
"So far we've presented programs that operate on integers.  In this chapter "
"we'll present examples of handling non-integral numbers and arrays of "
"numbers."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1242
#, no-wrap
msgid "An Example with Non-Integer Numbers"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1243
#, no-wrap
msgid "floating point example"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1251
msgid ""
"Here's a function that operates on and returns @dfn{floating point} numbers "
"that don't have to be integers.  Floating point represents a number as a "
"fraction together with a power of 2.  (For more detail, "
"@pxref{Floating-Point Data Types}.)  This example calculates the average of "
"three floating point numbers that are passed to it as arguments:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1258
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"double\n"
"average_of_three (double a, double b, double c)\n"
"@{\n"
"  return (a + b + c) / 3;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1263
msgid ""
"The values of the parameter @var{a}, @var{b} and @var{c} do not have to be "
"integers, and even when they happen to be integers, most likely their "
"average is not an integer."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1266
msgid ""
"@code{double} is the usual data type in C for calculations on floating-point "
"numbers."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1269
msgid ""
"To print a @code{double} with @code{printf}, we must use @samp{%f} instead "
"of @samp{%d}:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1273
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"printf (\"Average is %f\\n"
"\",\n"
"        average_of_three (1.1, 9.8, 3.62));\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1279
msgid ""
"The code that calls @code{printf} must pass a @code{double} for printing "
"with @samp{%f} and an @code{int} for printing with @samp{%d}.  If the "
"argument has the wrong type, @code{printf} will produce meaningless output."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1282
msgid ""
"Here's a complete program that computes the average of three specific "
"numbers and prints the result:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1289
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"double\n"
"average_of_three (double a, double b, double c)\n"
"@{\n"
"  return (a + b + c) / 3;\n"
"@}\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1297
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int\n"
"main (void)\n"
"@{\n"
"    printf (\"Average is %f\\n"
"\",\n"
"            average_of_three (1.1, 9.8, 3.62));\n"
"    return 0;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1302
msgid ""
"From now on we will not present examples of calls to @code{main}.  Instead "
"we encourage you to write them for yourself when you want to test executing "
"some code."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1304
#, no-wrap
msgid "An Example with Arrays"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1305
#, no-wrap
msgid "array example"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1313
msgid ""
"A function to take the average of three numbers is very specific and "
"limited.  A more general function would take the average of any number of "
"numbers.  That requires passing the numbers in an array.  An array is an "
"object in memory that contains a series of values of the same data type.  "
"This chapter presents the basic concepts and use of arrays through an "
"example; for the full explanation, see @ref{Arrays}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1319
msgid ""
"Here's a function definition to take the average of several floating-point "
"numbers, passed as type @code{double}.  The first parameter, @code{length}, "
"specifies how many numbers are passed.  The second parameter, "
"@code{input_data}, is an array that holds those numbers."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1326
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"double\n"
"avg_of_double (int length, double input_data[])\n"
"@{\n"
"  double sum = 0;\n"
"  int i;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1329
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  for (i = 0; i < length; i++)\n"
"    sum = sum + input_data[i];\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1332 c-intro-and-ref.texi:2917
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  return sum / length;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1339
msgid ""
"This introduces the expression to refer to an element of an array: "
"@code{input_data[i]} means the element at index @code{i} in "
"@code{input_data}.  The index of the element can be any expression with an "
"integer value; in this case, the expression is @code{i}.  @xref{Accessing "
"Array Elements}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1340
#, no-wrap
msgid "zero-origin indexing"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1344
msgid ""
"The lowest valid index in an array is 0, @emph{not} 1, and the highest valid "
"index is one less than the number of elements.  (This is known as "
"@dfn{zero-origin indexing}.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1352
msgid ""
"This example also introduces the way to declare that a function parameter is "
"an array.  Such declarations are modeled after the syntax for an element of "
"the array.  Just as @code{double foo} declares that @code{foo} is of type "
"@code{double}, @code{double input_data[]} declares that each element of "
"@code{input_data} is of type @code{double}.  Therefore, @code{input_data} "
"itself has type ``array of @code{double}.''"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1358
msgid ""
"When declaring an array parameter, it's not necessary to say how long the "
"array is.  In this case, the parameter @code{input_data} has no length "
"information.  That's why the function needs another parameter, "
"@code{length}, for the caller to provide that information to the function "
"@code{avg_of_double}."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1360
#, no-wrap
msgid "Calling the Array Example"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1364
msgid ""
"To call the function @code{avg_of_double} requires making an array and then "
"passing it as an argument.  Here is an example."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1371
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@{\n"
"  /* @r{The array of values to compute the average of.}  */\n"
"  double nums_to_average[5];\n"
"  /* @r{The average, once we compute it.}  */\n"
"  double average;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1373
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  /* @r{Fill in elements of @code{nums_to_average}.}  */\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1379
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  nums_to_average[0] = 58.7;\n"
"  nums_to_average[1] = 5.1;\n"
"  nums_to_average[2] = 7.7;\n"
"  nums_to_average[3] = 105.2;\n"
"  nums_to_average[4] = -3.14159;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1381
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  average = avg_of_double (5, nums_to_average);\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1384 c-intro-and-ref.texi:1467
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  /* @r{@dots{}now make use of @code{average}@dots{}} */\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1389
msgid ""
"This shows an array subscripting expression again, this time on the left "
"side of an assignment, storing a value into an element of an array."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1395
msgid ""
"It also shows how to declare a local variable that is an array: @code{double "
"nums_to_average[5];}.  Since this declaration allocates the space for the "
"array, it needs to know the array's length.  You can specify the length with "
"any expression whose value is an integer, but in this declaration the length "
"is a constant, the integer 5."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1400
msgid ""
"The name of the array, when used by itself as an expression, stands for the "
"address of the array's data, and that's what gets passed to the function "
"@code{avg_of_double} in @code{avg_of_double (5, nums_to_average)}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1405
msgid ""
"We can make the code easier to maintain by avoiding the need to write 5, the "
"array length, when calling @code{avg_of_double}.  That way, if we change the "
"array to include more elements, we won't have to change that call.  One way "
"to do this is with the @code{sizeof} operator:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1410
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  average = avg_of_double ((sizeof (nums_to_average)\n"
"                            / sizeof (nums_to_average[0])),\n"
"                           nums_to_average);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1415
msgid ""
"This computes the number of elements in @code{nums_to_average} by dividing "
"its total size by the size of one element.  @xref{Type Size}, for more "
"details of using @code{sizeof}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1420
msgid ""
"We don't show in this example what happens after storing the result of "
"@code{avg_of_double} in the variable @code{average}.  Presumably more code "
"would follow that uses that result somehow.  (Why compute the average and "
"not use it?)  But that isn't part of this topic."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1422
#, no-wrap
msgid "Variations for Array Example"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1426
msgid ""
"The code to call @code{avg_of_double} has two declarations that start with "
"the same data type:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1432
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  /* @r{The array of values to average.}  */\n"
"  double nums_to_average[5];\n"
"  /* @r{The average, once we compute it.}  */\n"
"  double average;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1435
msgid "In C, you can combine the two, like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1438
#, no-wrap
msgid "  double nums_to_average[5], average;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1443
msgid ""
"This declares @code{nums_to_average} so each of its elements is a "
"@code{double}, and @code{average} itself as a @code{double}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1449
msgid ""
"However, while you @emph{can} combine them, that doesn't mean you "
"@emph{should}.  If it is useful to write comments about the variables, and "
"usually it is, then it's clearer to keep the declarations separate so you "
"can put a comment on each one.  That also helps with using textual tools to "
"find occurrences of a variable in source files."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1453
msgid ""
"We set all of the elements of the array @code{nums_to_average} with "
"assignments, but it is more convenient to use an initializer in the "
"declaration:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1459
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@{\n"
"  /* @r{The array of values to average.}  */\n"
"  double nums_to_average[]\n"
"    = @{ 58.7, 5.1, 7.7, 105.2, -3.14159 @};\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1464
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  /* @r{The average, once we compute it.}  */\n"
"  average = avg_of_double ((sizeof (nums_to_average)\n"
"                            / sizeof (nums_to_average[0])),\n"
"                           nums_to_average);\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1471
msgid ""
"The array initializer is a comma-separated list of values, delimited by "
"braces.  @xref{Initializers}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1477
msgid ""
"Note that the declaration does not specify a size for "
"@code{nums_to_average}, so the size is determined from the initializer.  "
"There are five values in the initializer, so @code{nums_to_average} gets "
"length 5.  If we add another element to the initializer, "
"@code{nums_to_average} will have six elements."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1481
msgid ""
"Because the code computes the number of elements from the size of the array, "
"using @code{sizeof}, the program will operate on all the elements in the "
"initializer, regardless of how many those are."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1484
#, no-wrap
msgid "lexical syntax"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1485
#, no-wrap
msgid "token"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1494
msgid ""
"To start the full description of the C language, we explain the lexical "
"syntax and lexical units of C code.  The lexical units of a programming "
"language are known as @dfn{tokens}.  This chapter covers all the tokens of C "
"except for constants, which are covered in a later chapter "
"(@pxref{Constants}).  One vital kind of token is the @dfn{identifier} "
"(@pxref{Identifiers}), which is used for names of any kind."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1506
#, no-wrap
msgid "Write Programs in English!"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1515
msgid ""
"In principle, you can write the function and variable names in a program, "
"and the comments, in any human language.  C allows any kinds of Unicode "
"characters in comments, and you can put them into identifiers with a special "
"prefix (@pxref{Unicode Character Codes}).  However, to enable programmers in "
"all countries to understand and develop the program, it is best under "
"today's circumstances to write all identifiers and comments in English."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1524
msgid ""
"English is the common language of programmers; in all countries, programmers "
"generally learn English.  If names and comments in a program are written in "
"English, most programmers in Bangladesh, Belgium, Bolivia, Brazil, Bulgaria "
"and Burundi can understand them.  In all those countries, most programmers "
"can speak English, or at least read it, but they do not read each other's "
"languages at all.  In India, with so many languages, two programmers may "
"have no common language other than English."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1529
msgid ""
"If you don't feel confident in writing English, do the best you can, and "
"follow each English comment with a version in a language you write better; "
"add a note asking others to translate that to English.  Someone will "
"eventually do that."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1538
msgid ""
"The program's user interface is a different matter.  We don't need to choose "
"one language for that; it is easy to support multiple languages and let each "
"user choose the language for display.  This requires writing the program to "
"support localization of its interface.  (The @code{gettext} package exists "
"to support this; @pxref{Message Translation, The GNU C Library, , libc, The "
"GNU C Library Reference Manual}.)  Then a community-based translation effort "
"can provide support for all the languages users want to use."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1541
#, no-wrap
msgid "character set"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1542
#, no-wrap
msgid "Unicode"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1556
msgid ""
"GNU C source files are usually written in the "
"@url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ASCII,,ASCII} character set, which was "
"defined in the 1960s for English.  However, they can also include Unicode "
"characters represented in the "
"@url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UTF-8,,UTF-8} multibyte encoding.  This "
"makes it possible to represent accented letters such as @samp{á}, as well "
"as other scripts such as Arabic, Chinese, Cyrillic, Hebrew, Japanese, and "
"Korean.@footnote{On some obscure systems, GNU C uses UTF-EBCDIC instead of "
"UTF-8, but that is not worth describing in this manual.}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1560
msgid ""
"In C source code, non-ASCII characters are valid in comments, in wide "
"character constants (@pxref{Wide Character Constants}), and in string "
"constants (@pxref{String Constants})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1567
msgid ""
"Another way to specify non-ASCII characters in constants (character or "
"string) and identifiers is with an escape sequence starting with backslash, "
"specifying the intended Unicode character.  (@xref{Unicode Character "
"Codes}.)  This specifies non-ASCII characters without putting a real "
"non-ASCII character in the source file itself."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1570
msgid ""
"C accepts two-character aliases called @dfn{digraphs} for certain "
"characters.  @xref{Digraphs}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1573
#, no-wrap
msgid "whitespace characters in source files"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1574
#, no-wrap
msgid "space character in source"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1575
#, no-wrap
msgid "tab character in source"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1576
#, no-wrap
msgid "formfeed in source"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1577
#, no-wrap
msgid "linefeed in source"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1578
#, no-wrap
msgid "newline in source"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1579
#, no-wrap
msgid "carriage return in source"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1580
#, no-wrap
msgid "vertical tab in source"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1589
msgid ""
"Whitespace means characters that exist in a file but appear blank in a "
"printed listing of a file (or traditionally did appear blank, several "
"decades ago).  The C language requires whitespace in order to separate two "
"consecutive identifiers, or to separate an identifier from a numeric "
"constant.  Other than that, and a few special situations described later, "
"whitespace is optional; you can put it in when you wish, to make the code "
"easier to read."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1595
msgid ""
"Space and tab in C code are treated as whitespace characters.  So are line "
"breaks.  You can represent a line break with the newline character (also "
"called @dfn{linefeed} or LF), CR (carriage return), or the CRLF sequence "
"(two characters: carriage return followed by a newline character)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1603
msgid ""
"The @dfn{formfeed} character, Control-L, was traditionally used to divide a "
"file into pages.  It is still used this way in source code, and the tools "
"that generate nice printouts of source code still start a new page after "
"each ``formfeed'' character.  Dividing code into pages separated by formfeed "
"characters is a good way to break it up into comprehensible pieces and show "
"other programmers where they start and end."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1608
msgid ""
"The @dfn{vertical tab} character, Control-K, was traditionally used to make "
"printing advance down to the next section of a page.  We know of no "
"particular reason to use it in source code, but it is still accepted as "
"whitespace in C."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1610
msgid "Comments are also syntactically equivalent to whitespace."
msgstr ""

#. type: ifinfo
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1612
msgid "@xref{Comments}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1616
#, no-wrap
msgid "comments"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1620
msgid ""
"A comment encapsulates text that has no effect on the program's execution or "
"meaning."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1627
msgid ""
"The purpose of comments is to explain the code to people that read it.  "
"Writing good comments for your code is tremendously important---they should "
"provide background information that helps programmers understand the reasons "
"why the code is written the way it is.  You, returning to the code six "
"months from now, will need the help of these comments to remember why you "
"wrote it this way."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1631
msgid ""
"Outdated comments that become incorrect are counterproductive, so part of "
"the software developer's responsibility is to update comments as needed to "
"correspond with changes to the program code."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1635
msgid ""
"C allows two kinds of comment syntax, the traditional style and the C@t{++} "
"style.  A traditional C comment starts with @samp{/*} and ends with "
"@samp{*/}.  For instance,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1638
#, no-wrap
msgid "/* @r{This is a comment in traditional C syntax.} */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1643
msgid ""
"A traditional comment can contain @samp{/*}, but these delimiters do not "
"nest as pairs.  The first @samp{*/} ends the comment regardless of whether "
"it contains @samp{/*} sequences."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1646
#, no-wrap
msgid "/* @r{This} /* @r{is a comment} */ But this is not! */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1650
msgid ""
"A @dfn{line comment} starts with @samp{//} and ends at the end of the line.  "
"For instance,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1653
#, no-wrap
msgid "// @r{This is a comment in C@t{++} style.}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1657
msgid ""
"Line comments do nest, in effect, because @samp{//} inside a line comment is "
"part of that comment:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1661
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"// @r{this whole line is} // @r{one comment}\n"
"This is code, not comment.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1664
msgid "It is safe to put line comments inside block comments, or vice versa."
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1671
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"/* @r{traditional comment}\n"
"   // @r{contains line comment}\n"
"   @r{more traditional comment}\n"
" */ text here is not a comment\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1673
#, no-wrap
msgid "// @r{line comment} /* @r{contains traditional comment} */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1679
msgid ""
"But beware of commenting out one end of a traditional comment with a line "
"comment.  The delimiter @samp{/*} doesn't start a comment if it occurs "
"inside an already-started comment."
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1685
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" // @r{line comment}  /* @r{That would ordinarily begin a block comment.}\n"
"    Oops! The line comment has ended;\n"
"    this isn't a comment any more.  */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1691
msgid ""
"Comments are not recognized within string constants.  @t{@w{\"/* blah */\"}} "
"is the string constant @samp{@w{/* blah */}}, not an empty string."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1695
msgid ""
"In this manual we show the text in comments in a variable-width font, for "
"readability, but this font distinction does not exist in source files."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1698
msgid ""
"A comment is syntactically equivalent to whitespace, so it always separates "
"tokens.  Thus,"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1704
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  int/* @r{comment} */foo;\n"
"@r{is equivalent to}\n"
"  int foo;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1710
msgid ""
"but clean code always uses real whitespace to separate the comment visually "
"from surrounding code."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1713
#, no-wrap
msgid "identifiers"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1719
msgid ""
"An @dfn{identifier} (name) in C is a sequence of letters and digits, as well "
"as @samp{_}, that does not start with a digit.  Most C compilers also allow "
"@samp{$}; GNU C allows it.  An identifier can be as long as you like; for "
"example,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1722
#, no-wrap
msgid "int anti_dis_establishment_arian_ism;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1724
#, no-wrap
msgid "case of letters in identifiers"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1727
msgid ""
"Letters in identifiers are case-sensitive in C; thus, @code{a} and @code{A} "
"are two different identifiers."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1728
#, no-wrap
msgid "keyword"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1729
#, no-wrap
msgid "reserved words"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1736
msgid ""
"Identifiers in C are used as variable names, function names, typedef names, "
"enumeration constants, type tags, field names, and labels.  Certain "
"identifiers in C are @dfn{keywords}, which means they have specific "
"syntactic meanings.  Keywords in C are @dfn{reserved words}, meaning you "
"cannot use them in any other way.  For instance, you can't define a variable "
"or function named @code{return} or @code{if}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1743
msgid ""
"You can also include other characters, even non-ASCII characters, in "
"identifiers by writing their Unicode character names, which start with "
"@samp{\\u} or @samp{\\U}, in the identifier name.  @xref{Unicode Character "
"Codes}.  However, it is usually a bad idea to use non-ASCII characters in "
"identifiers, and when the names are written in English, they never need "
"non-ASCII characters.  @xref{English}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1747
msgid ""
"As stated above, whitespace is required to separate two consecutive "
"identifiers, or to separate an identifier from a preceding or following "
"numeric constant."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1749
#, no-wrap
msgid "Operators and Punctuation"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1750
#, no-wrap
msgid "operators"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1751
#, no-wrap
msgid "punctuation"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1756
msgid ""
"Here we describe the lexical syntax of operators and punctuation in C.  The "
"specific operators of C and their meanings are presented in subsequent "
"chapters."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1763
msgid ""
"Some characters that are generally considered punctuation have a different "
"sort of meaning in the C language.  C uses double-quote @samp{\"} to delimit "
"string constants (@pxref{String Constants}) and @samp{'} to delimit "
"character constants (@pxref{Character Constants}).  The characters @samp{$} "
"and @samp{_} can be part of an identifier or a keyword."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1769
msgid ""
"Most operators in C consist of one or two characters that can't be used in "
"identifiers.  The characters used for such operators in C are "
"@samp{!~^&|*/%+-=<>,.?:}.  (C preprocessing uses @dfn{preprocessing "
"operators}, based on @samp{#}, which are entirely different from these "
"operators; @ref{Preprocessing}.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1773
msgid ""
"Some operators are a single character.  For instance, @samp{-} is the "
"operator for negation (with one operand) and the operator for subtraction "
"(with two operands)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1778
msgid ""
"Some operators are two characters.  For example, @samp{++} is the increment "
"operator.  Recognition of multicharacter operators works by reading and "
"grouping as many successive characters as can constitute one operator, and "
"making them one token."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1787
msgid ""
"For instance, the character sequence @samp{++} is always interpreted as the "
"increment operator; therefore, if we want to write two consecutive instances "
"of the operator @samp{+}, we must separate them with a space so that they do "
"not combine as one token.  Applying the same rule, @code{a+++++b} is always "
"tokenized as @code{@w{a++ ++ + b}}, not as @code{@w{a++ + ++b}}, even though "
"the latter could be part of a valid C program and the former could not "
"(since @code{a++} is not an lvalue and thus can't be the operand of "
"@code{++})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1791
msgid ""
"A few C operators are keywords rather than special characters.  They include "
"@code{sizeof} (@pxref{Type Size}) and @code{_Alignof} (@pxref{Type "
"Alignment})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1798
msgid ""
"The characters @samp{;@{@}[]()} are used for punctuation and grouping.  "
"Semicolon (@samp{;}) ends a statement.  Braces (@samp{@{} and @samp{@}}) "
"begin and end a block at the statement level (@pxref{Blocks}), and surround "
"the initializer (@pxref{Initializers})  for a variable with multiple "
"elements or fields (such as arrays or structures)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1801
msgid ""
"Square brackets (@samp{[} and @samp{]}) do array indexing, as in "
"@code{array[5]}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1811
msgid ""
"Parentheses are used in expressions for explicit nesting of expressions "
"(@pxref{Basic Arithmetic}), around the parameter declarations in a function "
"declaration or definition, and around the arguments in a function call, as "
"in @code{printf (\"Foo %d\\n"
"\", i)} (@pxref{Function Calls}).  Several kinds of statements also use "
"parentheses as part of their syntax---for instance, @code{if} statements, "
"@code{for} statements, @code{while} statements, and @code{switch} "
"statements.  @xref{if Statement}, and following sections."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1815
msgid ""
"Parentheses are also required around the operand of the operator keywords "
"@code{sizeof} and @code{_Alignof} when the operand is a data type rather "
"than a value.  @xref{Type Size}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1818
#, no-wrap
msgid "line continuation"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1819
#, no-wrap
msgid "continuation of lines"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1828
msgid ""
"The sequence of a backslash and a newline is ignored absolutely anywhere in "
"a C program.  This makes it possible to split a single source line into "
"multiple lines in the source file.  GNU C tolerates and ignores other "
"whitespace between the backslash and the newline.  In particular, it always "
"ignores a CR (carriage return) character there, in case some text editor "
"decided to end the line with the CRLF sequence."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1831
msgid ""
"The main use of line continuation in C is for macro definitions that would "
"be inconveniently long for a single line (@pxref{Macros})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1837
msgid ""
"It is possible to continue a line comment onto another line with "
"backslash-newline.  You can put backslash-newline in the middle of an "
"identifier, even a keyword, or an operator.  You can even split @samp{/*}, "
"@samp{*/}, and @samp{//} onto multiple lines with backslash-newline.  Here's "
"an ugly example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1846
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"/\\\n"
"*\n"
"*/ fo\\\n"
"o +\\\n"
"= 1\\\n"
"0;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1851
msgid "That's equivalent to @samp{/* */ foo += 10;}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1854
msgid ""
"Don't do those things in real programs, since they make code hard to read."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1858
msgid ""
"@strong{Note:} For the sake of using certain tools on the source code, it is "
"wise to end every source file with a newline character which is not preceded "
"by a backslash, so that it really ends the last line."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1861
#, no-wrap
msgid "arithmetic operators"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1862
#, no-wrap
msgid "operators, arithmetic"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1873
msgid ""
"Arithmetic operators in C attempt to be as similar as possible to the "
"abstract arithmetic operations, but it is impossible to do this perfectly.  "
"Numbers in a computer have a finite range of possible values, and "
"non-integer values have a limit on their possible accuracy.  Nonetheless, "
"except when results are out of range, you will encounter no surprises in "
"using @samp{+} for addition, @samp{-} for subtraction, and @samp{*} for "
"multiplication."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1881
msgid ""
"Each C operator has a @dfn{precedence}, which is its rank in the grammatical "
"order of the various operators.  The operators with the highest precedence "
"grab adjoining operands first; these expressions then become operands for "
"operators of lower precedence.  We give some information about precedence of "
"operators in this chapter where we describe the operators; for the full "
"explanation, see @ref{Binary Operator Grammar}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1886
msgid ""
"The arithmetic operators always @dfn{promote} their operands before "
"operating on them.  This means converting narrow integer data types to a "
"wider data type (@pxref{Operand Promotions}).  If you are just learning C, "
"don't worry about this yet."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1893
msgid ""
"Given two operands that have different types, most arithmetic operations "
"convert them both to their @dfn{common type}.  For instance, if one is "
"@code{int} and the other is @code{double}, the common type is "
"@code{double}.  (That's because @code{double} can represent all the values "
"that an @code{int} can hold, but not vice versa.)  For the full details, see "
"@ref{Common Type}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1910
#, no-wrap
msgid "addition operator"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1911
#, no-wrap
msgid "subtraction operator"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1912
#, no-wrap
msgid "multiplication operator"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1913
#, no-wrap
msgid "division operator"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1914
#, no-wrap
msgid "negation operator"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1915
#, no-wrap
msgid "operator, addition"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1916
#, no-wrap
msgid "operator, subtraction"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1917
#, no-wrap
msgid "operator, multiplication"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1918
#, no-wrap
msgid "operator, division"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1919
#, no-wrap
msgid "operator, negation"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1926
msgid ""
"Basic arithmetic in C is done with the usual binary operators of algebra: "
"addition (@samp{+}), subtraction (@samp{-}), multiplication (@samp{*}) and "
"division (@samp{/}).  The unary operator @samp{-} is used to change the sign "
"of a number.  The unary @code{+} operator also exists; it yields its operand "
"unaltered."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1933
msgid ""
"@samp{/} is the division operator, but dividing integers may not give the "
"result you expect.  Its value is an integer, which is not equal to the "
"mathematical quotient when that is a fraction.  Use @samp{%} to get the "
"corresponding integer remainder when necessary.  @xref{Division and "
"Remainder}.  Floating-point division yields a value as close as possible to "
"the mathematical quotient."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1939
msgid ""
"These operators use algebraic syntax with the usual algebraic precedence "
"rule (@pxref{Binary Operator Grammar}) that multiplication and division are "
"done before addition and subtraction, but you can use parentheses to "
"explicitly specify how the operators nest.  They are left-associative "
"(@pxref{Associativity and Ordering}).  Thus,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1942
#, no-wrap
msgid "-a + b - c + d * e / f\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1946 c-intro-and-ref.texi:2565
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9363
msgid "is equivalent to"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1949
#, no-wrap
msgid "(((-a) + b) - c) + ((d * e) / f)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1953
#, no-wrap
msgid "integer arithmetic"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1958
msgid ""
"Each of the basic arithmetic operations in C has two variants for integers: "
"@dfn{signed} and @dfn{unsigned}.  The choice is determined by the data types "
"of their operands."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1963
msgid ""
"Each integer data type in C is either @dfn{signed} or @dfn{unsigned}.  A "
"signed type can hold a range of positive and negative numbers, with zero "
"near the middle of the range.  An unsigned type can hold only nonnegative "
"numbers; its range starts with zero and runs upward."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1970
msgid ""
"The most basic integer types are @code{int}, which normally can hold numbers "
"from @minus{}2,147,483,648 to 2,147,483,647, and @code{unsigned int}, which "
"normally can hold numbers from 0 to 4,294,967,295.  (This assumes @code{int} "
"is 32 bits wide, always true for GNU C on real computers but not always on "
"embedded controllers.)  @xref{Integer Types}, for full information about "
"integer types."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1974
msgid ""
"When a basic arithmetic operation is given two signed operands, it does "
"signed arithmetic.  Given two unsigned operands, it does unsigned arithmetic."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1979
msgid ""
"If one operand is @code{unsigned int} and the other is @code{int}, the "
"operator treats them both as unsigned.  More generally, the common type of "
"the operands determines whether the operation is signed or not.  "
"@xref{Common Type}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1984
msgid ""
"Printing the results of unsigned arithmetic with @code{printf} using "
"@samp{%d} can produce surprising results for values far away from zero.  "
"Even though the rules above say that the computation was done with unsigned "
"arithmetic, the printed result may appear to be signed!"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1990
msgid ""
"The explanation is that the bit pattern resulting from addition, subtraction "
"or multiplication is actually the same for signed and unsigned operations.  "
"The difference is only in the data type of the result, which affects the "
"@emph{interpretation} of the result bit pattern, and whether the arithmetic "
"operation can overflow (see the next section)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:1996
msgid ""
"But @samp{%d} doesn't know its argument's data type.  It sees only the "
"value's bit pattern, and it is defined to interpret that as @code{signed "
"int}.  To print it as unsigned requires using @samp{%u} instead of "
"@samp{%d}.  @xref{Formatted Output, The GNU C Library, , libc, The GNU C "
"Library Reference Manual}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2000
msgid ""
"Arithmetic in C never operates directly on narrow integer types (those with "
"fewer bits than @code{int}; @ref{Narrow Integers}).  Instead it ``promotes'' "
"them to @code{int}.  @xref{Operand Promotions}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2003
#, no-wrap
msgid "integer overflow"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2004
#, no-wrap
msgid "overflow, integer"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2009
msgid ""
"When the mathematical value of an arithmetic operation doesn't fit in the "
"range of the data type in use, that's called @dfn{overflow}.  When it "
"happens in integer arithmetic, it is @dfn{integer overflow}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2013
msgid ""
"Integer overflow happens only in arithmetic operations.  Type conversion "
"operations, by definition, do not cause overflow, not even when the result "
"can't fit in its new type.  @xref{Integer Conversion}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2018
msgid ""
"Signed numbers use two's-complement representation, in which the most "
"negative number lacks a positive counterpart (@pxref{Integers in Depth}).  "
"Thus, the unary @samp{-} operator on a signed integer can overflow."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2022 c-intro-and-ref.texi:2024
#, no-wrap
msgid "Unsigned Overflow"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2022
msgid "Overflow in unsigned integer arithmetic."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2022 c-intro-and-ref.texi:2064
#, no-wrap
msgid "Signed Overflow"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2022
msgid "Overflow in signed integer arithmetic."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2025
#, no-wrap
msgid "Overflow with Unsigned Integers"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2031
msgid ""
"Unsigned arithmetic in C ignores overflow; it produces the true result "
"modulo the @var{n}th power of 2, where @var{n} is the number of bits in the "
"data type.  We say it ``truncates'' the true result to the lowest @var{n} "
"bits."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2034
msgid ""
"A true result that is negative, when taken modulo the @var{n}th power of 2, "
"yields a positive number.  For instance,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2038
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"unsigned int x = 1;\n"
"unsigned int y;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2040
#, no-wrap
msgid "y = -x;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2049
msgid ""
"causes overflow because the negative number @minus{}1 can't be stored in an "
"unsigned type.  The actual result, which is @minus{}1 modulo the @var{n}th "
"power of 2, is one less than the @var{n}th power of 2.  That is the largest "
"value that the unsigned data type can store.  For a 32-bit @code{unsigned "
"int}, the value is 4,294,967,295.  @xref{Maximum and Minimum Values}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2051
msgid "Adding that number to itself, as here,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2054
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"unsigned int z;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2056
#, no-wrap
msgid "z = y + y;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2063
msgid ""
"ought to yield 8,489,934,590; however, that is again too large to fit, so "
"overflow truncates the value to 4,294,967,294.  If that were a signed "
"integer, it would mean @minus{}2, which (not by coincidence)  equals "
"@minus{}1 + @minus{}1."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2065
#, no-wrap
msgid "Overflow with Signed Integers"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2066
#, no-wrap
msgid "compiler options for integer overflow"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2067
#, no-wrap
msgid "integer overflow, compiler options"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2068
#, no-wrap
msgid "overflow, compiler options"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2076
msgid ""
"For signed integers, the result of overflow in C is @emph{in principle} "
"undefined, meaning that anything whatsoever could happen.  Therefore, C "
"compilers can do optimizations that treat the overflow case with total "
"unconcern.  (Since the result of overflow is undefined in principle, one "
"cannot claim that these optimizations are erroneous.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2079
msgid ""
"@strong{Watch out:} These optimizations can do surprising things.  For "
"instance,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2085
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int i;\n"
"@r{@dots{}}\n"
"if (i < i + 1)\n"
"  x = 5;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2090
msgid ""
"could be optimized to do the assignment unconditionally, because the "
"@code{if}-condition is always true if @code{i + 1} does not overflow."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2094
msgid ""
"GCC offers compiler options to control handling signed integer overflow.  "
"These options operate per module; that is, each module behaves according to "
"the options it was compiled with."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2097
msgid ""
"These two options specify particular ways to handle signed integer overflow, "
"other than the default way:"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2099
#, no-wrap
msgid "-fwrapv"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2106
msgid ""
"Make signed integer operations well-defined, like unsigned integer "
"operations: they produce the @var{n} low-order bits of the true result.  The "
"highest of those @var{n} bits is the sign bit of the result.  With "
"@option{-fwrapv}, these out-of-range operations are not considered overflow, "
"so (strictly speaking) integer overflow never happens."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2111
msgid ""
"The option @option{-fwrapv} enables some optimizations based on the defined "
"values of out-of-range results.  In GCC 8, it disables optimizations that "
"are based on assuming signed integer operations will not overflow."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2112
#, no-wrap
msgid "-ftrapv"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2116
msgid ""
"Generate a signal @code{SIGFPE} when signed integer overflow occurs.  This "
"terminates the program unless the program handles the signal.  "
"@xref{Signals}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2119
msgid "One other option is useful for finding where overflow occurs:"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2121
#, no-wrap
msgid "-fno-strict-overflow"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2127
#, no-wrap
msgid "-fsanitize=signed-integer-overflow"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2131
msgid ""
"Output a warning message at run time when signed integer overflow occurs.  "
"This checks the @samp{+}, @samp{*}, and @samp{-} operators.  This takes "
"priority over @option{-ftrapv}."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2134
#, no-wrap
msgid "Mixed-Mode Arithmetic"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2140
msgid ""
"Mixing integers and floating-point numbers in a basic arithmetic operation "
"converts the integers automatically to floating point.  In most cases, this "
"gives exactly the desired results.  But sometimes it matters precisely where "
"the conversion occurs."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2148
msgid ""
"If @code{i} and @code{j} are integers, @code{(i + j) * 2.0} adds them as an "
"integer, then converts the sum to floating point for the multiplication.  If "
"the addition causes an overflow, that is not equivalent to converting each "
"integer to floating point and then adding the two floating point numbers.  "
"You can get the latter result by explicitly converting the integers, as in "
"@code{((double) i + (double) j) * 2.0}.  @xref{Explicit Type Conversion}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2158
msgid ""
"Adding or multiplying several values, including some integers and some "
"floating point, performs the operations left to right.  Thus, @code{3.0 + i "
"+ j} converts @code{i} to floating point, then adds 3.0, then converts "
"@code{j} to floating point and adds that.  You can specify a different order "
"using parentheses: @code{3.0 + (i + j)} adds @code{i} and @code{j} first and "
"then adds that sum (converted to floating point) to 3.0.  In this respect, C "
"differs from other languages, such as Fortran."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2161
#, no-wrap
msgid "remainder operator"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2162
#, no-wrap
msgid "modulus"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2163
#, no-wrap
msgid "operator, remainder"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2167
msgid ""
"Division of integers in C rounds the result to an integer.  The result is "
"always rounded towards zero."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2173
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" 16 / 3  @result{} 5\n"
"-16 / 3  @result{} -5\n"
" 16 / -3 @result{} -5\n"
"-16 / -3 @result{} 5\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2177
msgid "To get the corresponding remainder, use the @samp{%} operator:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2183
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" 16 % 3  @result{} 1\n"
"-16 % 3  @result{} -1\n"
" 16 % -3 @result{} 1\n"
"-16 % -3 @result{} -1\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2187
msgid "@samp{%} has the same operator precedence as @samp{/} and @samp{*}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2190
msgid ""
"From the rounded quotient and the remainder, you can reconstruct the "
"dividend, like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2197
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int\n"
"original_dividend (int divisor, int quotient, int remainder)\n"
"@{\n"
"  return divisor * quotient + remainder;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2202
msgid ""
"To do unrounded division, use floating point.  If only one operand is "
"floating point, @samp{/} converts the other operand to floating point."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2208
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"16.0 / 3   @result{} 5.333333333333333\n"
"16   / 3.0 @result{} 5.333333333333333\n"
"16.0 / 3.0 @result{} 5.333333333333333\n"
"16   / 3   @result{} 5\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2216
msgid ""
"The remainder operator @samp{%} is not allowed for floating-point operands, "
"because it is not needed.  The concept of remainder makes sense for integers "
"because the result of division of integers has to be an integer.  For "
"floating point, the result of division is a floating-point number, in other "
"words a fraction, which will differ from the exact result only by a very "
"small amount."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2221
msgid ""
"There are functions in the standard C library to calculate remainders from "
"integral-values division of floating-point numbers.  @xref{Remainder "
"Functions, The GNU C Library, , libc, The GNU C Library Reference Manual}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2229
msgid ""
"Integer division overflows in one specific case: dividing the smallest "
"negative value for the data type (@pxref{Maximum and Minimum Values})  by "
"@minus{}1.  That's because the correct result, which is the corresponding "
"positive number, does not fit (@pxref{Integer Overflow})  in the same number "
"of bits.  On some computers now in use, this always causes a signal "
"@code{SIGFPE} (@pxref{Signals}), the same behavior that the option "
"@option{-ftrapv} specifies (@pxref{Signed Overflow})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2233
msgid ""
"Division by zero leads to unpredictable results---depending on the type of "
"computer, it might cause a signal @code{SIGFPE}, or it might produce a "
"numeric result."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2234
#, no-wrap
msgid "division by zero"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2235
#, no-wrap
msgid "zero, division by"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2239
msgid ""
"@strong{Watch out:} Make sure the program does not divide by zero.  If you "
"can't prove that the divisor is not zero, test whether it is zero, and skip "
"the division if so."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2242
#, no-wrap
msgid "numeric comparisons"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2243
#, no-wrap
msgid "comparisons"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2244
#, no-wrap
msgid "operators, comparison"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2245
#, no-wrap
msgid "equal operator"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2246
#, no-wrap
msgid "not-equal operator"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2247
#, no-wrap
msgid "less-than operator"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2248
#, no-wrap
msgid "greater-than operator"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2249
#, no-wrap
msgid "less-or-equal operator"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2250
#, no-wrap
msgid "greater-or-equal operator"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2251
#, no-wrap
msgid "operator, equal"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2252
#, no-wrap
msgid "operator, not-equal"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2253
#, no-wrap
msgid "operator, less-than"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2254
#, no-wrap
msgid "operator, greater-than"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2255
#, no-wrap
msgid "operator, less-or-equal"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2256
#, no-wrap
msgid "operator, greater-or-equal"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2257
#, no-wrap
msgid "truth value"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2263
msgid ""
"There are two kinds of comparison operators: @dfn{equality} and "
"@dfn{ordering}.  Equality comparisons test whether two expressions have the "
"same value.  The result is a @dfn{truth value}: a number that is 1 for "
"``true'' and 0 for ``false.''"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2267
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"a == b   /* @r{Test for equal.}  */\n"
"a != b   /* @r{Test for not equal.}  */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2271
msgid ""
"The equality comparison is written @code{==} because plain @code{=} is the "
"assignment operator."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2274
msgid ""
"Ordering comparisons test which operand is greater or less.  Their results "
"are truth values.  These are the ordering comparisons of C:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2280
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"a < b   /* @r{Test for less-than.}  */\n"
"a > b   /* @r{Test for greater-than.}  */\n"
"a <= b  /* @r{Test for less-than-or-equal.}  */\n"
"a >= b  /* @r{Test for greater-than-or-equal.}  */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2287
msgid ""
"For any integers @code{a} and @code{b}, exactly one of the comparisons "
"@code{a < b}, @code{a == b} and @code{a > b} is true, just as in "
"mathematics.  However, if @code{a} and @code{b} are special floating point "
"values (not ordinary numbers), all three can be false.  @xref{Special Float "
"Values}, and @ref{Invalid Optimizations}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2290
#, no-wrap
msgid "shift operators"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2291 c-intro-and-ref.texi:2292
#, no-wrap
msgid "operators, shift"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2293
#, no-wrap
msgid "shift count"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2298
msgid ""
"@dfn{Shifting} an integer means moving the bit values to the left or right "
"within the bits of the data type.  Shifting is defined only for integers.  "
"Here's the way to write it:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2302
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"/* @r{Left shift.}  */\n"
"5 << 2 @result{} 20\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2305
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"/* @r{Right shift.}  */\n"
"5 >> 2 @result{} 1\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2314
msgid ""
"The left operand is the value to be shifted, and the right operand says how "
"many bits to shift it (the @dfn{shift count}).  The left operand is promoted "
"(@pxref{Operand Promotions}), so shifting never operates on a narrow integer "
"type; it's always either @code{int} or wider.  The result of the shift "
"operation has the same type as the promoted left operand."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2318 c-intro-and-ref.texi:2402
msgid ""
"The examples in this section use binary constants, starting with @samp{0b} "
"(@pxref{Integer Constants}).  They stand for 32-bit integers of type "
"@code{int}."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2323 c-intro-and-ref.texi:2325
#, no-wrap
msgid "Bits Shifted In"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2323
msgid "How shifting makes new bits to shift in."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2323 c-intro-and-ref.texi:2363
#, no-wrap
msgid "Shift Caveats"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2323
msgid "Caveats of shift operations."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2323 c-intro-and-ref.texi:2391
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2392
#, no-wrap
msgid "Shift Hacks"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2323
msgid "Clever tricks with shift operations."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2326
#, no-wrap
msgid "Shifting Makes New Bits"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2330
msgid ""
"A shift operation shifts towards one end of the number and has to generate "
"new bits at the other end."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2334
msgid ""
"Shifting left one bit must generate a new least significant bit.  It always "
"brings in zero there.  It is equivalent to multiplying by the appropriate "
"power of 2.  For example,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2338
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"5 << 3     @r{is equivalent to}   5 * 2*2*2\n"
"-10 << 4   @r{is equivalent to}   -10 * 2*2*2*2\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2346
msgid ""
"The meaning of shifting right depends on whether the data type is signed or "
"unsigned (@pxref{Signed and Unsigned Types}).  For a signed data type, GNU C "
"performs ``arithmetic shift,'' which keeps the number's sign unchanged by "
"duplicating the sign bit.  For an unsigned data type, it performs ``logical "
"shift,'' which always shifts in zeros at the most significant bit."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2349
msgid ""
"In both cases, shifting right one bit is division by two, rounding towards "
"negative infinity.  For example,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2354
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"(unsigned) 19 >> 2 @result{} 4\n"
"(unsigned) 20 >> 2 @result{} 5\n"
"(unsigned) 21 >> 2 @result{} 5\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2359
msgid ""
"For a negative left operand @code{a}, @code{a >> 1} is not equivalent to "
"@code{a / 2}.  Both operations divide by 2, but @samp{/} rounds toward zero."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2362
msgid ""
"The shift count must be zero or greater.  Shifting by a negative number of "
"bits gives machine-dependent results."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2364
#, no-wrap
msgid "Caveats for Shift Operations"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2373
msgid ""
"@strong{Warning:} If the shift count is greater than or equal to the width "
"in bits of the promoted first operand, the results are machine-dependent.  "
"Logically speaking, the ``correct'' value would be either @minus{}1 (for "
"right shift of a negative number) or 0 (in all other cases), but the actual "
"result is whatever the machine's shift instruction does in that case.  So "
"unless you can prove that the second operand is not too large, write code to "
"check it at run time."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2378
msgid ""
"@strong{Warning:} Never rely on how the shift operators relate in precedence "
"to other arithmetic binary operators.  Programmers don't remember these "
"precedences, and won't understand the code.  Always use parentheses to "
"explicitly specify the nesting, like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2382
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"a + (b << 5)   /* @r{Shift first, then add.}  */\n"
"(a + b) << 5   /* @r{Add first, then shift.}  */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2390
msgid ""
"Note: according to the C standard, shifting of signed values isn't "
"guaranteed to work properly when the value shifted is negative, or becomes "
"negative during shifting.  However, only pedants have a reason to be "
"concerned about this; only computers with strange shift instructions could "
"plausibly do this wrong.  In GNU C, the operation always works as expected."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2398
msgid ""
"You can use the shift operators for various useful hacks.  For example, "
"given a date specified by day of the month @code{d}, month @code{m}, and "
"year @code{y}, you can store the entire date in a single integer @code{date}:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2411
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"unsigned int d = 12;\t\t/* @r{12 in binary is 0b1100.}  */\n"
"unsigned int m = 6;             /* @r{6 in binary is 0b110.}  */\n"
"unsigned int y = 1983;          /* @r{1983 in binary is 0b11110111111.}  */\n"
"unsigned int date = (((y << 4) + m) << 5) + d;\n"
"                                /* @r{Add 0b11110111111000000000}\n"
"                                   @r{and 0b11000000 and 0b1100.}\n"
"                                   @r{Sum is 0b11110111111011001100.}  */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2416
msgid ""
"To extract the day, month, and year out of @code{date}, use a combination of "
"shift and remainder:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2427
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"/* @r{32 in binary is 0b100000.}  */\n"
"/* @r{Remainder dividing by 32 gives lowest 5 bits, 0b1100.}  */\n"
"d = date % 32;\n"
"/* @r{Shifting 5 bits right discards the day, leaving 0b111101111110110.}\n"
"   @r{Remainder dividing by 16 gives lowest remaining 4 bits, 0b110.}  */\n"
"m = (date >> 5) % 16;\n"
"/* @r{Shifting 9 bits right discards day and month,}\n"
"   @r{leaving 0b11110111111.}  */\n"
"y = date >> 9;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2433
msgid ""
"@code{-1 << LOWBITS} is a clever way to make an integer whose @code{LOWBITS} "
"lowest bits are all 0 and the rest are all 1.  @code{-(1 << LOWBITS)} is "
"equivalent to that, since negating a value is equivalent to multiplying it "
"by @minus{}1."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2436
#, no-wrap
msgid "bitwise operators"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2437
#, no-wrap
msgid "operators, bitwise"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2438
#, no-wrap
msgid "negation, bitwise"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2439
#, no-wrap
msgid "conjunction, bitwise"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2440
#, no-wrap
msgid "disjunction, bitwise"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2444
msgid ""
"Bitwise operators operate on integers, treating each bit independently.  "
"They are not allowed for floating-point types."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2448
msgid ""
"As in the previous section, the examples in this section use binary "
"constants, starting with @samp{0b} (@pxref{Integer Constants}).  They stand "
"for 32-bit integers of type @code{int}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2450
#, no-wrap
msgid "~@code{a}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2453
msgid ""
"Unary operator for bitwise negation; this changes each bit of @code{a} from "
"1 to 0 or from 0 to 1."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2459
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"~0b10101000 @result{} 0b11111111111111111111111101010111\n"
"~0 @result{} 0b11111111111111111111111111111111\n"
"~0b11111111111111111111111111111111 @result{} 0\n"
"~ (-1) @result{} 0\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2465
msgid ""
"It is useful to remember that @code{~@var{x} + 1} equals @code{-@var{x}}, "
"for integers, and @code{~@var{x}} equals @code{-@var{x} - 1}.  The last "
"example above shows this with @minus{}1 as @var{x}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2466
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{a} & @code{b}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2469
msgid ""
"Binary operator for bitwise ``and'' or ``conjunction.'' Each bit in the "
"result is 1 if that bit is 1 in both @code{a} and @code{b}."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2472
#, no-wrap
msgid "0b10101010 & 0b11001100 @result{} 0b10001000\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2474
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{a} | @code{b}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2478
msgid ""
"Binary operator for bitwise ``or'' (``inclusive or'' or ``disjunction'').  "
"Each bit in the result is 1 if that bit is 1 in either @code{a} or @code{b}."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2481
#, no-wrap
msgid "0b10101010 | 0b11001100 @result{} 0b11101110\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2483
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{a} ^ @code{b}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2486
msgid ""
"Binary operator for bitwise ``xor'' (``exclusive or'').  Each bit in the "
"result is 1 if that bit is 1 in exactly one of @code{a} and @code{b}."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2489
#, no-wrap
msgid "0b10101010 ^ 0b11001100 @result{} 0b01100110\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2500
msgid ""
"To understand the effect of these operators on signed integers, keep in mind "
"that all modern computers use two's-complement representation "
"(@pxref{Integer Representations}) for negative integers.  This means that "
"the highest bit of the number indicates the sign; it is 1 for a negative "
"number and 0 for a positive number.  In a negative number, the value in the "
"other bits @emph{increases} as the number gets closer to zero, so that "
"@code{0b111@r{@dots{}}111} is @minus{}1 and @code{0b100@r{@dots{}}000} is "
"the most negative possible integer."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2508
msgid ""
"@strong{Warning:} C defines a precedence ordering for the bitwise binary "
"operators, but you should never rely on it.  Likewise, you should never rely "
"on how bitwise binary operators relate in precedence to the arithmetic and "
"shift binary operators.  Other programmers don't remember these aspects of "
"C's precedence ordering; to make your programs clear, always use parentheses "
"to explicitly specify the nesting among these operators."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2513
msgid ""
"For example, suppose @code{offset} is an integer that specifies the offset "
"within shared memory of a table, except that its bottom few bits "
"(@code{LOWBITS} says how many) are special flags.  Here's how to get just "
"that offset and add it to the base address."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2516
#, no-wrap
msgid "shared_mem_base + (offset & (-1 << LOWBITS))\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2524
msgid ""
"Thanks to the outer set of parentheses, we don't need to know whether "
"@samp{&} has higher precedence than @samp{+}.  Thanks to the inner set, we "
"don't need to know whether @samp{&} has higher precedence than @samp{<<}.  "
"But we can rely on all unary operators to have higher precedence than any "
"binary operator, so we don't need parentheses around the left operand of "
"@samp{<<}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2527
#, no-wrap
msgid "assignment expressions"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2528
#, no-wrap
msgid "operators, assignment"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2534
msgid ""
"As a general concept in programming, an @dfn{assignment} is a construct that "
"stores a new value into a place where values can be stored---for instance, "
"in a variable.  Such places are called @dfn{lvalues} (@pxref{Lvalues}) "
"because they are locations that hold a value."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2537
msgid ""
"In C, an assignment is an expression because it has a value; we call it an "
"@dfn{assignment expression}.  A simple assignment looks like"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2540
#, no-wrap
msgid "@var{lvalue} = @var{value-to-store}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2548
msgid ""
"We say it assigns the value of the expression @var{value-to-store} to the "
"location @var{lvalue}, or that it stores @var{value-to-store} there.  You "
"can think of the ``l'' in ``lvalue'' as standing for ``left,'' since that's "
"what you put on the left side of the assignment operator."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2553
msgid ""
"However, that's not the only way to use an lvalue, and not all lvalues can "
"be assigned to.  To use the lvalue in the left side of an assignment, it has "
"to be @dfn{modifiable}.  In C, that means it was not declared with the type "
"qualifier @code{const} (@pxref{const})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2558
msgid ""
"The value of the assignment expression is that of @var{lvalue} after the new "
"value is stored in it.  This means you can use an assignment inside other "
"expressions.  Assignment operators are right-associative so that"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2561
#, no-wrap
msgid "x = y = z = 0;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2568
#, no-wrap
msgid "x = (y = (z = 0));\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2572
msgid "This is the only useful way for them to associate; the other way,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2575
#, no-wrap
msgid "((x = y) = z) = 0;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2580
msgid ""
"would be invalid since an assignment expression such as @code{x = y} is not "
"a valid lvalue."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2585
msgid ""
"@strong{Warning:} Write parentheses around an assignment if you nest it "
"inside another expression, unless that containing expression is a "
"comma-separated series or another assignment.  For example, see "
"@ref{Logicals and Assignments}, and @ref{Uses of Comma}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2599
#, no-wrap
msgid "simple assignment"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2600
#, no-wrap
msgid "assignment, simple"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2605
msgid ""
"A @dfn{simple assignment expression} computes the value of the right operand "
"and stores it into the lvalue on the left.  Here is a simple assignment "
"expression that stores 5 in @code{i}:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2608
#, no-wrap
msgid "i = 5\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2615
msgid ""
"We say that this is an @dfn{assignment to} the variable @code{i} and that it "
"@dfn{assigns} @code{i} the value 5.  It has no semicolon because it is an "
"expression (so it has a value).  Adding a semicolon at the end would make it "
"a statement (@pxref{Expression Statement})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2619
msgid ""
"Here is another example of a simple assignment expression.  Its operands are "
"not simple, but the kind of assignment done here is simple assignment."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2622
#, no-wrap
msgid "x[foo ()] = y + 6\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2627
msgid ""
"A simple assignment with two different numeric data types converts the right "
"operand value to the lvalue's type, if possible.  It can convert any numeric "
"type to any other numeric type."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2631
msgid ""
"Simple assignment is also allowed on some non-numeric types: pointers "
"(@pxref{Pointers}), structures (@pxref{Structure Assignment}), and unions "
"(@pxref{Unions})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2636
msgid ""
"@strong{Warning:} Assignment is not allowed on arrays because there are no "
"array values in C; C variables can be arrays, but these arrays cannot be "
"manipulated as wholes.  @xref{Limitations of C Arrays}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2639
msgid ""
"@xref{Assignment Type Conversions}, for the complete rules about data types "
"used in assignments."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2642
#, no-wrap
msgid "lvalues"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2646
msgid ""
"An expression that identifies a memory space that holds a value is called an "
"@dfn{lvalue}, because it is a location that can hold a value."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2648
msgid "The standard kinds of lvalues are:"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2652
msgid "A variable."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2656
msgid ""
"A pointer-dereference expression (@pxref{Pointer Dereference}) using unary "
"@samp{*}, if its type is not a function type."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2660
msgid ""
"A structure field reference (@pxref{Structures}) using @samp{.}, if the "
"structure value is an lvalue."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2664
msgid ""
"A structure field reference using @samp{->}.  This is always an lvalue since "
"@samp{->} implies pointer dereference."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2668
msgid ""
"A union alternative reference (@pxref{Unions}), on the same conditions as "
"for structure fields."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2672
msgid ""
"An array-element reference using @samp{[@r{@dots{}}]}, if the array is an "
"lvalue."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2675
msgid "A string constant (@pxref{String Constants})."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2678
msgid "An array constructor (@pxref{Constructing Array Values})."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2681
msgid "A structure or union constructor (@pxref{Structure Constructors})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2687
msgid ""
"If an expression's outermost operation is any other operator, that "
"expression is not an lvalue.  Thus, the variable @code{x} is an lvalue, but "
"@code{x + 0} is not, even though these two expressions compute the same "
"value (assuming @code{x} is a number)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2692
msgid ""
"It is rare that a structure value or an array value is not an lvalue, but "
"that does happen---for instance, the result of a function call or a "
"conditional operator can have a structure or array type, but is never an "
"lvalue."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2700
msgid ""
"If an array is an lvalue, using the array in an expression still converts it "
"automatically to a pointer to the zeroth element.  The result of this "
"conversion is not an lvalue.  Thus, if the variable @code{a} is an array, "
"you can't use @code{a} by itself as the left operand of an assignment.  But "
"you can assign to an element of @code{a}, such as @code{a[0]}.  That is an "
"lvalue since @code{a} is an lvalue."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2703
#, no-wrap
msgid "modifying assignment"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2704
#, no-wrap
msgid "assignment, modifying"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2707
msgid "You can abbreviate the common construct"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2710
#, no-wrap
msgid "@var{lvalue} = @var{lvalue} + @var{expression}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2714
msgid "as"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2717
#, no-wrap
msgid "@var{lvalue} += @var{expression}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2720
msgid "This is known as a @dfn{modifying assignment}.  For instance,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2724
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"i = i + 5;\n"
"i += 5;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2729
msgid ""
"shows two statements that are equivalent.  The first uses simple assignment; "
"the second uses modifying assignment."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2732
msgid ""
"Modifying assignment works with any binary arithmetic operator.  For "
"instance, you can subtract something from an lvalue like this,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2735
#, no-wrap
msgid "@var{lvalue} -= @var{expression}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2739
msgid "or multiply it by a certain amount like this,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2742
#, no-wrap
msgid "@var{lvalue} *= @var{expression}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2746
msgid "or shift it by a certain amount like this."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2750
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@var{lvalue} <<= @var{expression}\n"
"@var{lvalue} >>= @var{expression}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2758
msgid ""
"In most cases, this feature adds no power to the language, but it provides "
"substantial convenience.  Also, when @var{lvalue} contains code that has "
"side effects, the simple assignment performs those side effects twice, while "
"the modifying assignment performs them once.  For instance, suppose that the "
"function @code{foo} has a side effect, perhaps changing static storage.  "
"This statement"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2761
#, no-wrap
msgid "x[foo ()] = x[foo ()] + 5;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2771
msgid ""
"calls @code{foo} twice.  If @code{foo} operates on static variables, it "
"could return a different value each time.  If @code{foo ()} will return 1 "
"the first time and 3 the second time, the effect could be to add @code{x[3]} "
"and 5 and store the result in @code{x[1]}, or to add @code{x[1]} and 5 and "
"store the result in @code{x[3]}.  We don't know which of the two it will do, "
"because C does not specify which call to @code{foo} is computed first."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2773
msgid "Such a statement is not well defined, and shouldn't be used."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2775
msgid "By contrast,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2778
#, no-wrap
msgid "x[foo ()] += 5;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2784
msgid ""
"is well defined: it calls @code{foo} only once to determine which element of "
"@code{x} to adjust, and it adjusts that element by adding 5 to it."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2786
#, no-wrap
msgid "Increment and Decrement Operators"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2787
#, no-wrap
msgid "increment operator"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2788
#, no-wrap
msgid "decrement operator"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2789
#, no-wrap
msgid "operator, increment"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2790
#, no-wrap
msgid "operator, decrement"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2791
#, no-wrap
msgid "preincrement expression"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2792
#, no-wrap
msgid "predecrement expression"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2798
msgid ""
"The operators @samp{++} and @samp{--} are the @dfn{increment} and "
"@dfn{decrement} operators.  When used on a numeric value, they add or "
"subtract 1.  We don't consider them assignments, but they are equivalent to "
"assignments."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2802
msgid ""
"Using @samp{++} or @samp{--} as a prefix, before an lvalue, is called "
"@dfn{preincrement} or @dfn{predecrement}.  This adds or subtracts 1 and the "
"result becomes the expression's value.  For instance,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2805 c-intro-and-ref.texi:2826
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2860 c-intro-and-ref.texi:11192
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"#include <stdio.h>   /* @r{Declares @code{printf}.} */\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2815
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int\n"
"main (void)\n"
"@{\n"
"  int i = 5;\n"
"  printf (\"%d\\n"
"\", i);\n"
"  printf (\"%d\\n"
"\", ++i);\n"
"  printf (\"%d\\n"
"\", i);\n"
"  return 0;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2821
msgid ""
"prints lines containing @samp{5}, @samp{6}, and @samp{6} again.  The "
"expression @code{++i} increments @code{i} from 5 to 6, and has the value 6, "
"so the output from @code{printf} on that line says @samp{6}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2823
msgid "Using @samp{--} instead, for predecrement,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2836
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int\n"
"main (void)\n"
"@{\n"
"  int i = 5;\n"
"  printf (\"%d\\n"
"\", i);\n"
"  printf (\"%d\\n"
"\", --i);\n"
"  printf (\"%d\\n"
"\", i);\n"
"  return 0;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2841
msgid ""
"prints three lines that contain (respectively) @samp{5}, @samp{4}, and again "
"@samp{4}."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2843
#, no-wrap
msgid "Postincrement and Postdecrement"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2844
#, no-wrap
msgid "postincrement expression"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2845
#, no-wrap
msgid "postdecrement expression"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2846
#, no-wrap
msgid "operator, postincrement"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2847
#, no-wrap
msgid "operator, postdecrement"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2855
msgid ""
"Using @samp{++} or @samp{--} @emph{after} an lvalue does something peculiar: "
"it gets the value directly out of the lvalue and @emph{then} increments or "
"decrements it.  Thus, the value of @code{i++} is the same as the value of "
"@code{i}, but @code{i++} also increments @code{i} ``a little later.'' This "
"is called @dfn{postincrement} or @dfn{postdecrement}."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2857 c-intro-and-ref.texi:4934
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12689
msgid "For example,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2870
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int\n"
"main (void)\n"
"@{\n"
"  int i = 5;\n"
"  printf (\"%d\\n"
"\", i);\n"
"  printf (\"%d\\n"
"\", i++);\n"
"  printf (\"%d\\n"
"\", i);\n"
"  return 0;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2877
msgid ""
"prints lines containing @samp{5}, again @samp{5}, and @samp{6}.  The "
"expression @code{i++} has the value 5, which is the value of @code{i} at the "
"time, but it increments @code{i} from 5 to 6 just a little later."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2882
msgid ""
"How much later is ``just a little later''? The compiler has some flexibility "
"in deciding that.  The rule is that the increment has to happen by the next "
"@dfn{sequence point}; in simple cases, that means by the end of the "
"statement.  @xref{Sequence Points}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2886
msgid ""
"Regardless of precisely where the compiled code increments the value of "
"@code{i}, the crucial thing is that the value of @code{i++} is the value "
"that @code{i} has @emph{before} incrementing it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2889
msgid ""
"If a unary operator precedes a postincrement or postdecrement expression, "
"the post-whatever expression nests inside:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2892
#, no-wrap
msgid "-a++   @r{is equivalent to}   -(a++)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2896
msgid ""
"In some cases, for instance this one, the other order would not even make "
"sense; @code{-a} is not an lvalue, so it can't be incremented."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2902
msgid ""
"The most common use of postincrement is with arrays.  Here's an example of "
"using postincrement to access one element of an array and advance the index "
"for the next access.  Compare this with the example @code{avg_of_double} "
"(@pxref{Array Example}), which is almost the same but doesn't use "
"postincrement for that."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2909
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"double\n"
"avg_of_double_alt (int length, double input_data[])\n"
"@{\n"
"  double sum = 0;\n"
"  int i;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2914
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  /* @r{Fetch each element and add it into @code{sum}.}  */\n"
"  for (i = 0; i < length;)\n"
"    /* @r{Use the index @code{i}, then increment it.}  */\n"
"    sum += input_data[i++];\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2920
#, no-wrap
msgid "Pitfall: Assignment in Subexpressions"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2921
#, no-wrap
msgid "assignment in subexpressions"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2922
#, no-wrap
msgid "subexpressions, assignment in"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2930
msgid ""
"In C, the order of computing parts of an expression is not fixed.  Aside "
"from a few special cases, the operations can be computed in any order.  If "
"one part of the expression has an assignment to @code{x} and another part of "
"the expression uses @code{x}, the result is unpredictable because that use "
"might be computed before or after the assignment."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2932
msgid "Here's an example of ambiguous code:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2936 c-intro-and-ref.texi:3559
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"x = 20;\n"
"printf (\"%d %d\\n"
"\", x, x = 4);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2942
msgid ""
"If the second argument, @code{x}, is computed before the third argument, "
"@code{x = 4}, the second argument's value will be 20.  If they are computed "
"in the other order, the second argument's value will be 4."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2944
msgid "Here's one way to make that code unambiguous:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2948
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"y = 20;\n"
"printf (\"%d %d\\n"
"\", y, x = 4);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2951
msgid "Here's another way, with the other meaning:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2955
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"x = 4;\n"
"printf (\"%d %d\\n"
"\", x, x);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2960
msgid ""
"This issue applies to all kinds of assignments, and to the increment and "
"decrement operators, which are equivalent to assignments.  @xref{Order of "
"Execution}, for more information about this."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2964
msgid ""
"However, it can be useful to write assignments inside an @code{if}-condition "
"or @code{while}-test along with logical operators.  @xref{Logicals and "
"Assignments}."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2966
#, no-wrap
msgid "Write Assignments in Separate Statements"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2971
msgid ""
"It is often convenient to write an assignment inside an @code{if}-condition, "
"but that can reduce the readability of the program.  Here's an example of "
"what to avoid:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2975
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"if (x = advance (x))\n"
"  @r{@dots{}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2982
msgid ""
"The idea here is to advance @code{x} and test if the value is nonzero.  "
"However, readers might miss the fact that it uses @samp{=} and not "
"@samp{==}.  In fact, writing @samp{=} where @samp{==} was intended inside a "
"condition is a common error, so GNU C can give warnings when @samp{=} "
"appears in a way that suggests it's an error."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2984
msgid ""
"It is much clearer to write the assignment as a separate statement, like "
"this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2989
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"x = advance (x);\n"
"if (x != 0)\n"
"  @r{@dots{}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2993
msgid "This makes it unmistakably clear that @code{x} is assigned a new value."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:2996
msgid ""
"Another method is to use the comma operator (@pxref{Comma Operator}), like "
"this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3000
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"if (x = advance (x), x != 0)\n"
"  @r{@dots{}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3005
msgid ""
"However, putting the assignment in a separate statement is usually clearer "
"(unless the assignment is very short), because it reduces nesting."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3008
#, no-wrap
msgid "execution control expressions"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3009
#, no-wrap
msgid "expressions, execution control"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3013
msgid ""
"This chapter describes the C operators that combine expressions to control "
"which of those expressions execute, or in which order."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3024
#, no-wrap
msgid "logical operators"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3025
#, no-wrap
msgid "operators, logical"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3026
#, no-wrap
msgid "conjunction operator"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3027
#, no-wrap
msgid "disjunction operator"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3028
#, no-wrap
msgid "negation operator, logical"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3037
msgid ""
"The @dfn{logical operators} combine truth values, which are normally "
"represented in C as numbers.  Any expression with a numeric value is a valid "
"truth value: zero means false, and any other value means true.  A pointer "
"type is also meaningful as a truth value; a null pointer (which is zero) "
"means false, and a non-null pointer means true (@pxref{Pointer Types}).  The "
"value of a logical operator is always 1 or 0 and has type @code{int} "
"(@pxref{Integer Types})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3042
msgid ""
"The logical operators are used mainly in the condition of an @code{if} "
"statement, or in the end test in a @code{for} statement or @code{while} "
"statement (@pxref{Statements}).  However, they are valid in any context "
"where an integer-valued expression is allowed."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3044
#, no-wrap
msgid "! @var{exp}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3047
msgid ""
"Unary operator for logical ``not.'' The value is 1 (true) if @var{exp} is 0 "
"(false), and 0 (false) if @var{exp} is nonzero (true)."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3050
msgid ""
"@strong{Warning:} If @var{exp} is anything but an lvalue or a function call, "
"you should write parentheses around it."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3051
#, no-wrap
msgid "@var{left} && @var{right}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3057
msgid ""
"The logical ``and'' binary operator computes @var{left} and, if necessary, "
"@var{right}.  If both of the operands are true, the @samp{&&} expression "
"gives the value 1 (true).  Otherwise, the @samp{&&} expression gives the "
"value 0 (false).  If @var{left} yields a false value, that determines the "
"overall result, so @var{right} is not computed."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3058
#, no-wrap
msgid "@var{left} || @var{right}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3064
msgid ""
"The logical ``or'' binary operator computes @var{left} and, if necessary, "
"@var{right}.  If at least one of the operands is true, the @samp{||} "
"expression gives the value 1 (which is true).  Otherwise, the @samp{||} "
"expression gives the value 0 (false).  If @var{left} yields a true value, "
"that determines the overall result, so @var{right} is not computed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3069
msgid ""
"@strong{Warning:} Never rely on the relative precedence of @samp{&&} and "
"@samp{||}.  When you use them together, always use parentheses to specify "
"explicitly how they nest, as shown here:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3074
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"if ((r != 0 && x % r == 0)\n"
"    ||\n"
"    (s != 0 && x % s == 0))\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3077
#, no-wrap
msgid "Logical Operators and Comparisons"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3085
msgid ""
"The most common thing to use inside the logical operators is a comparison.  "
"Conveniently, @samp{&&} and @samp{||} have lower precedence than comparison "
"operators and arithmetic operators, so we can write expressions like this "
"without parentheses and get the nesting that is natural: two comparison "
"operations that must both be true."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3088
#, no-wrap
msgid "if (r != 0 && x % r == 0)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3094
msgid ""
"This example also shows how it is useful that @samp{&&} guarantees to skip "
"the right operand if the left one turns out false.  Because of that, this "
"code never tries to divide by zero."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3096
msgid "This is equivalent:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3099
#, no-wrap
msgid "if (r && x % r == 0)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3106
msgid ""
"A truth value is simply a number, so using @code{r} as a truth value tests "
"whether it is nonzero.  But @code{r}'s meaning as an expression is not a "
"truth value---it is a number to divide by.  So it is clearer style to write "
"the explicit @code{!= 0}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3108
msgid "Here's another equivalent way to write it:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3111
#, no-wrap
msgid "if (!(r == 0) && x % r == 0)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3116
msgid ""
"This illustrates the unary @samp{!} operator, as well as the need to write "
"parentheses around its operand."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3118
#, no-wrap
msgid "Logical Operators and Assignments"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3128
msgid ""
"There are cases where assignments nested inside the condition can actually "
"make a program @emph{easier} to read.  Here is an example using a "
"hypothetical type @code{list} which represents a list; it tests whether the "
"list has at least two links, using hypothetical functions, @code{nonempty} "
"which is true if the argument is a nonempty list, and @code{list_next} which "
"advances from one list link to the next.  We assume that a list is never a "
"null pointer, so that the assignment expressions are always ``true.''"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3135
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"if (nonempty (list)\n"
"    && (temp1 = list_next (list))\n"
"    && nonempty (temp1)\n"
"    && (temp2 = list_next (temp1)))\n"
"  @r{@dots{}}  /* @r{use @code{temp1} and @code{temp2}} */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3141
msgid ""
"Here we take advantage of the @samp{&&} operator to avoid executing the rest "
"of the code if a call to @code{nonempty} returns ``false.'' The only natural "
"place to put the assignments is among those calls."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3146
msgid ""
"It would be possible to rewrite this as several statements, but that could "
"make it much more cumbersome.  On the other hand, when the test is even more "
"complex than this one, splitting it into multiple statements might be "
"necessary for clarity."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3149
msgid ""
"If an empty list is a null pointer, we can dispense with calling "
"@code{nonempty}:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3155
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"if (list\n"
"    && (temp1 = list_next (list))\n"
"    && (temp2 = list_next (temp1)))\n"
" @r{@dots{}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3159
#, no-wrap
msgid "conditional expression"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3160
#, no-wrap
msgid "expression, conditional"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3164
msgid ""
"C has a conditional expression that selects one of two expressions to "
"compute and get the value from.  It looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3167
#, no-wrap
msgid "@var{condition} ? @var{iftrue} : @var{iffalse}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3172 c-intro-and-ref.texi:3174
#, no-wrap
msgid "Conditional Rules"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3172
msgid "Rules for the conditional operator."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3172 c-intro-and-ref.texi:3216
#, no-wrap
msgid "Conditional Branches"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3172
msgid "About the two branches in a conditional."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3175
#, no-wrap
msgid "Rules for the Conditional Operator"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3185
msgid ""
"The first operand, @var{condition}, should be a value that can be compared "
"with zero---a number or a pointer.  If it is true (nonzero), then the "
"conditional expression computes @var{iftrue} and its value becomes the value "
"of the conditional expression.  Otherwise the conditional expression "
"computes @var{iffalse} and its value becomes the value of the conditional "
"expression.  The conditional expression always computes just one of "
"@var{iftrue} and @var{iffalse}, never both of them."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3188
msgid ""
"Here's an example: the absolute value of a number @code{x} can be written as "
"@code{(x >= 0 ? x : -x)}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3194
msgid ""
"@strong{Warning:} The conditional expression has rather low syntactic "
"precedence.  Except when the conditional expression is used as an argument "
"in a function call, write parentheses around it.  For clarity, always write "
"parentheses around it if it extends across more than one line."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3199
msgid ""
"@strong{Warning:} Assignment operators and the comma operator (@pxref{Comma "
"Operator}) have lower precedence than conditional expressions, so write "
"parentheses around those when they appear inside a conditional expression.  "
"@xref{Order of Execution}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3205
msgid ""
"@strong{Warning:} When nesting a conditional expression within another "
"conditional expression, unless a pair of matching delimiters surrounds the "
"inner conditional expression for some other reason, write parentheses around "
"it:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3209
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"((foo > 0 ? test1 : test2) ? (ifodd (foo) ? 5 : 10)\n"
"                           : (ifodd (whatever) ? 5 : 10));\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3215
msgid ""
"In the first operand, those parentheses are necessary to prevent incorrect "
"parsing.  In the second and third operands, the computer may not need the "
"parentheses, but they will help human beings."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3217
#, no-wrap
msgid "Conditional Operator Branches"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3218
#, no-wrap
msgid "branches of conditional expression"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3222
msgid ""
"We call @var{iftrue} and @var{iffalse} the @dfn{branches} of the conditional."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3226
msgid ""
"The two branches should normally have the same type, but a few exceptions "
"are allowed.  If they are both numeric types, the conditional converts both "
"to their common type (@pxref{Common Type})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3231
msgid ""
"With pointers (@pxref{Pointers}), the two values can be pointers to nearly "
"compatible types (@pxref{Compatible Types}).  In this case, the result type "
"is a similar pointer whose target type combines all the type qualifiers "
"(@pxref{Type Qualifiers}) of both branches."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3235
msgid ""
"If one branch has type @code{void *} and the other is a pointer to an object "
"(not to a function), the conditional converts the latter to @code{void *}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3238
msgid ""
"If one branch is an integer constant with value zero and the other is a "
"pointer, the conditional converts zero to the pointer's type."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3243
msgid ""
"In GNU C, you can omit @var{iftrue} in a conditional expression.  In that "
"case, if @var{condition} is nonzero, its value becomes the value of the "
"conditional expression, after conversion to the common type.  Thus,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3246
#, no-wrap
msgid "x ? : y\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3251
msgid ""
"has the value of @code{x} if that is nonzero; otherwise, the value of "
"@code{y}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3252
#, no-wrap
msgid "side effect in ?:"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3253
#, no-wrap
msgid "?: side effect"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3260
msgid ""
"Omitting @var{iftrue} is useful when @var{condition} has side effects.  In "
"that case, writing that expression twice would carry out the side effects "
"twice, but writing it once does them just once.  For example, if we suppose "
"that the function @code{next_element} advances a pointer variable to point "
"to the next element in a list and returns the new pointer,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3263
#, no-wrap
msgid "next_element () ? : default_pointer\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3269
msgid ""
"is a way to advance the pointer and use its new value if it isn't null, but "
"use @code{default_pointer} if that is null.  We cannot do it this way,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3272
#, no-wrap
msgid "next_element () ? next_element () : default_pointer\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3276
msgid "because that would advance the pointer a second time."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3279
#, no-wrap
msgid "comma operator"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3280
#, no-wrap
msgid "operator, comma"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3286
msgid ""
"The comma operator stands for sequential execution of expressions.  The "
"value of the comma expression comes from the last expression in the "
"sequence; the previous expressions are computed only for their side "
"effects.  It looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3289
#, no-wrap
msgid "@var{exp1}, @var{exp2} @r{@dots{}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3294
msgid ""
"You can bundle any number of expressions together this way, by putting "
"commas between them."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3299 c-intro-and-ref.texi:3301
#, no-wrap
msgid "Uses of Comma"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3299
msgid "When to use the comma operator."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3299 c-intro-and-ref.texi:3328
#, no-wrap
msgid "Clean Comma"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3299
msgid "Clean use of the comma operator."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3299 c-intro-and-ref.texi:3373
#, no-wrap
msgid "Avoid Comma"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3299
msgid "When to not use the comma operator."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3302
#, no-wrap
msgid "The Uses of the Comma Operator"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3307
msgid ""
"With commas, you can put several expressions into a place that allows one "
"expression---for example, in the header of a @code{for} statement.  This "
"statement"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3310 c-intro-and-ref.texi:3338
#, no-wrap
msgid "for (i = 0, j = 10, k = 20; i < n; i++)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3319
msgid ""
"contains three assignment expressions, to initialize @code{i}, @code{j} and "
"@code{k}.  The syntax of @code{for} requires just one expression for "
"initialization; to include three assignments, we use commas to bundle them "
"into a single larger expression, @code{i = 0, j = 10, k = 20}.  This "
"technique is also useful in the loop-advance expression, the last of the "
"three inside the @code{for} parentheses."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3323
msgid ""
"In the @code{for} statement and the @code{while} statement (@pxref{Loop "
"Statements}), a comma provides a way to perform some side effect before the "
"loop-exit test.  For example,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3326
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"while (printf (\"At the test, x = %d\\n"
"\", x), x != 0)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3329
#, no-wrap
msgid "Clean Use of the Comma Operator"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3335
msgid ""
"Always write parentheses around a series of comma operators, except when it "
"is at top level in an expression statement, or within the parentheses of an "
"@code{if}, @code{for}, @code{while}, or @code{switch} statement "
"(@pxref{Statements}).  For instance, in"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3343
msgid ""
"the commas between the assignments are clear because they are between a "
"parenthesis and a semicolon."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3346
msgid ""
"The arguments in a function call are also separated by commas, but that is "
"not an instance of the comma operator.  Note the difference between"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3349
#, no-wrap
msgid "foo (4, 5, 6)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3353
msgid "which passes three arguments to @code{foo} and"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3356
#, no-wrap
msgid "foo ((4, 5, 6))\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3361
msgid ""
"which uses the comma operator and passes just one argument (with value 6)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3367
msgid ""
"@strong{Warning:} Don't use the comma operator within an argument of a "
"function unless it makes the code more readable.  When you do so, don't put "
"part of another argument on the same line.  Instead, add a line break to "
"make the parentheses around the comma operator easier to see, like this."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3371
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"foo ((mumble (x, y), frob (z)),\n"
"     *p)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3374
#, no-wrap
msgid "When Not to Use the Comma Operator"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3380
msgid ""
"You can use a comma in any subexpression, but in most cases it only makes "
"the code confusing, and it is clearer to raise all but the last of the "
"comma-separated expressions to a higher level.  Thus, instead of this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3383
#, no-wrap
msgid "x = (y += 4, 8);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3387
msgid "it is much clearer to write this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3390
#, no-wrap
msgid "y += 4, x = 8;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3394
msgid "or this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3398
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"y += 4;\n"
"x = 8;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3402
msgid ""
"Use commas only in the cases where there is no clearer alternative involving "
"multiple statements."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3408
msgid ""
"By contrast, don't hesitate to use commas in the expansion in a macro "
"definition.  The trade-offs of code clarity are different in that case, "
"because the @emph{use} of the macro may improve overall clarity so much that "
"the ugliness of the macro's @emph{definition} is a small price to pay.  "
"@xref{Macros}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3411
#, no-wrap
msgid "binary operator grammar"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3412
#, no-wrap
msgid "grammar, binary operator"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3413
#, no-wrap
msgid "operator precedence"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3414
#, no-wrap
msgid "precedence, operator"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3415
#, no-wrap
msgid "left-associative"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3419
msgid ""
"@dfn{Binary operators} are those that take two operands, one on the left and "
"one on the right."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3428
msgid ""
"All the binary operators in C are syntactically left-associative.  This "
"means that @w{@code{a @var{op} b @var{op} c}} means @w{@code{(a @var{op} b) "
"@var{op} c}}.  However, the only operators you should repeat in this way "
"without parentheses are @samp{+}, @samp{-}, @samp{*} and @samp{/}, because "
"those cases are clear from algebra.  So it is OK to write @code{a + b + c} "
"or @code{a - b - c}, but never @code{a == b == c} or @code{a % b % c}.  For "
"those operators, use explicit parentheses to show how the operations nest."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3433
msgid ""
"Each C operator has a @dfn{precedence}, which is its rank in the grammatical "
"order of the various operators.  The operators with the highest precedence "
"grab adjoining operands first; these expressions then become operands for "
"operators of lower precedence."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3445
msgid ""
"The precedence order of operators in C is fully specified, so any "
"combination of operations leads to a well-defined nesting.  We state only "
"part of the full precedence ordering here because it is bad practice for C "
"code to depend on the other cases.  For cases not specified in this chapter, "
"always use parentheses to make the nesting explicit.@footnote{Personal note "
"from Richard Stallman: I wrote GCC without remembering anything about the C "
"precedence order beyond what's stated here.  I studied the full precedence "
"table to write the parser, and promptly forgot it again.  If you need to "
"look up the full precedence order to understand some C code, add enough "
"parentheses so nobody else needs to do that.}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3448
msgid ""
"Clean code can depend on this subsequence of the precedence ordering (stated "
"from highest precedence to lowest):"
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3454
msgid ""
"Postfix operations: access to a field or alternative (@samp{.} and "
"@samp{->}), array subscripting, function calls, and unary postfix operators."
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3457
msgid "Unary prefix operations."
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3460
msgid ""
"Multiplication, division, and remainder (they have the same precedence)."
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3463
msgid "Addition and subtraction (they have the same precedence)."
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3466
msgid "Comparisons---but watch out!"
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3469
msgid "Logical operations @samp{&&} and @samp{||}---but watch out!"
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3472 c-intro-and-ref.texi:3501
msgid "Conditional expression with @samp{?} and @samp{:}."
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3475
msgid "Assignments."
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3478
msgid "Sequential execution (the comma operator, @samp{,})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3485
msgid ""
"Two of the lines in the above list say ``but watch out!'' That means that "
"the line covers operations with subtly different precedence.  When you use "
"two comparison operations together, don't depend on the grammar of C to "
"control how they nest.  Instead, always use parentheses to show their "
"nesting."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3489
msgid ""
"You can let several @samp{&&} operations associate, or several @samp{||} "
"operations, but always use parentheses to show how @samp{&&} and @samp{||} "
"nest with each other.  @xref{Logical Operators}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3491
msgid "There is one other precedence ordering that clean code can depend on:"
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3495
msgid "Unary postfix operations."
msgstr ""

#. type: enumerate
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3498
msgid "Bitwise and shift operations---but watch out!"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3507
msgid ""
"The caveat for bitwise and shift operations is like that for logical "
"operators: you can let multiple uses of one bitwise operation associate, but "
"always use parentheses to control nesting of dissimilar operations."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3512
msgid ""
"These lists do not specify any precedence ordering between the bitwise and "
"shift operations of the second list and the binary operations above "
"conditional expressions in the first list.  When they come together, "
"parenthesize them.  @xref{Bitwise Operations}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3515
#, no-wrap
msgid "order of execution"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3519
msgid ""
"The order of execution of a C program is not always obvious, and not "
"necessarily predictable.  This chapter describes what you can count on."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3535 c-intro-and-ref.texi:3703
#, no-wrap
msgid "ordering of operands"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3536
#, no-wrap
msgid "reordering of operands"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3537
#, no-wrap
msgid "operand execution ordering"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3542
msgid ""
"The C language does not necessarily carry out operations within an "
"expression in the order they appear in the code.  For instance, in this "
"expression,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3545
#, no-wrap
msgid "foo () + bar ()\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3551
msgid ""
"@code{foo} might be called first or @code{bar} might be called first.  If "
"@code{foo} updates a datum and @code{bar} uses that datum, the results can "
"be unpredictable."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3555
msgid ""
"The unpredictable order of computation of subexpressions also makes a "
"difference when one of them contains an assignment.  We already saw this "
"example of bad code,"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3565
msgid ""
"in which the second argument, @code{x}, has a different value depending on "
"whether it is computed before or after the assignment in the third argument."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3568
#, no-wrap
msgid "associativity and ordering"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3579
msgid ""
"The bitwise binary operators, @code{&}, @code{|} and @code{^}, are "
"associative.  The arithmetic binary operators @code{+} and @code{*} are "
"associative if the operand type is unsigned.  An associative binary "
"operator, when used repeatedly, can combine any number of operands.  The "
"operands' values may be computed in any order, and since the operation is "
"associative, they can be combined in any order too."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3582
msgid ""
"Thus, given four functions that return @code{unsigned int}, calling them and "
"adding their results as here"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3585
#, no-wrap
msgid "(foo () + bar ()) + (baz () + quux ())\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3589
msgid "may add up the results in any order."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3597
msgid ""
"By contrast, arithmetic on signed integers is not always associative because "
"there is the possibility of overflow (@pxref{Integer Overflow}).  Thus, the "
"additions must be done in the order specified, obeying parentheses (or "
"left-association in the absence of parentheses).  That means computing "
"@code{(foo () + bar ())} and @code{(baz () + quux ())} first (in either "
"order), then adding the two."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3608
msgid ""
"The same applies to arithmetic on floating-point values, since that too is "
"not really associative.  However, the GCC option "
"@option{-funsafe-math-optimizations} allows the compiler to change the order "
"of calculation when an associative operation (associative in exact "
"mathematics) combines several operands.  The option takes effect when "
"compiling a module (@pxref{Compilation}).  Changing the order of association "
"can enable GCC to optimize the floating-point operations better."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3611
msgid ""
"In all these examples, the four function calls can be done in any order.  "
"There is no right or wrong about that."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3614
#, no-wrap
msgid "sequence points"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3615
#, no-wrap
msgid "full expression"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3620
msgid ""
"There are some points in the code where C makes limited guarantees about the "
"order of operations.  These are called @dfn{sequence points}.  Here is where "
"they occur:"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3627
msgid ""
"At the end of a @dfn{full expression}; that is to say, an expression that is "
"not part of a larger expression.  All side effects specified by that "
"expression are carried out before execution moves on to subsequent code."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3633
msgid ""
"At the end of the first operand of certain operators: @samp{,}, @samp{&&}, "
"@samp{||}, and @samp{?:}.  All side effects specified by that expression are "
"carried out before any execution of the next operand."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3638
msgid ""
"The commas that separate arguments in a function call are @emph{not} comma "
"operators, and they do not create sequence points.  The sequence-point rule "
"for function arguments and the rule for operands (@pxref{Ordering of "
"Operands}) are different."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3642
msgid ""
"Just before calling a function.  All side effects specified by the argument "
"expressions are carried out before calling the function."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3646
msgid ""
"If the function to be called is not constant---that is, if it is computed by "
"an expression---all side effects in that expression are carried out before "
"calling the function."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3652
msgid ""
"The ordering imposed by a sequence point applies locally to a limited range "
"of code, as stated above in each case.  For instance, the ordering imposed "
"by the comma operator does not apply to code outside the operands of that "
"comma operator.  Thus, in this code,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3655
#, no-wrap
msgid "(x = 5, foo (x)) + x * x\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3661
msgid ""
"the sequence point of the comma operator orders @code{x = 5} before "
"@code{foo (x)}, but @code{x * x} could be computed before or after them."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3664
#, no-wrap
msgid "postincrement and ordering"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3665
#, no-wrap
msgid "ordering and postincrement"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3672
msgid ""
"The ordering requirements for the postincrement and postdecrement operations "
"(@pxref{Postincrement/Postdecrement}) are loose: those side effects must "
"happen ``a little later,'' before the next sequence point.  That still "
"leaves room for various orders that give different results.  In this "
"expression,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3675
#, no-wrap
msgid "z = x++ - foo ()\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3681
msgid ""
"it's unpredictable whether @code{x} gets incremented before or after calling "
"the function @code{foo}.  If @code{foo} refers to @code{x}, it might see the "
"old value or it might see the incremented value."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3683
msgid "In this perverse expression,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3686
#, no-wrap
msgid "x = x++\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3696
msgid ""
"@code{x} will certainly be incremented but the incremented value may be "
"replaced with the old value.  That's because the incrementation and the "
"assignment may occur in either order.  If the incrementation of @code{x} "
"occurs after the assignment to @code{x}, the incremented value will remain "
"in place.  But if the incrementation happens first, the assignment will put "
"the not-yet-incremented value back into @code{x}, so the expression as a "
"whole will leave @code{x} unchanged."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3700
msgid ""
"The conclusion: @strong{avoid such expressions}.  Take care, when you use "
"postincrement and postdecrement, that the specific expression you use is not "
"ambiguous as to order of execution."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3704
#, no-wrap
msgid "operand ordering"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3708
msgid ""
"Operands and arguments can be computed in any order, but there are limits to "
"this intermixing in GNU C:"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3715
msgid ""
"The operands of a binary arithmetic operator can be computed in either "
"order, but they can't be intermixed: one of them has to come first, followed "
"by the other.  Any side effects in the operand that's computed first are "
"executed before the other operand is computed."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3719
msgid ""
"That applies to assignment operators too, except that, in simple assignment, "
"the previous value of the left operand is unused."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3726
msgid ""
"The arguments in a function call can be computed in any order, but they "
"can't be intermixed.  Thus, one argument is fully computed, then another, "
"and so on until they have all been done.  Any side effects in one argument "
"are executed before computation of another argument begins."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3731
msgid ""
"These rules don't cover side effects caused by postincrement and "
"postdecrement operators---those can be deferred up to the next sequence "
"point."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3739
msgid ""
"If you want to get pedantic, the fact is that GCC can reorder the "
"computations in many other ways provided that it doesn't alter the result of "
"running the program.  However, because it doesn't alter the result of "
"running the program, it is negligible, unless you are concerned with the "
"values in certain variables at various times as seen by other processes.  In "
"those cases, you should use @code{volatile} to prevent optimizations that "
"would make them behave strangely.  @xref{volatile}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3742
#, no-wrap
msgid "optimization and ordering"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3743
#, no-wrap
msgid "ordering and optimization"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3748
msgid ""
"Sequence points limit the compiler's freedom to reorder operations "
"arbitrarily, but optimizations can still reorder them if the compiler "
"concludes that this won't alter the results.  Thus, in this code,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3753
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"x++;\n"
"y = z;\n"
"x++;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3761
msgid ""
"there is a sequence point after each statement, so the code is supposed to "
"increment @code{x} once before the assignment to @code{y} and once after.  "
"However, incrementing @code{x} has no effect on @code{y} or @code{z}, and "
"setting @code{y} can't affect @code{x}, so the code could be optimized into "
"this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3765
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"y = z;\n"
"x += 2;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3772
msgid ""
"Normally that has no effect except to make the program faster.  But there "
"are special situations where it can cause trouble due to things that the "
"compiler cannot know about, such as shared memory.  To limit optimization in "
"those places, use the @code{volatile} type qualifier (@pxref{volatile})."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3775
#, no-wrap
msgid "primitive types"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3776
#, no-wrap
msgid "types, primitive"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3781
msgid ""
"This chapter describes all the primitive data types of C---that is, all the "
"data types that aren't built up from other types.  They include the types "
"@code{int} and @code{double} that we've already covered."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3789 c-intro-and-ref.texi:4191
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4192
#, no-wrap
msgid "Type Designators"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3789
msgid "Referring to a data type abstractly."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3792
msgid "These types are all made up of bytes (@pxref{Storage})."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3794
#, no-wrap
msgid "Integer Data Types"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3795
#, no-wrap
msgid "integer types"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3796
#, no-wrap
msgid "types, integer"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3801
msgid ""
"Here we describe all the integer types and their basic characteristics.  "
"@xref{Integers in Depth}, for more information about the bit-level integer "
"data representations and arithmetic."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3812 c-intro-and-ref.texi:3814
