#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3812 c-intro-and-ref.texi:3980
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3981
#, no-wrap
msgid "Integer Variations"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3812
msgid "Sizes of integer types can vary across platforms."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3817
#, no-wrap
msgid "char"
msgstr ""

#. type: code{#1}
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3818 c-intro-and-ref.texi:3846
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4346
#, no-wrap
msgid "int"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3819 c-intro-and-ref.texi:3843
#, no-wrap
msgid "short int"
msgstr ""

#. type: code{#1}
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3820 c-intro-and-ref.texi:3850
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4350
#, no-wrap
msgid "long int"
msgstr ""

#. type: code{#1}
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3821 c-intro-and-ref.texi:3856
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4354
#, no-wrap
msgid "long long int"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3827
msgid ""
"Integer data types in C can be signed or unsigned.  An unsigned type can "
"represent only positive numbers and zero.  A signed type can represent both "
"positive and negative numbers, in a range spread almost equally on both "
"sides of zero."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3831
msgid ""
"Aside from signedness, the integer data types vary in size: how many bytes "
"long they are.  The size determines the range of integer values the type can "
"hold."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3835
msgid ""
"Here's a list of the signed integer data types, with the sizes they have on "
"most computers.  Each has a corresponding unsigned type; see @ref{Signed and "
"Unsigned Types}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3837
#, no-wrap
msgid "signed char"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3841
msgid ""
"One byte (8 bits).  This integer type is used mainly for integers that "
"represent characters, usually as elements of arrays or fields of other data "
"structures."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3842
#, no-wrap
msgid "short"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3845
msgid "Two bytes (16 bits)."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3848
msgid "Four bytes (32 bits)."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3849
#, no-wrap
msgid "long"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3854
msgid ""
"Four bytes (32 bits) or eight bytes (64 bits), depending on the platform.  "
"Typically it is 32 bits on 32-bit computers and 64 bits on 64-bit computers, "
"but there are exceptions."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3855
#, no-wrap
msgid "long long"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3859
msgid ""
"Eight bytes (64 bits).  Supported in GNU C in the 1980s, and incorporated "
"into standard C as of ISO C99."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3863
msgid ""
"You can omit @code{int} when you use @code{long} or @code{short}.  This is "
"harmless and customary."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3866
#, no-wrap
msgid "signed types"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3867
#, no-wrap
msgid "unsigned types"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3868
#, no-wrap
msgid "types, signed"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3869
#, no-wrap
msgid "types, unsigned"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3870
#, no-wrap
msgid "signed"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3871
#, no-wrap
msgid "unsigned"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3880
msgid ""
"An unsigned integer type can represent only positive numbers and zero.  A "
"signed type can represent both positive and negative numbers, in a range "
"spread almost equally on both sides of zero.  For instance, @code{unsigned "
"char} holds numbers from 0 to 255 (on most computers), while @code{signed "
"char} holds numbers from @minus{}128 to 127.  Each of these types holds 256 "
"different possible values, since they are both 8 bits wide."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3885
msgid ""
"Write @code{signed} or @code{unsigned} before the type keyword to specify a "
"signed or an unsigned type.  However, the integer types other than "
"@code{char} are signed by default; with them, @code{signed} is a no-op."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3890
msgid ""
"Plain @code{char} may be signed or unsigned; this depends on the compiler, "
"the machine in use, and its operating system.  It is not @emph{the same "
"type} as either @code{signed char} or @code{unsigned char}, but it is always "
"equivalent to one of those two."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3898
msgid ""
"In many programs, it makes no difference whether the type @code{char} is "
"signed.  When signedness does matter for a certain value, don't leave it to "
"chance; declare it as @code{signed char} or @code{unsigned char} "
"instead.@footnote{Personal note from Richard Stallman: Eating with hackers "
"at a fish restaurant, I ordered arctic char.  When my meal arrived, I noted "
"that the chef had not signed it.  So I told other hackers, ``This char is "
"unsigned---I wanted a signed char!''}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3907
msgid ""
"The types that are narrower than @code{int} are rarely used for ordinary "
"variables---we declare them @code{int} instead.  This is because C converts "
"those narrower types to @code{int} for any arithmetic.  There is literally "
"no reason to declare a local variable @code{char}, for instance."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3915
msgid ""
"In particular, if the value is really a character, you should declare the "
"variable @code{int}.  Not @code{char}! Using that narrow type can force the "
"compiled code to truncate values to @code{char} before conversion, which is "
"a waste.  Furthermore, some functions return either a character value or "
"@minus{}1 for ``no character.'' Using type @code{int} makes it possible to "
"distinguish @minus{}1 from any character, by sign."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3919
msgid ""
"The narrow integer types are useful as parts of other objects, such as "
"arrays and structures.  Compare these array declarations, whose sizes on "
"32-bit processors are shown:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3925
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"signed char ac[1000];   /* @r{1000 bytes} */\n"
"short as[1000];         /* @r{2000 bytes} */\n"
"int ai[1000];           /* @r{4000 bytes} */\n"
"long long all[1000];    /* @r{8000 bytes} */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3931
msgid ""
"In addition, character strings must be made up of @code{char}s, because "
"that's what all the standard library string functions expect.  Thus, array "
"@code{ac} could be used as a character string, but the others could not be."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3933
#, no-wrap
msgid "Conversion among Integer Types"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3940
msgid ""
"C converts between integer types implicitly in many situations.  It converts "
"the narrow integer types, @code{char} and @code{short}, to @code{int} "
"whenever they are used in arithmetic.  Assigning a new value to an integer "
"variable (or other lvalue) converts the value to the variable's type."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3943
msgid ""
"You can also convert one integer type to another explicitly with a "
"@dfn{cast} operator.  @xref{Explicit Type Conversion}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3949
msgid ""
"The process of conversion to a wider type is straightforward: the value is "
"unchanged.  The only exception is when converting a negative value (in a "
"signed type, obviously) to a wider unsigned type.  In that case, the result "
"is a positive value with the same bits (@pxref{Integers in Depth}), padded "
"on the left with zeros."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3950
#, no-wrap
msgid "truncation"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3956
msgid ""
"Converting to a narrower type, also called @dfn{truncation}, involves "
"discarding some of the value's bits.  This is not considered overflow "
"(@pxref{Integer Overflow}) because loss of significant bits is a normal "
"consequence of truncation.  Likewise for conversion between signed and "
"unsigned types of the same width."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3960
msgid ""
"More information about conversion for assignment is in @ref{Assignment Type "
"Conversions}.  For conversion for arithmetic, see @ref{Argument Promotions}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3963
#, no-wrap
msgid "boolean type"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3964
#, no-wrap
msgid "type, boolean"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3965
#, no-wrap
msgid "bool"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3970
msgid ""
"The unsigned integer type @code{bool} holds truth values: its possible "
"values are 0 and 1.  Converting any nonzero value to @code{bool} results in "
"1.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3975
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"bool a = 0;\n"
"bool b = 1;\n"
"bool c = 4; /* @r{Stores the value 1 in @code{c}.}  */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3979
msgid ""
"Unlike @code{int}, @code{bool} is not a keyword.  It is defined in the "
"header file @file{stdbool.h}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3987
msgid ""
"The integer types of C have standard @emph{names}, but what they @emph{mean} "
"varies depending on the kind of platform in use: which kind of computer, "
"which operating system, and which compiler.  It may even depend on the "
"compiler options used."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3990
msgid ""
"Plain @code{char} may be signed or unsigned; this depends on the platform, "
"too.  Even for GNU C, there is no general rule."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:3997
msgid ""
"In theory, all of the integer types' sizes can vary.  @code{char} is always "
"considered one ``byte'' for C, but it is not necessarily an 8-bit byte; on "
"some platforms it may be more than 8 bits.  @code{short int} and @code{int} "
"are at least two bytes long (it may be longer).  @code{long int} is at least "
"four bytes long, and @code{long long int} at least eight bytes long."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4002
msgid ""
"It is possible that in the future GNU C will support platforms where "
"@code{int} is 64 bits long.  In practice, however, on today's real "
"computers, there is little variation; you can rely on the table given "
"previously (@pxref{Basic Integers})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4008
msgid ""
"To be completely sure of the size of an integer type, use the types "
"@code{int16_t}, @code{int32_t} and @code{int64_t}.  Their corresponding "
"unsigned types add @samp{u} at the front: @code{uint16_t}, @code{uint32_t} "
"and @code{uint64_t}.  To define all these types, include the header file "
"@file{stdint.h}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4015
msgid ""
"The GNU C Compiler can compile for some embedded controllers that use two "
"bytes for @code{int}.  On some, @code{int} is just one ``byte,'' and so is "
"@code{short int}---but that ``byte'' may contain 16 bits or even 32 bits.  "
"These processors can't support an ordinary operating system (they may have "
"their own specialized operating systems), and most C programs do not try to "
"support them."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4018
#, no-wrap
msgid "floating-point types"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4019
#, no-wrap
msgid "types, floating-point"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4020 c-intro-and-ref.texi:4046
#, no-wrap
msgid "double"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4021 c-intro-and-ref.texi:4053
#, no-wrap
msgid "float"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4022 c-intro-and-ref.texi:4065
#, no-wrap
msgid "long double"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4030
msgid ""
"@dfn{Floating point} is the binary analogue of scientific notation: "
"internally it represents a number as a fraction and a binary exponent; the "
"value is that fraction multiplied by the specified power of 2.  (The C "
"standard nominally permits other bases, but in GNU C the base is always 2.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4037
msgid ""
"For instance, to represent 6, the fraction would be 0.75 and the exponent "
"would be 3; together they stand for the value @math{0.75 * 2@sup{3}}, "
"meaning 0.75 * 8.  The value 1.5 would use 0.75 as the fraction and 1 as the "
"exponent.  The value 0.75 would use 0.75 as the fraction and 0 as the "
"exponent.  The value 0.375 would use 0.75 as the fraction and @minus{}1 as "
"the exponent."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4042
msgid ""
"These binary exponents are used by machine instructions.  You can write a "
"floating-point constant this way if you wish, using hexadecimal; but "
"normally we write floating-point numbers in decimal (base 10).  "
"@xref{Floating Constants}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4044
msgid "C has three floating-point data types:"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4052
msgid ""
"``Double-precision'' floating point, which uses 64 bits.  This is the normal "
"floating-point type, and modern computers normally do their floating-point "
"computations in this type, or some wider type.  Except when there is a "
"special reason to do otherwise, this is the type to use for floating-point "
"values."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4060
msgid ""
"``Single-precision'' floating point, which uses 32 bits.  It is useful for "
"floating-point values stored in structures and arrays, to save space when "
"the full precision of @code{double} is not needed.  In addition, "
"single-precision arithmetic is faster on some computers, and occasionally "
"that is useful.  But not often---most programs don't use the type "
"@code{float}."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4064
msgid ""
"C would be cleaner if @code{float} were the name of the type we use for most "
"floating-point values; however, for historical reasons, that's not so."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4070
msgid ""
"``Extended-precision'' floating point is either 80-bit or 128-bit precision, "
"depending on the machine in use.  On some machines, which have no "
"floating-point format wider than @code{double}, this is equivalent to "
"@code{double}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4074
msgid ""
"Floating-point arithmetic raises many subtle issues.  @xref{Floating Point "
"in Depth}, for more information."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4077
#, no-wrap
msgid "complex numbers"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4078
#, no-wrap
msgid "types, complex"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4079
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{_Complex} keyword"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4080
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{__complex__} keyword"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4081
#, no-wrap
msgid "_Complex"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4082
#, no-wrap
msgid "__complex__"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4088
msgid ""
"Complex numbers can include both a real part and an imaginary part.  The "
"numeric constants covered above have real-numbered values.  An "
"imaginary-valued constant is an ordinary real-valued constant followed by "
"@samp{i}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4094
msgid ""
"To declare numeric variables as complex, use the @code{_Complex} "
"keyword.@footnote{For compatibility with older versions of GNU C, the "
"keyword @code{__complex__} is also allowed.  Going forward, however, use the "
"new @code{_Complex} keyword as defined in ISO C11.} The standard C complex "
"data types are floating point,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4099
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"_Complex float foo;\n"
"_Complex double bar;\n"
"_Complex long double quux;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4103
msgid "but GNU C supports integer complex types as well."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4107
msgid ""
"Since @code{_Complex} is a keyword just like @code{float} and @code{double} "
"and @code{long}, the keywords can appear in any order, but the order shown "
"above seems most logical."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4112
msgid ""
"GNU C supports constants for complex values; for instance, @code{4.0 + 3.0i} "
"has the value 4 + 3i as type @code{_Complex double}.  @samp{j} is equivalent "
"to @samp{i}, as a numeric suffix.  @xref{Imaginary Constants}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4115
msgid ""
"To pull the real and imaginary parts of the number back out, GNU C provides "
"the keywords @code{__real__} and @code{__imag__}:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4118
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"_Complex double foo = 4.0 + 3.0i;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4121
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"double a = __real__ foo; /* @r{@code{a} is now 4.0.} */\n"
"double b = __imag__ foo; /* @r{@code{b} is now 3.0.} */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4130
msgid ""
"Standard C does not include these keywords, and instead relies on functions "
"defined in @code{complex.h} for accessing the real and imaginary parts of a "
"complex number: @code{crealf}, @code{creal}, and @code{creall} extract the "
"real part of a float, double, or long double complex number, respectively; "
"@code{cimagf}, @code{cimag}, and @code{cimagl} extract the imaginary part."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4131
#, no-wrap
msgid "complex conjugation"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4134
msgid ""
"GNU C also defines @samp{~} as an operator for complex conjugation, which "
"means negating the imaginary part of a complex number:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4138
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"_Complex double foo = 4.0 + 3.0i;\n"
"_Complex double bar = ~foo; /* @r{@code{bar} is now 4.0 @minus{} 3.0i.} */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4143
msgid ""
"For standard C compatibility, you can use the appropriate library function: "
"@code{conjf}, @code{conj}, or @code{conjl}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4146
#, no-wrap
msgid "void type"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4147
#, no-wrap
msgid "type, void"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4148
#, no-wrap
msgid "void"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4155
msgid ""
"The data type @code{void} is a dummy---it allows no operations.  It really "
"means ``no value at all.'' When a function is meant to return no value, we "
"write @code{void} for its return type.  Then @code{return} statements in "
"that function should not specify a value (@pxref{return Statement}).  Here's "
"an example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4166
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"print_if_positive (double x, double y)\n"
"@{\n"
"  if (x <= 0)\n"
"    return;\n"
"  if (y <= 0)\n"
"    return;\n"
"  printf (\"Next point is (%f,%f)\\n"
"\", x, y);\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4171
msgid ""
"A @code{void}-returning function is comparable to what some other languages "
"(for instance, Fortran and Pascal) call a ``procedure'' instead of a "
"``function.''"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4187
msgid ""
"Beyond the primitive types, C provides several ways to construct new data "
"types.  For instance, you can define @dfn{pointers}, values that represent "
"the addresses of other data (@pxref{Pointers}).  You can define "
"@dfn{structures}, as in many other languages (@pxref{Structures}), and "
"@dfn{unions}, which define multiple ways to interpret the contents of the "
"same memory space (@pxref{Unions}).  @dfn{Enumerations} are collections of "
"named integer codes (@pxref{Enumeration Types})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4190
msgid ""
"@dfn{Array types} in C are used for allocating space for objects, but C does "
"not permit operating on an array value as a whole.  @xref{Arrays}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4193
#, no-wrap
msgid "type designator"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4200
msgid ""
"Some C constructs require a way to designate a specific data type "
"independent of any particular variable or expression which has that type.  "
"The way to do this is with a @dfn{type designator}.  The constructs that "
"need one include casts (@pxref{Explicit Type Conversion}) and @code{sizeof} "
"(@pxref{Type Size})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4204
msgid ""
"We also use type designators to talk about the type of a value in C, so you "
"will see many type designators in this manual.  When we say, ``The value has "
"type @code{int},'' @code{int} is a type designator."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4208
msgid ""
"To make the designator for any type, imagine a variable declaration for a "
"variable of that type and delete the variable name and the final semicolon."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4213
msgid ""
"For example, to designate the type of full-word integers, we start with the "
"declaration for a variable @code{foo} with that type, which is this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4216 c-intro-and-ref.texi:9260
#, no-wrap
msgid "int foo;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4222
msgid ""
"Then we delete the variable name @code{foo} and the semicolon, leaving "
"@code{int}---exactly the keyword used in such a declaration.  Therefore, the "
"type designator for this type is @code{int}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4224
msgid "What about long unsigned integers? From the declaration"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4227
#, no-wrap
msgid "unsigned long int foo;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4231
msgid "we determine that the designator is @code{unsigned long int}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4236
msgid ""
"Following this procedure, the designator for any primitive type is simply "
"the set of keywords which specifies that type in a declaration.  The same is "
"true for structure types, union types, and enumeration types."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4244
msgid ""
"Designators for pointer types do follow the rule of deleting the variable "
"name and semicolon, but the result is not so simple.  @xref{Pointer Type "
"Designators}, as part of the chapter about pointers.  @xref{Array Type "
"Designators}, for designators for array types."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4249
msgid ""
"To understand what type a designator stands for, imagine a variable name "
"inserted into the right place in the designator to make a valid "
"declaration.  What type would that variable be declared as? That is the type "
"the designator designates."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4252
#, no-wrap
msgid "constants"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4258
msgid ""
"A @dfn{constant} is an expression that stands for a specific value by "
"explicitly representing the desired value.  C allows constants for numbers, "
"characters, and strings.  We have already seen numeric and string constants "
"in the examples."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4277
#, no-wrap
msgid "integer constants"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4278
#, no-wrap
msgid "constants, integer"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4282
msgid ""
"An integer constant consists of a number to specify the value, followed "
"optionally by suffix letters to specify the data type."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4287
msgid ""
"The simplest integer constants are numbers written in base 10 (decimal), "
"such as @code{5}, @code{77}, and @code{403}.  A decimal constant cannot "
"start with the character @samp{0} (zero) because that makes the constant "
"octal."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4292
msgid ""
"You can get the effect of a negative integer constant by putting a minus "
"sign at the beginning.  In grammatical terms, that is an arithmetic "
"expression rather than a constant, but it behaves just like a true constant."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4297
msgid ""
"Integer constants can also be written in octal (base 8), hexadecimal (base "
"16), or binary (base 2).  An octal constant starts with the character "
"@samp{0} (zero), followed by any number of octal digits (@samp{0} to "
"@samp{7}):"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4302
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"0      // @r{zero}\n"
"077    // @r{63}\n"
"0403   // @r{259}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4307
msgid ""
"Pedantically speaking, the constant @code{0} is an octal constant, but we "
"can think of it as decimal; it has the same value either way."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4311
msgid ""
"A hexadecimal constant starts with @samp{0x} (upper or lower case)  followed "
"by hex digits (@samp{0} to @samp{9}, as well as @samp{a} through @samp{f} in "
"upper or lower case):"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4316
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"0xff   // @r{255}\n"
"0XA0   // @r{160}\n"
"0xffFF // @r{65535}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4318
#, no-wrap
msgid "binary integer constants"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4321
msgid ""
"A binary constant starts with @samp{0b} (upper or lower case) followed by "
"bits (each represented by the characters @samp{0} or @samp{1}):"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4324
#, no-wrap
msgid "0b101  // @r{5}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4328
msgid "Binary constants are a GNU C extension, not part of the C standard."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4331
msgid ""
"Sometimes a space is needed after an integer constant to avoid lexical "
"confusion with the following tokens.  @xref{Invalid Numbers}."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4333
#, no-wrap
msgid "Integer Constant Data Types"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4334
#, no-wrap
msgid "integer constant data types"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4335
#, no-wrap
msgid "constant data types, integer"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4336
#, no-wrap
msgid "types of integer constants"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4342
msgid ""
"The type of an integer constant is normally @code{int}, if the value fits in "
"that type, but here are the complete rules.  The type of an integer constant "
"is the first one in this sequence that can properly represent the value,"
msgstr ""

#. type: code{#1}
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4348
msgid "unsigned int"
msgstr ""

#. type: code{#1}
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4352
msgid "unsigned long int"
msgstr ""

#. type: code{#1}
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4356
msgid "unsigned long long int"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4360
msgid "and that isn't excluded by the following rules."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4363
msgid ""
"If the constant has @samp{l} or @samp{L} as a suffix, that excludes the "
"first two types (those that are not @code{long})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4366
msgid ""
"If the constant has @samp{ll} or @samp{LL} as a suffix, that excludes first "
"four types (those that are not @code{long long})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4369
msgid ""
"If the constant has @samp{u} or @samp{U} as a suffix, that excludes the "
"signed types."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4374
msgid ""
"Otherwise, if the constant is decimal (not binary, octal, or hexadecimal), "
"that excludes the unsigned types."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4376
msgid "Here are some examples of the suffixes."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4386
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"3000000000u      // @r{three billion as @code{unsigned int}.}\n"
"0LL              // @r{zero as a @code{long long int}.}\n"
"0403l            // @r{259 as a @code{long int}.}\n"
"2147483648       // @r{This is of type @code{long long int}.}\n"
"                 // @r{on typical 32-bit machines,}\n"
"                 // @r{since it won't fit in 32 bits as a signed number.}\n"
"2147483648U      // @r{This is of type @code{unsigned int},}\n"
"                 // @r{since it fits in 32 unsigned bits.}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4391
msgid ""
"Suffixes in integer constants are rarely used.  When the precise type is "
"important, it is cleaner to convert explicitly (@pxref{Explicit Type "
"Conversion})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4393
msgid "@xref{Integer Types}."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4395
#, no-wrap
msgid "Floating-Point Constants"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4396
#, no-wrap
msgid "floating-point constants"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4397
#, no-wrap
msgid "constants, floating-point"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4404
msgid ""
"A floating-point decimal constant must have either a decimal point, an "
"exponent-of-ten, or both; they distinguish it from an integer constant.  "
"Just adding the floating-point suffix, @samp{f}, to an integer does not make "
"a valid floating-point constant, and adding @samp{l} would instead make it a "
"long integer."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4409
msgid ""
"To indicate an exponent, write @samp{e} or @samp{E}.  The exponent value "
"follows.  It is always written as a decimal number; it can optionally start "
"with a sign.  The exponent @var{n} means to multiply the constant's value by "
"ten to the @var{n}th power."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4416
msgid ""
"Thus, @samp{1500.0}, @samp{15e2}, @samp{15e+2}, @samp{15.0e2}, "
"@samp{1.5e+3}, @samp{.15e4}, and @samp{15000e-1} are six ways of writing a "
"floating-point number whose value is 1500.  They are all equivalent in "
"principle."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4418
msgid "Here are more examples with decimal points:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4425
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"1.0\n"
"1000.\n"
"3.14159\n"
".05\n"
".0005\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4429
msgid ""
"For each of them, here are some equivalent constants written with exponents:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4436
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"1e0, 1.0000e0\n"
"100e1, 100e+1, 100E+1, 1e3, 10000e-1\n"
"3.14159e0\n"
"5e-2, .0005e+2, 5E-2, .0005E2\n"
".05e-2\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4441
msgid ""
"A floating-point constant normally has type @code{double}.  You can force it "
"to type @code{float} by adding @samp{f} or @samp{F} at the end.  For example,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4449
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"3.14159f\n"
"3.14159e0f\n"
"1000.f\n"
"100E1F\n"
".0005f\n"
".05e-2f\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4453
msgid ""
"Likewise, @samp{l} or @samp{L} at the end forces the constant to type "
"@code{long double}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4454
#, no-wrap
msgid "hexadecimal floating constants"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4459
msgid ""
"There are also @dfn{hexadecimal floating constants}.  These @emph{must} have "
"an exponent, but since @samp{e} would be interpreted as a hexadecimal digit, "
"the character @samp{p} or @samp{P} (for ``power'') indicates the exponent."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4463
msgid ""
"The exponent in a hexadecimal floating constant is an optionally signed "
"decimal integer that specifies a power of 2 (@emph{not} 10 or 16) to "
"multiply into the number."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4465
msgid "Here are some examples:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4478
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"0xAp2        // @r{40 in decimal}\n"
"0xAp-1       // @r{5 in decimal}\n"
"0x2.0Bp4     // @r{32.6875 decimal}\n"
"0xE.2p3      // @r{113 decimal}\n"
"0x123.ABCp0  // @r{291.6708984375 in decimal}\n"
"0x123.ABCp4  // @r{4666.734375 in decimal}\n"
"0x100p-8     // @r{1}\n"
"0x10p-4      // @r{1}\n"
"0x1p+4       // @r{16}\n"
"0x1p+8       // @r{256}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4482
msgid "@xref{Floating-Point Data Types}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4485
#, no-wrap
msgid "imaginary constants"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4486
#, no-wrap
msgid "complex constants"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4487
#, no-wrap
msgid "constants, imaginary"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4494
msgid ""
"A complex number consists of a real part plus an imaginary part.  (You may "
"omit one part if it is zero.)  This section explains how to write numeric "
"constants with imaginary values.  By adding these to ordinary real-valued "
"numeric constants, we can make constants with complex values."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4500
msgid ""
"The simple way to write an imaginary-number constant is to attach the suffix "
"@samp{i} or @samp{I}, or @samp{j} or @samp{J}, to an integer or "
"floating-point constant.  For example, @code{2.5fi} has type @code{_Complex "
"float} and @code{3i} has type @code{_Complex int}.  The four alternative "
"suffix letters are all equivalent."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4501
#, no-wrap
msgid "_Complex_I"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4506
msgid ""
"The other way to write an imaginary constant is to multiply a real constant "
"by @code{_Complex_I}, which represents the imaginary number i.  Standard C "
"doesn't support suffixes for imaginary constants, so this clunky method is "
"needed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4509
msgid ""
"To write a complex constant with a nonzero real part and a nonzero imaginary "
"part, write the two separately and add them, like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4512
#, no-wrap
msgid "4.0 + 3.0i\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4516
msgid "That gives the value 4 + 3i, with type @code{_Complex double}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4519
msgid ""
"Such a sum can include multiple real constants, or none.  Likewise, it can "
"include multiple imaginary constants, or none.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4522
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"_Complex double foo, bar, quux;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4527
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"foo = 2.0i + 4.0 + 3.0i; /* @r{Imaginary part is 5.0.} */\n"
"bar = 4.0 + 12.0; /* @r{Imaginary part is 0.0.} */\n"
"quux = 3.0i + 15.0i; /* @r{Real part is 0.0.} */\n"
"buux = 3.0i + 15.0j; /* @r{Equal to @code{quux}.} */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4530
msgid "@xref{Complex Data Types}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4538
msgid ""
"Some number-like constructs which are not really valid as numeric constants "
"are treated as numbers in preprocessing directives.  If these constructs "
"appear outside of preprocessing, they are erroneous.  @xref{Preprocessing "
"Tokens}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4544
msgid ""
"Sometimes we need to insert spaces to separate tokens so that they won't be "
"combined into a single number-like construct.  For example, @code{0xE+12} is "
"a preprocessing number that is not a valid numeric constant, so it is a "
"syntax error.  If what we want is the three tokens @code{@w{0xE + 12}}, we "
"have to insert spaces as separators."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4547
#, no-wrap
msgid "character constants"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4548
#, no-wrap
msgid "constants, character"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4549
#, no-wrap
msgid "escape sequence"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4557
msgid ""
"A @dfn{character constant} is written with single quotes, as in "
"@code{'@var{c}'}.  In the simplest case, @var{c} is a single ASCII character "
"that the constant should represent.  The constant has type @code{int}, and "
"its value is the character code of that character.  For instance, @code{'a'} "
"represents the character code for the letter @samp{a}: 97, that is."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4563
msgid ""
"To put the @samp{'} character (single quote) in the character constant, "
"@dfn{escape} it with a backslash (@samp{\\}).  This character constant looks "
"like @code{'\\''}.  The backslash character here functions as an @dfn{escape "
"character}, and such a sequence, starting with @samp{\\}, is called an "
"@dfn{escape sequence}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4567
msgid ""
"To put the @samp{\\} character (backslash) in the character constant, escape "
"it with @samp{\\} (another backslash).  This character constant looks like "
"@code{'\\\\'}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4568
#, no-wrap
msgid "bell character"
msgstr ""

#. type: samp{#1}
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4569
#, no-wrap
msgid "\\a"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4570
#, no-wrap
msgid "backspace"
msgstr ""

#. type: samp{#1}
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4571
#, no-wrap
msgid "\\b"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4572
#, no-wrap
msgid "tab (ASCII character)"
msgstr ""

#. type: samp{#1}
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4573
#, no-wrap
msgid "\\t"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4574
#, no-wrap
msgid "vertical tab"
msgstr ""

#. type: samp{#1}
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4575
#, no-wrap
msgid "\\v"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4576
#, no-wrap
msgid "formfeed"
msgstr ""

#. type: samp{#1}
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4577
#, no-wrap
msgid "\\f"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4578
#, no-wrap
msgid "newline"
msgstr ""

#. type: samp{#1}
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4579
#, no-wrap
msgid "\\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4580
#, no-wrap
msgid "return (ASCII character)"
msgstr ""

#. type: samp{#1}
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4581
#, no-wrap
msgid "\\r"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4582
#, no-wrap
msgid "escape (ASCII character)"
msgstr ""

#. type: samp{#1}
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4583
#, no-wrap
msgid "\\e"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4589
msgid ""
"Here are all the escape sequences that represent specific characters in a "
"character constant.  The numeric values shown are the corresponding ASCII "
"character codes, as decimal numbers.  The comments give the characters' "
"conventional or traditional names, as well as the appearance for graphical "
"characters."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4603
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"'\\a' @result{} 7       /* @r{alarm, bell, @kbd{CTRL-g}} */\n"
"'\\b' @result{} 8       /* @r{backspace, @key{BS}, @kbd{CTRL-h}} */\n"
"'\\t' @result{} 9       /* @r{tab, @key{TAB}, @kbd{CTRL-i}} */\n"
"'\\n"
"' @result{} 10      /* @r{newline, @kbd{CTRL-j}} */\n"
"'\\v' @result{} 11      /* @r{vertical tab, @kbd{CTRL-k}} */\n"
"'\\f' @result{} 12      /* @r{formfeed, @kbd{CTRL-l}} */\n"
"'\\r' @result{} 13      /* @r{carriage return, @key{RET}, @kbd{CTRL-m}} */\n"
"'\\e' @result{} 27      /* @r{escape character, @key{ESC}, @kbd{CTRL-[}} */\n"
"'\\\\' @result{} 92      /* @r{backslash character, @kbd{\\}} */\n"
"'\\'' @result{} 39      /* @r{single quote character, @kbd{'}} */\n"
"'\\\"' @result{} 34      /* @r{double quote character, @kbd{\"}} */\n"
"'\\?' @result{} 63      /* @r{question mark, @kbd{?}} */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4609
msgid ""
"@samp{\\e} is a GNU C extension; to stick to standard C, write @samp{\\33}.  "
"(The number after @samp{\\} is octal.)  To specify a character constant "
"using decimal, use a cast; for instance, @code{(unsigned char) 27}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4615
msgid ""
"You can also write octal and hex character codes as @samp{\\@var{octalcode}} "
"or @samp{\\x@var{hexcode}}.  Decimal is not an option here, so octal codes "
"do not need to start with @samp{0}.  An octal code is limited to three octal "
"digits, and any non-octal character terminates it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4621
msgid ""
"The character constant's value has type @code{int}.  However, the character "
"code is treated initially as a @code{char} value, which is then converted to "
"@code{int}.  If the character code is greater than 127 (@code{0177} in "
"octal), the resulting @code{int} may be negative on a platform where the "
"type @code{char} is 8 bits long and signed."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4624
#, no-wrap
msgid "string constants"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4625
#, no-wrap
msgid "constants, string"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4632
msgid ""
"A @dfn{string constant} represents a series of characters.  It starts with "
"@samp{\"} and ends with @samp{\"}; in between are the contents of the "
"string.  Quoting special characters such as @samp{\"}, @samp{\\} and newline "
"in the contents works in string constants as in character constants.  In a "
"string constant, @samp{'} does not need to be quoted."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4639
msgid ""
"A string constant defines an array of characters which contains the "
"specified characters followed by the null character (code 0).  Using the "
"string constant is equivalent to using the name of an array with those "
"contents.  In simple cases, where there are no backslash escape sequences, "
"the length in bytes of the string constant is one greater than the number of "
"characters written in it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4648
msgid ""
"As with any array in C, using the string constant in an expression converts "
"the array to a pointer (@pxref{Pointers}) to the array's zeroth element "
"(@pxref{Accessing Array Elements}).  This pointer will have type @code{char "
"*} because it points to an element of type @code{char}.  @code{char *} is an "
"example of a type designator for a pointer type (@pxref{Pointer Type "
"Designators}).  That type is used for operating on strings generally, not "
"just the strings expressed as constants."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4651
msgid ""
"Thus, the string constant @code{\"Foo!\"} is almost equivalent to declaring "
"an array like this"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4654
#, no-wrap
msgid "char string_array_1[] = @{'F', 'o', 'o', '!', '\\0' @};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4659
msgid ""
"and then using @code{string_array_1} in the program (which converts it to "
"type @code{char *}).  There are two differences, however:"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4663
msgid "The string constant doesn't define a name for the array."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4666
msgid "The string constant is probably stored in a read-only area of memory."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4672
msgid ""
"Newlines are not allowed in the text of a string constant.  The motive for "
"this prohibition is to catch the error of omitting the closing @samp{\"}.  "
"To put a newline in a constant string, write it as @samp{\\n"
"} in the string constant."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4676
msgid ""
"A real null character in the source code inside a string constant causes a "
"warning.  To put a null character in the middle of a string constant, write "
"@samp{\\0} or @samp{\\000}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4678
msgid "Consecutive string constants are effectively concatenated.  Thus,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4681
#, no-wrap
msgid "\"Fo\" \"o!\"   @r{is equivalent to}   \"Foo!\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4685
msgid ""
"This is useful for writing a string containing multiple lines, like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4694
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\"This message is so long that it needs more than\\n"
"\"\n"
"\"a single line of text.  C does not allow a newline\\n"
"\"\n"
"\"to represent itself in a string constant, so we have to\\n"
"\"\n"
"\"write \\\\n"
" to put it in the string.  For readability of\\n"
"\"\n"
"\"the source code, it is advisable to put line breaks in\\n"
"\"\n"
"\"the source where they occur in the contents of the\\n"
"\"\n"
"\"constant.\\n"
"\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4699
msgid ""
"The sequence of a backslash and a newline is ignored anywhere in a C "
"program, and that includes inside a string constant.  Thus, you can write "
"multi-line string constants this way:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4703
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\"This is another way to put newlines in a string constant\\n"
"\\\n"
"and break the line after them in the source code.\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4707
msgid "However, concatenation is the recommended way to do this."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4709
msgid "You can also write perverse string constants like this,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4713
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\"Fo\\\n"
"o!\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4717
msgid "but don't do that---write it like this instead:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4720
#, no-wrap
msgid "\"Foo!\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4730
msgid ""
"Be careful to avoid passing a string constant to a function that modifies "
"the string it receives.  The memory where the string constant is stored may "
"be read-only, which would cause a fatal @code{SIGSEGV} signal that normally "
"terminates the function (@pxref{Signals}).  Even worse, the memory may not "
"be read-only.  Then the function might modify the string constant, thus "
"spoiling the contents of other string constants that are supposed to contain "
"the same value and are unified by the compiler."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4740
msgid ""
"Writing @samp{u8} immediately before a string constant, with no intervening "
"space, means to represent that string in UTF-8 encoding as a sequence of "
"bytes.  UTF-8 represents ASCII characters with a single byte, and represents "
"non-ASCII Unicode characters (codes 128 and up)  as multibyte sequences.  "
"Here is an example of a UTF-8 constant:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4743
#, no-wrap
msgid "u8\"A cónstàñt\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4747
msgid ""
"This constant occupies 13 bytes plus the terminating null, because each of "
"the accented letters is a two-byte sequence."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4751
msgid ""
"Concatenating an ordinary string with a UTF-8 string conceptually produces "
"another UTF-8 string.  However, if the ordinary string contains character "
"codes 128 and up, the results cannot be relied on."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4754
#, no-wrap
msgid "Unicode character codes"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4755
#, no-wrap
msgid "universal character names"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4756
#, no-wrap
msgid "code point"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4764
msgid ""
"You can specify Unicode characters using escape sequences called "
"@dfn{universal character names} that start with @samp{\\u} and @samp{\\U}.  "
"They are valid in C for individual character constants, inside string "
"constants (@pxref{String Constants}), and even in identifiers.  These escape "
"sequences include a hexadecimal Unicode character code, also called a "
"@dfn{code point} in Unicode terminology."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4769
msgid ""
"Use the @samp{\\u} escape sequence with a 16-bit hexadecimal Unicode "
"character code.  If the character's numeric code is too big for 16 bits, use "
"the @samp{\\U} escape sequence with a 32-bit hexadecimal Unicode character "
"code.  Here are some examples."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4773
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\\u6C34      /* @r{16-bit code (Chinese for ``water''), UTF-16} */\n"
"\\U0010ABCD  /* @r{32-bit code, UTF-32} */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4779
msgid ""
"One way to use these is in UTF-8 string constants (@pxref{UTF-8 String "
"Constants}).  For instance, here we use two of them, each preceded by a "
"space."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4782
#, no-wrap
msgid "u8\"fóó \\u6C34 \\U0010ABCD\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4786
msgid ""
"You can also use them in wide character constants (@pxref{Wide Character "
"Constants}), like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4790
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"u'\\u6C34'      /* @r{16-bit code (water)} */\n"
"U'\\U0010ABCD'  /* @r{32-bit code} */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4795
msgid ""
"and in wide string constants (@pxref{Wide String Constants}), like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4799
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"u\"\\u6C34\\u706B\"  /* @r{16-bit codes (water, fire)} */\n"
"U\"\\U0010ABCD\"    /* @r{32-bit code} */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4803
msgid "And in an identifier:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4806
#, no-wrap
msgid "int foo\\u6C34bar = 0;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4812
msgid ""
"Codes in the range of D800 through DFFF are invalid in universal character "
"names.  Trying to write them using @samp{\\u} causes an error.  Unicode "
"calls them ``surrogate code points'' and uses them in UTF-16 for purposes "
"too specialized to explain here."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4818
msgid ""
"Codes less than 00A0 are likewise invalid in universal character names, and "
"likewise cause errors, except for 0024 (@samp{$}), 0040 (@samp{@@}), and "
"0060 (@samp{`}).  Characters which can't be represented with universal "
"character names can be specified with octal or hexadecimal escape sequences "
"(@pxref{Character Constants})."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4821
#, no-wrap
msgid "wide character constants"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4822
#, no-wrap
msgid "constants, wide character"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4828
msgid ""
"A @dfn{wide character constant} represents characters with more than 8 bits "
"of character code.  This is an obscure feature that we need to document but "
"that you probably won't ever use.  If you're just learning C, you may as "
"well skip this section."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4836
msgid ""
"The original C wide character constant looks like @samp{L} (upper case!) "
"followed immediately by an ordinary character constant (with no intervening "
"space).  Its data type is @code{wchar_t}, which is an alias defined in "
"@file{stddef.h} for one of the standard integer types.  Depending on the "
"platform, it could be 16 bits or 32 bits.  If it is 16 bits, these character "
"constants use the UTF-16 form of Unicode; if 32 bits, UTF-32."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4846
msgid ""
"There are also Unicode wide character constants which explicitly specify the "
"width.  These constants start with @samp{u} or @samp{U} instead of "
"@samp{L}.  @samp{u} specifies a 16-bit Unicode wide character constant, and "
"@samp{U} a 32-bit Unicode wide character constant.  Their types are, "
"respectively, @code{char16_t} and @w{@code{char32_t}}; they are declared in "
"the header file @file{uchar.h}.  These character constants are valid even if "
"@file{uchar.h} is not included, but some uses of them may be inconvenient "
"without including it to declare those type names."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4850
msgid ""
"The character represented in a wide character constant can be an ordinary "
"ASCII character.  @code{L'a'}, @code{u'a'} and @code{U'a'} are all valid, "
"and they are all equal to @code{'a'}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4856
msgid ""
"In all three kinds of wide character constants, you can write a non-ASCII "
"Unicode character in the constant itself; the constant's value is the "
"character's Unicode character code.  Or you can specify the Unicode "
"character with an escape sequence (@pxref{Unicode Character Codes})."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4859
#, no-wrap
msgid "wide string constants"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4860
#, no-wrap
msgid "constants, wide string"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4865
msgid ""
"A @dfn{wide string constant} stands for an array of 16-bit or 32-bit "
"characters.  They are rarely used; if you're just learning C, you may as "
"well skip this section."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4874
msgid ""
"There are three kinds of wide string constants, which differ in the data "
"type used for each character in the string.  Each wide string constant is "
"equivalent to an array of integers, but the data type of those integers "
"depends on the kind of wide string.  Using the constant in an expression "
"will convert the array to a pointer to its zeroth element, as usual for "
"arrays in C (@pxref{Accessing Array Elements}).  For each kind of wide "
"string constant, we state here what type that pointer will be."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4876
#, no-wrap
msgid "char16_t"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4883
msgid ""
"This is a 16-bit Unicode wide string constant: each element is a 16-bit "
"Unicode character code with type @code{char16_t}, so the string has the "
"array type @code{char16_t[]}.  (That is a type designator; @pxref{Pointer "
"Type Designators}.)  The constant is written as @samp{u} (which must be "
"lower case) followed (with no intervening space) by a string constant with "
"the usual syntax."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4884
#, no-wrap
msgid "char32_t"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4889
msgid ""
"This is a 32-bit Unicode wide string constant: each element is a 32-bit "
"Unicode character code, and the string has type @code{char32_t[]}.  It's "
"written as @samp{U} (which must be upper case) followed (with no intervening "
"space) by a string constant with the usual syntax."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4890
#, no-wrap
msgid "wchar_t"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4895
msgid ""
"This is the original kind of wide string constant.  It's written as @samp{L} "
"(which must be upper case) followed (with no intervening space) by a string "
"constant with the usual syntax, and the string has type @code{wchar_t[]}."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4899
msgid ""
"The width of the data type @code{wchar_t} depends on the target platform, "
"which makes this kind of wide string somewhat less useful than the newer "
"kinds."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4903
msgid ""
"@code{char16_t} and @code{char32_t} are declared in the header file "
"@file{uchar.h}.  @code{wchar_t} is declared in @file{stddef.h}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4910
msgid ""
"Consecutive wide string constants of the same kind concatenate, just like "
"ordinary string constants.  A wide string constant concatenated with an "
"ordinary string constant results in a wide string constant.  You can't "
"concatenate two wide string constants of different kinds.  In addition, you "
"can't concatenate a wide string constant (of any kind) with a UTF-8 string "
"constant."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4913
#, no-wrap
msgid "type size"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4914
#, no-wrap
msgid "size of type"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4915
#, no-wrap
msgid "sizeof"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4920
msgid ""
"Each data type has a @dfn{size}, which is the number of bytes "
"(@pxref{Storage}) that it occupies in memory.  To refer to the size in a C "
"program, use @code{sizeof}.  There are two ways to use it:"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4922
#, no-wrap
msgid "sizeof @var{expression}"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4929
msgid ""
"This gives the size of @var{expression}, based on its data type.  It does "
"not calculate the value of @var{expression}, only its size, so if "
"@var{expression} includes side effects or function calls, they do not "
"happen.  Therefore, @code{sizeof} with an expression as argument is always a "
"compile-time operation that has zero run-time cost, unless it applies to a "
"variable-size array."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4932
msgid ""
"A value that is a bit field (@pxref{Bit Fields}) is not allowed as an "
"operand of @code{sizeof}."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4937
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"double a;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4939
#, no-wrap
msgid "i = sizeof a + 10;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4943
msgid ""
"sets @code{i} to 18 on most computers because @code{a} occupies 8 bytes."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4946
msgid "Here's how to determine the number of elements in an array @code{arr}:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4949
#, no-wrap
msgid "(sizeof arr / sizeof arr[0])\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4957
msgid ""
"The expression @code{sizeof arr} gives the size of the array, not the size "
"of a pointer to an element.  However, if @var{expression} is a function "
"parameter that was declared as an array, that variable really has a pointer "
"type (@pxref{Array Params are Ptrs}), so the result is the size of that "
"pointer."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4958
#, no-wrap
msgid "sizeof (@var{type})"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4961
msgid "This gives the size of @var{type}.  For example,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4964
#, no-wrap
msgid "i = sizeof (double) + 10;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4968
msgid "is equivalent to the previous example."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4972
msgid ""
"@strong{Warning:} If @var{type} contains expressions which have side "
"effects, those expressions are actually computed and any side effects in "
"them do occur."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4977
msgid ""
"You can't apply @code{sizeof} to an incomplete type (@pxref{Incomplete "
"Types}).  Using it on a function type or @code{void} gives 1 in GNU C, which "
"makes adding an integer to these pointer types work as desired "
"(@pxref{Pointer Arithmetic})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4981
msgid ""
"@strong{Warning}: When you use @code{sizeof} with a type instead of an "
"expression, you must write parentheses around the type."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4986
msgid ""
"@strong{Warning}: When applying @code{sizeof} to the result of a cast "
"(@pxref{Explicit Type Conversion}), you must write parentheses around the "
"cast expression to avoid an ambiguity in the grammar of C@.  Specifically,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4989
#, no-wrap
msgid "sizeof (int) -x\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4993
msgid "parses as"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:4996
#, no-wrap
msgid "(sizeof (int)) - x\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5000
msgid "If what you want is"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5003
#, no-wrap
msgid "sizeof ((int) -x)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5007
msgid "you must write it that way, with parentheses."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5012
msgid ""
"The data type of the value of the @code{sizeof} operator is always an "
"unsigned integer type; which one of those types depends on the machine.  The "
"header file @code{stddef.h} defines @code{size_t} as a name for such a "
"type.  @xref{Defining Typedef Names}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5015
#, no-wrap
msgid "pointers"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5024
msgid ""
"Among high-level languages, C is rather low-level, close to the machine.  "
"This is mainly because it has explicit @dfn{pointers}.  A pointer value is "
"the numeric address of data in memory.  The type of data to be found at that "
"address is specified by the data type of the pointer itself.  Nothing in C "
"can determine the ``correct'' data type of data in memory; it can only "
"blindly follow the data type of the pointer you use to access the data."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5028
msgid ""
"The unary operator @samp{*} gets the data that a pointer points to---this is "
"called @dfn{dereferencing the pointer}.  Its value always has the type that "
"the pointer points to."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5032
msgid ""
"C also allows pointers to functions, but since there are some differences in "
"how they work, we treat them later.  @xref{Function Pointers}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5055
#, no-wrap
msgid "address-of operator"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5058
msgid ""
"The most basic way to make a pointer is with the ``address-of'' operator, "
"@samp{&}.  Let's suppose we have these variables available:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5062 c-intro-and-ref.texi:5087
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int i;\n"
"double a[5];\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5069
msgid ""
"Now, @code{&i} gives the address of the variable @code{i}---a pointer value "
"that points to @code{i}'s location---and @code{&a[3]} gives the address of "
"the element 3 of @code{a}.  (By the usual 1-origin numbering convention of "
"ordinary English, it is actually the fourth element in the array, since the "
"element at the start has index 0.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5076
msgid ""
"The address-of operator is unusual because it operates on a place to store a "
"value (an lvalue, @pxref{Lvalues}), not on the value currently stored "
"there.  (The left argument of a simple assignment is unusual in the same "
"way.)  You can use it on any lvalue except a bit field (@pxref{Bit Fields}) "
"or a constructor (@pxref{Structure Constructors})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5083
msgid ""
"For each data type @var{t}, there is a type for pointers to type @var{t}.  "
"For these variables,"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5093
msgid ""
"@code{i} has type @code{int}; we say @code{&i} is a ``pointer to "
"@code{int}.''"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5097
msgid ""
"@code{a} has type @code{double[5]}; we say @code{&a} is a ``pointer to an "
"array of five @code{double}s.''"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5101
msgid ""
"@code{a[3]} has type @code{double}; we say @code{&a[3]} is a ``pointer to "
"@code{double}.''"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5104
#, no-wrap
msgid "Pointer-Variable Declarations"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5107
msgid "The way to declare that a variable @code{foo} points to type @var{t} is"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5110
#, no-wrap
msgid "@var{t} *foo;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5115
msgid ""
"To remember this syntax, think ``if you dereference @code{foo}, using the "
"@samp{*} operator, what you get is type @var{t}.  Thus, @code{foo} points to "
"type @var{t}.''"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5118
msgid ""
"Thus, we can declare variables that hold pointers to these three types, like "
"this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5123
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int *ptri;            /* @r{Pointer to @code{int}.} */\n"
"double *ptrd;         /* @r{Pointer to @code{double}.} */\n"
"double (*ptrda)[5];   /* @r{Pointer to @code{double[5]}.} */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5130
msgid ""
"@samp{int *ptri;} means, ``if you dereference @code{ptri}, you get an "
"@code{int}.'' @samp{double (*ptrda)[5];} means, ``if you dereference "
"@code{ptrda}, then subscript it by an integer less than 5, you get a "
"@code{double}.'' The parentheses express the point that you would "
"dereference it first, then subscript it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5132
msgid "Contrast the last one with this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5135
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"double *aptrd[5];     /* @r{Array of five pointers to @code{double}.} */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5143
msgid ""
"Because @samp{*} has lower syntactic precedence than subscripting, "
"@samp{double *aptrd[5]} means, ``if you subscript @code{aptrd} by an integer "
"less than 5, then dereference it, you get a @code{double}.'' Therefore, "
"@code{*aptrd[5]} declares an array of pointers, not a pointer to an array."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5145
#, no-wrap
msgid "Pointer-Type Designators"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5151
msgid ""
"Every type in C has a designator; you make it by deleting the variable name "
"and the semicolon from a declaration (@pxref{Type Designators}).  Here are "
"the designators for the pointer types of the example declarations in the "
"previous section:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5156
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int *           /* @r{Pointer to @code{int}.} */\n"
"double *        /* @r{Pointer to @code{double}.} */\n"
"double (*)[5]   /* @r{Pointer to @code{double[5]}.} */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5165
msgid ""
"Remember, to understand what type a designator stands for, imagine the "
"corresponding variable declaration with a variable name in it, and figure "
"out what type that variable would have.  Thus, the type designator "
"@code{double (*)[5]} corresponds to the variable declaration @code{double "
"(*@var{variable})[5]}.  That declares a pointer variable which, when "
"dereferenced, gives an array of 5 @code{double}s.  So the type designator "
"means, ``pointer to an array of 5 @code{double}s.''"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5167
#, no-wrap
msgid "Dereferencing Pointers"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5168
#, no-wrap
msgid "dereferencing pointers"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5169
#, no-wrap
msgid "pointer dereferencing"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5176
msgid ""
"The main use of a pointer value is to @dfn{dereference it} (access the data "
"it points at) with the unary @samp{*} operator.  For instance, @code{*&i} is "
"the value at @code{i}'s address---which is just @code{i}.  The two "
"expressions are equivalent, provided @code{&i} is valid."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5179
msgid ""
"A pointer-dereference expression whose type is data (not a function)  is an "
"lvalue."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5182
msgid ""
"Pointers become really useful when we store them somewhere and use them "
"later.  Here's a simple example to illustrate the practice:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5187
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@{\n"
"  int i;\n"
"  int *ptr;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5189
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  ptr = &i;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5191
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  i = 5;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5193
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  @r{@dots{}}\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5196
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  return *ptr;   /* @r{Returns 5, fetched from @code{i}.}  */\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5202
msgid ""
"This shows how to declare the variable @code{ptr} as type @code{int *} "
"(pointer to @code{int}), store a pointer value into it (pointing at "
"@code{i}), and use it later to get the value of the object it points at (the "
"value in @code{i})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5204
msgid "Here is another example of using a pointer to a variable."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5208
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"/* @r{Define global variable @code{i}.}  */\n"
"int i = 2;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5214
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int\n"
"foo (void)\n"
"@{\n"
"  /* @r{Save global variable @code{i}'s address.}  */\n"
"  int *global_i = &i;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5217
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  /* @r{Declare local @code{i}, shadowing the global @code{i}.}  */\n"
"  int i = 5;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5222
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  /* @r{Print value of global @code{i} and value of local @code{i}.}  */\n"
"  printf (\"global i: %d\\n"
"local i: %d\\n"
"\", *global_i, i);\n"
"  return i;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5229
msgid ""
"Of course, in a real program it would be much cleaner to use different names "
"for these two variables, rather than calling both of them @code{i}.  But it "
"is hard to illustrate this syntaxtical point with clean code.  If anyone can "
"provide a useful example to illustrate this point with, that would be "
"welcome."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5232
#, no-wrap
msgid "null pointers"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5233
#, no-wrap
msgid "pointers, null"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5243
msgid ""
"A pointer value can be @dfn{null}, which means it does not point to any "
"object.  The cleanest way to get a null pointer is by writing @code{NULL}, a "
"standard macro defined in @file{stddef.h}.  You can also do it by casting 0 "
"to the desired pointer type, as in @code{(char *) 0}.  (The cast operator "
"performs explicit type conversion; @xref{Explicit Type Conversion}.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5246
msgid ""
"You can store a null pointer in any lvalue whose data type is a pointer type:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5250
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"char *foo;\n"
"foo = NULL;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5254
msgid ""
"These two, if consecutive, can be combined into a declaration with "
"initializer,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5257
#, no-wrap
msgid "char *foo = NULL;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5261
msgid ""
"You can also explicitly cast @code{NULL} to the specific pointer type you "
"want---it makes no difference."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5265
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"char *foo;\n"
"foo = (char *) NULL;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5269
msgid ""
"To test whether a pointer is null, compare it with zero or @code{NULL}, as "
"shown here:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5274
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"if (p != NULL)\n"
"  /* @r{@code{p} is not null.}  */\n"
"  operate (p);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5279
msgid ""
"Since testing a pointer for not being null is basic and frequent, all but "
"beginners in C will understand the conditional without need for @code{!= "
"NULL}:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5284
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"if (p)\n"
"  /* @r{@code{p} is not null.}  */\n"
"  operate (p);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5287
#, no-wrap
msgid "Dereferencing Null or Invalid Pointers"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5292
msgid ""
"Trying to dereference a null pointer is an error.  On most platforms, it "
"generally causes a signal, usually @code{SIGSEGV} (@pxref{Signals})."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5296
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"char *foo = NULL;\n"
"c = *foo;    /* @r{This causes a signal and terminates.}  */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5302
msgid ""
"Likewise a pointer that has the wrong alignment for the target data type (on "
"most types of computer), or points to a part of memory that has not been "
"allocated in the process's address space."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5306
msgid ""
"The signal terminates the program, unless the program has arranged to handle "
"the signal (@pxref{Signal Handling, The GNU C Library, , libc, The GNU C "
"Library Reference Manual})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5312
msgid ""
"However, the signal might not happen if the dereference is optimized away.  "
"In the example above, if you don't subsequently use the value of @code{c}, "
"GCC might optimize away the code for @code{*foo}.  You can prevent such "
"optimization using the @code{volatile} qualifier, as shown here:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5317
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"volatile char *p;\n"
"volatile char c;\n"
"c = *p;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5322
msgid ""
"You can use this to test whether @code{p} points to unallocated memory.  Set "
"up a signal handler first, so the signal won't terminate the program."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5325
#, no-wrap
msgid "void pointers"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5326
#, no-wrap
msgid "pointers, void"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5331
msgid ""
"The peculiar type @code{void *}, a pointer whose target type is @code{void}, "
"is used often in C@.  It represents a pointer to we-don't-say-what.  Thus,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5334
#, no-wrap
msgid "void *numbered_slot_pointer (int);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5340
msgid ""
"declares a function @code{numbered_slot_pointer} that takes an integer "
"parameter and returns a pointer, but we don't say what type of data it "
"points to."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5344
msgid ""
"The functions for dynamic memory allocation (@pxref{Dynamic Memory "
"Allocation}) use type @code{void *} to refer to blocks of memory, regardless "
"of what sort of data the program stores in those blocks."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5349
msgid ""
"With type @code{void *}, you can pass the pointer around and test whether it "
"is null.  However, dereferencing it gives a @code{void} value that can't be "
"used (@pxref{The Void Type}).  To dereference the pointer, first convert it "
"to some other pointer type."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5353
msgid ""
"Assignments convert @code{void *} automatically to any other pointer type, "
"if the left operand has a pointer type; for instance,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5361
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@{\n"
"  int *p;\n"
"  /* @r{Converts return value to @code{int *}.}  */\n"
"  p = numbered_slot_pointer (5);\n"
"  @r{@dots{}}\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5368
msgid ""
"Passing an argument of type @code{void *} for a parameter that has a pointer "
"type also converts.  For example, supposing the function @code{hack} is "
"declared to require type @code{float *} for its parameter, this call to "
"@code{hack} will convert the argument to that type."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5381
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"/* @r{Declare @code{hack} that way.}\n"
"   @r{We assume it is defined somewhere else.}  */\n"
"void hack (float *);\n"
"@dots{}\n"
"/* @r{Now call @code{hack}.}  */\n"
"@{\n"
"  /* @r{Converts return value of @code{numbered_slot_pointer}}\n"
"     @r{to @code{float *} to pass it to @code{hack}.}  */\n"
"  hack (numbered_slot_pointer (5));\n"
"  @r{@dots{}}\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5385
msgid ""
"You can also convert to another pointer type with an explicit cast "
"(@pxref{Explicit Type Conversion}), like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5387
#, no-wrap
msgid "(int *) numbered_slot_pointer (5)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5391
msgid ""
"Here is an example which decides at run time which pointer type to convert "
"to:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5401
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"extract_int_or_double (void *ptr, bool its_an_int)\n"
"@{\n"
"  if (its_an_int)\n"
"    handle_an_int (*(int *)ptr);\n"
"  else\n"
"    handle_a_double (*(double *)ptr);\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5405
msgid ""
"The expression @code{*(int *)ptr} means to convert @code{ptr} to type "
"@code{int *}, then dereference it."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5408
#, no-wrap
msgid "pointer comparison"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5409
#, no-wrap
msgid "comparison, pointer"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5414
msgid ""
"Two pointer values are equal if they point to the same location, or if they "
"are both null.  You can test for this with @code{==} and @code{!=}.  Here's "
"a trivial example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5419
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@{\n"
"  int i;\n"
"  int *p, *q;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5427
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  p = &i;\n"
"  q = &i;\n"
"  if (p == q)\n"
"    printf (\"This will be printed.\\n"
"\");\n"
"  if (p != q)\n"
"    printf (\"This won't be printed.\\n"
"\");\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5434
msgid ""
"Ordering comparisons such as @code{>} and @code{>=} operate on pointers by "
"converting them to unsigned integers.  The C standard says the two pointers "
"must point within the same object in memory, but on GNU/Linux systems these "
"operations simply compare the numeric values of the pointers."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5439
msgid ""
"The pointer values to be compared should in principle have the same type, "
"but they are allowed to differ in limited cases.  First of all, if the two "
"pointers' target types are nearly compatible (@pxref{Compatible Types}), the "
"comparison is allowed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5445
msgid ""
"If one of the operands is @code{void *} (@pxref{Void Pointers}) and the "
"other is another pointer type, the comparison operator converts the "
"@code{void *} pointer to the other type so as to compare them.  (In standard "
"C, this is not allowed if the other type is a function pointer type, but it "
"works in GNU C@.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5449
msgid ""
"Comparison operators also allow comparing the integer 0 with a pointer "
"value.  This works by converting 0 to a null pointer of the same type as the "
"other operand."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5452
#, no-wrap
msgid "pointer arithmetic"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5453
#, no-wrap
msgid "arithmetic, pointer"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5460
msgid ""
"Adding an integer (positive or negative) to a pointer is valid in C@.  It "
"assumes that the pointer points to an element in an array, and advances or "
"retracts the pointer across as many array elements as the integer "
"specifies.  Here is an example, in which adding a positive integer advances "
"the pointer to a later element in the same array."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5467
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"incrementing_pointers ()\n"
"@{\n"
"  int array[5] = @{ 45, 29, 104, -3, 123456 @};\n"
"  int elt0, elt1, elt4;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5471
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  int *p = &array[0];\n"
"  /* @r{Now @code{p} points at element 0.  Fetch it.}  */\n"
"  elt0 = *p;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5475
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  ++p;\n"
"  /* @r{Now @code{p} points at element 1.  Fetch it.}  */\n"
"  elt1 = *p;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5479
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  p += 3;\n"
"  /* @r{Now @code{p} points at element 4 (the last).  Fetch it.}  */\n"
"  elt4 = *p;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5484
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  printf (\"elt0 %d  elt1 %d  elt4 %d.\\n"
"\",\n"
"          elt0, elt1, elt4);\n"
"  /* @r{Prints elt0 45  elt1 29  elt4 123456.}  */\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5488
msgid ""
"Here's an example where adding a negative integer retracts the pointer to an "
"earlier element in the same array."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5495
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"decrementing_pointers ()\n"
"@{\n"
"  int array[5] = @{ 45, 29, 104, -3, 123456 @};\n"
"  int elt0, elt3, elt4;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5499
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  int *p = &array[4];\n"
"  /* @r{Now @code{p} points at element 4 (the last).  Fetch it.}  */\n"
"  elt4 = *p;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5503
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  --p;\n"
"  /* @r{Now @code{p} points at element 3.  Fetch it.}  */\n"
"  elt3 = *p;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5507
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  p -= 3;\n"
"  /* @r{Now @code{p} points at element 0.  Fetch it.}  */\n"
"  elt0 = *p;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5512
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  printf (\"elt0 %d  elt3 %d  elt4 %d.\\n"
"\",\n"
"          elt0, elt3, elt4);\n"
"  /* @r{Prints elt0 45  elt3 -3  elt4 123456.}  */\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5517
msgid ""
"If one pointer value was made by adding an integer to another pointer value, "
"it should be possible to subtract the pointer values and recover that "
"integer.  That works too in C@."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5524
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"subtract_pointers ()\n"
"@{\n"
"  int array[5] = @{ 45, 29, 104, -3, 123456 @};\n"
"  int *p0, *p3, *p4;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5528
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  int *p = &array[4];\n"
"  /* @r{Now @code{p} points at element 4 (the last).  Save the value.}  */\n"
"  p4 = p;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5532
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  --p;\n"
"  /* @r{Now @code{p} points at element 3.  Save the value.}  */\n"
"  p3 = p;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5536
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  p -= 3;\n"
"  /* @r{Now @code{p} points at element 0.  Save the value.}  */\n"
"  p0 = p;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5541
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  printf (\"%d, %d, %d, %d\\n"
"\",\n"
"          p4 - p0, p0 - p0, p3 - p0, p0 - p3);\n"
"  /* @r{Prints 4, 0, 3, -3.}  */\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5547
msgid ""
"The addition operation does not know where arrays begin or end in memory.  "
"All it does is add the integer (multiplied by target object size) to the "
"numeric value of the pointer.  When the initial pointer and the result point "
"into the same array, the result is well-defined."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5550
msgid ""
"@strong{Warning:} Only experts should do pointer arithmetic involving "
"pointers into different memory objects."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5555
msgid ""
"The difference between two pointers has type @code{int}, or @code{long} if "
"necessary (@pxref{Integer Types}).  The clean way to declare it is to use "
"the typedef name @code{ptrdiff_t} defined in the file @file{stddef.h}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5562
msgid ""
"C defines pointer subtraction to be consistent with pointer-integer "
"addition, so that @code{(p3 - p1) + p1} equals @code{p3}, as in ordinary "
"algebra.  Pointer subtraction works by subtracting @code{p1}'s numeric value "
"from @code{p3}'s, and dividing by target object size.  The two pointer "
"arguments should point into the same array."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5568
msgid ""
"In standard C, addition and subtraction are not allowed on @code{void *}, "
"since the target type's size is not defined in that case.  Likewise, they "
"are not allowed on pointers to function types.  However, these operations "
"work in GNU C, and the ``size of the target type'' is taken as 1 byte."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5571
#, no-wrap
msgid "pointers and arrays"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5572
#, no-wrap
msgid "arrays and pointers"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5581
msgid ""
"The clean way to refer to an array element is "
"@code{@var{array}[@var{index}]}.  Another, complicated way to do the same "
"job is to get the address of that element as a pointer, then dereference it: "
"@code{* (&@var{array}[0] + @var{index})} (or equivalently @code{* "
"(@var{array} + @var{index})}).  This first gets a pointer to element zero, "
"then increments it with @code{+} to point to the desired element, then gets "
"the value from there."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5587
msgid ""
"That pointer-arithmetic construct is the @emph{definition} of square "
"brackets in C@.  @code{@var{a}[@var{b}]} means, by definition, "
"@code{*(@var{a} + @var{b})}.  This definition uses @var{a} and @var{b} "
"symmetrically, so one must be a pointer and the other an integer; it does "
"not matter which comes first."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5593
msgid ""
"Since indexing with square brackets is defined in terms of addition and "
"dereferencing, that too is symmetrical.  Thus, you can write @code{3[array]} "
"and it is equivalent to @code{array[3]}.  However, it would be foolish to "
"write @code{3[array]}, since it has no advantage and could confuse people "
"who read the code."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5601
msgid ""
"It may seem like a discrepancy that the definition @code{*(@var{a} + "
"@var{b})} requires a pointer, while @code{array[3]} uses an array value "
"instead.  Why is this valid? The name of the array, when used by itself as "
"an expression (other than in @code{sizeof}), stands for a pointer to the "
"array's zeroth element.  Thus, @code{array + 3} converts @code{array} "
"implicitly to @code{&array[0]}, and the result is a pointer to element 3, "
"equivalent to @code{&array[3]}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5605
msgid ""
"Since square brackets are defined in terms of such an addition, "
"@code{array[3]} first converts @code{array} to a pointer.  That's why it "
"works to use an array directly in that construct."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5607
#, no-wrap
msgid "Pointer Arithmetic at Low-Level"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5608
#, no-wrap
msgid "pointer arithmetic, low-level"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5609
#, no-wrap
msgid "low level pointer arithmetic"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5616
msgid ""
"The behavior of pointer arithmetic is theoretically defined only when the "
"pointer values all point within one object allocated in memory.  But the "
"addition and subtraction operators can't tell whether the pointer values are "
"all within one object.  They don't know where objects start and end.  So "
"what do they really do?"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5624
msgid ""
"Adding pointer @var{p} to integer @var{i} treats @var{p} as a memory "
"address, which is in fact an integer---call it @var{pint}.  It treats "
"@var{i} as a number of elements of the type that @var{p} points to.  These "
"elements' sizes add up to @code{@var{i} * sizeof (*@var{p})}.  So the sum, "
"as an integer, is @code{@var{pint} + @var{i} * sizeof (*@var{p})}.  This "
"value is reinterpreted as a pointer of the same type as @var{p}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5631
msgid ""
"If the starting pointer value @var{p} and the result do not point at parts "
"of the same object, the operation is not officially legitimate, and C code "
"is not ``supposed'' to do it.  But you can do it anyway, and it gives "
"precisely the results described by the procedure above.  In some special "
"situations it can do something useful, but non-wizards should avoid it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5634
msgid ""
"Here's a function to offset a pointer value @emph{as if} it pointed to an "
"object of any given size, by explicitly performing that calculation:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5637
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"#include <stdint.h>\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5646
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void *\n"
"ptr_add (void *p, int i, int objsize)\n"
"@{\n"
"  intptr_t p_address = (long) p;\n"
"  intptr_t totalsize = i * objsize;\n"
"  intptr_t new_address = p_address + totalsize;\n"
"  return (void *) new_address;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: code{#1}
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5649
#, no-wrap
msgid "intptr_t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5654
msgid ""
"This does the same job as @code{@var{p} + @var{i}} with the proper pointer "
"type for @var{p}.  It uses the type @code{intptr_t}, which is defined in the "
"header file @file{stdint.h}.  (In practice, @code{long long} would always "
"work, but it is cleaner to use @code{intptr_t}.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5656
#, no-wrap
msgid "Pointer Increment and Decrement"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5657
#, no-wrap
msgid "pointer increment and decrement"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5658
#, no-wrap
msgid "incrementing pointers"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5659
#, no-wrap
msgid "decrementing pointers"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5666
msgid ""
"The @samp{++} operator adds 1 to a variable.  We have seen it for integers "
"(@pxref{Increment/Decrement}), but it works for pointers too.  For instance, "
"suppose we have a series of positive integers, terminated by a zero, and we "
"want to add them up.  Here is a simple way to step forward through the array "
"by advancing a pointer."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5672
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int\n"
"sum_array_till_0 (int *p)\n"
"@{\n"
"  int sum = 0;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5683
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  for (;;)\n"
"    @{\n"
"      /* @r{Fetch the next integer.}  */\n"
"      int next = *p++;\n"
"      /* @r{Exit the loop if it's 0.}  */\n"
"      if (next == 0)\n"
"        break;\n"
"      /* @r{Add it into running total.}  */\n"
"      sum += next;\n"
"    @}\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5686 c-intro-and-ref.texi:5735
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  return sum;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5692
msgid ""
"The statement @samp{break;} will be explained further on (@pxref{break "
"Statement}).  Used in this way, it immediately exits the surrounding "
"@code{for} statement."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5698
msgid ""
"@code{*p++} uses postincrement (@code{++}; "
"@pxref{Postincrement/Postdecrement}) on the pointer @code{p}.  That "
"expression parses as @code{*(p++)}, because a postfix operator always takes "
"precedence over a prefix operator.  Therefore, it dereferences the entering "
"value of @code{p}, then increments @code{p} afterwards."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5704
msgid ""
"Incrementing a variable means adding 1 to it, as in @code{p = p + 1}.  Since "
"@code{p} is a pointer, adding 1 to it advances it by the width of the datum "
"it points to---in this case, @code{sizeof (int)}.  Therefore, each iteration "
"of the loop picks up the next integer from the series and puts it into "
"@code{next}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5711
msgid ""
"This @code{for}-loop has no initialization expression since @code{p} and "
"@code{sum} are already initialized, has no end-test since the @samp{break;} "
"statement will exit it, and needs no expression to advance it since that's "
"done within the loop by incrementing @code{p} and @code{sum}.  Thus, those "
"three expressions after @code{for} are left empty."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5714
msgid ""
"Another way to write this function is by keeping the parameter value "
"unchanged and using indexing to access the integers in the table."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5721
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int\n"
"sum_array_till_0_indexing (int *p)\n"
"@{\n"
"  int i;\n"
"  int sum = 0;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5732
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  for (i = 0; ; i++)\n"
"    @{\n"
"      /* @r{Fetch the next integer.}  */\n"
"      int next = p[i];\n"
"      /* @r{Exit the loop if it's 0.}  */\n"
"      if (next == 0)\n"
"        break;\n"
"      /* @r{Add it into running total.}  */\n"
"      sum += next;\n"
"    @}\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5740
msgid ""
"In this program, instead of advancing @code{p}, we advance @code{i} and add "
"it to @code{p}.  (Recall that @code{p[i]} means @code{*(p + i)}.)  Either "
"way, it uses the same address to get the next integer."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5744
msgid ""
"It makes no difference in this program whether we write @code{i++} or "
"@code{++i}, because the value @emph{of that expression} is not used.  We use "
"it for its effect, to increment @code{i}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5747
msgid ""
"The @samp{--} operator also works on pointers; it can be used to step "
"backwards through an array, like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5754
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int\n"
"after_last_nonzero (int *p, int len)\n"
"@{\n"
"  /* @r{Set up @code{q} to point just after the last array element.}  */\n"
"  int *q = p + len;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5760
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  while (q != p)\n"
"    /* @r{Step @code{q} back until it reaches a nonzero element.}  */\n"
"    if (*--q != 0)\n"
"      /* @r{Return the index of the element after that nonzero.}  */\n"
"      return q - p + 1;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5763
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  return 0;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5768
msgid ""
"That function returns the length of the nonzero part of the array specified "
"by its arguments; that is, the index of the first zero of the run of zeros "
"at the end."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5770
#, no-wrap
msgid "Drawbacks of Pointer Arithmetic"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5771
#, no-wrap
msgid "drawbacks of pointer arithmetic"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5772
#, no-wrap
msgid "pointer arithmetic, drawbacks"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5780
msgid ""
"Pointer arithmetic is clean and elegant, but it is also the cause of a major "
"security flaw in the C language.  Theoretically, it is only valid to adjust "
"a pointer within one object allocated as a unit in memory.  However, if you "
"unintentionally adjust a pointer across the bounds of the object and into "
"some other object, the system has no way to detect this error."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5785
msgid ""
"A bug which does that can easily result in clobbering (overwriting)  part of "
"another object.  For example, with @code{array[-1]} you can read or write "
"the nonexistent element before the beginning of an array---probably part of "
"some other data."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5789
msgid ""
"Combining pointer arithmetic with casts between pointer types, you can "
"create a pointer that fails to be properly aligned for its type.  For "
"example,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5794
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int a[2];\n"
"char *pa = (char *)a;\n"
"int *p = (int *)(pa + 1);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5803
msgid ""
"gives @code{p} a value pointing to an ``integer'' that includes part of "
"@code{a[0]} and part of @code{a[1]}.  Dereferencing that with @code{*p} can "
"cause a fatal @code{SIGSEGV} signal or it can return the contents of that "
"badly aligned @code{int} (@pxref{Signals}.  If it ``works,'' it may be quite "
"slow.  It can also cause aliasing confusions (@pxref{Aliasing})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5806
msgid ""
"@strong{Warning:} Using improperly aligned pointers is risky---don't do it "
"unless it is really necessary."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5809
#, no-wrap
msgid "pointer-integer conversion"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5810
#, no-wrap
msgid "conversion between pointers and integers"
msgstr ""

#. type: code{#1}
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5811
#, no-wrap
msgid "uintptr_t"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5819
msgid ""
"On modern computers, an address is simply a number.  It occupies the same "
"space as some size of integer.  In C, you can convert a pointer to the "
"appropriate integer types and vice versa, without losing information.  The "
"appropriate integer types are @code{uintptr_t} (an unsigned type) and "
"@code{intptr_t} (a signed type).  Both are defined in @file{stdint.h}."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5825
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"#include <stdint.h>\n"
"#include <stdio.h>\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5830
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"print_pointer (void *ptr)\n"
"@{\n"
"  uintptr_t converted = (uintptr_t) ptr;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5834
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  printf (\"Pointer value is 0x%x\\n"
"\",\n"
"          (unsigned int) converted);\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5842
msgid ""
"The specification @samp{%x} in the template (the first argument) for "
"@code{printf} means to represent this argument using hexadecimal notation.  "
"It's cleaner to use @code{uintptr_t}, since hexadecimal printing treats the "
"number as unsigned, but it won't actually matter: all @code{printf} gets to "
"see is the series of bits in the number."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5845
msgid ""
"@strong{Warning:} Converting pointers to integers is risky---don't do it "
"unless it is really necessary."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5851
msgid ""
"To print the numeric value of a pointer, use the @samp{%p} specifier.  For "
"example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5858
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"print_pointer (void *ptr)\n"
"@{\n"
"  printf (\"Pointer value is %p\\n"
"\", ptr);\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5863
msgid ""
"The specification @samp{%p} works with any pointer type.  It prints "
"@samp{0x} followed by the address in hexadecimal, printed as the appropriate "
"unsigned integer type."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5866
#, no-wrap
msgid "structures"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5867
#, no-wrap
msgid "struct"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5868
#, no-wrap
msgid "fields in structures"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5869
#, no-wrap
msgid "compound type"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5876
msgid ""
"A @dfn{structure} is a user-defined data type that holds various "
"@dfn{fields} of data.  Each field has a name and a data type specified in "
"the structure's definition.  Because a structure combines various fields, "
"each of its own type, we call a structure type a @dfn{compound type}.  "
"(Union types are also compound types; @pxref{Unions}.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5880
msgid ""
"Here we define a structure suitable for storing a linked list of integers.  "
"Each list item will hold one integer, plus a pointer to the next item."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5887
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct intlistlink\n"
"  @{\n"
"    int datum;\n"
"    struct intlistlink *next;\n"
"  @};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5893
msgid ""
"The structure definition has a @dfn{type tag} so that the code can refer to "
"this structure.  The type tag here is @code{intlistlink}.  The definition "
"refers recursively to the same structure through that tag."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5897
msgid ""
"You can define a structure without a type tag, but then you can't refer to "
"it again.  That is useful only in some special contexts, such as inside a "
"@code{typedef} or a @code{union}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5903
msgid ""
"The contents of the structure are specified by the @dfn{field declarations} "
"inside the braces.  Each field in the structure needs a declaration there.  "
"The fields in one structure definition must have distinct names, but these "
"names do not conflict with any other names in the program."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5907
msgid ""
"A field declaration looks just like a variable declaration.  You can combine "
"field declarations with the same beginning, just as you can combine variable "
"declarations."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5913
msgid ""
"This structure has two fields.  One, named @code{datum}, has type @code{int} "
"and will hold one integer in the list.  The other, named @code{next}, is a "
"pointer to another @code{struct intlistlink} which would be the rest of the "
"list.  In the last list item, it would be @code{NULL}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5920
msgid ""
"This structure definition is recursive, since the type of the @code{next} "
"field refers to the structure type.  Such recursion is not a problem; in "
"fact, you can use the type @code{struct intlistlink *} before the definition "
"of the type @code{struct intlistlink} itself.  That works because pointers "
"to all kinds of structures really look the same at the machine level."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5923
msgid ""
"After defining the structure, you can declare a variable of type "
"@code{struct intlistlink} like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5926 c-intro-and-ref.texi:6009
#, no-wrap
msgid "struct intlistlink foo;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5931
msgid ""
"The structure definition itself can serve as the beginning of a variable "
"declaration, so you can declare variables immediately after, like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5938
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct intlistlink\n"
"  @{\n"
"    int datum;\n"
"    struct intlistlink *next;\n"
"  @} foo;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5943
msgid ""
"But that is ugly.  It is almost always clearer to separate the definition of "
"the structure from its uses."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5948
msgid ""
"Declaring a structure type inside a block (@pxref{Blocks}) limits the scope "
"of the structure type name to that block.  That means the structure type is "
"recognized only within that block.  Declaring it in a function parameter "
"list, as here,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5951
#, no-wrap
msgid "int f (struct foo @{int a, b@} parm);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5957
msgid ""
"(assuming that @code{struct foo} is not already defined) limits the scope of "
"the structure type @code{struct foo} to that parameter list; that is "
"basically useless, so it triggers a warning."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5960
msgid ""
"Standard C requires at least one field in a structure.  GNU C does not "
"require this."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5991
#, no-wrap
msgid "Referencing Structure Fields"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5992
#, no-wrap
msgid "referencing structure fields"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5993
#, no-wrap
msgid "structure fields, referencing"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:5998
msgid ""
"To make a structure useful, there has to be a way to examine and store its "
"fields.  The @samp{.} (period) operator does that; its use looks like "
"@code{@var{object}.@var{field}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6000
msgid "Given this structure and variable,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6007
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct intlistlink\n"
"  @{\n"
"    int datum;\n"
"    struct intlistlink *next;\n"
"  @};\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6015
msgid ""
"you can write @code{foo.datum} and @code{foo.next} to refer to the two "
"fields in the value of @code{foo}.  These fields are lvalues, so you can "
"store values into them, and read the values out again."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6021
msgid ""
"Most often, structures are dynamically allocated (see the next section), and "
"we refer to the objects via pointers.  @code{(*p).@var{field}} is somewhat "
"cumbersome, so there is an abbreviation: @code{p->@var{field}}.  For "
"instance, assume the program contains this declaration:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6024
#, no-wrap
msgid "struct intlistlink *ptr;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6029
msgid ""
"You can write @code{ptr->datum} and @code{ptr->next} to refer to the two "
"fields in the object that @code{ptr} points to."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6032
msgid ""
"If a unary operator precedes an expression using @samp{->}, the @samp{->} "
"nests inside:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6035
#, no-wrap
msgid "  -ptr->datum   @r{is equivalent to}   -(ptr->datum)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6039
msgid ""
"You can intermix @samp{->} and @samp{.} without parentheses, as shown here:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6042
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct @{ double d; struct intlistlink l; @} foo;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6044
#, no-wrap
msgid "@r{@dots{}}foo.l.next->next->datum@r{@dots{}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6050
msgid "When you declare field in a structure as an array, as here:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6057
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct record\n"
"  @{\n"
"    char *name;\n"
"    int data[4];\n"
"  @};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6065
msgid ""
"Each @code{struct record} object holds one string (a pointer, of course) and "
"four integers, all part of a field called @code{data}.  If @code{recptr} is "
"a pointer of type @code{struct record *}, then it points to a @code{struct "
"record} which contains those things; you can access the second integer in "
"that record with @code{recptr->data[1]}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6068
msgid ""
"If you have two objects of type @code{struct record}, each one contains an "
"array.  With this declaration,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6071 c-intro-and-ref.texi:6628
#, no-wrap
msgid "struct record r1, r2;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6075
msgid ""
"@code{r1.data} holds space for 4 @code{int}s, and @code{r2.data} holds space "
"for another 4 @code{int}s,"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6078
#, no-wrap
msgid "dynamic memory allocation"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6079
#, no-wrap
msgid "memory allocation, dynamic"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6080
#, no-wrap
msgid "allocating memory dynamically"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6087
msgid ""
"To allocate an object dynamically, call the library function @code{malloc} "
"(@pxref{Basic Allocation, The GNU C Library,, libc, The GNU C Library "
"Reference Manual}).  Here is how to allocate an object of type @code{struct "
"intlistlink}.  To make this code work, include the file @file{stdlib.h}, "
"like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6091
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"#include <stddef.h>  /* @r{Defines @code{NULL}.} */\n"
"#include <stdlib.h>  /* @r{Declares @code{malloc}.}  */\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6093
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@dots{}\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6098
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct intlistlink *\n"
"alloc_intlistlink ()\n"
"@{\n"
"  struct intlistlink *p;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6100
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  p = malloc (sizeof (struct intlistlink));\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6103
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  if (p == NULL)\n"
"    fatal (\"Ran out of storage\");\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6107
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  /* @r{Initialize the contents.} */\n"
"  p->datum = 0;\n"
"  p->next = NULL;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6110
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  return p;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6117
msgid ""
"@code{malloc} returns @code{void *}, so the assignment to @code{p} will "
"automatically convert it to type @code{struct intlistlink *}.  The return "
"value of @code{malloc} is always sufficiently aligned (@pxref{Type "
"Alignment}) that it is valid for any data type."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6122
msgid ""
"The test for @code{p == NULL} is necessary because @code{malloc} returns a "
"null pointer if it cannot get any storage.  We assume that the program "
"defines the function @code{fatal} to report a fatal error to the user."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6124
msgid "Here's how to add one more integer to the front of such a list:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6127
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct intlistlink *my_list = NULL;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6132
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"add_to_mylist (int my_int)\n"
"@{\n"
"  struct intlistlink *p = alloc_intlistlink ();\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6137
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  p->datum = my_int;\n"
"  p->next = mylist;\n"
"  mylist = p;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6141
msgid ""
"The way to free the objects is by calling @code{free}.  Here's a function to "
"free all the links in one of these lists:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6153 c-intro-and-ref.texi:8119
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"free_intlist (struct intlistlink *p)\n"
"@{\n"
"  while (p)\n"
"    @{\n"
"      struct intlistlink *q = p;\n"
"      p = p->next;\n"
"      free (q);\n"
"    @}\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6160
msgid ""
"We must extract the @code{next} pointer from the object before freeing it, "
"because @code{free} can clobber the data that was in the object.  For the "
"same reason, the program must not use the list any more after freeing its "
"elements.  To make sure it won't, it is best to clear out the variable where "
"the list was stored, like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6163
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"free_intlist (mylist);\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6165
#, no-wrap
msgid "mylist = NULL;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6169
#, no-wrap
msgid "field offset"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6170
#, no-wrap
msgid "structure field offset"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6171
#, no-wrap
msgid "offset of structure fields"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6176
msgid ""
"To determine the offset of a given field @var{field} in a structure type "
"@var{type}, use the macro @code{offsetof}, which is defined in the file "
"@file{stddef.h}.  It is used like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6179
#, no-wrap
msgid "offsetof (@var{type}, @var{field})\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6182 c-intro-and-ref.texi:12769
msgid "Here is an example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6189
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct foo\n"
"@{\n"
"  int element;\n"
"  struct foo *next;\n"
"@};\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6192
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"offsetof (struct foo, next)\n"
"/* @r{On most machines that is 4.  It may be 8.}  */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6196
#, no-wrap
msgid "structure layout"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6197
#, no-wrap
msgid "layout of structures"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6201
msgid ""
"The rest of this chapter covers advanced topics about structures.  If you "
"are just learning C, you can skip it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6209
msgid ""
"The precise layout of a @code{struct} type is crucial when using it to "
"overlay hardware registers, to access data structures in shared memory, or "
"to assemble and disassemble packets for network communication.  It is also "
"important for avoiding memory waste when the program makes many objects of "
"that type.  However, the layout depends on the target platform.  Each "
"platform has conventions for structure layout, which compilers need to "
"follow."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6211
msgid "Here are the conventions used on most platforms."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6217
msgid ""
"The structure's fields appear in the structure layout in the order they are "
"declared.  When possible, consecutive fields occupy consecutive bytes within "
"the structure.  However, if a field's type demands more alignment than it "
"would get that way, C gives it the alignment it requires by leaving a gap "
"after the previous field."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6218
#, no-wrap
msgid "structure alignment"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6219
#, no-wrap
msgid "alignment of structures"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6225
msgid ""
"Once all the fields have been laid out, it is possible to determine the "
"structure's alignment and size.  The structure's alignment is the maximum "
"alignment of any of the fields in it.  Then the structure's size is rounded "
"up to a multiple of its alignment.  That may require leaving a gap at the "
"end of the structure."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6229
msgid ""
"Here are some examples, where we assume that @code{char} has size and "
"alignment 1 (always true), and @code{int} has size and alignment 4 (true on "
"most kinds of computers):"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6236
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct foo\n"
"@{\n"
"  char a, b;\n"
"  int c;\n"
"@};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6242
msgid ""
"This structure occupies 8 bytes, with an alignment of 4.  @code{a} is at "
"offset 0, @code{b} is at offset 1, and @code{c} is at offset 4.  There is a "
"gap of 2 bytes before @code{c}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6244
msgid "Contrast that with this structure:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6252
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct foo\n"
"@{\n"
"  char a;\n"
"  int c;\n"
"  char b;\n"
"@};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6257
msgid ""
"This structure has size 12 and alignment 4.  @code{a} is at offset 0, "
"@code{c} is at offset 4, and @code{b} is at offset 8.  There are two gaps: "
"three bytes before @code{c}, and three bytes at the end."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6262
msgid ""
"These two structures have the same contents at the C level, but one takes 8 "
"bytes and the other takes 12 bytes due to the ordering of the fields.  A "
"reliable way to avoid this sort of wastage is to order the fields by size, "
"biggest fields first."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6265
#, no-wrap
msgid "packed structures"
msgstr ""

#. type: code{#1}
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6266
#, no-wrap
msgid "__attribute__((packed))"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6271
msgid ""
"In GNU C you can force a structure to be laid out with no gaps by adding "
"@code{__attribute__((packed))} after @code{struct} (or at the end of the "
"structure type declaration).  Here's an example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6279
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct __attribute__((packed)) foo\n"
"@{\n"
"  char a;\n"
"  int c;\n"
"  char b;\n"
"@};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6285
msgid ""
"Without @code{__attribute__((packed))}, this structure occupies 12 bytes (as "
"described in the previous section), assuming 4-byte alignment for "
"@code{int}.  With @code{__attribute__((packed))}, it is only 6 bytes "
"long---the sum of the lengths of its fields."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6292
msgid ""
"Use of @code{__attribute__((packed))} often results in fields that don't "
"have the normal alignment for their types.  Taking the address of such a "
"field can result in an invalid pointer because of its improper alignment.  "
"Dereferencing such a pointer can cause a @code{SIGSEGV} signal on a machine "
"that doesn't, in general, allow unaligned pointers."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6294 c-intro-and-ref.texi:7777
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13027
msgid "@xref{Attributes}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6297
#, no-wrap
msgid "bit fields"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6303
msgid ""
"A structure field declaration with an integer type can specify the number of "
"bits the field should occupy.  We call that a @dfn{bit field}.  These are "
"useful because consecutive bit fields are packed into a larger storage "
"unit.  For instance,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6306
#, no-wrap
msgid "unsigned char opcode: 4;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6312
msgid ""
"specifies that this field takes just 4 bits.  Since it is unsigned, its "
"possible values range from 0 to 15.  A signed field with 4 bits, such as "
"this,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6315
#, no-wrap
msgid "signed char small: 4;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6319
msgid "can hold values from -8 to 7."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6321
msgid "You can subdivide a single byte into those two parts by writing"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6325
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"unsigned char opcode: 4;\n"
"signed char small: 4;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6330
msgid ""
"in the structure.  With bit fields, these two numbers fit into a single "
"@code{char}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6332
msgid "Here's how to declare a one-bit field that can hold either 0 or 1:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6335
#, no-wrap
msgid "unsigned char special_flag: 1;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6338
msgid "You can also use the @code{bool} type for bit fields:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6341
#, no-wrap
msgid "bool special_flag: 1;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6349
msgid ""
"Except when using @code{bool} (which is always unsigned, @pxref{Boolean "
"Type}), always specify @code{signed} or @code{unsigned} for a bit field.  "
"There is a default, if that's not specified: the bit field is signed if "
"plain @code{char} is signed, except that the option "
"@option{-funsigned-bitfields} forces unsigned as the default.  But it is "
"cleaner not to depend on this default."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6354
msgid ""
"Bit fields are special in that you cannot take their address with @samp{&}.  "
"They are not stored with the size and alignment appropriate for the "
"specified type, so they cannot be addressed through pointers to that type."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6361
msgid ""
"Programs to communicate with low-level hardware interfaces need to define "
"bit fields laid out to match the hardware data.  This section explains how "
"to do that."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6364
msgid ""
"Consecutive bit fields are packed together, but each bit field must fit "
"within a single object of its specified type.  In this example,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6367
#, no-wrap
msgid "unsigned short a : 3, b : 3, c : 3, d : 3, e : 3;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6372
msgid ""
"all five fields fit consecutively into one two-byte @code{short}.  They need "
"15 bits, and one @code{short} provides 16.  By contrast,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6375
#, no-wrap
msgid "unsigned char a : 3, b : 3, c : 3, d : 3, e : 3;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6383
msgid ""
"needs three bytes.  It fits @code{a} and @code{b} into one @code{char}, but "
"@code{c} won't fit in that @code{char} (they would add up to 9 bits).  So "
"@code{c} and @code{d} go into a second @code{char}, leaving a gap of two "
"bits between @code{b} and @code{c}.  Then @code{e} needs a third "
"@code{char}.  By contrast,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6388
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"unsigned char a : 3, b : 3;\n"
"unsigned int c : 3;\n"
"unsigned char d : 3, e : 3;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6393
msgid ""
"needs only two bytes: the type @code{unsigned int} allows @code{c} to "
"straddle bytes that are in the same word."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6398
msgid ""
"You can leave a gap of a specified number of bits by defining a nameless bit "
"field.  This looks like @code{@var{type} : @var{nbits};}.  It is allocated "
"space in the structure just as a named bit field would be allocated."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6401
msgid ""
"You can force the following bit field to advance to the following aligned "
"memory object with @code{@var{type} : 0;}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6404
msgid ""
"Both of these constructs can syntactically share @var{type} with ordinary "
"bit fields.  This example illustrates both:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6407
#, no-wrap
msgid "unsigned int a : 5, : 3, b : 5, : 0, c : 5, : 3, d : 5;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6413
msgid ""
"It puts @code{a} and @code{b} into one @code{int}, with a 3-bit gap between "
"them.  Then @code{: 0} advances to the next @code{int}, so @code{c} and "
"@code{d} fit into that one."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6417
msgid ""
"These rules for packing bit fields apply to most target platforms, including "
"all the usual real computers.  A few embedded controllers have special "
"layout rules."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6419
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{const} Fields"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6420
#, no-wrap
msgid "const fields"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6421
#, no-wrap
msgid "structure fields, constant"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6428
msgid ""
"A structure field declared @code{const} cannot be assigned to "
"(@pxref{const}).  For instance, let's define this modified version of "
"@code{struct intlistlink}:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6435
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct intlistlink_ro  /* @r{``ro'' for read-only.}  */\n"
"  @{\n"
"    const int datum;\n"
"    struct intlistlink *next;\n"
"  @};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6439
msgid ""
"This structure can be used to prevent part of the code from modifying the "
"@code{datum} field:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6445
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"/* @r{@code{p} has type @code{struct intlistlink *}.}\n"
"   @r{Convert it to @code{struct intlistlink_ro *}.}  */\n"
"struct intlistlink_ro *q\n"
"  = (struct intlistlink_ro *) p;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6449
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"q->datum = 5;     /* @r{Error!} */\n"
"p->datum = 5;     /* @r{Valid since @code{*p} is}\n"
"                     @r{not a @code{struct intlistlink_ro}.}  */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6454
msgid ""
"A @code{const} field can get a value in two ways: by initialization of the "
"whole structure, and by making a pointer-to-structure point to an object in "
"which that field already has a value."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6459
msgid ""
"Any @code{const} field in a structure type makes assignment impossible for "
"structures of that type (@pxref{Structure Assignment}).  That is because "
"structure assignment works by assigning the structure's fields, one by one."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6461
#, no-wrap
msgid "Arrays of Length Zero"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6462
#, no-wrap
msgid "array of length zero"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6463
#, no-wrap
msgid "zero-length arrays"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6464
#, no-wrap
msgid "length-zero arrays"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6470
msgid ""
"GNU C allows zero-length arrays.  They are useful as the last field of a "
"structure that is really a header for a variable-length object.  Here's an "
"example, where we construct a variable-size structure to hold a line which "
"is @code{this_length} characters long:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6476
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct line @{\n"
"  int length;\n"
"  char contents[0];\n"
"@};\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6482
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct line *thisline\n"
"  = ((struct line *)\n"
"     malloc (sizeof (struct line)\n"
"             + this_length));\n"
"thisline->length = this_length;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6486
msgid ""
"In ISO C90, we would have to give @code{contents} a length of 1, which means "
"either wasting space or complicating the argument to @code{malloc}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6489
#, no-wrap
msgid "flexible array fields"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6490
#, no-wrap
msgid "array fields, flexible"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6495
msgid ""
"The C99 standard adopted a more complex equivalent of zero-length array "
"fields.  It's called a @dfn{flexible array}, and it's indicated by omitting "
"the length, like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6502
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct line\n"
"@{\n"
"  int length;\n"
"  char contents[];\n"
"@};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6506
msgid ""
"The flexible array has to be the last field in the structure, and there must "
"be other fields before it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6509
msgid ""
"Under the C standard, a structure with a flexible array can't be part of "
"another structure, and can't be an element of an array."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6512
msgid ""
"GNU C allows static initialization of flexible array fields.  The effect is "
"to ``make the array long enough'' for the initializer."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6516
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct f1 @{ int x; int y[]; @} f1\n"
"  = @{ 1, @{ 2, 3, 4 @} @};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6522
msgid ""
"This defines a structure variable named @code{f1} whose type is @code{struct "
"f1}.  In C, a variable name or function name never conflicts with a "
"structure type tag."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6527
msgid ""
"Omitting the flexible array field's size lets the initializer determine it.  "
"This is allowed only when the flexible array is defined in the outermost "
"structure and you declare a variable of that structure type.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6531
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct foo @{ int x; int y[]; @};\n"
"struct bar @{ struct foo z; @};\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6536
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct foo a = @{ 1, @{ 2, 3, 4 @} @};        // @r{Valid.}\n"
"struct bar b = @{ @{ 1, @{ 2, 3, 4 @} @} @};    // @r{Invalid.}\n"
"struct bar c = @{ @{ 1, @{ @} @} @};            // @r{Valid.}\n"
"struct foo d[1] = @{ @{ 1 @{ 2, 3, 4 @} @} @};  // @r{Invalid.}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6539
#, no-wrap
msgid "Overlaying Different Structures"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6540
#, no-wrap
msgid "overlaying structures"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6541
#, no-wrap
msgid "structures, overlaying"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6547
msgid ""
"Be careful about using different structure types to refer to the same memory "
"within one function, because GNU C can optimize code assuming it never does "
"that.  @xref{Aliasing}.  Here's an example of the kind of aliasing that can "
"cause the problem:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6554
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct a @{ int size; char *data; @};\n"
"struct b @{ int size; char *data; @};\n"
"struct a foo;\n"
"struct a *p = &foo;\n"
"struct b *q = (struct b *) &foo;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6561
msgid ""
"Here @code{q} points to the same memory that the variable @code{foo} "
"occupies, but they have two different types.  The two types @code{struct a} "
"and @code{struct b} are defined alike, but they are not the same type.  "
"Interspersing references using the two types, like this,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6566
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"p->size = 0;\n"
"q->size = 1;\n"
"x = p->size;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6573
msgid ""
"allows GNU C to assume that @code{p->size} is still zero when it is copied "
"into @code{x}.  The GNU C compiler ``knows'' that @code{q} points to a "
"@code{struct b} and this is not supposed to overlap with a @code{struct a}.  "
"Other compilers might also do this optimization."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6576
msgid ""
"The ISO C standard considers such code erroneous, precisely so that this "
"optimization will not be incorrect."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6579
#, no-wrap
msgid "structure assignment"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6580
#, no-wrap
msgid "assigning structures"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6584
msgid ""
"Assignment operating on a structure type copies the structure.  The left and "
"right operands must have the same type.  Here is an example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6589
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"#include <stddef.h>  /* @r{Defines @code{NULL}.} */\n"
"#include <stdlib.h>  /* @r{Declares @code{malloc}.}  */\n"
"@r{@dots{}}\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6591 c-intro-and-ref.texi:9438
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct point @{ double x, y; @};\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6602
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct point *\n"
"copy_point (struct point point)\n"
"@{\n"
"  struct point *p\n"
"    = (struct point *) malloc (sizeof (struct point));\n"
"  if (p == NULL)\n"
"    fatal (\"Out of memory\");\n"
"  *p = point;\n"
"  return p;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6607
msgid ""
"Notionally, assignment on a structure type works by copying each of the "
"fields.  Thus, if any of the fields has the @code{const} qualifier, that "
"structure type does not allow assignment:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6610
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct point @{ const double x, y; @};\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6612
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct point a, b;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6614
#, no-wrap
msgid "a = b;            /* @r{Error!} */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6617
msgid "@xref{Assignment Expressions}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6619
msgid "When a structure type has a field which is an array, as here,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6626
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct record\n"
"  @{\n"
"    char *name;\n"
"    int data[4];\n"
"  @};\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6633
msgid ""
"structure assignment such as @code{r1 = r2} copies array fields' contents "
"just as it copies all the other fields."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6638
msgid ""
"This is the only way in C that you can operate on the whole contents of a "
"array with one operation: when the array is contained in a @code{struct}.  "
"You can't copy the contents of the @code{data} field as an array, because"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6641
#, no-wrap
msgid "r1.data = r2.data;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6647
msgid ""
"would convert the array objects (as always) to pointers to the zeroth "
"elements of the arrays (of type @code{int *}), and the assignment would be "
"invalid because the left operand is not an lvalue."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6650
#, no-wrap
msgid "unions"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6651
#, no-wrap
msgid "union"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6658
msgid ""
"A @dfn{union type} defines alternative ways of looking at the same piece of "
"memory.  Each alternative view is defined with a data type, and identified "
"by a name.  Because a union combines various types, it is considered a "
"@dfn{compound type}, like structures (@pxref{Structures}).  A union "
"definition looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6664
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"union @var{name}\n"
"@{\n"
"  @var{alternative declarations}@r{@dots{}}\n"
"@};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6668
msgid ""
"Each alternative declaration looks like a structure field declaration, "
"except that it can't be a bit field.  For instance,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6675
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"union number\n"
"@{\n"
"  long int integer;\n"
"  double float;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6685
msgid ""
"lets you store either an integer (type @code{long int}) or a floating point "
"number (type @code{double}) in the same place in memory.  The length and "
"alignment of the union type are the maximum of all the alternatives---they "
"do not have to be the same.  In this union example, @code{double} probably "
"takes more space than @code{long int}, but that doesn't cause a problem in "
"programs that use the union in the normal way."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6688
msgid ""
"The members don't have to be different in data type.  Sometimes each member "
"pertains to a way the data will be used.  For instance,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6698
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"union datum\n"
"@{\n"
"  double latitude;\n"
"  double longitude;\n"
"  double height;\n"
"  double weight;\n"
"  int continent;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6705
msgid ""
"This union holds one of several kinds of data; most kinds are floating "
"points, but the value can also be a code for a continent which is an "
"integer.  You @emph{could} use one member of type @code{double} to access "
"all the values which have that type, but the different member names will "
"make the program clearer."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6710
msgid ""
"The alignment of a union type is the maximum of the alignments of the "
"alternatives.  The size of the union type is the maximum of the sizes of the "
"alternatives, rounded up to a multiple of the alignment (because every "
"type's size must be a multiple of its alignment)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6715
msgid ""
"All the union alternatives start at the address of the union itself.  If an "
"alternative is shorter than the union as a whole, it occupies the first part "
"of the union's storage, leaving the last part unused @emph{for that "
"alternative}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6720
msgid ""
"@strong{Warning:} If the code stores data using one union alternative and "
"accesses it with another, the results depend on the kind of computer in "
"use.  Only wizards should try to do this.  However, when you need to do "
"this, a union is a clean way to do it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6722
msgid "Assignment works on any union type by copying the entire value."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6728
msgid ""
"Sometimes we design a union with the intention of packing various kinds of "
"objects into a certain amount of memory space.  For example."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6737
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"union bytes8\n"
"@{\n"
"  long long big_int_elt;\n"
"  double double_elt;\n"
"  struct @{ int first, second; @} two_ints;\n"
"  struct @{ void *first, *second; @} two_ptrs;\n"
"@};\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6739
#, no-wrap
msgid "union bytes8 *p;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6747
msgid ""
"This union makes it possible to look at 8 bytes of data that @code{p} points "
"to as a single 8-byte integer (@code{p->big_int_elt}), as a single "
"floating-point number (@code{p->double_elt}), as a pair of integers "
"(@code{p->two_ints.first} and @code{p->two_ints.second}), or as a pair of "
"pointers (@code{p->two_ptrs.first} and @code{p->two_ptrs.second})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6757
msgid ""
"To pack storage with such a union makes assumptions about the sizes of all "
"the types involved.  This particular union was written expecting a pointer "
"to have the same size as @code{int}.  On a machine where one pointer takes 8 "
"bytes, the code using this union probably won't work as expected.  The "
"union, as such, will function correctly---if you store two values through "
"@code{two_ints} and extract them through @code{two_ints}, you will get the "
"same integers back---but the part of the program that expects the union to "
"be 8 bytes long could malfunction, or at least use too much space."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6761
msgid ""
"The above example shows one case where a @code{struct} type with no tag can "
"be useful.  Another way to get effectively the same result is with arrays as "
"members of the union:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6770
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"union eight_bytes\n"
"@{\n"
"  long long big_int_elt;\n"
"  double double_elt;\n"
"  int two_ints[2];\n"
"  void *two_ptrs[2];\n"
"@};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6773
#, no-wrap
msgid "Cast to a Union Type"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6774
#, no-wrap
msgid "cast to a union"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6775
#, no-wrap
msgid "union, casting to a"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6779
msgid ""
"In GNU C, you can explicitly cast any of the alternative types to the union "
"type; for instance,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6782
#, no-wrap
msgid "(union eight_bytes) (long long) 5\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6787
msgid ""
"makes a value of type @code{union eight_bytes} which gets its contents "
"through the alternative named @code{big_int_elt}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6790
msgid ""
"The value being cast must exactly match the type of the alternative, so this "
"is not valid:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6793
#, no-wrap
msgid "(union eight_bytes) 5  /* @r{Error!  5 is @code{int}.} */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6799
msgid ""
"A cast to union type looks like any other cast, except that the type "
"specified is a union type.  You can specify the type either with @code{union "
"@var{tag}} or with a typedef name (@pxref{Defining Typedef Names})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6802
msgid ""
"Using the cast as the right-hand side of an assignment to a variable of "
"union type is equivalent to storing in an alternative of the union:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6806
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"/* @r{Define the union @code{foo}.}  */\n"
"union foo @{ int i; double d; @};\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6809
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"/* @r{Declare the union-valued variable, @code{u}.}  */\n"
"union foo u;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6811
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int x;  double y;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6813
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"u = (union foo) x   @r{means}   u.i = x\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6815
#, no-wrap
msgid "u = (union foo) y   @r{means}   u.d = y\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6818
msgid "You can also use the union cast as a function argument:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6823
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void hack (union foo);\n"
"@r{@dots{}}\n"
"hack ((union foo) x);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6827
#, no-wrap
msgid "structure constructors"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6828
#, no-wrap
msgid "constructors, structure"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6833
msgid ""
"You can construct a structure value by writing its type in parentheses, "
"followed by an initializer that would be valid in a declaration for that "
"type.  For instance, given this declaration,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6836
#, no-wrap
msgid "struct foo @{int a; char b[2];@} structure;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6840
msgid "you can create a @code{struct foo} value as follows:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6843
#, no-wrap
msgid "((struct foo) @{x + y, 'a', 0@})\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6849
msgid ""
"This specifies @code{x + y} for field @code{a}, the character @samp{a} for "
"field @code{b}'s element 0, and the null character for field @code{b}'s "
"element 1."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6853
msgid ""
"The parentheses around that constructor are not necessary, but we recommend "
"writing them to make the nesting of the containing expression clearer."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6856
msgid "You can also show the nesting of the two by writing it like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6859
#, no-wrap
msgid "((struct foo) @{x + y, @{'a', 0@} @})\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6863
msgid ""
"Each of those is equivalent to writing the following statement expression "
"(@pxref{Statement Exprs}):"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6869
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"(@{\n"
"  struct foo temp = @{x + y, 'a', 0@};\n"
"  temp;\n"
"@})\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6874
msgid ""
"You can also use field labels in the structure constructor to indicate which "
"fields you're specifying values for, instead of using the order of the "
"fields to specify that:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6877
#, no-wrap
msgid "(struct foo) @{.a = x + y, .b = @{'a', 0@}@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6881
msgid ""
"You can also create a union value this way, but it is not especially useful "
"since that is equivalent to doing a cast:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6886
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  ((union whosis) @{@var{value}@})\n"
"@r{is equivalent to}\n"
"  ((union whosis) (@var{value}))\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6890
#, no-wrap
msgid "unnamed structures"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6891
#, no-wrap
msgid "unnamed unions"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6892
#, no-wrap
msgid "structures, unnamed"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6893
#, no-wrap
msgid "unions, unnamed"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6897
msgid ""
"A structure or a union can contain, as fields, unnamed structures and "
"unions.  Here's an example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6909
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct\n"
"@{\n"
"  int a;\n"
"  union\n"
"  @{\n"
"    int b;\n"
"    float c;\n"
"  @};\n"
"  int d;\n"
"@} foo;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6914
msgid ""
"You can access the fields of the unnamed union within @code{foo} as if they "
"were individual fields at the same level as the union definition:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6920
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"foo.a = 42;\n"
"foo.b = 47;\n"
"foo.c = 5.25; // @r{Overwrites the value in @code{foo.b}}.\n"
"foo.d = 314;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6924
msgid ""
"Avoid using field names that could cause ambiguity.  For example, with this "
"definition:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6935
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct\n"
"@{\n"
"  int a;\n"
"  struct\n"
"  @{\n"
"    int a;\n"
"    float b;\n"
"  @};\n"
"@} foo;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6940
msgid ""
"it is impossible to tell what @code{foo.a} refers to.  GNU C reports an "
"error when a definition is ambiguous in this way."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6943
#, no-wrap
msgid "incomplete types"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6944
#, no-wrap
msgid "types, incomplete"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6950
msgid ""
"A type that has not been fully defined is called an @dfn{incomplete type}.  "
"Structure and union types are incomplete when the code makes a forward "
"reference, such as @code{struct foo}, before defining the type.  An array "
"type is incomplete when its length is unspecified."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6955
msgid ""
"You can't use an incomplete type to declare a variable or field, or use it "
"for a function parameter or return type.  The operators @code{sizeof} and "
"@code{_Alignof} give errors when used on an incomplete type."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6961
msgid ""
"However, you can define a pointer to an incomplete type, and declare a "
"variable or field with such a pointer type.  In general, you can do "
"everything with such pointers except dereference them, increment or "
"decrement them, or do pointer arithmetic with them (not even @code{p + 0}).  "
"For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6964
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"extern void bar (struct mysterious_value *);\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6970
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"foo (struct mysterious_value *arg)\n"
"@{\n"
"  bar (arg);\n"
"@}\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6972
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@r{@dots{}}\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6975
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@{\n"
"  struct mysterious_value *p, **q;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6979
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  p = *q;\n"
"  foo (p);\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6988
msgid ""
"These examples are valid because the code doesn't try to understand what "
"@code{p} points to; it just passes the pointer around.  (Presumably "
"@code{bar} is defined in some other file that really does have a definition "
"for @code{struct mysterious_value}.)  However, dereferencing the pointer "
"would get an error; that requires a definition for the structure type."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:6995
msgid ""
"When several structure types contain pointers to each other, you can define "
"the types in any order because pointers to types that come later are "
"incomplete types.  Here is an example."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7005
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"/* @r{An employee record points to a group.}  */\n"
"struct employee\n"
"@{\n"
"  char *name;\n"
"  @r{@dots{}}\n"
"  struct group *group;  /* @r{incomplete type.}  */\n"
"  @r{@dots{}}\n"
"@};\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7013
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"/* @r{An employee list points to employees.}  */\n"
"struct employee_list\n"
"@{\n"
"  struct employee *this_one;\n"
"  struct employee_list *next;  /* @r{incomplete type.}  */\n"
"  @r{@dots{}}\n"
"@};\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7022
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"/* @r{A group points to one employee_list.}  */\n"
"struct group\n"
"@{\n"
"  char *name;\n"
"  @r{@dots{}}\n"
"  struct employee_list *employees;\n"
"  @r{@dots{}}\n"
"@};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7026
#, no-wrap
msgid "type tags"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7033
msgid ""
"The name that follows @code{struct} (@pxref{Structures}), @code{union} "
"(@pxref{Unions}, or @code{enum} (@pxref{Enumeration Types}) is called a "
"@dfn{type tag}.  In C, a type tag never conflicts with a variable name or "
"function name; the type tags have a separate @dfn{name space}.  Thus, there "
"is no name conflict in this code:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7037
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct pair @{ int a, b; @};\n"
"int pair = 1;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7041
msgid "nor in this one:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7044
#, no-wrap
msgid "struct pair @{ int a, b; @} pair;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7048
msgid "where @code{pair} is both a structure type tag and a variable name."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7051
msgid ""
"However, @code{struct}, @code{union}, and @code{enum} share the same name "
"space of tags, so this is a conflict:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7055
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct pair @{ int a, b; @};\n"
"enum pair @{ c, d @};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7059
msgid "and so is this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7063
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct pair @{ int a, b; @};\n"
"struct pair @{ int c, d; @};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7069
msgid ""
"When the code defines a type tag inside a block, the tag's scope is limited "
"to that block (as for local variables).  Two definitions for one type tag do "
"not conflict if they are in different scopes; rather, each is valid in its "
"scope.  For example,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7072 c-intro-and-ref.texi:7093
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct pair @{ int a, b; @};\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7079
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"pair_up_doubles (int len, double array[])\n"
"@{\n"
"  struct pair @{ double a, b; @};\n"
"  @r{@dots{}}\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7086
msgid ""
"has two definitions for @code{struct pair} which do not conflict.  The one "
"inside the function applies only within the definition of "
"@code{pair_up_doubles}.  Within its scope, that definition @dfn{shadows} the "
"outer definition."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7090
msgid ""
"If @code{struct pair} appears inside the function body, before the inner "
"definition, it refers to the outer definition---the only one that has been "
"seen at that point.  Thus, in this code,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7101
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"pair_up_doubles (int len, double array[])\n"
"@{\n"
"  struct two_pairs @{ struct pair *p, *q; @};\n"
"  struct pair @{ double a, b; @};\n"
"  @r{@dots{}}\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7106
msgid ""
"the structure @code{two_pairs} has pointers to the outer definition of "
"@code{struct pair}, which is probably not desirable."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7112
msgid ""
"To prevent that, you can write @code{struct pair;} inside the function body "
"as a variable declaration with no variables.  This is a @dfn{forward "
"declaration} of the type tag @code{pair}: it makes the type tag local to the "
"current block, with the details of the type to come later.  Here's an "
"example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7124
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"pair_up_doubles (int len, double array[])\n"
"@{\n"
"  /* @r{Forward declaration for @code{pair}.}  */\n"
"  struct pair;\n"
"  struct two_pairs @{ struct pair *p, *q; @};\n"
"  /* @r{Give the details.}  */\n"
"  struct pair @{ double a, b; @};\n"
"  @r{@dots{}}\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7127
msgid "However, the cleanest practice is to avoid shadowing type tags."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7130
#, no-wrap
msgid "array"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7131
#, no-wrap
msgid "elements of arrays"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7136
msgid ""
"An @dfn{array} is a data object that holds a series of @dfn{elements}, all "
"of the same data type.  Each element is identified by its numeric "
"@var{index} within the array."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7141
msgid ""
"We presented arrays of numbers in the sample programs early in this manual "
"(@pxref{Array Example}).  However, arrays can have elements of any data "
"type, including pointers, structures, unions, and other arrays."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7145
msgid ""
"If you know another programming language, you may suppose that you know all "
"about arrays, but C arrays have special quirks, so in this chapter we "
"collect all the information about arrays in C@."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7149
msgid ""
"The elements of a C array are allocated consecutively in memory, with no "
"gaps between them.  Each element is aligned as required for its data type "
"(@pxref{Type Alignment})."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7160 c-intro-and-ref.texi:7372
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7373
#, no-wrap
msgid "Array Type Designators"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7160
msgid "Referring to a specific array type."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7164
#, no-wrap
msgid "accessing array elements"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7165
#, no-wrap
msgid "array elements, accessing"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7170
msgid ""
"If the variable @code{a} is an array, the @var{n}th element of @code{a} is "
"@code{a[@var{n}]}.  You can use that expression to access an element's value "
"or to assign to it:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7174
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"x = a[5];\n"
"a[6] = 1;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7179
msgid ""
"Since the variable @code{a} is an lvalue, @code{a[@var{n}]} is also an "
"lvalue."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7182
msgid ""
"The lowest valid index in an array is 0, @emph{not} 1, and the highest valid "
"index is one less than the number of elements."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7186
msgid ""
"The C language does not check whether array indices are in bounds, so if the "
"code uses an out-of-range index, it will access memory outside the array."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7189
msgid ""
"@strong{Warning:} Using only valid index values in C is the programmer's "
"responsibility."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7194
msgid ""
"Array indexing in C is not a primitive operation: it is defined in terms of "
"pointer arithmetic and dereferencing.  Now that we know @emph{what} "
"@code{a[i]} does, we can ask @emph{how} @code{a[i]} does its job."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7198
msgid ""
"In C, @code{@var{x}[@var{y}]} is an abbreviation for "
"@code{*(@var{x}+@var{y})}.  Thus, @code{a[i]} really means @code{*(a+i)}.  "
"@xref{Pointers and Arrays}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7204
msgid ""
"When an expression with array type (such as @code{a}) appears as part of a "
"larger C expression, it is converted automatically to a pointer to element "
"zero of that array.  For instance, @code{a} in an expression is equivalent "
"to @code{&a[0]}.  Thus, @code{*(a+i)} is computed as @code{*(&a[0]+i)}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7208
msgid ""
"Now we can analyze how that expression gives us the desired element of the "
"array.  It makes a pointer to element 0 of @code{a}, advances it by the "
"value of @code{i}, and dereferences that pointer."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7210
msgid "Another equivalent way to write the expression is @code{(&a[0])[i]}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7213
#, no-wrap
msgid "declaring an array"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7214 c-intro-and-ref.texi:9284
#, no-wrap
msgid "array, declaring"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7221
msgid ""
"To make an array declaration, write @code{[@var{length}]} after the name "
"being declared.  This construct is valid in the declaration of a variable, a "
"function parameter, a function value type (the value can't be an array, but "
"it can be a pointer to one), a structure field, or a union alternative."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7225
msgid ""
"The surrounding declaration specifies the element type of the array; that "
"can be any type of data, but not @code{void} or a function type.  For "
"instance,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7228
#, no-wrap
msgid "double a[5];\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7232
msgid "declares @code{a} as an array of 5 @code{double}s."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7235
#, no-wrap
msgid "struct foo bstruct[length];\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7241
msgid ""
"declares @code{bstruct} as an array of @code{length} objects of type "
"@code{struct foo}.  A variable array size like this is allowed when the "
"array is not file-scope."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7244
msgid ""
"Other declaration constructs can nest within the array declaration "
"construct.  For instance:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7247
#, no-wrap
msgid "struct foo *b[length];\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7253
msgid ""
"declares @code{b} as an array of @code{length} pointers to @code{struct "
"foo}.  This shows that the length need not be a constant (@pxref{Arrays of "
"Variable Length})."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7256
#, no-wrap
msgid "double (*c)[5];\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7260
msgid "declares @code{c} as a pointer to an array of 5 @code{double}s, and"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7263
#, no-wrap
msgid "char *(*f (int))[5];\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7269
msgid ""
"declares @code{f} as a function taking an @code{int} argument and returning "
"a pointer to an array of 5 strings (pointers to @code{char}s)."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7272
#, no-wrap
msgid "double aa[5][10];\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7278
msgid ""
"declares @code{aa} as an array of 5 elements, each of which is an array of "
"10 @code{double}s.  This shows how to declare a multidimensional array in C "
"(@pxref{Multidimensional Arrays})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7281
msgid ""
"All these declarations specify the array's length, which is needed in these "
"cases in order to allocate storage for the array."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7284
#, no-wrap
msgid "string"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7290
msgid ""
"A string in C is a sequence of elements of type @code{char}, terminated with "
"the null character, the character with code zero.  However, the C code that "
"operates on strings normally uses the pointer type @code{char *} to do it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7296
msgid ""
"Programs often need to use strings with specific, fixed contents.  To write "
"one in a C program, use a @dfn{string constant} such as @code{\"Take me to "
"your leader!\"}.  The data type of a string constant is @code{char *}.  For "
"the full syntactic details of writing string constants, @ref{String "
"Constants}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7300
msgid ""
"To declare a place to store a non-constant string, declare an array of "
"@code{char}.  Keep in mind that it must include one extra @code{char} for "
"the terminating null.  For instance,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7303
#, no-wrap
msgid "char text[] = @{ 'H', 'e', 'l', 'l', 'o', 0 @};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7309
msgid ""
"declares an array named @samp{text} with six elements---five letters and the "
"terminating null character.  An equivalent way to get the same result is "
"this,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7312
#, no-wrap
msgid "char text[] = \"Hello\";\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7317
msgid ""
"which copies the elements of the string constant, including @emph{its} "
"terminating null character."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7320
#, no-wrap
msgid "char message[200];\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7325
msgid ""
"declares an array long enough to hold a string of 199 ASCII characters plus "
"the terminating null character."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7328
msgid ""
"When you store a string into @code{message} be sure to check or prove that "
"the length does not exceed its size.  For example,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7343
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"set_message (char *text)\n"
"@{\n"
"  int i;\n"
"  /* @r{Recall that @code{message} is declared above.}  */\n"
"  for (i = 0; i < sizeof (message); i++)\n"
"    @{\n"
"      message[i] = text[i];\n"
"      if (text[i] == 0)\n"
"        return;\n"
"    @}\n"
"  fatal_error (\"Message is too long for `message'\\n"
"\");\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7351
msgid ""
"It's easy to do this with the standard library function @code{strncpy}, "
"which fills out the whole destination array (up to a specified length) with "
"null characters.  Thus, if the last character of the destination is not "
"null, the string did not fit.  Many system libraries, including the GNU C "
"library, hand-optimize @code{strncpy} to run faster than an explicit "
"@code{for}-loop."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7353
msgid "Here's what the code looks like:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7362
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"set_message (char *text)\n"
"@{\n"
"  strncpy (message, text, sizeof (message));\n"
"  if (message[sizeof (message) - 1] != 0)\n"
"    fatal_error (\"Message is too long for `message');\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7367
msgid ""
"@xref{String and Array Utilities, The GNU C Library, , libc, The GNU C "
"Library Reference Manual}, for more information about the standard library "
"functions for operating on strings."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7371
msgid ""
"You can avoid putting a fixed length limit on strings you construct or "
"operate on by allocating the space for them dynamically.  @xref{Dynamic "
"Memory Allocation}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7379
msgid ""
"Every C type has a type designator, which you make by deleting the variable "
"name and the semicolon from a declaration (@pxref{Type Designators}).  The "
"designators for array types follow this rule, but they may appear surprising."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7384
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@r{type}   int a[5];           @r{designator}   int [5]\n"
"@r{type}   double a[5][3];     @r{designator}   double [5][3]\n"
"@r{type}   struct foo *a[5];   @r{designator}   struct foo *[5]\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7388
#, no-wrap
msgid "incomplete array types"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7389
#, no-wrap
msgid "array types, incomplete"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7396
msgid ""
"An array is equivalent, for most purposes, to a pointer to its zeroth "
"element.  When that is true, the length of the array is irrelevant.  The "
"length needs to be known only for allocating space for the array, or for "
"@code{sizeof} and @code{typeof} (@pxref{Auto Type}).  Thus, in some contexts "
"C allows"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7402
msgid ""
"An @code{extern} declaration says how to refer to a variable allocated "
"elsewhere.  It does not need to allocate space for the variable, so if it is "
"an array, you can omit the length.  For example,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7405
#, no-wrap
msgid "extern int foo[];\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7412
msgid ""
"When declaring a function parameter as an array, the argument value passed "
"to the function is really a pointer to the array's zeroth element.  This "
"value does not say how long the array really is, there is no need to declare "
"it.  For example,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7416
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int\n"
"func (int foo[])\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7424
msgid ""
"These declarations are examples of @dfn{incomplete} array types, types that "
"are not fully specified.  The incompleteness makes no difference for "
"accessing elements of the array, but it matters for some other things.  For "
"instance, @code{sizeof} is not allowed on an incomplete type."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7431
msgid ""
"With multidimensional arrays, only the first dimension can be omitted.  For "
"example, suppose we want to represent the positions of pieces on a "
"chessboard which has the usual 8 files (columns), but more (or fewer)  ranks "
"(rows) than the usual 8.  This declaration could hold a pointer to a "
"two-dimensional array that can hold that data.  Each element of the array "
"holds one row."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7434
#, no-wrap
msgid "struct chesspiece *funnyboard[][8];\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7439
msgid ""
"Since it is just a pointer to the start of an array, its type can be "
"incomplete, but it must state how big each array element is---the number of "
"elements in each row."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7442
#, no-wrap
msgid "limitations of C arrays"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7443
#, no-wrap
msgid "first-class object"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7447
msgid ""
"Arrays have quirks in C because they are not ``first-class objects'': there "
"is no way in C to operate on an array as a unit."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7453
msgid ""
"The other composite objects in C, structures and unions, are first-class "
"objects: a C program can copy a structure or union value in an assignment, "
"or pass one as an argument to a function, or make a function return one.  "
"You can't do those things with an array in C@.  That is because a value you "
"can operate on never has an array type."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7459
msgid ""
"An expression in C can have an array type, but that doesn't produce the "
"array as a value.  Instead it is converted automatically to a pointer to the "
"array's element at index zero.  The code can operate on the pointer, and "
"through that on individual elements of the array, but it can't get and "
"operate on the array as a unit."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7462
msgid ""
"There are three exceptions to this conversion rule, but none of them offers "
"a way to operate on the array as a whole."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7468
msgid ""
"First, @samp{&} applied to an expression with array type gives you the "
"address of the array, as an array type.  However, you can't operate on the "
"whole array that way---if you apply @samp{*} to get the array back, that "
"expression converts, as usual, to a pointer to its zeroth element."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7473
msgid ""
"Second, the operators @code{sizeof}, @code{_Alignof}, and @code{typeof} do "
"not convert the array to a pointer; they leave it as an array.  But they "
"don't operate on the array's data---they only give information about its "
"type."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7477
msgid ""
"Third, a string constant used as an initializer for an array is not "
"converted to a pointer---rather, the declaration copies the @emph{contents} "
"of that string in that one special case."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7483
msgid ""
"You @emph{can} copy the contents of an array, just not with an assignment "
"operator.  You can do it by calling the library function @code{memcpy} or "
"@code{memmove} (@pxref{Copying and Concatenation, The GNU C Library, , libc, "
"The GNU C Library Reference Manual}).  Also, when a structure contains just "
"an array, you can copy that structure."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7488
msgid ""
"An array itself is an lvalue if it is a declared variable, or part of a "
"structure or union that is an lvalue.  When you construct an array from "
"elements (@pxref{Constructing Array Values}), that array is not an lvalue."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7491
#, no-wrap
msgid "multidimensional arrays"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7492
#, no-wrap
msgid "array, multidimensional"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7497
msgid ""
"Strictly speaking, all arrays in C are unidimensional.  However, you can "
"create an array of arrays, which is more or less equivalent to a "
"multidimensional array.  For example,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7500
#, no-wrap
msgid "struct chesspiece *board[8][8];\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7509
msgid ""
"declares an array of 8 arrays of 8 pointers to @code{struct chesspiece}.  "
"This data type could represent the state of a chess game.  To access one "
"square's contents requires two array index operations, one for each "
"dimension.  For instance, you can write @code{board[row][column]}, assuming "
"@code{row} and @code{column} are variables with integer values in the proper "
"range."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7515
msgid ""
"How does C understand @code{board[row][column]}? First of all, @code{board} "
"is converted automatically to a pointer to the zeroth element (at index "
"zero) of @code{board}.  Adding @code{row} to that makes it point to the "
"desired element.  Thus, @code{board[row]}'s value is an element of "
"@code{board}---an array of 8 pointers."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7520
msgid ""
"However, as an expression with array type, it is converted automatically to "
"a pointer to the array's zeroth element.  The second array index operation, "
"@code{[column]}, accesses the chosen element from that array."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7524
msgid ""
"As this shows, pointer-to-array types are meaningful in C@.  You can declare "
"a variable that points to a row in a chess board like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7527
#, no-wrap
msgid "struct chesspiece *(*rowptr)[8];\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7532
msgid ""
"This points to an array of 8 pointers to @code{struct chesspiece}.  You can "
"assign to it as follows:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7535
#, no-wrap
msgid "rowptr = &board[5];\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7540
msgid ""
"The dimensions don't have to be equal in length.  Here we declare "
"@code{statepop} as an array to hold the population of each state in the "
"United States for each year since 1900:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7548
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"#define NSTATES 50\n"
"@{\n"
"  int nyears = current_year - 1900 + 1;\n"
"  int statepop[NSTATES][nyears];\n"
"  @r{@dots{}}\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7555
msgid ""
"The variable @code{statepop} is an array of @code{NSTATES} subarrays, each "
"indexed by the year (counting from 1900).  Thus, to get the element for a "
"particular state and year, we must subscript it first by the number that "
"indicates the state, and second by the index for the year:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7558
#, no-wrap
msgid "statepop[state][year - 1900]\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7560
#, no-wrap
msgid "array, layout in memory"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7568
msgid ""
"The subarrays within the multidimensional array are allocated consecutively "
"in memory, and within each subarray, its elements are allocated "
"consecutively in memory.  The most efficient way to process all the elements "
"in the array is to scan the last subscript in the innermost loop.  This "
"means consecutive accesses go to consecutive memory locations, which "
"optimizes use of the processor's memory cache.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7572
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int total = 0;\n"
"float average;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7580
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"for (int state = 0; state < NSTATES, ++state)\n"
"  @{\n"
"    for (int year = 0; year < nyears; ++year)\n"
"      @{\n"
"        total += statepop[state][year];\n"
"      @}\n"
"  @}\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7582
#, no-wrap
msgid "average = total / nyears;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7590
msgid ""
"C's layout for multidimensional arrays is different from Fortran's layout.  "
"In Fortran, a multidimensional array is not an array of arrays; rather, "
"multidimensional arrays are a primitive feature, and it is the first index "
"that varies most rapidly between consecutive memory locations.  Thus, the "
"memory layout of a 50x114 array in C matches that of a 114x50 array in "
"Fortran."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7593
#, no-wrap
msgid "constructing array values"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7594
#, no-wrap
msgid "array values, constructing"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7600
msgid ""
"You can construct an array from elements by writing them inside braces, and "
"preceding all that with the array type's designator in parentheses.  There "
"is no need to specify the array length, since the number of elements "
"determines that.  The constructor looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7603
#, no-wrap
msgid "(@var{elttype}[]) @{ @var{elements} @};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7606
msgid "Here is an example, which constructs an array of string pointers:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7609
#, no-wrap
msgid "(char *[]) @{ \"x\", \"y\", \"z\" @};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7613
msgid ""
"That's equivalent in effect to declaring an array with the same initializer, "
"like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7616
#, no-wrap
msgid "char *array[] = @{ \"x\", \"y\", \"z\" @};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7619
msgid "and then using the array."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7624
msgid ""
"If all the elements are simple constant expressions, or made up of such, "
"then the compound literal can be coerced to a pointer to its zeroth element "
"and used to initialize a file-scope variable (@pxref{File-Scope Variables}), "
"as shown here:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7627
#, no-wrap
msgid "char **foo = (char *[]) @{ \"x\", \"y\", \"z\" @};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7633
msgid ""
"The data type of @code{foo} is @code{char **}, which is a pointer type, not "
"an array type.  The declaration is equivalent to defining and then using an "
"array-type variable:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7637
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"char *nameless_array[] = @{ \"x\", \"y\", \"z\" @};\n"
"char **foo = &nameless_array[0];\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7641
#, no-wrap
msgid "array of variable length"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7642
#, no-wrap
msgid "variable-length arrays"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7648
msgid ""
"In GNU C, you can declare variable-length arrays like any other arrays, but "
"with a length that is not a constant expression.  The storage is allocated "
"at the point of declaration and deallocated when the block scope containing "
"the declaration exits.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7652
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"#include <stdio.h>  /* @r{Defines @code{FILE}.} */\n"
"#include <string.h> /* @r{Declares @code{str}.} */\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7661
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"FILE *\n"
"concat_fopen (char *s1, char *s2, char *mode)\n"
"@{\n"
"  char str[strlen (s1) + strlen (s2) + 1];\n"
"  strcpy (str, s1);\n"
"  strcat (str, s2);\n"
"  return fopen (str, mode);\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7666
msgid ""
"(This uses some standard library functions; see @ref{String and Array "
"Utilities, , , libc, The GNU C Library Reference Manual}.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7670
msgid ""
"The length of an array is computed once when the storage is allocated and is "
"remembered for the scope of the array in case it is used in @code{sizeof}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7675
msgid ""
"@strong{Warning:} Don't allocate a variable-length array if the size might "
"be very large (more than 100,000), or in a recursive function, because that "
"is likely to cause stack overflow.  Allocate the array dynamically instead "
"(@pxref{Dynamic Memory Allocation})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7679
msgid ""
"Jumping or breaking out of the scope of the array name deallocates the "
"storage.  Jumping into the scope is not allowed; that gives an error message."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7681
msgid "You can also use variable-length arrays as arguments to functions:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7688 c-intro-and-ref.texi:11259
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct entry\n"
"tester (int len, char data[len][len])\n"
"@{\n"
"  @r{@dots{}}\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7694
msgid ""
"As usual, a function argument declared with an array type is really a "
"pointer to an array that already exists.  Calling the function does not "
"allocate the array, so there's no particular danger of stack overflow in "
"using this construct."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7698
msgid ""
"To pass the array first and the length afterward, use a forward declaration "
"in the function's parameter list (another GNU extension).  For example,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7705 c-intro-and-ref.texi:11309
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct entry\n"
"tester (int len; char data[len][len], int len)\n"
"@{\n"
"  @r{@dots{}}\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7710
msgid ""
"The @code{int len} before the semicolon is a @dfn{parameter forward "
"declaration}, and it serves the purpose of making the name @code{len} known "
"when the declaration of @code{data} is parsed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7717
msgid ""
"You can write any number of such parameter forward declarations in the "
"parameter list.  They can be separated by commas or semicolons, but the last "
"one must end with a semicolon, which is followed by the ``real'' parameter "
"declarations.  Each forward declaration must match a ``real'' declaration in "
"parameter name and data type.  ISO C11 does not support parameter forward "
"declarations."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7720
#, no-wrap
msgid "enumeration types"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7721
#, no-wrap
msgid "types, enumeration"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7722
#, no-wrap
msgid "enumerator"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7727
msgid ""
"An @dfn{enumeration type} represents a limited set of integer values, each "
"with a name.  It is effectively equivalent to a primitive integer type."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7731
msgid ""
"Suppose we have a list of possible emotional states to store in an integer "
"variable.  We can give names to these alternative values with an enumeration:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7735
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"enum emotion_state @{ neutral, happy, sad, worried,\n"
"                     calm, nervous @};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7740
msgid ""
"(Never mind that this is a simplistic way to classify emotional states; it's "
"just a code example.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7746
msgid ""
"The names inside the enumeration are called @dfn{enumerators}.  The "
"enumeration type defines them as constants, and their values are consecutive "
"integers; @code{neutral} is 0, @code{happy} is 1, @code{sad} is 2, and so "
"on.  Alternatively, you can specify values for the enumerators explicitly "
"like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7751
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"enum emotion_state @{ neutral = 2, happy = 5,\n"
"                     sad = 20, worried = 10,\n"
"                     calm = -5, nervous = -300 @};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7755
msgid ""
"Each enumerator which does not specify a value gets value zero (if it is at "
"the beginning) or the next consecutive integer."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7762
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"/* @r{@code{neutral} is 0 by default,}\n"
"   @r{and @code{worried} is 21 by default.}  */\n"
"enum emotion_state @{ neutral,\n"
"                      happy = 5, sad = 20, worried,\n"
"                      calm = -5, nervous = -300 @};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7768
msgid ""
"If an enumerator is obsolete, you can specify that using it should cause a "
"warning, by including an attribute in the enumerator's declaration.  Here is "
"how @code{happy} would look with this attribute:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7774
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"happy __attribute__\n"
"      ((deprecated\n"
"        (\"impossible under plutocratic rule\")))\n"
"      = 5,\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7779
msgid "You can declare variables with the enumeration type:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7782
#, no-wrap
msgid "enum emotion_state feelings_now;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7791
msgid ""
"In the C code itself, this is equivalent to declaring the variable "
"@code{int}.  (If all the enumeration values are positive, it is equivalent "
"to @code{unsigned int}.)  However, declaring it with the enumeration type "
"has an advantage in debugging, because GDB knows it should display the "
"current value of the variable using the corresponding name.  If the "
"variable's type is @code{int}, GDB can only show the value as a number."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7796
msgid ""
"The identifier that follows @code{enum} is called a @dfn{type tag} since it "
"distinguishes different enumeration types.  Type tags are in a separate name "
"space and belong to scopes like most other names in C@.  @xref{Type Tags}, "
"for explanation."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7799
msgid ""
"You can predeclare an @code{enum} type tag like a structure or union type "
"tag, like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7802
#, no-wrap
msgid "enum foo;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7806
msgid "The @code{enum} type is incomplete until you finish defining it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7809
msgid ""
"You can optionally include a trailing comma at the end of a list of "
"enumeration values:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7813
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"enum emotion_state @{ neutral, happy, sad, worried,\n"
"                     calm, nervous, @};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7820
msgid ""
"This is useful in some macro definitions, since it enables you to assemble "
"the list of enumerators without knowing which one is last.  The extra comma "
"does not change the meaning of the enumeration in any way."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7823
#, no-wrap
msgid "typedef names"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7824
#, no-wrap
msgid "typedef"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7830
msgid ""
"You can define a data type keyword as an alias for any type, and then use "
"the alias syntactically like a built-in type keyword such as @code{int}.  "
"You do this using @code{typedef}, so these aliases are also called "
"@dfn{typedef names}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7834
msgid ""
"@code{typedef} is followed by text that looks just like a variable "
"declaration, but instead of declaring variables it defines data type "
"keywords."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7838
msgid ""
"Here's how to define @code{fooptr} as a typedef alias for the type "
"@code{struct foo *}, then declare @code{x} and @code{y} as variables with "
"that type:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7841
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"typedef struct foo *fooptr;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7843
#, no-wrap
msgid "fooptr x, y;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7847
msgid "That declaration is equivalent to the following one:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7850
#, no-wrap
msgid "struct foo *x, *y;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7854
msgid ""
"You can define a typedef alias for any type.  For instance, this makes "
"@code{frobcount} an alias for type @code{int}:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7857
#, no-wrap
msgid "typedef int frobcount;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7864
msgid ""
"This doesn't define a new type distinct from @code{int}.  Rather, "
"@code{frobcount} is another name for the type @code{int}.  Once the variable "
"is declared, it makes no difference which name the declaration used."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7869
msgid ""
"There is a syntactic difference, however, between @code{frobcount} and "
"@code{int}: A typedef name cannot be used with @code{signed}, "
"@code{unsigned}, @code{long} or @code{short}.  It has to specify the type "
"all by itself.  So you can't write this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7872
#, no-wrap
msgid "unsigned frobcount f1;  /* @r{Error!} */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7875
msgid "But you can write this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7878
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"typedef unsigned int unsigned_frobcount;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7880
#, no-wrap
msgid "unsigned_frobcount f1;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7885
msgid ""
"In other words, a typedef name is not an alias for @emph{a keyword} such as "
"@code{int}.  It stands for a @emph{type}, and that could be the type "
"@code{int}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7890
msgid ""
"Typedef names are in the same namespace as functions and variables, so you "
"can't use the same name for a typedef and a function, or a typedef and a "
"variable.  When a typedef is declared inside a code block, it is in scope "
"only in that block."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7893
msgid ""
"@strong{Warning:} Avoid defining typedef names that end in @samp{_t}, "
"because many of these have standard meanings."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7898
msgid ""
"You can redefine a typedef name to the exact same type as its first "
"definition, but you cannot redefine a typedef name to a different type, even "
"if the two types are compatible. For example, this is valid:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7904
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"typedef int frobcount;\n"
"typedef int frotzcount;\n"
"typedef frotzcount frobcount;\n"
"typedef frobcount frotzcount;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7909
msgid ""
"because each typedef name is always defined with the same type (@code{int}), "
"but this is not valid:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7914
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"enum foo @{f1, f2, f3@};\n"
"typedef enum foo frobcount;\n"
"typedef int frobcount;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7919
msgid ""
"Even though the type @code{enum foo} is compatible with @code{int}, they are "
"not the @emph{same} type."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7922
#, no-wrap
msgid "statements"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7928
msgid ""
"A @dfn{statement} specifies computations to be done for effect; it does not "
"produce a value, as an expression would.  In general a statement ends with a "
"semicolon (@samp{;}), but blocks (which are statements, more or less) are an "
"exception to that rule."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7938
msgid ""
"The places to use statements are inside a block, and inside a complex "
"statement.  A @dfn{complex statement} contains one or two components that "
"are nested statements.  Each such component must consist of one and only one "
"statement.  The way to put multiple statements in such a component is to "
"group them into a @dfn{block} (@pxref{Blocks}), which counts as one "
"statement."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7940
msgid "The following sections describe the various kinds of statement."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7963
#, no-wrap
msgid "expression statement"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7964
#, no-wrap
msgid "statement, expression"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7972
msgid ""
"The most common kind of statement in C is an @dfn{expression statement}.  It "
"consists of an expression followed by a semicolon.  The expression's value "
"is discarded, so the expressions that are useful are those that have side "
"effects: assignment expressions, increment and decrement expressions, and "
"function calls.  Here are examples of expression statements:"
msgstr ""

#. type: smallexample
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7979
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"x = 5;              /* @r{Assignment expression.} */\n"
"p++;                /* @r{Increment expression.} */\n"
"printf (\"Done\\n"
"\");  /* @r{Function call expression.} */\n"
"*p;                 /* @r{Cause @code{SIGSEGV} signal if @code{p} is null.} "
"*/\n"
"x + y;              /* @r{Useless statement without effect.} */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7983
msgid ""
"In very unusual circumstances we use an expression statement whose purpose "
"is to get a fault if an address is invalid:"
msgstr ""

#. type: smallexample
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7988
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"volatile char *p;\n"
"@r{@dots{}}\n"
"*p;                 /* @r{Cause signal if @code{p} is null.} */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7993
msgid ""
"If the target of @code{p} is not declared @code{volatile}, the compiler "
"might optimize away the memory access, since it knows that the value isn't "
"really used.  @xref{volatile}."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7995
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{if} Statement"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7996
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{if} statement"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7997
#, no-wrap
msgid "statement, @code{if}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:7998
#, no-wrap
msgid "if"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8003
msgid ""
"An @code{if} statement computes an expression to decide whether to execute "
"the following statement or not.  It looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8007
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"if (@var{condition})\n"
"  @var{execute-if-true}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8014
msgid ""
"The first thing this does is compute the value of @var{condition}.  If that "
"is true (nonzero), then it executes the statement @var{execute-if-true}.  If "
"the value of @var{condition} is false (zero), it doesn't execute "
"@var{execute-if-true}; instead, it does nothing."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8019
msgid ""
"This is a @dfn{complex statement} because it contains a component "
"@var{execute-if-true} that is a nested statement.  It must be one and only "
"one statement.  The way to put multiple statements there is to group them "
"into a @dfn{block} (@pxref{Blocks})."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8021
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{if-else} Statement"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8022
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{if}@dots{}@code{else} statement"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8023
#, no-wrap
msgid "statement, @code{if}@dots{}@code{else}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8024
#, no-wrap
msgid "else"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8029
msgid ""
"An @code{if}-@code{else} statement computes an expression to decide which of "
"two nested statements to execute.  It looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8035
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"if (@var{condition})\n"
"  @var{if-true-substatement}\n"
"else\n"
"  @var{if-false-substatement}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8041
msgid ""
"The first thing this does is compute the value of @var{condition}.  If that "
"is true (nonzero), then it executes the statement "
"@var{if-true-substatement}.  If the value of @var{condition} is false "
"(zero), then it executes the statement @var{if-false-substatement} instead."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8047
msgid ""
"This is a @dfn{complex statement} because it contains components "
"@var{if-true-substatement} and @var{if-else-substatement} that are nested "
"statements.  Each must be one and only one statement.  The way to put "
"multiple statements in such a component is to group them into a @dfn{block} "
"(@pxref{Blocks})."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8050
#, no-wrap
msgid "block"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8051
#, no-wrap
msgid "compound statement"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8057
msgid ""
"A @dfn{block} is a construct that contains multiple statements of any kind.  "
"It begins with @samp{@{} and ends with @samp{@}}, and has a series of "
"statements and declarations in between.  Another name for blocks is "
"@dfn{compound statements}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8062
msgid ""
"Is a block a statement? Yes and no.  It doesn't @emph{look} like a normal "
"statement---it does not end with a semicolon.  But you can @emph{use} it "
"like a statement; anywhere that a statement is required or allowed, you can "
"write a block and consider that block a statement."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8068
msgid ""
"So far it seems that a block is a kind of statement with an unusual syntax.  "
"But that is not entirely true: a function body is also a block, and that "
"block is definitely not a statement.  The text after a function header is "
"not treated as a statement; only a function body is allowed there, and "
"nothing else would be meaningful there."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8073
msgid ""
"In a formal grammar we would have to choose---either a block is a kind of "
"statement or it is not.  But this manual is meant for humans, not for parser "
"generators.  The clearest answer for humans is, ``a block is a statement, in "
"some ways.''"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8074
#, no-wrap
msgid "nested block"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8075
#, no-wrap
msgid "internal block"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8080
msgid ""
"A block that isn't a function body is called an @dfn{internal block} or a "
"@dfn{nested block}.  You can put a nested block directly inside another "
"block, but more often the nested block is inside some complex statement, "
"such as a @code{for} statement or an @code{if} statement."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8082
msgid "There are two uses for nested blocks in C:"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8087
msgid ""
"To specify the scope for local declarations.  For instance, a local "
"variable's scope is the rest of the innermost containing block."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8093
msgid ""
"To write a series of statements where, syntactically, one statement is "
"called for.  For instance, the @var{execute-if-true} of an @code{if} "
"statement is one statement.  To put multiple statements there, they have to "
"be wrapped in a block, like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8100
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"if (x < 0)\n"
"  @{\n"
"    printf (\"x was negative\\n"
"\");\n"
"    x = -x;\n"
"  @}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8107
msgid ""
"This example (repeated from above) shows a nested block which serves both "
"purposes: it includes two statements (plus a declaration) in the body of a "
"@code{while} statement, and it provides the scope for the declaration of "
"@code{q}."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8122
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{return} Statement"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8123
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{return} statement"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8124
#, no-wrap
msgid "statement, @code{return}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8130
msgid ""
"The @code{return} statement makes the containing function return "
"immediately.  It has two forms.  This one specifies no value to return:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8133
#, no-wrap
msgid "return;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8140
msgid ""
"That form is meant for functions whose return type is @code{void} "
"(@pxref{The Void Type}).  You can also use it in a function that returns "
"nonvoid data, but that's a bad idea, since it makes the function return "
"garbage."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8142
msgid "The form that specifies a value looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8152
msgid ""
"which computes the expression @var{value} and makes the function return "
"that.  If necessary, the value undergoes type conversion to the function's "
"declared return value type, which works like assigning the value to a "
"variable of that type."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8155
#, no-wrap
msgid "loop statements"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8156
#, no-wrap
msgid "statements, loop"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8157
#, no-wrap
msgid "iteration"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8163
msgid ""
"You can use a loop statement when you need to execute a series of statements "
"repeatedly, making an @dfn{iteration}.  C provides several different kinds "
"of loop statements, described in the following subsections."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8167
msgid ""
"Every kind of loop statement is a complex statement because contains a "
"component, here called @var{body}, which is a nested statement.  Most often "
"the body is a block."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8178 c-intro-and-ref.texi:8180
#, no-wrap
msgid "while Statement"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8178
msgid "Loop as long as a test expression is true."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8178 c-intro-and-ref.texi:8215
#, no-wrap
msgid "do-while Statement"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8178
msgid ""
"Execute a loop once, with further looping as long as a test expression is "
"true."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8178 c-intro-and-ref.texi:8244
#, no-wrap
msgid "break Statement"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8178
msgid "End a loop immediately."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8178 c-intro-and-ref.texi:8303
#, no-wrap
msgid "for Statement"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8178
msgid "Iterative looping."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8178 c-intro-and-ref.texi:8331
#, no-wrap
msgid "Example of for"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8178
msgid "An example of iterative looping."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8178 c-intro-and-ref.texi:8363
#, no-wrap
msgid "Omitted for-Expressions"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8178
msgid "for-loop expression options."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8178 c-intro-and-ref.texi:8432
#, no-wrap
msgid "for-Index Declarations"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8178
msgid "for-loop declaration options."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8178 c-intro-and-ref.texi:8471
#, no-wrap
msgid "continue Statement"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8178
msgid "Begin the next cycle of a loop."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8181
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{while} Statement"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8182
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{while} statement"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8183
#, no-wrap
msgid "statement, @code{while}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8184
#, no-wrap
msgid "while"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8188
msgid ""
"The @code{while} statement is the simplest loop construct.  It looks like "
"this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8192
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"while (@var{test})\n"
"  @var{body}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8199
msgid ""
"Here, @var{body} is a statement (often a nested block) to repeat, and "
"@var{test} is the test expression that controls whether to repeat it again.  "
"Each iteration of the loop starts by computing @var{test} and, if it is true "
"(nonzero), that means the loop should execute @var{body} again and then "
"start over."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8202
msgid ""
"Here's an example of advancing to the last structure in a chain of "
"structures chained through the @code{next} field:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8208
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"#include <stddef.h> /* @r{Defines @code{NULL}.} */\n"
"@r{@dots{}}\n"
"while (chain->next != NULL)\n"
"  chain = chain->next;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8214
msgid ""
"This code assumes the chain isn't empty to start with; if the chain is empty "
"(that is, if @code{chain} is a null pointer), the code gets a @code{SIGSEGV} "
"signal trying to dereference that null pointer (@pxref{Signals})."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8216
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{do-while} Statement"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8217
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{do}--@code{while} statement"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8218
#, no-wrap
msgid "statement, @code{do}--@code{while}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8219
#, no-wrap
msgid "do"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8223
msgid ""
"The @code{do}--@code{while} statement is a simple loop construct that "
"performs the test at the end of the iteration."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8228
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"do\n"
"  @var{body}\n"
"while (@var{test});\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8232
msgid ""
"Here, @var{body} is a statement (possibly a block) to repeat, and @var{test} "
"is an expression that controls whether to repeat it again."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8237
msgid ""
"Each iteration of the loop starts by executing @var{body}.  Then it computes "
"@var{test} and, if it is true (nonzero), that means to go back and start "
"over with @var{body}.  If @var{test} is false (zero), then the loop stops "
"repeating and execution moves on past it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8243
msgid ""
"@strong{Warning:} Human beings tend to confuse the @code{do}--@code{while} "
"statement with the @code{while} statement using the null statement as its "
"@var{body} (@pxref{Null Statement}).  To avoid that, consistently mark such "
"constructs with a specific comment or with clearly different indent styles."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8245
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{break} Statement"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8246
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{break} statement"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8247
#, no-wrap
msgid "statement, @code{break}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8248
#, no-wrap
msgid "break"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8253
msgid ""
"The @code{break} statement looks like @samp{break;}.  Its effect is to exit "
"immediately from the innermost loop construct or @code{switch} statement "
"(@pxref{switch Statement})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8256
msgid ""
"For example, this loop advances @code{p} until the next null character or "
"newline."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8265
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"while (*p)\n"
"  @{\n"
"    /* @r{End loop if we have reached a newline.}  */\n"
"    if (*p == '\\n"
"')\n"
"      break;\n"
"    p++\n"
"  @}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8269
msgid ""
"When there are nested loops, the @code{break} statement exits from the "
"innermost loop containing it."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8277
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct list_if_tuples\n"
"@{\n"
"  struct list_if_tuples *next;\n"
"  int length;\n"
"  data *contents;\n"
"@};\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8294
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"process_all_elements (struct list_if_tuples *list)\n"
"@{\n"
"  while (list)\n"
"    @{\n"
"      /* @r{Process all the elements in this node's vector,}\n"
"         @r{stopping when we reach one that is null.}  */\n"
"      for (i = 0; i < list->length; i++)\n"
"        @{\n"
"          /* @r{Null element terminates this node's vector.}  */\n"
"          if (list->contents[i] == NULL)\n"
"            /* @r{Exit the @code{for} loop.}  */\n"
"            break;\n"
"          /* @r{Operate on the next element.}  */\n"
"          process_element (list->contents[i]);\n"
"        @}\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8298
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"      list = list->next;\n"
"    @}\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8302
msgid ""
"The only way in C to exit from an outer loop is with @code{goto} "
"(@pxref{goto Statement})."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8304
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{for} Statement"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8305
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{for} statement"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8306
#, no-wrap
msgid "statement, @code{for}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8307
#, no-wrap
msgid "for"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8315
msgid ""
"A @code{for} statement uses three expressions written inside a parenthetical "
"group to define the repetition of the loop.  The first expression says how "
"to prepare to start the loop.  The second says how to test, before each "
"iteration, whether to continue looping.  The third says how to advance, at "
"the end of an iteration, for the next iteration.  All together, it looks "
"like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8319 c-intro-and-ref.texi:8371
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"for (@var{start}; @var{continue-test}; @var{advance})\n"
"  @var{body}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8325
msgid ""
"The first thing the @code{for} statement does is compute @var{start}.  The "
"next thing it does is compute the expression @var{continue-test}.  If that "
"expression is false (zero), the @code{for} statement finishes immediately, "
"so @var{body} is executed zero times."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8330
msgid ""
"However, if @var{continue-test} is true (nonzero), the @code{for} statement "
"executes @var{body}, then @var{advance}.  Then it loops back to the "
"not-quite-top to test @var{continue-test} again.  But it does not compute "
"@var{start} again."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8332
#, no-wrap
msgid "Example of @code{for}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8336
msgid "Here is the @code{for} statement from the iterative Fibonacci function:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8355
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int i;\n"
"for (i = 1; i < n; ++i)\n"
"  /* @r{If @code{n} is 1 or less, the loop runs zero times,}  */\n"
"  /* @r{since @code{i < n} is false the first time.}  */\n"
"  @{\n"
"    /* @r{Now @var{last} is @code{fib (@var{i})}}\n"
"       @r{and @var{prev} is @code{fib (@var{i} @minus{} 1)}.}  */\n"
"    /* @r{Compute @code{fib (@var{i} + 1)}.}  */\n"
"    int next = prev + last;\n"
"    /* @r{Shift the values down.}  */\n"
"    prev = last;\n"
"    last = next;\n"
"    /* @r{Now @var{last} is @code{fib (@var{i} + 1)}}\n"
"       @r{and @var{prev} is @code{fib (@var{i})}.}\n"
"       @r{But that won't stay true for long,}\n"
"       @r{because we are about to increment @var{i}.}  */\n"
"  @}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8362
msgid ""
"In this example, @var{start} is @code{i = 1}, meaning set @code{i} to 1.  "
"@var{continue-test} is @code{i < n}, meaning keep repeating the loop as long "
"as @code{i} is less than @code{n}.  @var{advance} is @code{i++}, meaning "
"increment @code{i} by 1.  The body is a block that contains a declaration "
"and two statements."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8364
#, no-wrap
msgid "Omitted @code{for}-Expressions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8367
msgid "A fully-fleshed @code{for} statement contains all these parts,"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8379
msgid ""
"but you can omit any of the three expressions inside the parentheses.  The "
"parentheses and the two semicolons are required syntactically, but the "
"expressions between them may be missing.  A missing expression means this "
"loop doesn't use that particular feature of the @code{for} statement."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8384
msgid ""
"Instead of using @var{start}, you can do the loop preparation before the "
"@code{for} statement: the effect is the same.  So we could have written the "
"beginning of the previous example this way:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8388
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int i = 0;\n"
"for (; i < n; ++i)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8392
msgid "instead of this way:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8396
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int i;\n"
"for (i = 0; i < n; ++i)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8403
msgid ""
"Omitting @var{continue-test} means the loop runs forever (or until something "
"else causes exit from it).  Statements inside the loop can test conditions "
"for termination and use @samp{break;} to exit.  This is more flexible since "
"you can put those tests anywhere in the loop, not solely at the beginning."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8408
msgid ""
"Putting an expression in @var{advance} is almost equivalent to writing it at "
"the end of the loop body; it does almost the same thing.  The only "
"difference is for the @code{continue} statement (@pxref{continue "
"Statement}).  So we could have written this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8415
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"for (i = 0; i < n;)\n"
"  @{\n"
"    @r{@dots{}}\n"
"    ++i;\n"
"  @}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8419
msgid "instead of this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8425
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"for (i = 0; i < n; ++i)\n"
"  @{\n"
"    @r{@dots{}}\n"
"  @}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8431
msgid ""
"The choice is mainly a matter of what is more readable for programmers.  "
"However, there is also a syntactic difference: @var{advance} is an "
"expression, not a statement.  It can't include loops, blocks, declarations, "
"etc."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8433
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{for}-Index Declarations"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8437
msgid ""
"You can declare loop-index variables directly in the @var{start} portion of "
"the @code{for}-loop, like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8443
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i)\n"
"  @{\n"
"    @r{@dots{}}\n"
"  @}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8448
msgid ""
"This kind of @var{start} is limited to a single declaration; it can declare "
"one or more variables, separated by commas, all of which are the same "
"@var{basetype} (@code{int}, in this example):"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8454
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"for (int i = 0, j = 1, *p = NULL; i < n; ++i, ++j, ++p)\n"
"  @{\n"
"    @r{@dots{}}\n"
"  @}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8459
msgid ""
"The scope of these variables is the @code{for} statement as a whole.  See "
"@ref{Variable Declarations} for an explanation of @var{basetype}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8463
msgid ""
"Variables declared in @code{for} statements should have initializers.  "
"Omitting the initialization gives the variables unpredictable initial "
"values, so this code is erroneous."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8469
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"for (int i; i < n; ++i)\n"
"  @{\n"
"    @r{@dots{}}\n"
"  @}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8472
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{continue} Statement"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8473
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{continue} statement"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8474
#, no-wrap
msgid "statement, @code{continue}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8475
#, no-wrap
msgid "continue"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8481
msgid ""
"The @code{continue} statement looks like @samp{continue;}, and its effect is "
"to jump immediately to the end of the innermost loop construct.  If it is a "
"@code{for}-loop, the next thing that happens is to execute the loop's "
"@var{advance} expression."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8485
msgid ""
"For example, this loop increments @code{p} until the next null character or "
"newline, and operates (in some way not shown) on all the characters in the "
"line except for spaces.  All it does with spaces is skip them."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8498
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"for (;*p; ++p)\n"
"  @{\n"
"    /* @r{End loop if we have reached a newline.}  */\n"
"    if (*p == '\\n"
"')\n"
"      break;\n"
"    /* @r{Pay no attention to spaces.}  */\n"
"    if (*p == ' ')\n"
"      continue;\n"
"    /* @r{Operate on the next character.}  */\n"
"    @r{@dots{}}\n"
"  @}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8503
msgid ""
"Executing @samp{continue;} skips the loop body but it does not skip the "
"@var{advance} expression, @code{p++}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8505
msgid "We could also write it like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8519
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"for (;*p; ++p)\n"
"  @{\n"
"    /* @r{Exit if we have reached a newline.}  */\n"
"    if (*p == '\\n"
"')\n"
"      break;\n"
"    /* @r{Pay no attention to spaces.}  */\n"
"    if (*p != ' ')\n"
"      @{\n"
"        /* @r{Operate on the next character.}  */\n"
"        @r{@dots{}}\n"
"      @}\n"
"  @}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8523
msgid ""
"The advantage of using @code{continue} is that it reduces the depth of "
"nesting."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8526
msgid ""
"Contrast @code{continue} with the @code{break} statement.  @xref{break "
"Statement}."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8528
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{switch} Statement"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8529
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{switch} statement"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8530
#, no-wrap
msgid "statement, @code{switch}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8531
#, no-wrap
msgid "switch"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8532
#, no-wrap
msgid "case"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8533
#, no-wrap
msgid "default"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8539
msgid ""
"The @code{switch} statement selects code to run according to the value of an "
"expression.  The expression, in parentheses, follows the keyword "
"@code{switch}.  After that come all the cases to select among, inside "
"braces.  It looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8545
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"switch (@var{selector})\n"
"  @{\n"
"    @var{cases}@r{@dots{}}\n"
"  @}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8548
msgid "A case can look like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8553
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"case @var{value}:\n"
"  @var{statements}\n"
"  break;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8558
msgid ""
"which means ``come here if @var{selector} happens to have the value "
"@var{value},'' or like this (a GNU C extension):"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8563
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"case @var{rangestart} ... @var{rangeend}:\n"
"  @var{statements}\n"
"  break;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8569
msgid ""
"which means ``come here if @var{selector} happens to have a value between "
"@var{rangestart} and @var{rangeend} (inclusive).'' @xref{Case Ranges}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8576
msgid ""
"The values in @code{case} labels must reduce to integer constants.  They can "
"use arithmetic, and @code{enum} constants, but they cannot refer to data in "
"memory, because they have to be computed at compile time.  It is an error if "
"two @code{case} labels specify the same value, or ranges that overlap, or if "
"one is a range and the other is a value in that range."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8579
msgid ""
"You can also define a default case to handle ``any other value,'' like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8584
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"default:\n"
"  @var{statements}\n"
"  break;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8588
msgid ""
"If the @code{switch} statement has no @code{default:} label, then it does "
"nothing when the value matches none of the cases."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8594
msgid ""
"The brace-group inside the @code{switch} statement is a block, and you can "
"declare variables with that scope just as in any other block "
"(@pxref{Blocks}).  However, initializers in these declarations won't "
"necessarily be executed every time the @code{switch} statement runs, so it "
"is best to avoid giving them initializers."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8597
msgid ""
"@code{break;} inside a @code{switch} statement exits immediately from the "
"@code{switch} statement.  @xref{break Statement}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8602
msgid ""
"If there is no @code{break;} at the end of the code for a case, execution "
"continues into the code for the following case.  This happens more often by "
"mistake than intentionally, but since this feature is used in real code, we "
"cannot eliminate it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8607
msgid ""
"@strong{Warning:} When one case is intended to fall through to the next, "
"write a comment like @samp{falls through} to say it's intentional.  That "
"way, other programmers won't assume it was an error and ``fix'' it "
"erroneously."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8611
msgid ""
"Consecutive @code{case} statements could, pedantically, be considered an "
"instance of falling through, but we don't consider or treat them that way "
"because they won't confuse anyone."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8613
#, no-wrap
msgid "Example of @code{switch}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8617
msgid ""
"Here's an example of using the @code{switch} statement to distinguish among "
"characters:"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8618
#, no-wrap
msgid "counting vowels and punctuation"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8621
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct vp @{ int vowels, punct; @};\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8632
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct vp\n"
"count_vowels_and_punct (char *string)\n"
"@{\n"
"  int c;\n"
"  int vowels = 0;\n"
"  int punct = 0;\n"
"  /* @r{Don't change the parameter itself.}  */\n"
"  /* @r{That helps in debugging.}  */\n"
"  char *p = string;\n"
"  struct vp value;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8642
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  while (c = *p++)\n"
"    switch (c)\n"
"      @{\n"
"        case 'y':\n"
"        case 'Y':\n"
"          /* @r{We assume @code{y_is_consonant} will check surrounding\n"
"                letters to determine whether this y is a vowel.}  */\n"
"          if (y_is_consonant (p - 1))\n"
"            break;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8644
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"          /* @r{Falls through} */\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8657
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        case 'a':\n"
"        case 'e':\n"
"        case 'i':\n"
"        case 'o':\n"
"        case 'u':\n"
"        case 'A':\n"
"        case 'E':\n"
"        case 'I':\n"
"        case 'O':\n"
"        case 'U':\n"
"          vowels++;\n"
"          break;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8669
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"        case '.':\n"
"        case ',':\n"
"        case ':':\n"
"        case ';':\n"
"        case '?':\n"
"        case '!':\n"
"        case '\\\"':\n"
"        case '\\'':\n"
"          punct++;\n"
"          break;\n"
"      @}\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8672
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  value.vowels = vowels;\n"
"  value.punct = punct;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8675
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  return value;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8678
#, no-wrap
msgid "Duff's Device"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8679
#, no-wrap
msgid "Duff's device"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8684
msgid ""
"The cases in a @code{switch} statement can be inside other control "
"constructs.  For instance, we can use a technique known as @dfn{Duff's "
"device} to optimize this simple function,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8692
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"copy (char *to, char *from, int count)\n"
"@{\n"
"  while (count > 0)\n"
"    *to++ = *from++, count--;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8697
msgid ""
"which copies memory starting at @var{from} to memory starting at @var{to}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8701
msgid ""
"Duff's device involves unrolling the loop so that it copies several "
"characters each time around, and using a @code{switch} statement to enter "
"the loop body at the proper point:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8723
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"copy (char *to, char *from, int count)\n"
"@{\n"
"  if (count <= 0)\n"
"    return;\n"
"  int n = (count + 7) / 8;\n"
"  switch (count % 8)\n"
"    @{\n"
"      do @{\n"
"        case 0: *to++ = *from++;\n"
"        case 7: *to++ = *from++;\n"
"        case 6: *to++ = *from++;\n"
"        case 5: *to++ = *from++;\n"
"        case 4: *to++ = *from++;\n"
"        case 3: *to++ = *from++;\n"
"        case 2: *to++ = *from++;\n"
"        case 1: *to++ = *from++;\n"
"        @} while (--n > 0);\n"
"    @}\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8727
#, no-wrap
msgid "case ranges"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8728
#, no-wrap
msgid "ranges in case statements"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8732
msgid ""
"You can specify a range of consecutive values in a single @code{case} label, "
"like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8735
#, no-wrap
msgid "case @var{low} ... @var{high}:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8740
msgid ""
"This has the same effect as the proper number of individual @code{case} "
"labels, one for each integer value from @var{low} to @var{high}, inclusive."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8742
msgid "This feature is especially useful for ranges of ASCII character codes:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8745
#, no-wrap
msgid "case 'A' ... 'Z':\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8749
msgid ""
"@strong{Be careful:} with integers, write spaces around the @code{...} to "
"prevent it from being parsed wrong.  For example, write this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8752
#, no-wrap
msgid "case 1 ... 5:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8756
msgid "rather than this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8759
#, no-wrap
msgid "case 1...5:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8763
#, no-wrap
msgid "null statement"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8764
#, no-wrap
msgid "statement, null"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8767
msgid "A @dfn{null statement} is just a semicolon.  It does nothing."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8773
msgid ""
"A null statement is a placeholder for use where a statement is grammatically "
"required, but there is nothing to be done.  For instance, sometimes all the "
"work of a @code{for}-loop is done in the @code{for}-header itself, leaving "
"no work for the body.  Here is an example that searches for the first "
"newline in @code{array}:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8777
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"for (p = array; *p != '\\n"
"'; p++)\n"
"  ;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8780
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{goto} Statement and Labels"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8781
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{goto} statement"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8782
#, no-wrap
msgid "statement, @code{goto}"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8783
#, no-wrap
msgid "label"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8784
#, no-wrap
msgid "goto"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8787
msgid "The @code{goto} statement looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8790
#, no-wrap
msgid "goto @var{label};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8795
msgid ""
"Its effect is to transfer control immediately to another part of the current "
"function---where the label named @var{label} is defined."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8797
msgid "An ordinary label definition looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8800
#, no-wrap
msgid "@var{label}:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8806
msgid ""
"and it can appear before any statement.  You can't use @code{default} as a "
"label, since that has a special meaning for @code{switch} statements."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8809
msgid ""
"An ordinary label doesn't need a separate declaration; defining it is enough."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8812
msgid ""
"Here's an example of using @code{goto} to implement a loop equivalent to "
"@code{do}--@code{while}:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8820
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@{\n"
" loop_restart:\n"
"  @var{body}\n"
"  if (@var{condition})\n"
"    goto loop_restart;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8824
msgid ""
"The name space of labels is separate from that of variables and functions.  "
"Thus, there is no error in using a single name in both ways:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8833
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@{\n"
"  int foo;    // @r{Variable @code{foo}.}\n"
" foo:         // @r{Label @code{foo}.}\n"
"  @var{body}\n"
"  if (foo > 0)  // @r{Variable @code{foo}.}\n"
"    goto foo;   // @r{Label @code{foo}.}\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8838
msgid ""
"Blocks have no effect on ordinary labels; each label name is defined "
"throughout the whole of the function it appears in.  It looks strange to "
"jump into a block with @code{goto}, but it works.  For example,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8848
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"if (x < 0)\n"
"  goto negative;\n"
"if (y < 0)\n"
"  @{\n"
"   negative:\n"
"    printf (\"Negative\\n"
"\");\n"
"    return;\n"
"  @}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8852
msgid ""
"If the goto jumps into the scope of a variable, it does not initialize the "
"variable.  For example, if @code{x} is negative,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8863
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"if (x < 0)\n"
"  goto negative;\n"
"if (y < 0)\n"
"  @{\n"
"    int i = 5;\n"
"   negative:\n"
"    printf (\"Negative, and i is %d\\n"
"\", i);\n"
"    return;\n"
"  @}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8867
msgid "prints junk because @code{i} was not initialized."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8871
msgid ""
"If the block declares a variable-length automatic array, jumping into it "
"gives a compilation error.  However, jumping out of the scope of a "
"variable-length array works fine, and deallocates its storage."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8874
msgid ""
"A label can't come directly before a declaration, so the code can't jump "
"directly to one.  For example, this is not allowed:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8882
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@{\n"
"  goto foo;\n"
"foo:\n"
"  int x = 5;\n"
"  bar(&x);\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8887
msgid ""
"The workaround is to add a statement, even an empty statement, directly "
"after the label.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8896
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@{\n"
"  goto foo;\n"
"foo:\n"
"  ;\n"
"  int x = 5;\n"
"  bar(&x);\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8900
msgid ""
"Likewise, a label can't be the last thing in a block.  The workaround "
"solution is the same: add a semicolon after the label."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8904
msgid ""
"These unnecessary restrictions on labels make no sense, and ought in "
"principle to be removed; but they do only a little harm since labels and "
"@code{goto} are rarely the best way to write a program."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8908
msgid ""
"These examples are all artificial; it would be more natural to write them in "
"other ways, without @code{goto}.  For instance, the clean way to write the "
"example that prints @samp{Negative} is this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8915
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"if (x < 0 || y < 0)\n"
"  @{\n"
"    printf (\"Negative\\n"
"\");\n"
"    return;\n"
"  @}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8922
msgid ""
"It is hard to construct simple examples where @code{goto} is actually the "
"best way to write a program.  Its rare good uses tend to be in complex code, "
"thus not apt for the purpose of explaining the meaning of @code{goto}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8929
msgid ""
"The only good time to use @code{goto} is when it makes the code simpler than "
"any alternative.  Jumping backward is rarely desirable, because usually the "
"other looping and control constructs give simpler code.  Using @code{goto} "
"to jump forward is more often desirable, for instance when a function needs "
"to do some processing in an error case and errors can occur at various "
"different places within the function."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8931
#, no-wrap
msgid "Locally Declared Labels"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8932
#, no-wrap
msgid "local labels"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8933
#, no-wrap
msgid "macros, local labels"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8934
#, no-wrap
msgid "__label__"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8940
msgid ""
"In GNU C you can declare @dfn{local labels} in any nested block scope.  A "
"local label is used in a @code{goto} statement just like an ordinary label, "
"but you can only reference it within the block in which it was declared."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8942
msgid "A local label declaration looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8945
#, no-wrap
msgid "__label__ @var{label};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8949 c-intro-and-ref.texi:13008
msgid "or"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8952
#, no-wrap
msgid "__label__ @var{label1}, @var{label2}, @r{@dots{}};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8956
msgid ""
"Local label declarations must come at the beginning of the block, before any "
"ordinary declarations or statements."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8960
msgid ""
"The label declaration declares the label @emph{name}, but does not define "
"the label itself.  That's done in the usual way, with @code{@var{label}:}, "
"before one of the statements in the block."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8967
msgid ""
"The local label feature is useful for complex macros.  If a macro contains "
"nested loops, a @code{goto} can be useful for breaking out of them.  "
"However, an ordinary label whose scope is the whole function cannot be used: "
"if the macro can be expanded several times in one function, the label will "
"be multiply defined in that function.  A local label avoids this problem.  "
"For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8983
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"#define SEARCH(value, array, target)              \\\n"
"do @{                                              \\\n"
"  __label__ found;                                \\\n"
"  __auto_type _SEARCH_target = (target);          \\\n"
"  __auto_type _SEARCH_array = (array);            \\\n"
"  int i, j;                                       \\\n"
"  int value;                                      \\\n"
"  for (i = 0; i < max; i++)                       \\\n"
"    for (j = 0; j < max; j++)                     \\\n"
"      if (_SEARCH_array[i][j] == _SEARCH_target)  \\\n"
"        @{ (value) = i; goto found; @}              \\\n"
"  (value) = -1;                                   \\\n"
" found:;                                          \\\n"
"@} while (0)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:8987
msgid ""
"This could also be written using a statement expression (@pxref{Statement "
"Exprs}):"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9004
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"#define SEARCH(array, target)                     \\\n"
"(@{                                                \\\n"
"  __label__ found;                                \\\n"
"  __auto_type _SEARCH_target = (target);      \\\n"
"  __auto_type _SEARCH_array = (array);     \\\n"
"  int i, j;                                       \\\n"
"  int value;                                      \\\n"
"  for (i = 0; i < max; i++)                       \\\n"
"    for (j = 0; j < max; j++)                     \\\n"
"      if (_SEARCH_array[i][j] == _SEARCH_target)  \\\n"
"        @{ value = i; goto found; @}                \\\n"
"  value = -1;                                     \\\n"
" found:                                           \\\n"
"  value;                                          \\\n"
"@})\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9010
msgid ""
"Ordinary labels are visible throughout the function where they are defined, "
"and only in that function.  However, explicitly declared local labels of a "
"block are visible in nested function definitions inside that block.  "
"@xref{Nested Functions}, for details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9012 c-intro-and-ref.texi:9046
msgid "@xref{goto Statement}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9015
#, no-wrap
msgid "labels as values"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9016
#, no-wrap
msgid "computed gotos"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9017
#, no-wrap
msgid "goto with computed label"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9018
#, no-wrap
msgid "address of a label"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9025
msgid ""
"In GNU C, you can get the address of a label defined in the current function "
"(or a local label defined in the containing function) with the unary "
"operator @samp{&&}.  The value has type @code{void *}.  This value is a "
"constant and can be used wherever a constant of that type is valid.  For "
"example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9030
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void *ptr;\n"
"@r{@dots{}}\n"
"ptr = &&foo;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9037
msgid ""
"To use these values requires a way to jump to one.  This is done with the "
"computed goto statement@footnote{The analogous feature in Fortran is called "
"an assigned goto, but that name seems inappropriate in C, since you can do "
"more with label addresses than store them in special label variables.}, "
"@code{goto *@var{exp};}.  For example,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9040
#, no-wrap
msgid "goto *ptr;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9044
msgid "Any expression of type @code{void *} is allowed."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9050 c-intro-and-ref.texi:9052
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9053
#, no-wrap
msgid "Label Value Uses"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9050
msgid "Examples of using label values."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9050 c-intro-and-ref.texi:9094
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9095
#, no-wrap
msgid "Label Value Caveats"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9050
msgid "Limitations of label values."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9057
msgid ""
"One use for label-valued constants is to initialize a static array to serve "
"as a jump table:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9060
#, no-wrap
msgid "static void *array[] = @{ &&foo, &&bar, &&hack @};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9063
msgid "Then you can select a label with indexing, like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9066
#, no-wrap
msgid "goto *array[i];\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9071
msgid ""
"Note that this does not check whether the subscript is in bounds---array "
"indexing in C never checks that."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9074
msgid ""
"You can make the table entries offsets instead of addresses by subtracting "
"one label from the others.  Here is an example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9079
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"static const int array[] = @{ &&foo - &&foo, &&bar - &&foo,\n"
"                             &&hack - &&foo @};\n"
"goto *(&&foo + array[i]);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9085
msgid ""
"Using offsets is preferable in shared libraries, as it avoids the need for "
"dynamic relocation of the array elements; therefore, the array can be "
"read-only."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9089
msgid ""
"An array of label values or offsets serves a purpose much like that of the "
"@code{switch} statement.  The @code{switch} statement is cleaner, so use "
"@code{switch} by preference when feasible."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9093
msgid ""
"Another use of label values is in an interpreter for threaded code.  The "
"labels within the interpreter function can be stored in the threaded code "
"for super-fast dispatching."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9099
msgid ""
"Jumping to a label defined in another function does not work.  It can cause "
"unpredictable results."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9103
msgid ""
"The best way to avoid this is to store label values only in automatic "
"variables, or static variables whose names are declared within the "
"function.  Never pass them as arguments."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9104
#, no-wrap
msgid "cloning"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9110
msgid ""
"An optimization known as @dfn{cloning} generates multiple simplified "
"variants of a function's code, for use with specific fixed arguments.  Using "
"label values in certain ways, such as saving the address in one call to the "
"function and using it again in another call, would make cloning give "
"incorrect results.  These functions must disable cloning."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9117
msgid ""
"Inlining calls to the function would also result in multiple copies of the "
"code, each with its own value of the same label.  Using the label in a "
"computed goto is no problem, because the computed goto inhibits inlining.  "
"However, using the label value in some other way, such as an indication of "
"where an error occurred, would be optimized wrong.  These functions must "
"disable inlining."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9121
msgid ""
"To prevent inlining or cloning of a function, specify "
"@code{__attribute__((__noinline__,__noclone__))} in its definition.  "
"@xref{Attributes}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9124
msgid ""
"When a function uses a label value in a static variable initializer, that "
"automatically prevents inlining or cloning the function."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9126
#, no-wrap
msgid "Statements and Declarations in Expressions"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9127
#, no-wrap
msgid "statements inside expressions"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9128
#, no-wrap
msgid "declarations inside expressions"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9129
#, no-wrap
msgid "expressions containing statements"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9136
msgid ""
"A block enclosed in parentheses can be used as an expression in GNU C@.  "
"This provides a way to use local variables, loops and switches within an "
"expression.  We call it a @dfn{statement expression}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9140
msgid ""
"Recall that a block is a sequence of statements surrounded by braces.  In "
"this construct, parentheses go around the braces.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9146
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"(@{ int y = foo (); int z;\n"
"   if (y > 0) z = y;\n"
"   else z = - y;\n"
"   z; @})\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9151
msgid ""
"is a valid (though slightly more complex than necessary) expression for the "
"absolute value of @code{foo ()}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9157
msgid ""
"The last statement in the block should be an expression statement; an "
"expression followed by a semicolon, that is.  The value of this expression "
"serves as the value of statement expression.  If the last statement is "
"anything else, the statement expression's value is @code{void}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9160
msgid ""
"This feature is mainly useful in making macro definitions compute each "
"operand exactly once.  @xref{Macros and Auto Type}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9164
msgid ""
"Statement expressions are not allowed in expressions that must be constant, "
"such as the value for an enumerator, the width of a bit-field, or the "
"initial value of a static variable."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9169
msgid ""
"Jumping into a statement expression---with @code{goto}, or using a "
"@code{switch} statement outside the statement expression---is an error.  "
"With a computed @code{goto} (@pxref{Labels as Values}), the compiler can't "
"detect the error, but it still won't work."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9174
msgid ""
"Jumping out of a statement expression is permitted, but since subexpressions "
"in C are not computed in a strict order, it is unpredictable which other "
"subexpressions will have been computed by then.  For example,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9177
#, no-wrap
msgid "  foo (), ((@{ bar1 (); goto a; 0; @}) + bar2 ()), baz();\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9183
msgid ""
"calls @code{foo} and @code{bar1} before it jumps, and never calls "
"@code{baz}, but may or may not call @code{bar2}.  If @code{bar2} does get "
"called, that occurs after @code{foo} and before @code{bar1}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9186
#, no-wrap
msgid "variables"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9194
msgid ""
"Every variable used in a C program needs to be made known by a "
"@dfn{declaration}.  It can be used only after it has been declared.  It is "
"an error to declare a variable name more than once in the same scope; an "
"exception is that @code{extern} declarations and tentative definitions can "
"coexist with another declaration of the same variable."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9198
msgid ""
"Variables can be declared anywhere within a block or file. (Older versions "
"of C required that all variable declarations within a block occur before any "
"statements.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9203
msgid ""
"Variables declared within a function or block are @dfn{local} to it.  This "
"means that the variable name is visible only until the end of that function "
"or block, and the memory space is allocated only while control is within it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9207
msgid ""
"Variables declared at the top level in a file are called @dfn{file-scope}.  "
"They are assigned fixed, distinct memory locations, so they retain their "
"values for the whole execution of the program."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9230
#, no-wrap
msgid "variable declarations"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9231
#, no-wrap
msgid "declaration of variables"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9234
msgid "Here's what a variable declaration looks like:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9237
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@var{keywords} @var{basetype} @var{decorated-variable} @r{[}= "
"@var{init}@r{]};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9242
msgid ""
"The @var{keywords} specify how to handle the scope of the variable name and "
"the allocation of its storage.  Most declarations have no keywords because "
"the defaults are right for them."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9247
msgid ""
"C allows these keywords to come before or after @var{basetype}, or even in "
"the middle of it as in @code{unsigned static int}, but don't do that---it "
"would surprise other programmers.  Always write the keywords first."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9253
msgid ""
"The @var{basetype} can be any of the predefined types of C, or a type "
"keyword defined with @code{typedef}.  It can also be @code{struct "
"@var{tag}}, @code{union @var{tag}}, or @code{enum @var{tag}}.  In addition, "
"it can include type qualifiers such as @code{const} and @code{volatile} "
"(@pxref{Type Qualifiers})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9257
msgid ""
"In the simplest case, @var{decorated-variable} is just the variable name.  "
"That declares the variable with the type specified by @var{basetype}.  For "
"instance,"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9266
msgid ""
"uses @code{int} as the @var{basetype} and @code{foo} as the "
"@var{decorated-variable}.  It declares @code{foo} with type @code{int}."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9269
#, no-wrap
msgid "struct tree_node foo;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9273
msgid "declares @code{foo} with type @code{struct tree_node}."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9279 c-intro-and-ref.texi:9281
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9282
#, no-wrap
msgid "Declaring Arrays and Pointers"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9279
msgid "Declaration syntax for variables of array and pointer types."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9279 c-intro-and-ref.texi:9346
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9347
#, no-wrap
msgid "Combining Variable Declarations"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9279
msgid "More than one variable declaration in a single statement."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9283
#, no-wrap
msgid "declaring arrays and pointers"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9285
#, no-wrap
msgid "pointers, declaring"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9289
msgid ""
"To declare a variable that is an array, write "
"@code{@var{variable}[@var{length}]} for @var{decorated-variable}:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9292
#, no-wrap
msgid "int foo[5];\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9296
msgid ""
"To declare a variable that has a pointer type, write @code{*@var{variable}} "
"for @var{decorated-variable}:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9299
#, no-wrap
msgid "struct list_elt *foo;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9302
msgid "These constructs nest.  For instance,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9305
#, no-wrap
msgid "int foo[3][5];\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9309
msgid "declares @code{foo} as an array of 3 arrays of 5 integers each,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9312
#, no-wrap
msgid "struct list_elt *foo[5];\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9316
msgid "declares @code{foo} as an array of 5 pointers to structures, and"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9319
#, no-wrap
msgid "struct list_elt **foo;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9323
msgid "declares @code{foo} as a pointer to a pointer to a structure."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9326
#, no-wrap
msgid "int **(*foo[30])(int, double);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9332
msgid ""
"declares @code{foo} as an array of 30 pointers to functions (@pxref{Function "
"Pointers}), each of which must accept two arguments (one @code{int} and one "
"@code{double}) and return type @code{int **}."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9340
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"bar (int size)\n"
"@{\n"
"  int foo[size];\n"
"  @r{@dots{}}\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9345
msgid ""
"declares @code{foo} as an array of integers with a size specified at run "
"time when the function @code{bar} is called."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9348
#, no-wrap
msgid "combining variable declarations"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9349
#, no-wrap
msgid "variable declarations, combining"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9350
#, no-wrap
msgid "declarations, combining"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9354
msgid ""
"When multiple declarations have the same @var{keywords} and @var{basetype}, "
"you can combine them using commas.  Thus,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9359
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@var{keywords} @var{basetype}\n"
"   @var{decorated-variable-1} @r{[}= @var{init1}@r{]},\n"
"   @var{decorated-variable-2} @r{[}= @var{init2}@r{]};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9369
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@var{keywords} @var{basetype}\n"
"   @var{decorated-variable-1} @r{[}= @var{init1}@r{]};\n"
"@var{keywords} @var{basetype}\n"
"   @var{decorated-variable-2} @r{[}= @var{init2}@r{]};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9372
msgid "Here are some simple examples:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9378
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int a, b;\n"
"int a = 1, b = 2;\n"
"int a, *p, array[5];\n"
"int a = 0, *p = &a, array[5] = @{1, 2@};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9385
msgid ""
"In the last two examples, @code{a} is an @code{int}, @code{p} is a pointer "
"to @code{int}, and @code{array} is an array of 5 @code{int}s.  Since the "
"initializer for @code{array} specifies only two elements, the other three "
"elements are initialized to zero."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9388
#, no-wrap
msgid "initializers"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9394
msgid ""
"A variable's declaration, unless it is @code{extern}, should also specify "
"its initial value.  For numeric and pointer-type variables, the initializer "
"is an expression for the value.  If necessary, it is converted to the "
"variable's type, just as in an assignment."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9401
msgid ""
"You can also initialize a local structure-type (@pxref{Structures}) or local "
"union-type (@pxref{Unions}) variable this way, from an expression whose "
"value has the same type.  But you can't initialize an array this way "
"(@pxref{Arrays}), since arrays are not first-class objects in C "
"(@pxref{Limitations of C Arrays}) and there is no array assignment."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9405
msgid ""
"You can initialize arrays and structures componentwise, with a list of the "
"elements or components.  You can initialize a union with any one of its "
"alternatives."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9412
msgid ""
"A component-wise initializer for an array consists of element values "
"surrounded by @samp{@{@r{@dots{}}@}}.  If the values in the initializer "
"don't cover all the elements in the array, the remaining elements are "
"initialized to zero."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9415
msgid ""
"You can omit the size of the array when you declare it, and let the "
"initializer specify the size:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9418
#, no-wrap
msgid "int array[] = @{ 3, 9, 12 @};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9426
msgid ""
"A component-wise initializer for a structure consists of field values "
"surrounded by @samp{@{@r{@dots{}}@}}.  Write the field values in the same "
"order as the fields are declared in the structure.  If the values in the "
"initializer don't cover all the fields in the structure, the remaining "
"fields are initialized to zero."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9431
msgid ""
"The initializer for a union-type variable has the form @code{@{ @var{value} "
"@}}, where @var{value} initializes the @emph{first alternative} in the union "
"definition."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9435
msgid ""
"For an array of arrays, a structure containing arrays, an array of "
"structures, etc., you can nest these constructs.  For example,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9441
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct point series[]\n"
"  = @{ @{0, 0@}, @{1.5, 2.8@}, @{99, 100.0004@} @};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9447
msgid ""
"You can omit a pair of inner braces if they contain the right number of "
"elements for the sub-value they initialize, so that no elements or fields "
"need to be filled in with zeros.  But don't do that very much, as it gets "
"confusing."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9453
msgid ""
"An array of @code{char} can be initialized using a string constant.  Recall "
"that the string constant includes an implicit null character at the end "
"(@pxref{String Constants}).  Using a string constant as initializer means to "
"use its contents as the initial values of the array elements.  Here are "
"examples:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9461
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"char text[6] = \"text!\";     /* @r{Includes the null.} */\n"
"char text[5] = \"text!\";     /* @r{Excludes the null.} */\n"
"char text[] = \"text!\";      /* @r{Gets length 6.} */\n"
"char text[]\n"
"  = @{ 't', 'e', 'x', 't', '!', 0 @};  /* @r{same as above.} */\n"
"char text[] = @{ \"text!\" @};  /* @r{Braces are optional.} */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9466
msgid ""
"and this kind of initializer can be nested inside braces to initialize "
"structures or arrays that contain a @code{char}-array."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9469
msgid ""
"In like manner, you can use a wide string constant to initialize an array of "
"@code{wchar_t}."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9471
#, no-wrap
msgid "Designated Initializers"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9472
#, no-wrap
msgid "initializers with labeled elements"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9473
#, no-wrap
msgid "labeled elements in initializers"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9474
#, no-wrap
msgid "case labels in initializers"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9475
#, no-wrap
msgid "designated initializers"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9479
msgid ""
"In a complex structure or long array, it's useful to indicate which field or "
"element we are initializing."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9483
msgid ""
"To designate specific array elements during initialization, include the "
"array index in brackets, and an assignment operator, for each element:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9486
#, no-wrap
msgid "int foo[10] = @{ [3] = 42, [7] = 58 @};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9490
msgid "This does the same thing as:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9493
#, no-wrap
msgid "int foo[10] = @{ 0, 0, 0, 42, 0, 0, 0, 58, 0, 0 @};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9498
msgid ""
"The array initialization can include non-designated element values alongside "
"designated indices; these follow the expected ordering of the array "
"initialization, so that"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9501
#, no-wrap
msgid "int foo[10] = @{ [3] = 42, 43, 44, [7] = 58 @};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9505
msgid "does the same thing as:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9508
#, no-wrap
msgid "int foo[10] = @{ 0, 0, 0, 42, 43, 44, 0, 58, 0, 0 @};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9512
msgid ""
"Note that you can only use constant expressions as array index values, not "
"variables."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9515
msgid ""
"If you need to initialize a subsequence of sequential array elements to the "
"same value, you can specify a range:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9518
#, no-wrap
msgid "int foo[100] = @{ [0 ... 19] = 42, [20 ... 99] = 43 @};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9522
msgid "Using a range this way is a GNU C extension."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9526
msgid ""
"When subsequence ranges overlap, each element is initialized by the last "
"specification that applies to it.  Thus, this initialization is equivalent "
"to the previous one."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9529
#, no-wrap
msgid "int foo[100] = @{ [0 ... 99] = 43, [0 ... 19] = 42 @};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9533
msgid "as the second overrides the first for elements 0 through 19."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9536
msgid ""
"The value used to initialize a range of elements is evaluated only once, for "
"the first element in the range.  So for example, this code"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9540
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int random_values[100]\n"
"  = @{ [0 ... 99] = get_random_number() @};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9545
msgid ""
"would initialize all 100 elements of the array @code{random_values} to the "
"same value---probably not what is intended."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9548
msgid ""
"Similarly, you can initialize specific fields of a structure variable by "
"specifying the field name prefixed with a dot:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9551
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct point @{ int x; int y; @};\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9553
#, no-wrap
msgid "struct point foo = @{ .y = 42 @};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9557
msgid "The same syntax works for union variables as well:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9560
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"union int_double @{ int i; double d; @};\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9562
#, no-wrap
msgid "union int_double foo = @{ .d = 34 @};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9567
msgid ""
"This casts the integer value 34 to a double and stores it in the union "
"variable @code{foo}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9571
msgid ""
"You can designate both array elements and structure elements in the same "
"initialization; for example, here's an array of point structures:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9574
#, no-wrap
msgid "struct point point_array[10] = @{ [4].y = 32, [6].y = 39 @};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9579
msgid ""
"Along with the capability to specify particular array and structure elements "
"to initialize comes the possibility of initializing the same element more "
"than once:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9582
#, no-wrap
msgid "int foo[10] = @{ [4] = 42, [4] = 98 @};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9586
msgid "In such a case, the last initialization value is retained."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9588
#, no-wrap
msgid "Referring to a Type with @code{__auto_type}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9589
#, no-wrap
msgid "__auto_type"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9590
#, no-wrap
msgid "typeof"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9591
#, no-wrap
msgid "macros, types of arguments"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9596
msgid ""
"You can declare a variable copying the type from the initializer by using "
"@code{__auto_type} instead of a particular type.  Here's an example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9602
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"#define max(a,b) \\\n"
"  (@{ __auto_type _a = (a); \\\n"
"      __auto_type _b = (b); \\\n"
"    _a > _b ? _a : _b @})\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9608
msgid ""
"This defines @code{_a} to be of the same type as @code{a}, and @code{_b} to "
"be of the same type as @code{b}.  This is a useful thing to do in a macro "
"that ought to be able to handle any type of data (@pxref{Macros and Auto "
"Type})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9612
msgid ""
"The original GNU C method for obtaining the type of a value is to use "
"@code{typeof}, which takes as an argument either a value or the name of a "
"type.  The previous example could also be written as:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9618
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"#define max(a,b) \\\n"
"  (@{ typeof(a) _a = (a); \\\n"
"      typeof(b) _b = (b); \\\n"
"    _a > _b ? _a : _b @})\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9623
msgid ""
"@code{typeof} is more flexible than @code{__auto_type}; however, the "
"principal use case for @code{typeof} is in variable declarations with "
"initialization, which is exactly what @code{__auto_type} handles."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9626
#, no-wrap
msgid "local variables"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9627
#, no-wrap
msgid "variables, local"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9634
msgid ""
"Declaring a variable inside a function definition (@pxref{Function "
"Definitions}) makes the variable name @dfn{local} to the containing "
"block---that is, the containing pair of braces.  More precisely, the "
"variable's name is visible starting just after where it appears in the "
"declaration, and its visibility continues until the end of the block."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9642
msgid ""
"Local variables in C are generally @dfn{automatic} variables: each "
"variable's storage exists only from the declaration to the end of the "
"block.  Execution of the declaration allocates the storage, computes the "
"initial value, and stores it in the variable.  The end of the block "
"deallocates the storage.@footnote{Due to compiler optimizations, allocation "
"and deallocation don't necessarily really happen at those times.}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9645
msgid ""
"@strong{Warning:} Two declarations for the same local variable in the same "
"scope are an error."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9650
msgid ""
"@strong{Warning:} Automatic variables are stored in the run-time stack.  The "
"total space for the program's stack may be limited; therefore, in using very "
"large arrays, it may be necessary to allocate them in some other way to stop "
"the program from crashing."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9654
msgid ""
"@strong{Warning:} If the declaration of an automatic variable does not "
"specify an initial value, the variable starts out containing garbage.  In "
"this example, the value printed could be anything at all:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9658
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@{\n"
"  int i;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9661
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  printf (\"Print junk %d\\n"
"\", i);\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9666
msgid ""
"In a simple test program, that statement is likely to print 0, simply "
"because every process starts with memory zeroed.  But don't rely on it to be "
"zero---that is erroneous."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9669
msgid ""
"@strong{Note:} Make sure to store a value into each local variable (by "
"assignment, or by initialization) before referring to its value."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9672
#, no-wrap
msgid "file-scope variables"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9673
#, no-wrap
msgid "global variables"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9674
#, no-wrap
msgid "variables, file-scope"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9675
#, no-wrap
msgid "variables, global"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9681
msgid ""
"A variable declaration at the top level in a file (not inside a function "
"definition) declares a @dfn{file-scope variable}.  Loading a program "
"allocates the storage for all the file-scope variables in it, and "
"initializes them too."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9687
msgid ""
"Each file-scope variable is either @dfn{static} (limited to one compilation "
"module) or @dfn{global} (shared with all compilation modules in the "
"program).  To make the variable static, write the keyword @code{static} at "
"the start of the declaration.  Omitting @code{static} makes the variable "
"global."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9690
msgid ""
"The initial value for a file-scope variable can't depend on the contents of "
"storage, and can't call any functions."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9695
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int foo = 5;         /* @r{Valid.} */\n"
"int bar = foo;       /* @r{Invalid!} */\n"
"int bar = sin (1.0); /* @r{Invalid!} */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9698
msgid "But it can use the address of another file-scope variable:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9705
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int foo;\n"
"int *bar = &foo;     /* @r{Valid.} */\n"
"int arr[5];\n"
"int *bar3 = &arr[3]; /* @r{Valid.} */\n"
"int *bar4 = arr + 4; /* @r{Valid.} */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9710
msgid ""
"It is valid for a module to have multiple declarations for a file-scope "
"variable, as long as they are all global or all static, but at most one "
"declaration can specify an initial value for it."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9713
#, no-wrap
msgid "static local variables"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9714
#, no-wrap
msgid "variables, static local"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9715 c-intro-and-ref.texi:10380
#, no-wrap
msgid "static"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9720
msgid ""
"The keyword @code{static} in a local variable declaration says to allocate "
"the storage for the variable permanently, just like a file-scope variable, "
"even if the declaration is within a function."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9722
msgid "Here's an example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9730
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int\n"
"increment_counter ()\n"
"@{\n"
"  static int counter = 0;\n"
"  return ++counter;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9737
msgid ""
"The scope of the name @code{counter} runs from the declaration to the end of "
"the containing block, just like an automatic local variable, but its storage "
"is permanent, so the value persists from one call to the next.  As a result, "
"each call to @code{increment_counter} returns a different, unique value."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9743
msgid ""
"The initial value of a static local variable has the same limitations as for "
"file-scope variables: it can't depend on the contents of storage or call any "
"functions.  It can use the address of a file-scope variable or a static "
"local variable, because those addresses are determined before the program "
"runs."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9745
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{extern} Declarations"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9746
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{extern} declarations"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9747
#, no-wrap
msgid "declarations, @code{extern}"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9748
#, no-wrap
msgid "extern"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9753
msgid ""
"An @code{extern} declaration is used to refer to a global variable whose "
"principal declaration comes elsewhere---in the same module, or in another "
"compilation module.  It looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9756
#, no-wrap
msgid "extern @var{basetype} @var{decorated-variable};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9761
msgid ""
"Its meaning is that, in the current scope, the variable name refers to the "
"file-scope variable of that name---which needs to be declared in a "
"non-@code{extern}, non-@code{static} way somewhere else."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9764
msgid ""
"For instance, if one compilation module has this global variable declaration"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9767
#, no-wrap
msgid "int error_count = 0;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9771
msgid "then other compilation modules can specify this"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9774
#, no-wrap
msgid "extern int error_count;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9778
msgid "to allow reference to the same variable."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9783
msgid ""
"The usual place to write an @code{extern} declaration is at top level in a "
"source file, but you can write an @code{extern} declaration inside a block "
"to make a global or static file-scope variable accessible in that block."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9786
msgid ""
"Since an @code{extern} declaration does not allocate space for the variable, "
"it can omit the size of an array:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9789
#, no-wrap
msgid "extern int array[];\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9794
msgid ""
"You can use @code{array} normally in all contexts where it is converted "
"automatically to a pointer.  However, to use it as the operand of "
"@code{sizeof} is an error, since the size is unknown."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9800
msgid ""
"It is valid to have multiple @code{extern} declarations for the same "
"variable, even in the same scope, if they give the same type.  They do not "
"conflict---they agree.  For an array, it is legitimate for some "
"@code{extern} declarations can specify the size while others omit it.  "
"However, if two declarations give different sizes, that is an error."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9805
msgid ""
"Likewise, you can use @code{extern} declarations at file scope "
"(@pxref{File-Scope Variables}) followed by an ordinary global (non-static) "
"declaration of the same variable.  They do not conflict, because they say "
"compatible things about the same meaning of the variable."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9807
#, no-wrap
msgid "Allocating File-Scope Variables"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9808
#, no-wrap
msgid "allocation file-scope variables"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9809
#, no-wrap
msgid "file-scope variables, allocating"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9813
msgid ""
"Some file-scope declarations allocate space for the variable, and some don't."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9817
msgid ""
"A file-scope declaration with an initial value @emph{must} allocate space "
"for the variable; if there are two of such declarations for the same "
"variable, even in different compilation modules, they conflict."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9823
msgid ""
"An @code{extern} declaration @emph{never} allocates space for the variable.  "
"If all the top-level declarations of a certain variable are @code{extern}, "
"the variable never gets memory space.  If that variable is used anywhere in "
"the program, the use will be reported as an error, saying that the variable "
"is not defined."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9824
#, no-wrap
msgid "tentative definition"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9834
msgid ""
"A file-scope declaration without an initial value is called a @dfn{tentative "
"definition}.  This is a strange hybrid: it @emph{can} allocate space for the "
"variable, but does not insist.  So it causes no conflict, no error, if the "
"variable has another declaration that allocates space for it, perhaps in "
"another compilation module.  But if nothing else allocates space for the "
"variable, the tentative definition will do it.  Any number of compilation "
"modules can declare the same variable in this way, and that is sufficient "
"for all of them to use the variable."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9842
msgid ""
"In programs that are very large or have many contributors, it may be wise to "
"adopt the convention of never using tentative definitions.  You can use the "
"compilation option @option{-fno-common} to make them an error, or "
"@option{-fcommon} to enable them.  The default depends on the version of GCC "
"and its target."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9845
msgid ""
"If a file-scope variable gets its space through a tentative definition, it "
"starts out containing all zeros."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9847
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{auto} and @code{register}"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9848
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{auto} declarations"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9849
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{register} declarations"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9850
#, no-wrap
msgid "auto"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9851
#, no-wrap
msgid "register"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9856
msgid ""
"For historical reasons, you can write @code{auto} or @code{register} before "
"a local variable declaration.  @code{auto} merely emphasizes that the "
"variable isn't static; it changes nothing."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9860
msgid ""
"@code{register} suggests to the compiler storing this variable in a "
"register.  However, GNU C ignores this suggestion, since it can choose the "
"best variables to store in registers without any hints."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9865
msgid ""
"It is an error to take the address of a variable declared @code{register}, "
"so you cannot use the unary @samp{&} operator on it.  If the variable is an "
"array, you can't use it at all (other than as the operand of @code{sizeof}), "
"which makes it rather useless."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9867
#, no-wrap
msgid "Omitting Types in Declarations"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9868
#, no-wrap
msgid "omitting types in declarations"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9874
msgid ""
"The syntax of C traditionally allows omitting the data type in a declaration "
"if it specifies a storage class, a type qualifier (see the next chapter), or "
"@code{auto} or @code{register}.  Then the type defaults to @code{int}.  For "
"example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9877
#, no-wrap
msgid "auto foo = 42;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9880
msgid "This is bad practice; if you see it, fix it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9892
msgid ""
"A declaration can include type qualifiers to advise the compiler about how "
"the variable will be used.  There are three different qualifiers, "
"@code{const}, @code{volatile} and @code{restrict}.  They pertain to "
"different issues, so you can use more than one together.  For instance, "
"@code{const volatile} describes a value that the program is not allowed to "
"change, but might have a different value each time the program examines it.  "
"(This might perhaps be a special hardware register, or part of shared "
"memory.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9894
msgid "If you are just learning C, you can skip this chapter."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9905
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{const} Variables and Fields"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9906
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{const} variables and fields"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9907
#, no-wrap
msgid "variables, @code{const}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9915
msgid ""
"You can mark a variable as ``constant'' by writing @code{const} in front of "
"the declaration.  This says to treat any assignment to that variable as an "
"error.  It may also permit some compiler optimizations---for instance, to "
"fetch the value only once to satisfy multiple references to it.  The "
"construct looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9918
#, no-wrap
msgid "const double pi = 3.14159;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9922
msgid ""
"After this definition, the code can use the variable @code{pi} but cannot "
"assign a different value to it."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9925
#, no-wrap
msgid "pi = 3.0; /* @r{Error!} */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9930
msgid ""
"Simple variables that are constant can be used for the same purposes as "
"enumeration constants, and they are not limited to integers.  The "
"constantness of the variable propagates into pointers, too."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9936
msgid ""
"A pointer type can specify that the @emph{target} is constant.  For example, "
"the pointer type @code{const double *} stands for a pointer to a constant "
"@code{double}.  That's the type that results from taking the address of "
"@code{pi}.  Such a pointer can't be dereferenced in the left side of an "
"assignment."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9939
#, no-wrap
msgid "*(&pi) = 3.0; /* @r{Error!} */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9943
msgid ""
"Nonconstant pointers can be converted automatically to constant pointers, "
"but not vice versa.  For instance,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9947
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"const double *cptr;\n"
"double *ptr;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9952
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"cptr = &pi;    /* @r{Valid.} */\n"
"cptr = ptr;    /* @r{Valid.} */\n"
"ptr = cptr;    /* @r{Error!} */\n"
"ptr = &pi;     /* @r{Error!} */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9956
msgid ""
"This is not an ironclad protection against modifying the value.  You can "
"always cast the constant pointer to a nonconstant pointer type:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9960
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"ptr = (double *)cptr;    /* @r{Valid.} */\n"
"ptr = (double *)&pi;     /* @r{Valid.} */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9965
msgid ""
"However, @code{const} provides a way to show that a certain function won't "
"modify the data structure whose address is passed to it.  Here's an example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9975
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int\n"
"string_length (const char *string)\n"
"@{\n"
"  int count = 0;\n"
"  while (*string++)\n"
"    count++;\n"
"  return count;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9980
msgid ""
"Using @code{const char *} for the parameter is a way of saying this function "
"never modifies the memory of the string itself."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9984
msgid ""
"In calling @code{string_length}, you can specify an ordinary @code{char *} "
"since that can be converted automatically to @code{const char *}."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9986
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{volatile} Variables and Fields"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9987
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{volatile} variables and fields"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9988
#, no-wrap
msgid "variables, @code{volatile}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9993
msgid ""
"The GNU C compiler often performs optimizations that eliminate the need to "
"write or read a variable.  For instance,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:9998
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int foo;\n"
"foo = 1;\n"
"foo++;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10003
msgid ""
"might simply store the value 2 into @code{foo}, without ever storing 1.  "
"These optimizations can also apply to structure fields in some cases."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10008
msgid ""
"If the memory containing @code{foo} is shared with another program, or if it "
"is examined asynchronously by hardware, such optimizations could confuse the "
"communication.  Using @code{volatile} is one way to prevent them."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10014
msgid ""
"Writing @code{volatile} with the type in a variable or field declaration "
"says that the value may be examined or changed for reasons outside the "
"control of the program at any moment.  Therefore, the program must execute "
"in a careful way to assure correct interaction with those accesses, whenever "
"they may occur."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10016
msgid "The simplest use looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10019
#, no-wrap
msgid "volatile int lock;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10025
msgid ""
"This directs the compiler not to do certain common optimizations on use of "
"the variable @code{lock}.  All the reads and writes for a volatile variable "
"or field are really done, and done in the order specified by the source "
"code.  Thus, this code:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10032
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"lock = 1;\n"
"list = list->next;\n"
"if (lock)\n"
"  lock_broken (&lock);\n"
"lock = 0;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10038
msgid ""
"really stores the value 1 in @code{lock}, even though there is no sign it is "
"really used, and the @code{if} statement reads and checks the value of "
"@code{lock}, rather than assuming it is still 1."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10042
msgid ""
"A limited amount of optimization can be done, in principle, on "
"@code{volatile} variables and fields: multiple references between two "
"sequence points (@pxref{Sequence Points}) can be simplified together."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10048
msgid ""
"Use of @code{volatile} does not eliminate the flexibility in ordering the "
"computation of the operands of most operators.  For instance, in @code{lock "
"+ foo ()}, the order of accessing @code{lock} and calling @code{foo} is not "
"specified, so they may be done in either order; the fact that @code{lock} is "
"@code{volatile} has no effect on that."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10050
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{restrict}-Qualified Pointers"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10051
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{restrict} pointers"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10052
#, no-wrap
msgid "pointers, @code{restrict}-qualified"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10053
#, no-wrap
msgid "restrict"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10057
msgid ""
"You can declare a pointer as ``restricted'' using the @code{restrict} type "
"qualifier, like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10060
#, no-wrap
msgid "int *restrict p = x;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10064
msgid "This enables better optimization of code that uses the pointer."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10070
msgid ""
"If @code{p} is declared with @code{restrict}, and then the code references "
"the object that @code{p} points to (using @code{*p} or @code{p[@var{i}]}), "
"the @code{restrict} declaration promises that the code will not access that "
"object in any other way---only through @code{p}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10073
msgid ""
"For instance, it means the code must not use another pointer to access the "
"same space, as shown here:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10078
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int *restrict p = @var{whatever};\n"
"int *q = p;\n"
"foo (*p, *q);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10084
msgid ""
"That contradicts the @code{restrict} promise by accessing the object that "
"@code{p} points to using @code{q}, which bypasses @code{p}.  Likewise, it "
"must not do this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10090
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int *restrict p = @var{whatever};\n"
"struct @{ int *a, *b; @} s;\n"
"s.a = p;\n"
"foo (*p, *s.a);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10096
msgid ""
"This example uses a structure field instead of the variable @code{q} to hold "
"the other pointer, and that contradicts the promise just the same."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10100
msgid ""
"The keyword @code{restrict} also promises that @code{p} won't point to the "
"allocated space of any automatic or static variable.  So the code must not "
"do this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10105
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int a;\n"
"int *restrict p = &a;\n"
"foo (*p, a);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10110
msgid ""
"because that does direct access to the object (@code{a}) that @code{p} "
"points to, which bypasses @code{p}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10113
msgid ""
"If the code makes such promises with @code{restrict} then breaks them, "
"execution is unpredictable."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10115
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{restrict} Pointer Example"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10118
msgid "Here are examples where @code{restrict} enables real optimization."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10121
msgid ""
"In this example, @code{restrict} assures GCC that the array @code{out} "
"points to does not overlap with the array @code{in} points to."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10131
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"process_data (const char *in,\n"
"              char * restrict out,\n"
"              size_t size)\n"
"@{\n"
"  for (i = 0; i < size; i++)\n"
"    out[i] = in[i] + in[i + 1];\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10135
msgid ""
"Here's a simple tree structure, where each tree node holds data of type "
"@code{PAYLOAD} plus two subtrees."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10143
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct foo\n"
"  @{\n"
"    PAYLOAD payload;\n"
"    struct foo *left;\n"
"    struct foo *right;\n"
"  @};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10147
msgid ""
"Now here's a function to null out both pointers in the @code{left} subtree."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10155
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"null_left (struct foo *a)\n"
"@{\n"
"  a->left->left = NULL;\n"
"  a->left->right = NULL;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10161
msgid ""
"Since @code{*a} and @code{*a->left} have the same data type, they could "
"legitimately alias (@pxref{Aliasing}).  Therefore, the compiled code for "
"@code{null_left} must read @code{a->left} again from memory when executing "
"the second assignment statement."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10165
msgid ""
"We can enable optimization, so that it does not need to read @code{a->left} "
"again, by writing @code{null_left} in a less obvious way."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10174
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"null_left (struct foo *a)\n"
"@{\n"
"  struct foo *b = a->left;\n"
"  b->left = NULL;\n"
"  b->right = NULL;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10177
msgid "A more elegant way to fix this is with @code{restrict}."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10185
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"null_left (struct foo *restrict a)\n"
"@{\n"
"  a->left->left = NULL;\n"
"  a->left->right = NULL;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10192
msgid ""
"Declaring @code{a} as @code{restrict} asserts that other pointers such as "
"@code{a->left} will not point to the same memory space as @code{a}.  "
"Therefore, the memory location @code{a->left->left} cannot be the same "
"memory as @code{a->left}.  Knowing this, the compiled code may avoid "
"reloading @code{a->left} for the second statement."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10195
#, no-wrap
msgid "functions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10201
msgid ""
"We have already presented many examples of functions, so if you've read this "
"far, you basically understand the concept of a function.  It is vital, "
"nonetheless, to have a chapter in the manual that collects all the "
"information about functions."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10212 c-intro-and-ref.texi:11227
#, no-wrap
msgid "Advanced Definitions"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10212
msgid "Advanced features of function definitions."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10212 c-intro-and-ref.texi:11729
#, no-wrap
msgid "Obsolete Definitions"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10212
msgid "Obsolete features still used in function definitions in old code."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10216
#, no-wrap
msgid "function definitions"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10217
#, no-wrap
msgid "defining functions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10221
msgid ""
"We have already presented many examples of function definitions.  To "
"summarize the rules, a function definition looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10228
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@var{returntype}\n"
"@var{functionname} (@var{parm_declarations}@r{@dots{}})\n"
"@{\n"
"  @var{body}\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10231
msgid "The part before the open-brace is called the @dfn{function header}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10234
msgid ""
"Write @code{void} as the @var{returntype} if the function does not return a "
"value."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10243 c-intro-and-ref.texi:10245
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10246
#, no-wrap
msgid "Function Parameter Variables"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10243
msgid "Syntax and semantics of function parameters."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10243 c-intro-and-ref.texi:10354
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10355
#, no-wrap
msgid "Forward Function Declarations"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10243
msgid "Functions can only be called after they have been defined or declared."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10243 c-intro-and-ref.texi:10376
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10377
#, no-wrap
msgid "Static Functions"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10243
msgid "Limiting visibility of a function."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10243 c-intro-and-ref.texi:10418
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10419
#, no-wrap
msgid "Arrays as Parameters"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10243
msgid "Functions that accept array arguments."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10243 c-intro-and-ref.texi:10613
#, no-wrap
msgid "Structs as Parameters"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10243
msgid "Functions that accept structure arguments."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10247
#, no-wrap
msgid "function parameter variables"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10248
#, no-wrap
msgid "parameter variables in functions"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10249
#, no-wrap
msgid "parameter list"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10256
msgid ""
"A function parameter variable is a local variable (@pxref{Local Variables}) "
"used within the function to store the value passed as an argument in a call "
"to the function.  Usually we say ``function parameter'' or ``parameter'' for "
"short, not mentioning the fact that it's a variable."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10259
msgid ""
"We declare these variables in the beginning of the function definition, in "
"the @dfn{parameter list}.  For example,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10262
#, no-wrap
msgid "fib (int n)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10267
msgid ""
"has a parameter list with one function parameter @code{n}, which has type "
"@code{int}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10270
msgid ""
"Function parameter declarations differ from ordinary variable declarations "
"in several ways:"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10277
msgid ""
"Inside the function definition header, commas separate parameter "
"declarations, and each parameter needs a complete declaration including the "
"type.  For instance, if a function @code{foo} has two @code{int} parameters, "
"write this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10280
#, no-wrap
msgid "foo (int a, int b)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10283
msgid "You can't share the common @code{int} between the two declarations:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10286
#, no-wrap
msgid "foo (int a, b)      /* @r{Invalid!} */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10292
msgid ""
"A function parameter variable is initialized to whatever value is passed in "
"the function call, so its declaration cannot specify an initial value."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10297
msgid ""
"Writing an array type in a function parameter declaration has the effect of "
"declaring it as a pointer.  The size specified for the array has no effect "
"at all, and we normally omit the size.  Thus,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10302
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"foo (int a[5])\n"
"foo (int a[])\n"
"foo (int *a)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10306
msgid "are equivalent."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10311
msgid ""
"The scope of the parameter variables is the entire function body, "
"notwithstanding the fact that they are written in the function header, which "
"is just outside the function body."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10317
msgid ""
"If a function has no parameters, it would be most natural for the list of "
"parameters in its definition to be empty.  But that, in C, has a special "
"meaning for historical reasons: ``Do not check that calls to this function "
"have the right number of arguments.'' Thus,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10324
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int\n"
"foo ()\n"
"@{\n"
"  return 5;\n"
"@}\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10330 c-intro-and-ref.texi:10348
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int\n"
"bar (int x)\n"
"@{\n"
"  return foo (x);\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10335
msgid ""
"would not report a compilation error in passing @code{x} as an argument to "
"@code{foo}.  By contrast,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10342
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int\n"
"foo (void)\n"
"@{\n"
"  return 5;\n"
"@}\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10353
msgid ""
"would report an error because @code{foo} is supposed to receive no arguments."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10356
#, no-wrap
msgid "forward function declarations"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10357
#, no-wrap
msgid "function declarations, forward"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10362
msgid ""
"The order of the function definitions in the source code makes no "
"difference, except that each function needs to be defined or declared before "
"code uses it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10372
msgid ""
"The definition of a function also declares its name for the rest of the "
"containing scope.  But what if you want to call the function before its "
"definition? To permit that, write a compatible declaration of the same "
"function, before the first call.  A declaration that prefigures a subsequent "
"definition in this way is called a @dfn{forward declaration}.  The function "
"declaration can be at top level or within a block, and it applies until the "
"end of the containing scope."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10375
msgid ""
"@xref{Function Declarations}, for more information about these declarations."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10378
#, no-wrap
msgid "static functions"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10379
#, no-wrap
msgid "functions, static"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10387
msgid ""
"The keyword @code{static} in a function definition limits the visibility of "
"the name to the current compilation module.  (That's the same thing "
"@code{static} does in variable declarations; @pxref{File-Scope Variables}.)  "
"For instance, if one compilation module contains this code:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10394
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"static int\n"
"foo (void)\n"
"@{\n"
"  @r{@dots{}}\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10400
msgid ""
"then the code of that compilation module can call @code{foo} anywhere after "
"the definition, but other compilation modules cannot refer to it at all."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10401
#, no-wrap
msgid "forward declaration"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10402
#, no-wrap
msgid "static function, declaration"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10406
msgid ""
"To call @code{foo} before its definition, it needs a forward declaration, "
"which should use @code{static} since the function definition does.  For this "
"function, it looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10409
#, no-wrap
msgid "static int foo (void);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10417
msgid ""
"It is generally wise to use @code{static} on the definitions of functions "
"that won't be called from outside the same compilation module.  This makes "
"sure that calls are not added in other modules.  If programmers decide to "
"change the function's calling convention, or understand all the consequences "
"of its use, they will only have to check for calls in the same compilation "
"module."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10420
#, no-wrap
msgid "arrays as parameters"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10421
#, no-wrap
msgid "functions with array parameters"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10427
msgid ""
"Arrays in C are not first-class objects: it is impossible to copy them.  So "
"they cannot be passed as arguments like other values.  @xref{Limitations of "
"C Arrays}.  Rather, array parameters work in a special way."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10432 c-intro-and-ref.texi:10434
#, no-wrap
msgid "Array Params are Ptrs"
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10432 c-intro-and-ref.texi:10483
#, no-wrap
msgid "Passing Array Args"
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10432 c-intro-and-ref.texi:10558
#, no-wrap
msgid "Array Parm Qualifiers"
msgstr ""

#. type: subsubsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10435
#, no-wrap
msgid "Array parameters are pointers"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10443
msgid ""
"Declaring a function parameter variable as an array really gives it a "
"pointer type.  C does this because an expression with array type, if used as "
"an argument in a function call, is converted automatically to a pointer (to "
"the zeroth element of the array).  If you declare the corresponding "
"parameter as an ``array'', it will work correctly with the pointer value "
"that really gets passed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10446
msgid ""
"This relates to the fact that C does not check array bounds in access to "
"elements of the array (@pxref{Accessing Array Elements})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10448
msgid "For example, in this function,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10455
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"clobber4 (int array[20])\n"
"@{\n"
"  array[4] = 0;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10461
msgid ""
"the parameter @code{array}'s real type is @code{int *}; the specified "
"length, 20, has no effect on the program.  You can leave out the length and "
"write this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10468
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"clobber4 (int array[])\n"
"@{\n"
"  array[4] = 0;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10472
msgid "or write the parameter declaration explicitly as a pointer:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10479
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"clobber4 (int *array)\n"
"@{\n"
"  array[4] = 0;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10482
msgid "They are all equivalent."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsubsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10484
#, no-wrap
msgid "Passing array arguments"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10493
msgid ""
"The function call passes this pointer by value, like all argument values in "
"C@.  However, the result is paradoxical in that the array itself is passed "
"by reference: its contents are treated as shared memory---shared between the "
"caller and the called function, that is.  When @code{clobber4} assigns to "
"element 4 of @code{array}, the effect is to alter element 4 of the array "
"specified in the call."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10498
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"#include <stddef.h>  /* @r{Defines @code{NULL}.} */\n"
"#include <stdlib.h>  /* @r{Declares @code{malloc},} */\n"
"                     /* @r{Defines @code{EXIT_SUCCESS}.} */\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10504
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int\n"
"main (void)\n"
"@{\n"
"  int data[] = @{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6@};\n"
"  int i;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10508
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  /* @r{Show the initial value of element 4.} */\n"
"  for (i = 0; i < 6; i++)\n"
"    printf (\"data[%d] = %d\\n"
"\", i, data[i]);\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10510 c-intro-and-ref.texi:10518
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  printf (\"\\n"
"\");\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10512
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  clobber4 (data);\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10516
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  /* @r{Show that element 4 has been changed.} */\n"
"  for (i = 0; i < 6; i++)\n"
"    printf (\"data[%d] = %d\\n"
"\", i, data[i]);\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10521
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  return EXIT_SUCCESS;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10526
msgid ""
"shows that @code{data[4]} has become zero after the call to @code{clobber4}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10533
msgid ""
"The array @code{data} has 6 elements, but passing it to a function whose "
"argument type is written as @code{int [20]} is not an error, because that "
"really stands for @code{int *}.  The pointer that is the real argument "
"carries no indication of the length of the array it points into.  It is not "
"required to point to the beginning of the array, either.  For instance,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10536
#, no-wrap
msgid "clobber4 (data+1);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10541
msgid ""
"passes an ``array'' that starts at element 1 of @code{data}, and the effect "
"is to zero @code{data[5]} instead of @code{data[4]}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10544
msgid ""
"If all calls to the function will provide an array of a particular size, you "
"can specify the size of the array to be @code{static}:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10549
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"clobber4 (int array[static 20])\n"
"@r{@dots{}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10557
msgid ""
"This is a promise to the compiler that the function will always be called "
"with an array of 20 elements, so that the compiler can optimize code "
"accordingly.  If the code breaks this promise and calls the function with, "
"for example, a shorter array, unpredictable things may happen."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsubsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10559
#, no-wrap
msgid "Type qualifiers on array parameters"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10563
msgid ""
"You can use the type qualifiers @code{const}, @code{restrict}, and "
"@code{volatile} with array parameters; for example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10568
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"clobber4 (volatile int array[20])\n"
"@r{@dots{}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10573
msgid ""
"denotes that @code{array} is equivalent to a pointer to a volatile "
"@code{int}.  Alternatively:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10578
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"clobber4 (int array[const 20])\n"
"@r{@dots{}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10584
msgid ""
"makes the array parameter equivalent to a constant pointer to an "
"@code{int}.  If we want the @code{clobber4} function to succeed, it would "
"not make sense to write"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10589
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"clobber4 (const int array[20])\n"
"@r{@dots{}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10595
msgid ""
"as this would tell the compiler that the parameter should point to an array "
"of constant @code{int} values, and then we would not be able to store zeros "
"in them."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10598
msgid ""
"In a function with multiple array parameters, you can use @code{restrict} to "
"tell the compiler that each array parameter passed in will be distinct:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10603
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"foo (int array1[restrict 10], int array2[restrict 10])\n"
"@r{@dots{}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10612
msgid ""
"Using @code{restrict} promises the compiler that callers will not pass in "
"the same array for more than one @code{restrict} array parameter.  Knowing "
"this enables the compiler to perform better code optimization. This is the "
"same effect as using @code{restrict} pointers (@pxref{restrict Pointers}), "
"but makes it clear when reading the code that an array of a specific size is "
"expected."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10614
#, no-wrap
msgid "Functions That Accept Structure Arguments"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10621
msgid ""
"Structures in GNU C are first-class objects, so using them as function "
"parameters and arguments works in the natural way.  This function "
"@code{swapfoo} takes a @code{struct foo} with two fields as argument, and "
"returns a structure of the same type but with the fields exchanged."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10624
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct foo @{ int a, b; @};\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10626
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct foo x;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10635
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct foo\n"
"swapfoo (struct foo inval)\n"
"@{\n"
"  struct foo outval;\n"
"  outval.a = inval.b;\n"
"  outval.b = inval.a;\n"
"  return outval;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10640
msgid ""
"This simpler definition of @code{swapfoo} avoids using a local variable to "
"hold the result about to be return, by using a structure constructor "
"(@pxref{Structure Constructors}), like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10647
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct foo\n"
"swapfoo (struct foo inval)\n"
"@{\n"
"  return (struct foo) @{ inval.b, inval.a @};\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10651
msgid ""
"It is valid to define a structure type in a function's parameter list, as in"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10658
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int\n"
"frob_bar (struct bar @{ int a, b; @} inval)\n"
"@{\n"
"  @var{body}\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10666
msgid ""
"and @var{body} can access the fields of @var{inval} since the structure type "
"@code{struct bar} is defined for the whole function body.  However, there is "
"no way to create a @code{struct bar} argument to pass to @code{frob_bar}, "
"except with kludges.  As a result, defining a structure type in a parameter "
"list is useless in practice."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10669
#, no-wrap
msgid "function declarations"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10670
#, no-wrap
msgid "declararing functions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10678
msgid ""
"To call a function, or use its name as a pointer, a @dfn{function "
"declaration} for the function name must be in effect at that point in the "
"code.  The function's definition serves as a declaration of that function "
"for the rest of the containing scope, but to use the function in code before "
"the definition, or from another compilation module, a separate function "
"declaration must precede the use."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10685
msgid ""
"A function declaration looks like the start of a function definition.  It "
"begins with the return value type (@code{void} if none) and the function "
"name, followed by argument declarations in parentheses (though these can "
"sometimes be omitted).  But that's as far as the similarity goes: instead of "
"the function body, the declaration uses a semicolon."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10686
#, no-wrap
msgid "function prototype"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10687
#, no-wrap
msgid "prototype of a function"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10692
msgid ""
"A declaration that specifies argument types is called a @dfn{function "
"prototype}.  You can include the argument names or omit them.  The names, if "
"included in the declaration, have no effect, but they may serve as "
"documentation."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10694
msgid "This form of prototype specifies fixed argument types:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10697
#, no-wrap
msgid "@var{rettype} @var{function} (@var{argtypes}@r{@dots{}});\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10701
msgid "This form says the function takes no arguments:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10704
#, no-wrap
msgid "@var{rettype} @var{function} (void);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10709
msgid ""
"This form declares types for some arguments, and allows additional arguments "
"whose types are not specified:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10712
#, no-wrap
msgid "@var{rettype} @var{function} (@var{argtypes}@r{@dots{}}, ...);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10717
msgid ""
"For a parameter that's an array of variable length, you can write its "
"declaration with @samp{*} where the ``length'' of the array would normally "
"go; for example, these are all equivalent."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10723
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"double maximum (int n, int m, double a[n][m]);\n"
"double maximum (int n, int m, double a[*][*]);\n"
"double maximum (int n, int m, double a[ ][*]);\n"
"double maximum (int n, int m, double a[ ][m]);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10728
msgid ""
"The old-fashioned form of declaration, which is not a prototype, says "
"nothing about the types of arguments or how many they should be:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10731
#, no-wrap
msgid "@var{rettype} @var{function} ();\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10737
msgid ""
"@strong{Warning:} Arguments passed to a function declared without a "
"prototype are converted with the default argument promotions "
"(@pxref{Argument Promotions}.  Likewise for additional arguments whose types "
"are unspecified."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10741
msgid ""
"Function declarations are usually written at the top level in a source file, "
"but you can also put them inside code blocks.  Then the function name is "
"visible for the rest of the containing scope.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10749
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"foo (char *file_name)\n"
"@{\n"
"  void save_file (char *);\n"
"  save_file (file_name);\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10756
msgid ""
"If another part of the code tries to call the function @code{save_file}, "
"this declaration won't be in effect there.  So the function will get an "
"implicit declaration of the form @code{extern int save_file ();}.  That "
"conflicts with the explicit declaration here, and the discrepancy generates "
"a warning."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10760 c-intro-and-ref.texi:11775
msgid ""
"The syntax of C traditionally allows omitting the data type in a function "
"declaration if it specifies a storage class or a qualifier.  Then the type "
"defaults to @code{int}.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10763 c-intro-and-ref.texi:11778
#, no-wrap
msgid "static foo (double x);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10768 c-intro-and-ref.texi:11783
msgid ""
"defaults the return type to @code{int}.  This is bad practice; if you see "
"it, fix it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10772
msgid ""
"Calling a function that is undeclared has the effect of creating an "
"@dfn{implicit} declaration in the innermost containing scope, equivalent to "
"this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10775
#, no-wrap
msgid "extern int @dfn{function} ();\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10782
msgid ""
"This declaration says that the function returns @code{int} but leaves its "
"argument types unspecified.  If that does not accurately fit the function, "
"then the program @strong{needs} an explicit declaration of the function with "
"argument types in order to call it correctly."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10785
msgid ""
"Implicit declarations are deprecated, and a function call that creates one "
"causes a warning."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10788
#, no-wrap
msgid "function calls"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10789
#, no-wrap
msgid "calling functions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10795
msgid ""
"Starting a program automatically calls the function named @code{main} "
"(@pxref{The main Function}).  Aside from that, a function does nothing "
"except when it is @dfn{called}.  That occurs during the execution of a "
"function-call expression specifying that function."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10797
msgid "A function-call expression looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10800
#, no-wrap
msgid "@var{function} (@var{arguments}@r{@dots{}})\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10805
msgid ""
"Most of the time, @var{function} is a function name.  However, it can also "
"be an expression with a function pointer value; that way, the program can "
"determine at run time which function to call."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10808
msgid ""
"The @var{arguments} are a series of expressions separated by commas.  Each "
"expression specifies one argument to pass to the function."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10812
msgid ""
"The list of arguments in a function call looks just like use of the comma "
"operator (@pxref{Comma Operator}), but the fact that it fills the "
"parentheses of a function call gives it a different meaning."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10815
msgid ""
"Here's an example of a function call, taken from an example near the "
"beginning (@pxref{Complete Program})."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10819
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"printf (\"Fibonacci series item %d is %d\\n"
"\",\n"
"        19, fib (19));\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10823
msgid ""
"The three arguments given to @code{printf} are a constant string, the "
"integer 19, and the integer returned by @code{fib (19)}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10826
#, no-wrap
msgid "function call semantics"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10827
#, no-wrap
msgid "semantics of function calls"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10828
#, no-wrap
msgid "call-by-value"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10836
msgid ""
"The meaning of a function call is to compute the specified argument "
"expressions, convert their values according to the function's declaration, "
"then run the function giving it copies of the converted values.  (This "
"method of argument passing is known as @dfn{call-by-value}.)  When the "
"function finishes, the value it returns becomes the value of the "
"function-call expression."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10839
msgid ""
"Call-by-value implies that an assignment to the function argument variable "
"has no direct effect on the caller.  For instance,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10843
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"#include <stdlib.h>  /* @r{Defines @code{EXIT_SUCCESS}.} */\n"
"#include <stdio.h>   /* @r{Declares @code{printf}.} */\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10849
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"subroutine (int x)\n"
"@{\n"
"  x = 5;\n"
"@}\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10858
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"main (void)\n"
"@{\n"
"  int y = 20;\n"
"  subroutine (y);\n"
"  printf (\"y is %d\\n"
"\", y);\n"
"  return EXIT_SUCCESS;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10865
msgid ""
"prints @samp{y is 20}.  Calling @code{subroutine} initializes @code{x} from "
"the value of @code{y}, but this does not establish any other relationship "
"between the two variables.  Thus, the assignment to @code{x}, inside "
"@code{subroutine}, changes only @emph{that} @code{x}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10869
msgid ""
"If an argument's type is specified by the function's declaration, the "
"function call converts the argument expression to that type if possible.  If "
"the conversion is impossible, that is an error."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10873
msgid ""
"If the function's declaration doesn't specify the type of that argument, "
"then the @emph{default argument promotions} apply.  @xref{Argument "
"Promotions}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10876
#, no-wrap
msgid "function pointers"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10877
#, no-wrap
msgid "pointers to functions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10883
msgid ""
"A function name refers to a fixed function.  Sometimes it is useful to call "
"a function to be determined at run time; to do this, you can use a "
"@dfn{function pointer value} that points to the chosen function "
"(@pxref{Pointers})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10890
msgid ""
"Pointer-to-function types can be used to declare variables and other data, "
"including array elements, structure fields, and union alternatives.  They "
"can also be used for function arguments and return values.  These types have "
"the peculiarity that they are never converted automatically to @code{void *} "
"or vice versa.  However, you can do that conversion with a cast."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10895 c-intro-and-ref.texi:10897
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10898
#, no-wrap
msgid "Declaring Function Pointers"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10895
msgid "How to declare a pointer to a function."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10895 c-intro-and-ref.texi:10985
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10986
#, no-wrap
msgid "Assigning Function Pointers"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10895
msgid "How to assign values to function pointers."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10895 c-intro-and-ref.texi:11022
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11023
#, no-wrap
msgid "Calling Function Pointers"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10895
msgid "How to call functions through pointers."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10899
#, no-wrap
msgid "declaring function pointers"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10900
#, no-wrap
msgid "function pointers, declaring"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10908
msgid ""
"The declaration of a function pointer variable (or structure field)  looks "
"almost like a function declaration, except it has an additional @samp{*} "
"just before the variable name.  Proper nesting requires a pair of "
"parentheses around the two of them.  For instance, @code{int (*a) ();} says, "
"``Declare @code{a} as a pointer such that @code{*a} is an "
"@code{int}-returning function.''"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10910
msgid "Contrast these three declarations:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10918
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"/* @r{Declare a function returning @code{char *}.}  */\n"
"char *a (char *);\n"
"/* @r{Declare a pointer to a function returning @code{char}.}  */\n"
"char (*a) (char *);\n"
"/* @r{Declare a pointer to a function returning @code{char *}.}  */\n"
"char *(*a) (char *);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10923
msgid ""
"The possible argument types of the function pointed to are the same as in a "
"function declaration.  You can write a prototype that specifies all the "
"argument types:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10926
#, no-wrap
msgid "@var{rettype} (*@var{function}) (@var{arguments}@r{@dots{}});\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10930
msgid "or one that specifies some and leaves the rest unspecified:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10933
#, no-wrap
msgid "@var{rettype} (*@var{function}) (@var{arguments}@r{@dots{}}, ...);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10937
msgid "or one that says there are no arguments:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10940
#, no-wrap
msgid "@var{rettype} (*@var{function}) (void);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10944
msgid ""
"You can also write a non-prototype declaration that says nothing about the "
"argument types:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10947
#, no-wrap
msgid "@var{rettype} (*@var{function}) ();\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10951
msgid ""
"For example, here's a declaration for a variable that should point to some "
"arithmetic function that operates on two @code{double}s:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10954
#, no-wrap
msgid "double (*binary_op) (double, double);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10960
msgid ""
"Structure fields, union alternatives, and array elements can be function "
"pointers; so can parameter variables.  The function pointer declaration "
"construct can also be combined with other operators allowed in "
"declarations.  For instance,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10963
#, no-wrap
msgid "int **(*foo)();\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10968
msgid ""
"declares @code{foo} as a pointer to a function that returns type @code{int "
"**}, and"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10971
#, no-wrap
msgid "int **(*foo[30])();\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10976
msgid ""
"declares @code{foo} as an array of 30 pointers to functions that return type "
"@code{int **}."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10979
#, no-wrap
msgid "int **(**foo)();\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10984
msgid ""
"declares @code{foo} as a pointer to a pointer to a function that returns "
"type @code{int **}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10987
#, no-wrap
msgid "assigning function pointers"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10988
#, no-wrap
msgid "function pointers, assigning"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:10994
msgid ""
"Assuming we have declared the variable @code{binary_op} as in the previous "
"section, giving it a value requires a suitable function to use.  So let's "
"define a function suitable for the variable to point to.  Here's one:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11001
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"double\n"
"double_add (double a, double b)\n"
"@{\n"
"  return a+b;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11004
msgid "Now we can give it a value:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11007
#, no-wrap
msgid "binary_op = double_add;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11011
msgid ""
"The target type of the function pointer must be upward compatible with the "
"type of the function (@pxref{Compatible Types})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11017
msgid ""
"There is no need for @samp{&} in front of @code{double_add}.  Using a "
"function name such as @code{double_add} as an expression automatically "
"converts it to the function's address, with the appropriate function pointer "
"type.  However, it is ok to use @samp{&} if you feel that is clearer:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11020
#, no-wrap
msgid "binary_op = &double_add;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11024
#, no-wrap
msgid "calling function pointers"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11025
#, no-wrap
msgid "function pointers, calling"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11030
msgid ""
"To call the function specified by a function pointer, just write the "
"function pointer value in a function call.  For instance, here's a call to "
"the function @code{binary_op} points to:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11033
#, no-wrap
msgid "binary_op (x, 5)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11038
msgid ""
"Since the data type of @code{binary_op} explicitly specifies type "
"@code{double} for the arguments, the call converts @code{x} and 5 to "
"@code{double}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11043
msgid ""
"The call conceptually dereferences the pointer @code{binary_op} to ``get'' "
"the function it points to, and calls that function.  If you wish, you can "
"explicitly represent the dereference by writing the @code{*} operator:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11046
#, no-wrap
msgid "(*binary_op) (x, 5)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11050
msgid ""
"The @samp{*} reminds people reading the code that @code{binary_op} is a "
"function pointer rather than the name of a specific function."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11052
#, no-wrap
msgid "The @code{main} Function"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11053
#, no-wrap
msgid "@code{main} function"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11054
#, no-wrap
msgid "main"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11061
msgid ""
"Every complete executable program requires at least one function, called "
"@code{main}, which is where execution begins.  You do not have to explicitly "
"declare @code{main}, though GNU C permits you to do so.  Conventionally, "
"@code{main} should be defined to follow one of these calling conventions:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11066
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int main (void) @{@r{@dots{}}@}\n"
"int main (int argc, char *argv[]) @{@r{@dots{}}@}\n"
"int main (int argc, char *argv[], char *envp[]) @{@r{@dots{}}@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11073
msgid ""
"Using @code{void} as the parameter list means that @code{main} does not use "
"the arguments.  You can write @code{char **argv} instead of @code{char "
"*argv[]}, and likewise for @code{envp}, as the two constructs are equivalent."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11084
msgid ""
"You can call @code{main} from C code, as you can call any other function, "
"though that is an unusual thing to do.  When you do that, you must write the "
"call to pass arguments that match the parameters in the definition of "
"@code{main}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11090
msgid ""
"The @code{main} function is not actually the first code that runs when a "
"program starts.  In fact, the first code that runs is system code from the "
"file @file{crt0.o}.  In Unix, this was hand-written assembler code, but in "
"GNU we replaced it with C code.  Its job is to find the arguments for "
"@code{main} and call that."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11096 c-intro-and-ref.texi:11098
#, no-wrap
msgid "Values from main"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11096
msgid "Returning values from the main function."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11096 c-intro-and-ref.texi:11138
#, no-wrap
msgid "Command-Line Parameters"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11096
msgid "Accessing command-line parameters provided to the program."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11096 c-intro-and-ref.texi:11180
#, no-wrap
msgid "Environment Variables"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11096
msgid "Accessing system environment variables."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11099
#, no-wrap
msgid "Returning Values from @code{main}"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11100
#, no-wrap
msgid "returning values from @code{main}"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11101
#, no-wrap
msgid "success"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11102
#, no-wrap
msgid "failure"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11103
#, no-wrap
msgid "exit status"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11110
msgid ""
"When @code{main} returns, the process terminates.  Whatever value "
"@code{main} returns becomes the exit status which is reported to the parent "
"process.  While nominally the return value is of type @code{int}, in fact "
"the exit status gets truncated to eight bits; if @code{main} returns the "
"value 256, the exit status is 0."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11115
msgid ""
"Normally, programs return only one of two values: 0 for success, and 1 for "
"failure. For maximum portability, use the macro values @code{EXIT_SUCCESS} "
"and @code{EXIT_FAILURE} defined in @code{stdlib.h}.  Here's an example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: code{#1}
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11116
#, no-wrap
msgid "EXIT_FAILURE"
msgstr ""

#. type: code{#1}
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11117
#, no-wrap
msgid "EXIT_SUCCESS"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11121
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"#include <stdlib.h>  /* @r{Defines @code{EXIT_SUCCESS}} */\n"
"                     /* @r{and @code{EXIT_FAILURE}.} */\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11131
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int\n"
"main (void)\n"
"@{\n"
"  @r{@dots{}}\n"
"  if (foo)\n"
"    return EXIT_SUCCESS;\n"
"  else\n"
"    return EXIT_FAILURE;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11137
msgid ""
"Some types of programs maintain special conventions for various return "
"values; for example, comparison programs including @code{cmp} and "
"@code{diff} return 1 to indicate a mismatch, and 2 to indicate that the "
"comparison couldn't be performed."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11139
#, no-wrap
msgid "Accessing Command-Line Parameters"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11140
#, no-wrap
msgid "command-line parameters"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11141
#, no-wrap
msgid "parameters, command-line"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11147
msgid ""
"If the program was invoked with any command-line arguments, it can access "
"them through the arguments of @code{main}, @code{argc} and @code{argv}.  "
"(You can give these arguments any names, but the names @code{argc} and "
"@code{argv} are customary.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11154
msgid ""
"The value of @code{argv} is an array containing all of the command-line "
"arguments as strings, with the name of the command invoked as the first "
"string.  @code{argc} is an integer that says how many strings @code{argv} "
"contains.  Here is an example of accessing the command-line parameters, "
"retrieving the program's name and checking for the standard "
"@option{--version} and @option{--help} options:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11157
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"#include <string.h> /* @r{Declare @code{strcmp}.} */\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11162
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int\n"
"main (int argc, char *argv[])\n"
"@{\n"
"  char *program_name = argv[0];\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11178
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  for (int i = 1; i < argc; i++)\n"
"    @{\n"
"      if (!strcmp (argv[i], \"--version\"))\n"
"        @{\n"
"          /* @r{Print version information and exit.} */\n"
"          @r{@dots{}}\n"
"        @}\n"
"      else if (!strcmp (argv[i], \"--help\"))\n"
"        @{\n"
"          /* @r{Print help information and exit.} */\n"
"          @r{@dots{}}\n"
"        @}\n"
"    @}\n"
"  @r{@dots{}}\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11181
#, no-wrap
msgid "Accessing Environment Variables"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11182
#, no-wrap
msgid "environment variables"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11189
msgid ""
"You can optionally include a third parameter to @code{main}, another array "
"of strings, to capture the environment variables available to the program.  "
"Unlike what happens with @code{argv}, there is no additional parameter for "
"the count of environment variables; rather, the array of environment "
"variables concludes with a null pointer."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11204
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int\n"
"main (int argc, char *argv[], char *envp[])\n"
"@{\n"
"  /* @r{Print out all environment variables.} */\n"
"  int i = 0;\n"
"  while (envp[i])\n"
"    @{\n"
"      printf (\"%s\\n"
"\", envp[i]);\n"
"      i++;\n"
"    @}\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11211
msgid ""
"Another method of retrieving environment variables is to use the library "
"function @code{getenv}, which is defined in @code{stdlib.h}.  Using "
"@code{getenv} does not require defining @code{main} to accept the "
"@code{envp} pointer.  For example, here is a program that fetches and prints "
"the user's home directory (if defined):"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11215
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"#include <stdlib.h>  /* @r{Declares @code{getenv}.} */\n"
"#include <stdio.h>   /* @r{Declares @code{printf}.} */\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11225
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int\n"
"main (void)\n"
"@{\n"
"  char *home_directory = getenv (\"HOME\");\n"
"  if (home_directory)\n"
"    printf (\"My home directory is: %s\\n"
"\", home_directory);\n"
"  else\n"
"    printf (\"My home directory is not defined!\\n"
"\");\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11228
#, no-wrap
msgid "Advanced Function Features"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11233
msgid ""
"This section describes some advanced or obscure features for GNU C function "
"definitions.  If you are just learning C, you can skip the rest of this "
"chapter."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11240 c-intro-and-ref.texi:11242
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11243
#, no-wrap
msgid "Variable-Length Array Parameters"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11240
msgid "Functions that accept arrays of variable length."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11240 c-intro-and-ref.texi:11323
#, no-wrap
msgid "Variable Number of Arguments"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11240
msgid "Variadic functions."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11240 c-intro-and-ref.texi:11452
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11453
#, no-wrap
msgid "Nested Functions"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11240
msgid "Defining functions within functions."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11240 c-intro-and-ref.texi:11603
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11604
#, no-wrap
msgid "Inline Function Definitions"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11240
msgid "A function call optimization technique."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11244
#, no-wrap
msgid "variable-length array parameters"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11245
#, no-wrap
msgid "array parameters, variable-length"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11246
#, no-wrap
msgid "functions that accept variable-length arrays"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11252
msgid ""
"An array parameter can have variable length: simply declare the array type "
"with a size that isn't constant.  In a nested function, the length can refer "
"to a variable defined in a containing scope.  In any function, it can refer "
"to a previous parameter, like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11265
msgid ""
"Alternatively, in function declarations (but not in function definitions), "
"you can use @code{[*]} to denote that the array parameter is of a variable "
"length, such that these two declarations mean the same thing:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11269
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct entry\n"
"tester (int len, char data[len][len]);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11274
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct entry\n"
"tester (int len, char data[*][*]);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11280
msgid ""
"The two forms of input are equivalent in GNU C, but emphasizing that the "
"array parameter is variable-length may be helpful to those studying the code."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11283
msgid ""
"You can also omit the length parameter, and instead use some other in-scope "
"variable for the length in the function definition:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11295
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct entry\n"
"tester (char data[*][*]);\n"
"@r{@dots{}}\n"
"int data_length = 20;\n"
"@r{@dots{}}\n"
"struct entry\n"
"tester (char data[data_length][data_length])\n"
"@{\n"
"  @r{@dots{}}\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11299
#, no-wrap
msgid "parameter forward declaration"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11302
msgid ""
"In GNU C, to pass the array first and the length afterward, you can use a "
"@dfn{parameter forward declaration}, like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11314
msgid ""
"The @samp{int len} before the semicolon is the parameter forward "
"declaration; it serves the purpose of making the name @code{len} known when "
"the declaration of @code{data} is parsed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11320
msgid ""
"You can write any number of such parameter forward declarations in the "
"parameter list.  They can be separated by commas or semicolons, but the last "
"one must end with a semicolon, which is followed by the ``real'' parameter "
"declarations.  Each forward declaration must match a subsequent ``real'' "
"declaration in parameter name and data type."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11322
msgid "Standard C does not support parameter forward declarations."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11324
#, no-wrap
msgid "Variable-Length Parameter Lists"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11325
#, no-wrap
msgid "variable-length parameter lists"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11326
#, no-wrap
msgid "parameters lists, variable length"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11327
#, no-wrap
msgid "function parameter lists, variable length"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11329
#, no-wrap
msgid "variadic function"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11335
msgid ""
"A function that takes a variable number of arguments is called a "
"@dfn{variadic function}.  In C, a variadic function must specify at least "
"one fixed argument with an explicitly declared data type.  Additional "
"arguments can follow, and can vary in both quantity and data type."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11339
msgid ""
"In the function header, declare the fixed parameters in the normal way, then "
"write a comma and an ellipsis: @samp{, ...}.  Here is an example of a "
"variadic function header:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11342
#, no-wrap
msgid "int add_multiple_values (int number, ...)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: code{#1}
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11344
#, no-wrap
msgid "va_list"
msgstr ""

#. type: code{#1}
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11345
#, no-wrap
msgid "va_start"
msgstr ""

#. type: code{#1}
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11346
#, no-wrap
msgid "va_end"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11352
msgid ""
"The function body can refer to fixed arguments by their parameter names, but "
"the additional arguments have no names.  Accessing them in the function body "
"uses certain standard macros.  They are defined in the library header file "
"@file{stdarg.h}, so the code must @code{#include} that file."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11354
msgid "In the body, write"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11358
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"va_list ap;\n"
"va_start (ap, @var{last_fixed_parameter});\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11363
msgid ""
"This declares the variable @code{ap} (you can use any name for it)  and then "
"sets it up to point before the first additional argument."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11365
msgid "Then, to fetch the next consecutive additional argument, write this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11368
#, no-wrap
msgid "va_arg (ap, @var{type})\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11372
msgid ""
"After fetching all the additional arguments (or as many as need to be used), "
"write this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11375
#, no-wrap
msgid "va_end (ap);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11380
msgid ""
"Here's an example of a variadic function definition that adds any number of "
"@code{int} arguments.  The first (fixed) argument says how many more "
"arguments follow."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11384
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"#include <stdarg.h> /* @r{Defines @code{va}@r{@dots{}} macros.} */\n"
"@r{@dots{}}\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11389
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int\n"
"add_multiple_values (int argcount, ...)\n"
"@{\n"
"  int counter, total = 0;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11392 c-intro-and-ref.texi:11819
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  /* @r{Declare a variable of type @code{va_list}.} */\n"
"  va_list argptr;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11395
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  /* @r{Initialize that variable..} */\n"
"  va_start (argptr, argcount);\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11401 c-intro-and-ref.texi:11831
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  for (counter = 0; counter < argcount; counter++)\n"
"    @{\n"
"      /* @r{Get the next additional argument.} */\n"
"      total += va_arg (argptr, int);\n"
"    @}\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11404 c-intro-and-ref.texi:11834
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  /* @r{End use of the @code{argptr} variable.} */\n"
"  va_end (argptr);\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11407 c-intro-and-ref.texi:11837
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  return total;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11414
msgid ""
"With GNU C, @code{va_end} is superfluous, but some other compilers might "
"make @code{va_start} allocate memory so that calling @code{va_end} is "
"necessary to avoid a memory leak.  Before doing @code{va_start} again with "
"the same variable, do @code{va_end} first."
msgstr ""

#. type: code{#1}
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11415
#, no-wrap
msgid "va_copy"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11423
msgid ""
"Because of this possible memory allocation, it is risky (in principle)  to "
"copy one @code{va_list} variable to another with assignment.  Instead, use "
"@code{va_copy}, which copies the substance but allocates separate memory in "
"the variable you copy to.  The call looks like @code{va_copy (@var{to}, "
"@var{from})}, where both @var{to} and @var{from} should be variables of type "
"@code{va_list}.  In principle, do @code{va_end} on each of these variables "
"before its scope ends."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11431
msgid ""
"Since the additional arguments' types are not specified in the function's "
"definition, the default argument promotions (@pxref{Argument Promotions}) "
"apply to them in function calls.  The function definition must take account "
"of this; thus, if an argument was passed as @code{short}, the function "
"should get it as @code{int}.  If an argument was passed as @code{float}, the "
"function should get it as @code{double}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11437
msgid ""
"C has no mechanism to tell the variadic function how many arguments were "
"passed to it, so its calling convention must give it a way to determine "
"this.  That's why @code{add_multiple_values} takes a fixed argument that "
"says how many more arguments follow.  Thus, you can call the function like "
"this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11441
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"sum = add_multiple_values (3, 12, 34, 190);\n"
"/* @r{Value is 12+34+190.} */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11446
msgid ""
"In GNU C, there is no actual need to use the @code{va_end} function.  In "
"fact, it does nothing.  It's used for compatibility with other compilers, "
"when that matters."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11451
msgid ""
"It is a mistake to access variables declared as @code{va_list} except in the "
"specific ways described here.  Just what that type consists of is an "
"implementation detail, which could vary from one platform to another."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11454
#, no-wrap
msgid "nested functions"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11455
#, no-wrap
msgid "functions, nested"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11456
#, no-wrap
msgid "downward funargs"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11457
#, no-wrap
msgid "thunks"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11464
msgid ""
"A @dfn{nested function} is a function defined inside another function.  (The "
"ability to do this is indispensable for automatic translation of certain "
"programming languages into C.)  The nested function's name is local to the "
"block where it is defined.  For example, here we define a nested function "
"named @code{square}, then call it twice:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11470
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"foo (double a, double b)\n"
"@{\n"
"  double square (double z) @{ return z * z; @}\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11473
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  return square (a) + square (b);\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11480
msgid ""
"The nested function definition can access all the variables of the "
"containing function that are visible at the point of its definition.  This "
"is called @dfn{lexical scoping}.  For example, here we show a nested "
"function that uses an inherited variable named @code{offset}:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11492
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"bar (int *array, int offset, int size)\n"
"@{\n"
"  int access (int *array, int index)\n"
"    @{ return array[index + offset]; @}\n"
"  int i;\n"
"  @r{@dots{}}\n"
"  for (i = 0; i < size; i++)\n"
"    @r{@dots{}} access (array, i) @r{@dots{}}\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11498
msgid ""
"Nested function definitions can appear wherever automatic variable "
"declarations are allowed; that is, in any block, interspersed with the other "
"declarations and statements in the block."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11505
msgid ""
"The nested function's name is visible only within the parent block; the "
"name's scope starts from its definition and continues to the end of the "
"containing block.  If the nested function's name is the same as the parent "
"function's name, there will be no way to refer to the parent function inside "
"the scope of the name of the nested function."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11508
msgid ""
"Using @code{extern} or @code{static} on a nested function definition is an "
"error."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11512
msgid ""
"It is possible to call the nested function from outside the scope of its "
"name by storing its address or passing the address to another function.  You "
"can do this safely, but you must be careful:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11519
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"hack (int *array, int size, int addition)\n"
"@{\n"
"  void store (int index, int value)\n"
"    @{ array[index] = value + addition; @}\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11522
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  intermediate (store, size);\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11529
msgid ""
"Here, the function @code{intermediate} receives the address of @code{store} "
"as an argument.  If @code{intermediate} calls @code{store}, the arguments "
"given to @code{store} are used to store into @code{array}.  @code{store} "
"also accesses @code{hack}'s local variable @code{addition}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11533
msgid ""
"It is safe for @code{intermediate} to call @code{store} because "
"@code{hack}'s stack frame, with its arguments and local variables, continues "
"to exist during the call to @code{intermediate}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11539
msgid ""
"Calling the nested function through its address after the containing "
"function has exited is asking for trouble.  If it is called after a "
"containing scope level has exited, and if it refers to some of the variables "
"that are no longer in scope, it will refer to memory containing junk or "
"other data.  It's not wise to take the risk."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11544
msgid ""
"The GNU C Compiler implements taking the address of a nested function using "
"a technique called @dfn{trampolines}.  This technique was described in "
"@cite{Lexical Closures for C@t{++}} (Thomas M. Breuel, USENIX C@t{++} "
"Conference Proceedings, October 17--21, 1988)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11551
msgid ""
"A nested function can jump to a label inherited from a containing function, "
"provided the label was explicitly declared in the containing function "
"(@pxref{Local Labels}).  Such a jump returns instantly to the containing "
"function, exiting the nested function that did the @code{goto} and any "
"intermediate function invocations as well.  Here is an example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11566
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"bar (int *array, int offset, int size)\n"
"@{\n"
"  /* @r{Explicitly declare the label @code{failure}.} */\n"
"  __label__ failure;\n"
"  int access (int *array, int index)\n"
"    @{\n"
"      if (index > size)\n"
"        /* @r{Exit this function,}\n"
"           @r{and return to @code{bar}.} */\n"
"        goto failure;\n"
"      return array[index + offset];\n"
"    @}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11575
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  int i;\n"
"  @r{@dots{}}\n"
"  for (i = 0; i < size; i++)\n"
"    @r{@dots{}} access (array, i) @r{@dots{}}\n"
"  @r{@dots{}}\n"
"  return 0;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11581
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
" /* @r{Control comes here from @code{access}\n"
"    if it does the @code{goto}.}  */\n"
" failure:\n"
"  return -1;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11587
msgid ""
"To declare the nested function before its definition, use @code{auto} (which "
"is otherwise meaningless for function declarations; @pxref{auto and "
"register}).  For example,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11601
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"bar (int *array, int offset, int size)\n"
"@{\n"
"  auto int access (int *, int);\n"
"  @r{@dots{}}\n"
"  @r{@dots{}} access (array, i) @r{@dots{}}\n"
"  @r{@dots{}}\n"
"  int access (int *array, int index)\n"
"    @{\n"
"      @r{@dots{}}\n"
"    @}\n"
"  @r{@dots{}}\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11605
#, no-wrap
msgid "inline function definitions"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11606
#, no-wrap
msgid "function definitions, inline"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11607
#, no-wrap
msgid "inline"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11613
msgid ""
"To declare a function inline, use the @code{inline} keyword in its "
"definition.  Here's code to define functions to access two fields in a "
"structure, and inlines them so that there is no cost to accessing them by "
"calling the functions."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11619
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct list\n"
"@{\n"
"  struct list *first, *second;\n"
"@};\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11625
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"inline struct list *\n"
"list_first (struct list *p)\n"
"@{\n"
"  return p->first;\n"
"@}\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11631
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"inline struct list *\n"
"list_second (struct list *p)\n"
"@{\n"
"  return p->second;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11637
msgid ""
"Optimized compilation can substitute the inline function's body for any call "
"to it.  This is called @emph{inlining} the function.  It makes the code that "
"contains the call run faster, significantly so if the inline function is "
"small."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11639
msgid "Here's a function that uses @code{list_second}:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11652
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int\n"
"pairlist_length (struct list *l)\n"
"@{\n"
"  int length = 0;\n"
"  while (l)\n"
"    @{\n"
"      length++;\n"
"      l = list_second (l);\n"
"    @}\n"
"  return length;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11656
msgid ""
"Substituting the code of @code{list_second} into the definition of "
"@code{pairlist_length} results in this code, in effect:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11669
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int\n"
"pairlist_length (struct list *l)\n"
"@{\n"
"  int length = 0;\n"
"  while (l)\n"
"    @{\n"
"      length++;\n"
"      l = l->second;\n"
"    @}\n"
"  return length;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11676
msgid ""
"Since the definition of @code{list_second} does not say @code{extern} or "
"@code{static}, that definition is used only for inlining.  It doesn't "
"generate code that can be called at run time.  If not all the calls to the "
"function are inlined, there must be a definition of the same function name "
"in another module for them to call."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11677
#, no-wrap
msgid "inline functions, omission of"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11686
msgid ""
"Adding @code{static} to an inline function definition means the function "
"definition is limited to this compilation module.  Also, it generates "
"run-time code if necessary for the sake of any calls that were not inlined.  "
"If all calls are inlined then the function definition does not generate "
"run-time code, but you can force generation of run-time code with the option "
"@option{-fkeep-inline-functions}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11687
#, no-wrap
msgid "extern inline function"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11693
msgid ""
"Specifying @code{extern} along with @code{inline} means the function is "
"external and generates run-time code to be called from other separately "
"compiled modules, as well as inlined.  You can define the function as "
"@code{inline} without @code{extern} in other modules so as to inline calls "
"to the same function in those modules."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11696
msgid ""
"Why are some calls not inlined? First of all, inlining is an optimization, "
"so non-optimized compilation does not inline."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11704
msgid ""
"Some calls cannot be inlined for technical reasons.  Also, certain usages in "
"a function definition can make it unsuitable for inline substitution.  Among "
"these usages are: variadic functions, use of @code{alloca}, use of computed "
"goto (@pxref{Labels as Values}), and use of nonlocal goto.  The option "
"@option{-Winline} requests a warning when a function marked @code{inline} is "
"unsuitable to be inlined.  The warning explains what obstacle makes it "
"unsuitable."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11708
msgid ""
"Just because a call @emph{can} be inlined does not mean it @emph{should} be "
"inlined.  The GNU C compiler weighs costs and benefits to decide whether "
"inlining a particular call is advantageous."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11712
msgid ""
"You can force inlining of all calls to a given function that can be inlined, "
"even in a non-optimized compilation. by specifying the @samp{always_inline} "
"attribute for the function, like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11716
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"/* @r{Prototype.}  */\n"
"inline void foo (const char) __attribute__((always_inline));\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11720
msgid "This is a GNU C extension.  @xref{Attributes}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11728
msgid ""
"A function call may be inlined even if not declared @code{inline} in special "
"cases where the compiler can determine this is correct and desirable.  For "
"instance, when a static function is called only once, it will very likely be "
"inlined.  With @option{-flto}, link-time optimization, any function might be "
"inlined.  To absolutely prevent inlining of a specific function, specify "
"@code{__attribute__((__noinline__))} in the function's definition."
msgstr ""

#. type: section
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11730
#, no-wrap
msgid "Obsolete Function Features"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11735
msgid ""
"These features of function definitions are still used in old programs, but "
"you shouldn't write code this way today.  If you are just learning C, you "
"can skip this section."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11739 c-intro-and-ref.texi:11741
#, no-wrap
msgid "Old GNU Inlining"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11739
msgid "An older inlining technique."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11739 c-intro-and-ref.texi:11766
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11767
#, no-wrap
msgid "Old-Style Function Definitions"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11739
msgid "Original K&R style functions."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11742
#, no-wrap
msgid "Older GNU C Inlining"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11751
msgid ""
"The GNU C spec for inline functions, before GCC version 5, defined "
"@code{extern inline} on a function definition to mean to inline calls to it "
"but @emph{not} generate code for the function that could be called at run "
"time.  By contrast, @code{inline} without @code{extern} specified to "
"generate run-time code for the function.  In effect, ISO incompatibly "
"flipped the meanings of these two cases.  We changed GCC in version 5 to "
"adopt the ISO specification."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11756
msgid ""
"Many programs still use these cases with the previous GNU C meanings.  You "
"can specify use of those meanings with the option @option{-fgnu89-inline}.  "
"You can also specify this for a single function with @code{__attribute__ "
"((gnu_inline))}.  Here's an example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11764
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"inline __attribute__ ((gnu_inline))\n"
"int\n"
"inc (int *a)\n"
"@{\n"
"  (*a)++;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11768
#, no-wrap
msgid "old-style function definitions"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11769
#, no-wrap
msgid "function definitions, old-style"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11770
#, no-wrap
msgid "K&R-style function definitions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11786
msgid ""
"An @dfn{old-style} (or ``K&R'') function definition is the way function "
"definitions were written in the 1980s.  It looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11794
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@var{rettype}\n"
"@var{function} (@var{parmnames})\n"
"  @var{parm_declarations}\n"
"@{\n"
"  @var{body}\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11801
msgid ""
"In @var{parmnames}, only the parameter names are listed, separated by "
"commas.  Then @var{parm_declarations} declares their data types; these "
"declarations look just like variable declarations.  If a parameter is listed "
"in @var{parmnames} but has no declaration, it is implicitly declared "
"@code{int}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11804
msgid ""
"There is no reason to write a definition this way nowadays, but they can "
"still be seen in older GNU programs."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11806
msgid "An old-style variadic function definition looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11809
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"#include <varargs.h>\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11816
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int\n"
"add_multiple_values (va_alist)\n"
"    va_dcl\n"
"@{\n"
"  int argcount;\n"
"  int counter, total = 0;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11822
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  /* @r{Initialize that variable.} */\n"
"  va_start (argptr);\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11825
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  /* @r{Get the first argument (fixed).} */\n"
"  argcount = va_arg (int);\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11842
msgid ""
"Note that the old-style variadic function definition has no fixed parameter "
"variables; all arguments must be obtained with @code{va_arg}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11845
#, no-wrap
msgid "compatible types"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11846
#, no-wrap
msgid "types, compatible"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11852
msgid ""
"Declaring a function or variable twice is valid in C only if the two "
"declarations specify @dfn{compatible} types.  In addition, some operations "
"on pointers require operands to have compatible target types."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11857
msgid ""
"In C, two different primitive types are never compatible.  Likewise for the "
"defined types @code{struct}, @code{union} and @code{enum}: two separately "
"defined types are incompatible unless they are defined exactly the same way."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11860
msgid "However, there are a few cases where different types can be compatible:"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11866
msgid ""
"Every enumeration type is compatible with some integer type.  In GNU C, the "
"choice of integer type depends on the largest enumeration value."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11879
msgid ""
"Array types are compatible if the element types are compatible and the sizes "
"(when specified) match."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11883
msgid ""
"Pointer types are compatible if the pointer target types are compatible."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11889
msgid ""
"Function types that specify argument types are compatible if the return "
"types are compatible and the argument types are compatible, argument by "
"argument.  In addition, they must all agree in whether they use @code{...} "
"to allow additional arguments."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11893
msgid ""
"Function types that don't specify argument types are compatible if the "
"return types are."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11899
msgid ""
"Function types that specify the argument types are compatible with function "
"types that omit them, if the return types are compatible and the specified "
"argument types are unaltered by the argument promotions (@pxref{Argument "
"Promotions})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11905
msgid ""
"In order for types to be compatible, they must agree in their type "
"qualifiers.  Thus, @code{const int} and @code{int} are incompatible.  It "
"follows that @code{const int *} and @code{int *} are incompatible too (they "
"are pointers to types that are not compatible)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11912
msgid ""
"If two types are compatible ignoring the qualifiers, we call them "
"@dfn{nearly compatible}.  (If they are array types, we ignore qualifiers on "
"the element types.@footnote{This is a GNU C extension.})  Comparison of "
"pointers is valid if the pointers' target types are nearly compatible.  "
"Likewise, the two branches of a conditional expression may be pointers to "
"nearly compatible target types."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11918
msgid ""
"If two types are compatible ignoring the qualifiers, and the first type has "
"all the qualifiers of the second type, we say the first is @dfn{upward "
"compatible} with the second.  Assignment of pointers requires the assigned "
"pointer's target type to be upward compatible with the right operand (the "
"new value)'s target type."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11921
#, no-wrap
msgid "type conversions"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11922
#, no-wrap
msgid "conversions, type"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11926
msgid ""
"C converts between data types automatically when that seems clearly "
"necessary.  In addition, you can convert explicitly with a @dfn{cast}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11937
#, no-wrap
msgid "cast"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11938
#, no-wrap
msgid "explicit type conversion"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11944
msgid ""
"You can do explicit conversions using the unary @dfn{cast} operator, which "
"is written as a type designator (@pxref{Type Designators}) in parentheses.  "
"For example, @code{(int)} is the operator to cast to type @code{int}.  "
"Here's an example of using it:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11948
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"@{\n"
"  double d = 5.5;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11952
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  printf (\"Floating point value: %f\\n"
"\", d);\n"
"  printf (\"Rounded to integer: %d\\n"
"\", (int) d);\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11958
msgid ""
"Using @code{(int) d} passes an @code{int} value as argument to "
"@code{printf}, so you can print it with @samp{%d}.  Using just @code{d} "
"without the cast would pass the value as @code{double}.  That won't work at "
"all with @samp{%d}; the results would be gibberish."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11961
msgid ""
"To divide one integer by another without rounding, cast either of the "
"integers to @code{double} first:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11965
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"(double) @var{dividend} / @var{divisor}\n"
"@var{dividend} / (double) @var{divisor}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11969
msgid ""
"It is enough to cast one of them, because that forces the common type to "
"@code{double} so the other will be converted automatically."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11971
msgid "The valid cast conversions are:"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11975
msgid "One numerical type to another."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11980
msgid ""
"One pointer type to another.  (Converting between pointers that point to "
"functions and pointers that point to data is not standard C.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11983
msgid "A pointer type to an integer type."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11986
msgid "An integer type to a pointer type."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11990
msgid ""
"To a union type, from the type of any alternative in the union "
"(@pxref{Unions}).  (This is a GNU extension.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11993
msgid "Anything, to @code{void}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:11997
#, no-wrap
msgid "assignment type conversions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12002
msgid ""
"Certain type conversions occur automatically in assignments and certain "
"other contexts.  These are the conversions assignments can do:"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12006
msgid "Converting any numeric type to any other numeric type."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12010
msgid ""
"Converting @code{void *} to any other pointer type (except "
"pointer-to-function types)."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12014
msgid ""
"Converting any other pointer type to @code{void *}.  (except "
"pointer-to-function types)."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12017
msgid "Converting 0 (a null pointer constant) to any pointer type."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12021
msgid ""
"Converting any pointer type to @code{bool}.  (The result is 1 if the pointer "
"is not null.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12026
msgid ""
"Converting between pointer types when the left-hand target type is upward "
"compatible with the right-hand target type.  @xref{Compatible Types}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12030
msgid ""
"These type conversions occur automatically in certain contexts, which are:"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12035
msgid ""
"An assignment converts the type of the right-hand expression to the type "
"wanted by the left-hand expression.  For example,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12039
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"double i;\n"
"i = 5;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12043 c-intro-and-ref.texi:12055
msgid "converts 5 to @code{double}."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12047
msgid ""
"A function call, when the function specifies the type for that argument, "
"converts the argument value to that type.  For example,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12051
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void foo (double);\n"
"foo (5);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12059
msgid ""
"A @code{return} statement converts the specified value to the type that the "
"function is declared to return.  For example,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12066
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"double\n"
"foo ()\n"
"@{\n"
"  return 5;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12070
msgid "also converts 5 to @code{double}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12074
msgid ""
"In all three contexts, if the conversion is impossible, that constitutes an "
"error."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12077
#, no-wrap
msgid "argument promotions"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12078
#, no-wrap
msgid "promotion of arguments"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12083
msgid ""
"When a function's definition or declaration does not specify the type of an "
"argument, that argument is passed without conversion in whatever type it "
"has, with these exceptions:"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12093
msgid ""
"Some narrow numeric values are @dfn{promoted} to a wider type.  If the "
"expression is a narrow integer, such as @code{char} or @code{short}, the "
"call converts it automatically to @code{int} (@pxref{Integer "
"Types}).@footnote{On an embedded controller where @code{char} or "
"@code{short} is the same width as @code{int}, @code{unsigned char} or "
"@code{unsigned short} promotes to @code{unsigned int}, but that never occurs "
"in GNU C on real computers.}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12095
msgid "In this example, the expression @code{c} is passed as an @code{int}:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12098
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"char c = '$';\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12100
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"printf (\"Character c is '%c'\\n"
"\", c);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12106
msgid ""
"If the expression has type @code{float}, the call converts it automatically "
"to @code{double}."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12109
msgid "An array as argument is converted to a pointer to its zeroth element."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12112
msgid "A function name as argument is converted to a pointer to that function."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12116
#, no-wrap
msgid "operand promotions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12122
msgid ""
"The operands in arithmetic operations undergo type conversion "
"automatically.  These @dfn{operand promotions} are the same as the argument "
"promotions except without converting @code{float} to @code{double}.  In "
"other words, the operand promotions convert"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12126
msgid "@code{char} or @code{short} (whether signed or not) to @code{int}."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12129
msgid "an array to a pointer to its zeroth element, and"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12132
msgid "a function name to a pointer to that function."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12136
#, no-wrap
msgid "common type"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12142
msgid ""
"Arithmetic binary operators (except the shift operators) convert their "
"operands to the @dfn{common type} before operating on them.  Conditional "
"expressions also convert the two possible results to their common type.  "
"Here are the rules for determining the common type."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12145
msgid ""
"If one of the numbers has a floating-point type and the other is an integer, "
"the common type is that floating-point type.  For instance,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12148
#, no-wrap
msgid "5.6 * 2   @result{} 11.2 /* @r{a @code{double} value} */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12152
msgid ""
"If both are floating point, the type with the larger range is the common "
"type."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12155
msgid ""
"If both are integers but of different widths, the common type is the wider "
"of the two."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12160
msgid ""
"If they are integer types of the same width, the common type is unsigned if "
"either operand is unsigned, and it's @code{long} if either operand is "
"@code{long}.  It's @code{long long} if either operand is @code{long long}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12165
msgid ""
"These rules apply to addition, subtraction, multiplication, division, "
"remainder, comparisons, and bitwise operations.  They also apply to the two "
"branches of a conditional expression, and to the arithmetic done in a "
"modifying assignment operation."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12168
#, no-wrap
msgid "scope"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12169
#, no-wrap
msgid "block scope"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12170
#, no-wrap
msgid "function scope"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12171
#, no-wrap
msgid "function prototype scope"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12176
msgid ""
"Each definition or declaration of an identifier is visible in certain parts "
"of the program, which is typically less than the whole of the program.  The "
"parts where it is visible are called its @dfn{scope}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12181
msgid ""
"Normally, declarations made at the top-level in the source---that is, not "
"within any blocks and function definitions---are visible for the entire "
"contents of the source file after that point.  This is called @dfn{file "
"scope} (@pxref{File-Scope Variables})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12185
msgid ""
"Declarations made within blocks of code, including within function "
"definitions, are visible only within those blocks.  This is called "
"@dfn{block scope}.  Here is an example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12193
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"foo (void)\n"
"@{\n"
"  int x = 42;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12201
msgid ""
"In this example, the variable @code{x} has block scope; it is visible only "
"within the @code{foo} function definition block.  Thus, other blocks could "
"have their own variables, also named @code{x}, without any conflict between "
"those variables."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12204
msgid ""
"A variable declared inside a subblock has a scope limited to that subblock,"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12215
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"foo (void)\n"
"@{\n"
"  @{\n"
"    int x = 42;\n"
"  @}\n"
"  // @r{@code{x} is out of scope here.}\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12221
msgid ""
"If a variable declared within a block has the same name as a variable "
"declared outside of that block, the definition within the block takes "
"precedence during its scope:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12225 c-intro-and-ref.texi:12266
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int x = 42;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12232
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"foo (void)\n"
"@{\n"
"  int x = 17;\n"
"  printf (\"%d\\n"
"\", x);\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12241
msgid ""
"This prints 17, the value of the variable @code{x} declared in the function "
"body block, rather than the value of the variable @code{x} at file scope.  "
"We say that the inner declaration of @code{x} @dfn{shadows} the outer "
"declaration, for the extent of the inner declaration's scope."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12244
msgid ""
"A declaration with block scope can be shadowed by another declaration with "
"the same name in a subblock."
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12257
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"foo (void)\n"
"@{\n"
"  char *x = \"foo\";\n"
"  @{\n"
"    int x = 42;\n"
"    @r{@dots{}}\n"
"    exit (x / 6);\n"
"  @}\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12262
msgid ""
"A function parameter's scope is the entire function body, but it can be "
"shadowed.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12272
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"foo (int x)\n"
"@{\n"
"  printf (\"%d\\n"
"\", x);\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12278
msgid ""
"This prints the value of @code{x} the function parameter, rather than the "
"value of the file-scope variable @code{x}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12282
msgid ""
"Labels (@pxref{goto Statement}) have @dfn{function} scope: each label is "
"visible for the whole of the containing function body, both before and after "
"the label declaration:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12297
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void\n"
"foo (void)\n"
"@{\n"
"  @r{@dots{}}\n"
"  goto bar;\n"
"  @r{@dots{}}\n"
"  @{  // @r{Subblock does not affect labels.}\n"
"    bar:\n"
"    @r{@dots{}}\n"
"  @}\n"
"  goto bar;\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12302
msgid ""
"Except for labels, a declared identifier is not visible to code before its "
"declaration.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12307
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int x = 5;\n"
"int y = x + 10;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12312
msgid "will work, but:"
msgstr ""

#. type: group
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12317
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int x = y + 10;\n"
"int y = 5;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12322
msgid "cannot refer to the variable @code{y} before its declaration."
msgstr ""

#. type: include
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12323
#, no-wrap
msgid "cpp.texi"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12331
msgid ""
"This chapter explains the machine-level details of integer types: how they "
"are represented as bits in memory, and the range of possible values for each "
"integer type."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12340
#, no-wrap
msgid "integer representations"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12341
#, no-wrap
msgid "representation of integers"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12351
msgid ""
"Modern computers store integer values as binary (base-2) numbers that occupy "
"a single unit of storage, typically either as an 8-bit @code{char}, a 16-bit "
"@code{short int}, a 32-bit @code{int}, or possibly, a 64-bit @code{long long "
"int}.  Whether a @code{long int} is a 32-bit or a 64-bit value is system "
"dependent.@footnote{In theory, any of these types could have some other "
"size, bit it's not worth even a minute to cater to that possibility.  It "
"never happens on GNU/Linux.}"
msgstr ""

#. type: code{#1}
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12352
#, no-wrap
msgid "CHAR_BIT"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12356
msgid ""
"The macro @code{CHAR_BIT}, defined in @file{limits.h}, gives the number of "
"bits in type @code{char}.  On any real operating system, the value is 8."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12360
msgid ""
"The fixed sizes of numeric types necessarily limit their @dfn{range of "
"values}, and the particular encoding of integers decides what that range is."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12361
#, no-wrap
msgid "two's-complement representation"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12368
msgid ""
"For unsigned integers, the entire space is used to represent a nonnegative "
"value.  Signed integers are stored using @dfn{two's-complement "
"representation}: a signed integer with @var{n} bits has a range from "
"@math{-2@sup{(@var{n} - 1)}} to @minus{}1 to 0 to 1 to @math{+2@sup{(@var{n} "
"- 1)} - 1}, inclusive.  The leftmost, or high-order, bit is called the "
"@dfn{sign bit}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12373
msgid ""
"In two's-complement representation, there is only one value that means zero, "
"and the most negative number lacks a positive counterpart.  As a result, "
"negating that number causes overflow; in practice, its result is that number "
"back again.  We will revisit that peculiarity shortly."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12378
msgid ""
"For example, a two's-complement signed 8-bit integer can represent all "
"decimal numbers from @minus{}128 to +127.  Negating @minus{}128 ought to "
"give +128, but that value won't fit in 8 bits, so the operation yields "
"@minus{}128."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12382
msgid ""
"Decades ago, there were computers that used other representations for signed "
"integers, but they are long gone and not worth any effort to support.  The "
"GNU C language does not support them."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12389
msgid ""
"When an arithmetic operation produces a value that is too big to represent, "
"the operation is said to @dfn{overflow}.  In C, integer overflow does not "
"interrupt the control flow or signal an error.  What it does depends on "
"signedness."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12396
msgid ""
"For unsigned arithmetic, the result of an operation that overflows is the "
"@var{n} low-order bits of the correct value.  If the correct value is "
"representable in @var{n} bits, that is always the result; thus we often say "
"that ``integer arithmetic is exact,'' omitting the crucial qualifying phrase "
"``as long as the exact result is representable.''"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12400
msgid ""
"In principle, a C program should be written so that overflow never occurs "
"for signed integers, but in GNU C you can specify various ways of handling "
"such overflow (@pxref{Integer Overflow})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12404
msgid ""
"Integer representations are best understood by looking at a table for a tiny "
"integer size; here are the possible values for an integer with three bits:"
msgstr ""

#. type: multitable
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12407
msgid "@headitem Unsigned"
msgstr ""

#. type: multitable
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12407
msgid "Signed"
msgstr ""

#. type: multitable
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12407
msgid "Bits"
msgstr ""

#. type: multitable
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12407
msgid "2s Complement"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12407
#, no-wrap
msgid "0 @tab 0 @tab 000 @tab 000 (0)"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12408
#, no-wrap
msgid "1 @tab 1 @tab 001 @tab 111 (-1)"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12409
#, no-wrap
msgid "2 @tab 2 @tab 010 @tab 110 (-2)"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12410
#, no-wrap
msgid "3 @tab 3 @tab 011 @tab 101 (-3)"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12411
#, no-wrap
msgid "4 @tab -4 @tab 100 @tab 100 (-4)"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12412
#, no-wrap
msgid "5 @tab -3 @tab 101 @tab 011 (3)"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12413
#, no-wrap
msgid "6 @tab -2 @tab 110 @tab 010 (2)"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12414
#, no-wrap
msgid "7 @tab -1 @tab 111 @tab 001 (1)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12421
msgid ""
"The parenthesized decimal numbers in the last column represent the signed "
"meanings of the two's-complement of the line's value.  Recall that, in "
"two's-complement encoding, the high-order bit is 0 when the number is "
"nonnegative."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12426
msgid ""
"We can now understand the peculiar behavior of negation of the most negative "
"two's-complement integer: start with 0b100, invert the bits to get 0b011, "
"and add 1: we get 0b100, the value we started with."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12428
msgid "We can also see overflow behavior in two's-complement:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12433
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"3 + 1 = 0b011 + 0b001 = 0b100 = (-4)\n"
"3 + 2 = 0b011 + 0b010 = 0b101 = (-3)\n"
"3 + 3 = 0b011 + 0b011 = 0b110 = (-2)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12439
msgid ""
"A sum of two nonnegative signed values that overflows has a 1 in the sign "
"bit, so the exact positive result is truncated to a negative value."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12444
#, no-wrap
msgid "maximum integer values"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12445
#, no-wrap
msgid "minimum integer values"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12446
#, no-wrap
msgid "integer ranges"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12447
#, no-wrap
msgid "ranges of integer types"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12448
#, no-wrap
msgid "INT_MAX"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12449
#, no-wrap
msgid "UINT_MAX"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12450
#, no-wrap
msgid "SHRT_MAX"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12451
#, no-wrap
msgid "LONG_MAX"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12452
#, no-wrap
msgid "LLONG_MAX"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12453
#, no-wrap
msgid "USHRT_MAX"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12454
#, no-wrap
msgid "ULONG_MAX"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12455
#, no-wrap
msgid "ULLONG_MAX"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12456
#, no-wrap
msgid "CHAR_MAX"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12457
#, no-wrap
msgid "SCHAR_MAX"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12458
#, no-wrap
msgid "UCHAR_MAX"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12469
msgid ""
"For each primitive integer type, there is a standard macro defined in "
"@file{limits.h} that gives the largest value that type can hold.  For "
"instance, for type @code{int}, the maximum value is @code{INT_MAX}.  On a "
"32-bit computer, that is equal to 2,147,483,647.  The maximum value for "
"@code{unsigned int} is @code{UINT_MAX}, which on a 32-bit computer is equal "
"to 4,294,967,295.  Likewise, there are @code{SHRT_MAX}, @code{LONG_MAX}, and "
"@code{LLONG_MAX}, and corresponding unsigned limits @code{USHRT_MAX}, "
"@code{ULONG_MAX}, and @code{ULLONG_MAX}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12473
msgid ""
"Since there are three ways to specify a @code{char} type, there are also "
"three limits: @code{CHAR_MAX}, @code{SCHAR_MAX}, and @code{UCHAR_MAX}."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12474
#, no-wrap
msgid "INT_MIN"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12480
msgid ""
"For each type that is or might be signed, there is another symbol that gives "
"the minimum value it can hold.  (Just replace @code{MAX} with @code{MIN} in "
"the names listed above.)  There is no minimum limit symbol for types "
"specified with @code{unsigned} because the minimum for them is universally "
"zero."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12488
msgid ""
"@code{INT_MIN} is not the negative of @code{INT_MAX}.  In two's-complement "
"representation, the most negative number is 1 less than the negative of the "
"most positive number.  Thus, @code{INT_MIN} on a 32-bit computer has the "
"value @minus{}2,147,483,648.  You can't actually write the value that way in "
"C, since it would overflow.  That's a good reason to use @code{INT_MIN} to "
"specify that value.  Its definition is written to avoid overflow."
msgstr ""

#. type: include
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12489
#, no-wrap
msgid "fp.texi"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12493
#, no-wrap
msgid "object file"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12494
#, no-wrap
msgid "compilation module"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12495
#, no-wrap
msgid "make rules"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12496
#, no-wrap
msgid "link"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12506
msgid ""
"Early in the manual we explained how to compile a simple C program that "
"consists of a single source file (@pxref{Compile Example}).  However, we "
"handle only short programs that way.  A typical C program consists of many "
"source files, each of which is usually a separate @dfn{compilation "
"module}---meaning that it has to be compiled separately.  (The source files "
"that are not separate compilation modules are those that are used via "
"@code{#include}; see @ref{Header Files}.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12511
msgid ""
"To compile a multi-module program, you compile each of the program's "
"compilation modules, making an @dfn{object file} for that module.  The last "
"step is to @dfn{link} the many object files together into a single "
"executable for the whole program."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12517
msgid ""
"For the full details of how to compile C programs (and other languages' "
"programs) with GCC, see @ref{Top,,, gcc, Using the GNU Compiler "
"Collection}.  On the Web, all is available through "
"@url{https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/}.  Here we give only a simple "
"introduction."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12520
msgid ""
"These commands compile two compilation modules, @file{foo.c} and "
"@file{bar.c}, running the compiler for each module:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12524
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"gcc -c -O -g foo.c\n"
"gcc -c -O -g bar.c\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12532
msgid ""
"In these commands, @option{-g} says to generate debugging information, "
"@option{-O} says to do some optimization, and @option{-c} says to put the "
"compiled code for that module into a corresponding object file and go no "
"further.  The object file for @file{foo.c} is automatically called "
"@file{foo.o}, and so on."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12536
msgid ""
"If you wish, you can specify the additional compilation options.  For "
"instance, @option{-Wformat -Wparenthesis -Wstrict-prototypes} request "
"additional warnings."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12537
#, no-wrap
msgid "linking object files"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12540
msgid ""
"After you compile all the program's modules, you link the object files into "
"a combined executable, like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12543
#, no-wrap
msgid "gcc -o foo foo.o bar.o\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12550
msgid ""
"In this command, @option{-o foo} species the file name for the executable "
"file, and the other arguments are the object files to link.  Always specify "
"the executable file name in a command that generates one."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12556
msgid ""
"One reason to divide a large program into multiple compilation modules is to "
"control how each module can access the internals of the others.  When a "
"module declares a function or variable @code{extern}, other modules can "
"access it.  The other functions and variables defined in a module can't be "
"accessed from outside that module."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12563
msgid ""
"The other reason for using multiple modules is so that changing one source "
"file does not require recompiling all of them in order to try the modified "
"program.  It is sufficient to recompile the source file that you changed, "
"then link them all again.  Dividing a large program into many substantial "
"modules in this way typically makes recompilation much faster."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12569
msgid ""
"Normally we don't run any of these commands directly.  Instead we write a "
"set of @dfn{make rules} for the program, then use the @command{make} program "
"to recompile only the source files that need to be recompiled, by following "
"those rules.  @xref{Top, The GNU Make Manual, , make, The GNU Make Manual}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12576
msgid ""
"This chapter describes C constructs that don't alter the program's meaning "
"@emph{as such}, but rather direct the compiler how to treat some aspects of "
"the program."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12587
msgid ""
"A @dfn{pragma} is an annotation in a program that gives direction to the "
"compiler."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12592 c-intro-and-ref.texi:12596
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12597
#, no-wrap
msgid "Pragma Basics"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12592
msgid "Pragma syntax and usage."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12592 c-intro-and-ref.texi:12724
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12725
#, no-wrap
msgid "Severity Pragmas"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12592
msgid "Settings for compile-time pragma output."
msgstr ""

#. type: subsection
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12592 c-intro-and-ref.texi:12801
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12802
#, no-wrap
msgid "Optimization Pragmas"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12592
msgid "Controlling optimizations."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12602
msgid ""
"C defines two syntactical forms for pragmas, the line form and the token "
"form.  You can write any pragma in either form, with the same meaning."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12604
msgid "The line form is a line in the source code, like this:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12607
#, no-wrap
msgid "#pragma @var{line}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12612
msgid ""
"The line pragma has no effect on the parsing of the lines around it.  This "
"form has the drawback that it can't be generated by a macro expansion."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12615
msgid ""
"The token form is a series of tokens; it can appear anywhere in the program "
"between the other tokens."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12618
#, no-wrap
msgid "_Pragma (@var{stringconstant})\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12623
msgid ""
"The pragma has no effect on the syntax of the tokens that surround it; thus, "
"here's a pragma in the middle of an @code{if} statement:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12626
#, no-wrap
msgid "if _Pragma (\"hello\") (x > 1)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12632
msgid ""
"However, that's an unclear thing to do; for the sake of understandability, "
"it is better to put a pragma on a line by itself and not embedded in the "
"middle of another construct."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12638
msgid ""
"Both forms of pragma have a textual argument.  In a line pragma, the text is "
"the rest of the line.  The textual argument to @code{_Pragma} uses the same "
"syntax as a C string constant: surround the text with two @samp{\"} "
"characters, and add a backslash before each @samp{\"} or @samp{\\} character "
"in it."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12642
msgid ""
"With either syntax, the textual argument specifies what to do.  It begins "
"with one or several words that specify the operation.  If the compiler does "
"not recognize them, it ignores the pragma."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12644
msgid "Here are the pragma operations supported in GNU C@."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12647
#, no-wrap
msgid "#pragma GCC dependency \"@var{file}\" [@var{message}]"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12648
#, no-wrap
msgid "_Pragma (\"GCC dependency \\\"@var{file}\\\" [@var{message}]\")"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12652
msgid ""
"Declares that the current source file depends on @var{file}, so GNU C "
"compares the file times and gives a warning if @var{file} is newer than the "
"current source file."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12655
msgid ""
"This directive searches for @var{file} the way @code{#include} searches for "
"a non-system header file."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12657
msgid "If @var{message} is given, the warning message includes that text."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12659
msgid "Examples:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12664
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"#pragma GCC dependency \"parse.y\"\n"
"_pragma (\"GCC dependency \\\"/usr/include/time.h\\\" \\\n"
"rerun fixincludes\")\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12666
#, no-wrap
msgid "#pragma GCC poison @var{identifiers}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12667
#, no-wrap
msgid "_Pragma (\"GCC poison @var{identifiers}\")"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12669
msgid "Poisons the identifiers listed in @var{identifiers}."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12674
msgid ""
"This is useful to make sure all mention of @var{identifiers} has been "
"deleted from the program and that no reference to them creeps back in.  If "
"any of those identifiers appears anywhere in the source after the directive, "
"it causes a compilation error.  For example,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12678
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"#pragma GCC poison printf sprintf fprintf\n"
"sprintf(some_string, \"hello\");\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12682
msgid "generates an error."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12687
msgid ""
"If a poisoned identifier appears as part of the expansion of a macro that "
"was defined before the identifier was poisoned, it will @emph{not} cause an "
"error.  Thus, system headers that define macros that use the identifier will "
"not cause errors."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12694
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"#define strrchr rindex\n"
"_Pragma (\"GCC poison rindex\")\n"
"strrchr(some_string, 'h');\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12698
msgid "does not cause a compilation error."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12699
#, no-wrap
msgid "#pragma GCC system_header"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12700
#, no-wrap
msgid "_Pragma (\"GCC system_header\")"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12704
msgid ""
"Specify treating the rest of the current source file as if it came from a "
"system header file.  @xref{System Headers, System Headers, System Headers, "
"gcc, Using the GNU Compiler Collection}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12705
#, no-wrap
msgid "#pragma GCC warning @var{message}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12706
#, no-wrap
msgid "_Pragma (\"GCC warning @var{message}\")"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12709
msgid ""
"Equivalent to @code{#warning}.  Its advantage is that the @code{_Pragma} "
"form can be included in a macro definition."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12710
#, no-wrap
msgid "#pragma GCC error @var{message}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12711
#, no-wrap
msgid "_Pragma (\"GCC error @var{message}\")"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12714
msgid ""
"Equivalent to @code{#error}.  Its advantage is that the @code{_Pragma} form "
"can be included in a macro definition."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12715
#, no-wrap
msgid "#pragma message @var{message}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12716
#, no-wrap
msgid "_Pragma (\"message @var{message}\")"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12722
msgid ""
"Similar to @samp{GCC warning} and @samp{GCC error}, this simply prints an "
"informational message, and could be used to include additional warning or "
"error text without triggering more warnings or errors.  (Note that unlike "
"@samp{GCC warning} and @samp{GCC error}, @samp{message} does not include "
"@samp{GCC} as part of the pragma.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12730
msgid ""
"These pragmas control the severity of classes of diagnostics.  You can "
"specify the class of diagnostic with the GCC option that causes those "
"diagnostics to be generated."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12732
#, no-wrap
msgid "#pragma GCC diagnostic error @var{option}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12733
#, no-wrap
msgid "_Pragma (\"GCC diagnostic error @var{option}\")"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12736
msgid ""
"For code following this pragma, treat diagnostics of the variety specified "
"by @var{option} as errors.  For example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12739
#, no-wrap
msgid "_Pragma (\"GCC diagnostic error -Wformat\")\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12744
msgid ""
"specifies to treat diagnostics enabled by the @var{-Wformat} option as "
"errors rather than warnings."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12745
#, no-wrap
msgid "#pragma GCC diagnostic warning @var{option}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12746
#, no-wrap
msgid "_Pragma (\"GCC diagnostic warning @var{option}\")"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12750
msgid ""
"For code following this pragma, treat diagnostics of the variety specified "
"by @var{option} as warnings.  This overrides the @var{-Werror} option which "
"says to treat warnings as errors."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12751
#, no-wrap
msgid "#pragma GCC diagnostic ignore @var{option}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12752
#, no-wrap
msgid "_Pragma (\"GCC diagnostic ignore @var{option}\")"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12755
msgid ""
"For code following this pragma, refrain from reporting any diagnostics of "
"the variety specified by @var{option}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12756
#, no-wrap
msgid "#pragma GCC diagnostic push"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12757
#, no-wrap
msgid "_Pragma (\"GCC diagnostic push\")"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12758
#, no-wrap
msgid "#pragma GCC diagnostic pop"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12759
#, no-wrap
msgid "_Pragma (\"GCC diagnostic pop\")"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12764
msgid ""
"These pragmas maintain a stack of states for severity settings.  @samp{GCC "
"diagnostic push} saves the current settings on the stack, and @samp{GCC "
"diagnostic pop} pops the last stack item and restores the current settings "
"from that."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12767
msgid ""
"@samp{GCC diagnostic pop} when the severity setting stack is empty restores "
"the settings to what they were at the start of compilation."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12772
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"_Pragma (\"GCC diagnostic error -Wformat\")\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12774
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"/* @r{@option{-Wformat} messages treated as errors. } */\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12777
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"_Pragma (\"GCC diagnostic push\")\n"
"_Pragma (\"GCC diagnostic warning -Wformat\")\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12779
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"/* @r{@option{-Wformat} messages treated as warnings. } */\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12782
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"_Pragma (\"GCC diagnostic push\")\n"
"_Pragma (\"GCC diagnostic ignored -Wformat\")\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12784
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"/* @r{@option{-Wformat} messages suppressed. } */\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12786 c-intro-and-ref.texi:12790
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"_Pragma (\"GCC diagnostic pop\")\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12788
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"/* @r{@option{-Wformat} messages treated as warnings again. } */\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12792
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"/* @r{@option{-Wformat} messages treated as errors again. } */\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12795
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"/* @r{This is an excess @samp{pop} that matches no @samp{push}. } */\n"
"_Pragma (\"GCC diagnostic pop\")\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12798
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"/* @r{@option{-Wformat} messages treated once again}\n"
"   @r{as specified by the GCC command-line options.}  */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12808
msgid ""
"These pragmas enable a particular optimization for specific function "
"definitions.  The settings take effect at the end of a function definition, "
"so the clean place to use these pragmas is between function definitions."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12810
#, no-wrap
msgid "#pragma GCC optimize @var{optimization}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12811
#, no-wrap
msgid "_Pragma (\"GCC optimize @var{optimization}\")"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12814
msgid ""
"These pragmas enable the optimization @var{optimization} for the following "
"functions.  For example,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12817
#, no-wrap
msgid "_Pragma (\"GCC optimize -fforward-propagate\")\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12824
msgid ""
"says to apply the @samp{forward-propagate} optimization to all following "
"function definitions.  Specifying optimizations for individual functions, "
"rather than for the entire program, is rare but can be useful for getting "
"around a bug in the compiler."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12829
msgid ""
"If @var{optimization} does not correspond to a defined optimization option, "
"the pragma is erroneous.  To turn off an optimization, use the corresponding "
"@samp{-fno-} option, such as @samp{-fno-forward-propagate}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12830
#, no-wrap
msgid "#pragma GCC target @var{optimizations}"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12831
#, no-wrap
msgid "_Pragma (\"GCC target @var{optimizations}\")"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12834
msgid ""
"The pragma @samp{GCC target} is similar to @samp{GCC optimize} but is used "
"for platform-specific optimizations.  Thus,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12837
#, no-wrap
msgid "_Pragma (\"GCC target popcnt\")\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12843
msgid ""
"activates the optimization @samp{popcnt} for all following function "
"definitions.  This optimization is supported on a few common targets but not "
"on others."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12844
#, no-wrap
msgid "#pragma GCC push_options"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12845
#, no-wrap
msgid "_Pragma (\"GCC push_options\")"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12848
msgid ""
"The @samp{push_options} pragma saves on a stack the current settings "
"specified with the @samp{target} and @samp{optimize} pragmas."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12849
#, no-wrap
msgid "#pragma GCC pop_options"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12850
#, no-wrap
msgid "_Pragma (\"GCC pop_options\")"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12852
msgid "The @samp{pop_options} pragma pops saved settings from that stack."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12854
msgid "Here's an example of using this stack."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12858
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"_Pragma (\"GCC push_options\")\n"
"_Pragma (\"GCC optimize forward-propagate\")\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12861
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"/* @r{Functions to compile}\n"
"   @r{with the @code{forward-propagate} optimization.} */\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12864
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"_Pragma (\"GCC pop_options\")\n"
"/* @r{Ends enablement of @code{forward-propagate}.} */\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12866
#, no-wrap
msgid "#pragma GCC reset_options"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemx
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12867
#, no-wrap
msgid "_Pragma (\"GCC reset_options\")"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12870
msgid ""
"Clears all pragma-defined @samp{target} and @samp{optimize} optimization "
"settings."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12874
#, no-wrap
msgid "static assertions"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12875
#, no-wrap
msgid "_Static_assert"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12881
msgid ""
"You can add compiler-time tests for necessary conditions into your code "
"using @code{_Static_assert}.  This can be useful, for example, to check that "
"the compilation target platform supports the type sizes that the code "
"expects.  For example,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12885
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"_Static_assert ((sizeof (long int) >= 8),\n"
"    \"long int needs to be at least 8 bytes\");\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12891
msgid ""
"reports a compile-time error if compiled on a system with long integers "
"smaller than 8 bytes, with @samp{long int needs to be at least 8 bytes} as "
"the error message."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12897
msgid ""
"Since calls @code{_Static_assert} are processed at compile time, the "
"expression must be computable at compile time and the error message must be "
"a literal string.  The expression can refer to the sizes of variables, but "
"can't refer to their values.  For example, the following static assertion is "
"invalid for two reasons:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12902
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"char *error_message\n"
"  = \"long int needs to be at least 8 bytes\";\n"
"int size_of_long_int = sizeof (long int);\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12904
#, no-wrap
msgid "_Static_assert (size_of_long_int == 8, error_message);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12909
msgid ""
"The expression @code{size_of_long_int == 8} isn't computable at compile "
"time, and the error message isn't a literal string."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12912
msgid ""
"You can, though, use preprocessor definition values with "
"@code{_Static_assert}:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12916
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"#define LONG_INT_ERROR_MESSAGE \"long int needs to be \\\n"
"at least 8 bytes\"\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12919
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"_Static_assert ((sizeof (long int) == 8),\n"
"  LONG_INT_ERROR_MESSAGE);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12924
msgid ""
"Static assertions are permitted wherever a statement or declaration is "
"permitted, including at top level in the file, and also inside the "
"definition of a type."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12927
msgid ""
"@strong{Note:} The @code{==} used instead of @code{>=} probably makes the "
"program wrong but not invalid."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12936
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"union y\n"
"@{\n"
"  int i;\n"
"  int *ptr;\n"
"  _Static_assert (sizeof (int *) == sizeof (int),\n"
"\t\t  \"Pointer and int not same size\");\n"
"@};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12940
#, no-wrap
msgid "type alignment"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12941
#, no-wrap
msgid "alignment of type"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12942
#, no-wrap
msgid "_Alignof"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12943
#, no-wrap
msgid "__alignof__"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12948
msgid ""
"Code for device drivers and other communication with low-level hardware "
"sometimes needs to be concerned with the alignment of data objects in memory."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12956
msgid ""
"Each data type has a required @dfn{alignment}, always a power of 2, that "
"says at which memory addresses an object of that type can validly start.  A "
"valid address for the type must be a multiple of its alignment.  If a type's "
"alignment is 1, that means it can validly start at any address.  If a type's "
"alignment is 2, that means it can only start at an even address.  If a "
"type's alignment is 4, that means it can only start at an address that is a "
"multiple of 4."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12963
msgid ""
"The alignment of a type (except @code{char}) can vary depending on the kind "
"of computer in use.  To refer to the alignment of a type in a C program, use "
"@code{_Alignof}, whose syntax parallels that of @code{sizeof}.  Like "
"@code{sizeof}, @code{_Alignof} is a compile-time operation, and it doesn't "
"compute the value of the expression used as its argument."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12968
msgid ""
"Nominally, each integer and floating-point type has an alignment equal to "
"the largest power of 2 that divides its size.  Thus, @code{int} with size 4 "
"has a nominal alignment of 4, and @code{long long int} with size 8 has a "
"nominal alignment of 8."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12973
msgid ""
"However, each kind of computer generally has a maximum alignment, and no "
"type needs more alignment than that.  If the computer's maximum alignment is "
"4 (which is common), then no type's alignment is more than 4."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12977
msgid ""
"The size of any type is always a multiple of its alignment; that way, in an "
"array whose elements have that type, all the elements are properly aligned "
"if the first one is."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12982
msgid ""
"These rules apply to all real computers today, but some embedded controllers "
"have odd exceptions.  We don't have references to cite for them."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12985
msgid ""
"Ordinary C code guarantees that every object of a given type is in fact "
"aligned as that type requires."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12990
msgid ""
"If the operand of @code{_Alignof} is a structure field, the value is the "
"alignment it requires.  It may have a greater alignment by coincidence, due "
"to the other fields, but @code{_Alignof} is not concerned about that.  "
"@xref{Structures}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12994
msgid ""
"Older versions of GNU C used the keyword @code{__alignof__} for this, but "
"now that the feature has been standardized, it is better to use the standard "
"keyword @code{_Alignof}."
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12995
#, no-wrap
msgid "_Alignas"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:12996
#, no-wrap
msgid "__aligned__"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13001
msgid ""
"You can explicitly specify an alignment requirement for a particular "
"variable or structure field by adding @code{_Alignas (@var{alignment})} to "
"the declaration, where @var{alignment} is a power of 2 or a type name.  For "
"instance:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13004
#, no-wrap
msgid "char _Alignas (8) x;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13011
#, no-wrap
msgid "char _Alignas (double) x;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13017
msgid ""
"specifies that @code{x} must start on an address that is a multiple of 8.  "
"However, if @var{alignment} exceeds the maximum alignment for the machine, "
"that maximum is how much alignment @code{x} will get."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13021
msgid ""
"The older GNU C syntax for this feature looked like @code{__attribute__ "
"((__aligned__ (@var{alignment})))} to the declaration, and was added after "
"the variable.  For instance:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13024
#, no-wrap
msgid "char x __attribute__ ((__aligned__ 8));\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13030
#, no-wrap
msgid "aliasing (of storage)"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13031
#, no-wrap
msgid "pointer type conversion"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13032
#, no-wrap
msgid "type conversion, pointer"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13037
msgid ""
"We have already presented examples of casting a @code{void *} pointer to "
"another pointer type, and casting another pointer type to @code{void *}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13043
msgid ""
"One common kind of pointer cast is guaranteed safe: casting the value "
"returned by @code{malloc} and related functions (@pxref{Dynamic Memory "
"Allocation}).  It is safe because these functions do not save the pointer "
"anywhere else; the only way the program will access the newly allocated "
"memory is via the pointer just returned."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13047
msgid ""
"In fact, C allows casting any pointer type to any other pointer type.  Using "
"this to access the same place in memory using two different data types is "
"called @dfn{aliasing}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13054
msgid ""
"Aliasing is necessary in some programs that do sophisticated memory "
"management, such as GNU Emacs, but most C programs don't need to do "
"aliasing.  When it isn't needed, @strong{stay away from it!} To do aliasing "
"correctly requires following the rules stated below.  Otherwise, the "
"aliasing may result in malfunctions when the program runs."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13056
msgid "The rest of this appendix explains the pitfalls and rules of aliasing."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13064 c-intro-and-ref.texi:13067
#, no-wrap
msgid "Aliasing Alignment"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13064
msgid "Memory alignment considerations for casting between pointer types."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13064 c-intro-and-ref.texi:13092
#, no-wrap
msgid "Aliasing Length"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13064
msgid "Type size considerations for casting between pointer types."
msgstr ""

#. type: node
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13064 c-intro-and-ref.texi:13123
#, no-wrap
msgid "Aliasing Type Rules"
msgstr ""

#. type: menuentry
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13064
msgid ""
"Even when type alignment and size matches, aliasing can still have "
"surprising results."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13069
msgid "@appendixsection Aliasing and Alignment"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13078
msgid ""
"In order for a type-converted pointer to be valid, it must have the "
"alignment that the new pointer type requires.  For instance, on most "
"computers, @code{int} has alignment 4; the address of an @code{int} must be "
"a multiple of 4.  However, @code{char} has alignment 1, so the address of a "
"@code{char} is usually not a multiple of 4.  Taking the address of such a "
"@code{char} and casting it to @code{int *} probably results in an invalid "
"pointer.  Trying to dereference it may cause a @code{SIGBUS} signal, "
"depending on the platform in use (@pxref{Signals})."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13086
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"foo ()\n"
"@{\n"
"  char i[4];\n"
"  int *p = (int *) &i[1]; /* @r{Misaligned pointer!} */\n"
"  return *p;              /* @r{Crash!} */\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13091
msgid ""
"This requirement is never a problem when casting the return value of "
"@code{malloc} because that function always returns a pointer with as much "
"alignment as any type can require."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13094
msgid "@appendixsection Aliasing and Length"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13099
msgid ""
"When converting a pointer to a different pointer type, make sure the object "
"it really points to is at least as long as the target of the converted "
"pointer.  For instance, suppose @code{p} has type @code{int *} and it's cast "
"as follows:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13102
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int *p;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13107
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct\n"
"  @{\n"
"    double d, e, f;\n"
"  @} foo;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13109
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct foo *q = (struct foo *)p;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13111
#, no-wrap
msgid "q->f = 5.14159;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13122
msgid ""
"the value @code{q->f} will run past the end of the @code{int} that @code{p} "
"points to.  If @code{p} was initialized to the start of an array of type "
"@code{int[6]}, the object is long enough for three @code{double}s.  But if "
"@code{p} points to something shorter, @code{q->f} will run on beyond the end "
"of that, overlaying some other data.  Storing that will garble that other "
"data.  Or it could extend past the end of memory space and cause a "
"@code{SIGSEGV} signal (@pxref{Signals})."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13125
msgid "@appendixsection Type Rules for Aliasing"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13131
msgid ""
"C code that converts a pointer to a different pointer type can use the "
"pointers to access the same memory locations with two different data types.  "
"If the same address is accessed with different types in a single control "
"thread, optimization can make the code do surprising things (in effect, make "
"it malfunction)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13136
msgid ""
"Here's a concrete example where aliasing that can change the code's behavior "
"when it is optimized.  We assume that @code{float} is 4 bytes long, like "
"@code{int}, and so is every pointer.  Thus, the structures @code{struct a} "
"and @code{struct b} are both 8 bytes."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13141
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"#include <stdio.h>\n"
"struct a @{ int size; char *data; @};\n"
"struct b @{ float size; char *data; @};\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13152
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"void sub (struct a *p, struct b *q)\n"
"@{\n"
"  int x;\n"
"  p->size = 0;\n"
"  q->size = 1;\n"
"  x = p->size;\n"
"  printf(\"x       =%d\\n"
"\", x);\n"
"  printf(\"p->size =%d\\n"
"\", (int)p->size);\n"
"  printf(\"q->size =%d\\n"
"\", (int)q->size);\n"
"@}\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13158
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int main(void)\n"
"@{\n"
"  struct a foo;\n"
"  struct a *p = &foo;\n"
"  struct b *q = (struct b *) &foo;\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13161
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  sub (p, q);\n"
"@}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13167
msgid ""
"This code works as intended when compiled without optimization.  All the "
"operations are carried out sequentially as written.  The code sets @code{x} "
"to @code{p->size}, but what it actually gets is the bits of the floating "
"point number 1, as type @code{int}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13171
msgid ""
"However, when optimizing, the compiler is allowed to assume (mistakenly, "
"here) that @code{q} does not point to the same storage as @code{p}, because "
"their data types are not allowed to alias."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13176
msgid ""
"From this assumption, the compiler can deduce (falsely, here) that the "
"assignment into @code{q->size} has no effect on the value of @code{p->size}, "
"which must therefore still be 0.  Thus, @code{x} will be set to 0."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13180
msgid ""
"GNU C, following the C standard, @emph{defines} this optimization as "
"legitimate.  Code that misbehaves when optimized following these rules is, "
"by definition, incorrect C code."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13185
msgid ""
"The rules for storage aliasing in C are based on the two data types: the "
"type of the object, and the type it is accessed through.  The rules permit "
"accessing part of a storage object of type @var{t} using only these types:"
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13189
msgid "@var{t}."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13192
msgid "A type compatible with @var{t}.  @xref{Compatible Types}."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13195
msgid "A signed or unsigned version of one of the above."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13199
msgid "A qualified version of one of the above.  @xref{Type Qualifiers}."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13208
msgid ""
"An array, structure (@pxref{Structures}), or union type (@code{Unions}) that "
"contains one of the above, either directly as a field or through multiple "
"levels of fields.  If @var{t} is @code{double}, this would include "
"@code{struct s @{ union @{ double d[2]; int i[4]; @} u; int i; @};} because "
"there's a @code{double} inside it somewhere."
msgstr ""

#. type: itemize
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13211
msgid "A character type."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13214
msgid "What do these rules say about the example in this subsection?"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13221
msgid ""
"For @code{foo.size} (equivalently, @code{a->size}), @var{t} is @code{int}.  "
"The type @code{float} is not allowed as an aliasing type by those rules, so "
"@code{b->size} is not supposed to alias with elements of @code{a}.  Based on "
"that assumption, GNU C makes a permitted optimization that was not, in this "
"case, consistent with what the programmer intended the program to do."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13226
msgid ""
"Whether GCC actually performs type-based aliasing analysis depends on the "
"details of the code.  GCC has other ways to determine (in some cases)  "
"whether objects alias, and if it gets a reliable answer that way, it won't "
"fall back on type-based heuristics."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13233
msgid ""
"The importance of knowing the type-based aliasing rules is not so as to "
"ensure that the optimization is done where it would be safe, but so as to "
"ensure it is @emph{not} done in a way that would break the program.  You can "
"turn off type-based aliasing analysis by giving GCC the option "
"@option{-fno-strict-aliasing}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13236
#, no-wrap
msgid "digraphs"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13242
msgid ""
"C accepts aliases for certain characters.  Apparently in the 1990s some "
"computer systems had trouble inputting these characters, or trouble "
"displaying them.  These digraphs almost never appear in C programs nowadays, "
"but we mention them for completeness."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13244
#, no-wrap
msgid "<:"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13246
msgid "An alias for @samp{[}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13246
#, no-wrap
msgid ":>"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13248
msgid "An alias for @samp{]}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13248
#, no-wrap
msgid "<%"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13250
msgid "An alias for @samp{@{}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13250
#, no-wrap
msgid "%>"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13252
msgid "An alias for @samp{@}}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13252
#, no-wrap
msgid "%:"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13256
msgid ""
"An alias for @samp{#}, used for preprocessing directives "
"(@pxref{Directives}) and macros (@pxref{Macros})."
msgstr ""

#. type: appendix
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13259
#, no-wrap
msgid "Attributes in Declarations"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13260
#, no-wrap
msgid "attributes"
msgstr ""

#. type: findex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13261
#, no-wrap
msgid "__attribute__"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13267
msgid ""
"You can specify certain additional requirements in a declaration, to get "
"fine-grained control over code generation, and helpful informational "
"messages during compilation.  We use a few attributes in code examples "
"throughout this manual, including"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13269
#, no-wrap
msgid "aligned"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13272
msgid ""
"The @code{aligned} attribute specifies a minimum alignment for a variable or "
"structure field, measured in bytes:"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13275
#, no-wrap
msgid "int foo __attribute__ ((aligned (8))) = 0;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13281
msgid ""
"This directs GNU C to allocate @code{foo} at an address that is a multiple "
"of 8 bytes.  However, you can't force an alignment bigger than the "
"computer's maximum meaningful alignment."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13282
#, no-wrap
msgid "packed"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13285
msgid ""
"The @code{packed} attribute specifies to compact the fields of a structure "
"by not leaving gaps between fields.  For example,"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13292
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"struct __attribute__ ((packed)) bar\n"
"@{\n"
"  char a;\n"
"  int b;\n"
"@};\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13299
msgid ""
"allocates the integer field @code{b} at byte 1 in the structure, immediately "
"after the character field @code{a}.  The packed structure is just 5 bytes "
"long (assuming @code{int} is 4 bytes) and its alignment is 1, that of "
"@code{char}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13300
#, no-wrap
msgid "deprecated"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13304
msgid ""
"Applicable to both variables and functions, the @code{deprecated} attribute "
"tells the compiler to issue a warning if the variable or function is ever "
"used in the source file."
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13307
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"int old_foo __attribute__ ((deprecated));\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: example
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13309
#, no-wrap
msgid "int old_quux () __attribute__ ((deprecated));\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13311
#, no-wrap
msgid "__noinline__"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13317
msgid ""
"The @code{__noinline__} attribute, in a function's declaration or "
"definition, specifies never to inline calls to that function.  All calls to "
"that function, in a compilation unit where it has this attribute, will be "
"compiled to invoke the separately compiled function.  @xref{Inline Function "
"Definitions}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13318
#, no-wrap
msgid "__noclone__"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13323
msgid ""
"The @code{__noclone__} attribute, in a function's declaration or definition, "
"specifies never to clone that function.  Thus, there will be only one "
"compiled version of the function.  @xref{Label Value Caveats}, for more "
"information about cloning."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13324
#, no-wrap
msgid "always_inline"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13330
msgid ""
"The @code{always_inline} attribute, in a function's declaration or "
"definition, specifies to inline all calls to that function (unless something "
"about the function makes inlining impossible).  This applies to all calls to "
"that function in a compilation unit where it has this attribute.  "
"@xref{Inline Function Definitions}."
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13331
#, no-wrap
msgid "gnu_inline"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13336
msgid ""
"The @code{gnu_inline} attribute, in a function's declaration or definition, "
"specifies to handle the @code{inline} keyword the way GNU C originally "
"implemented it, many years before ISO C said anything about inlining.  "
"@xref{Inline Function Definitions}."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13341
msgid ""
"For full documentation of attributes, see the GCC manual.  @xref{Attribute "
"Syntax, Attribute Syntax, System Headers, gcc, Using the GNU Compiler "
"Collection}."
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13344
#, no-wrap
msgid "signal"
msgstr ""

#. type: cindex
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13345
#, no-wrap
msgid "handler (for signal)"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13346 c-intro-and-ref.texi:13357
#, no-wrap
msgid "SIGSEGV"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13347 c-intro-and-ref.texi:13362
#, no-wrap
msgid "SIGFPE"
msgstr ""

#. type: item
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13348 c-intro-and-ref.texi:13367
#, no-wrap
msgid "SIGBUS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13352
msgid ""
"Some program operations bring about an error condition called a "
"@dfn{signal}.  These signals terminate the program, by default."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13355
msgid ""
"There are various different kinds of signals, each with a name.  We have "
"seen several such error conditions through this manual:"
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13361
msgid ""
"This signal is generated when a program tries to read or write outside the "
"memory that is allocated for it, or to write memory that can only be read.  "
"The name is an abbreviation for ``segmentation violation''."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13366
msgid ""
"This signal indicates a fatal arithmetic error.  The name is an abbreviation "
"for ``floating-point exception'', but covers all types of arithmetic errors, "
"including division by zero and overflow."
msgstr ""

#. type: table
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13373
msgid ""
"This signal is generated when an invalid pointer is dereferenced, typically "
"the result of dereferencing an uninitialized pointer.  It is similar to "
"@code{SIGSEGV}, except that @code{SIGSEGV} indicates invalid access to valid "
"memory, while @code{SIGBUS} indicates an attempt to access an invalid "
"address."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13378
msgid ""
"These kinds of signal allow the program to specify a function as a "
"@dfn{signal handler}.  When a signal has a handler, it doesn't terminate the "
"program; instead it calls the handler."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: c-intro-and-ref.texi:13385
msgid ""
"There are many other kinds of signal; here we list only those that come from "
"run-time errors in C operations.  The rest have to do with the functioning "
"of the operating system.  The GNU C Library Reference Manual gives more "
"explanation about signals (@pxref{Program Signal Handling, The GNU C "
"Library, , libc, The GNU C Library Reference Manual})."
msgstr ""

